window.SEARCH_INDEX = [{"u": "index.html", "t": "RingDotNet", "h": "", "x": "RingDotNet Call any .NET class from Ring with simple syntax. Build Windows Forms and WPF programs, web servers with ASP.NET Core, and databases with Entity Framework \u2014 all in Ring. Get started New to Ring? See the applications Function index 122 Namespaces 5,972 Wrapped classes 34 Forms samples 10 WPF samples 45 Applications 60 Test files"}, {"u": "index.html#a-first-taste", "t": "RingDotNet", "h": "A first taste", "x": "A first taste # load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.Text\" } oSB = new StringBuilder(\"Hello\") oSB.Append(\", \").Append(\"Ring!\") ? oSB.ToString() # Hello, Ring! ? oSB.Length # 12 ? new Math().Max(3,9) # 9 ? new Math().Sqrt(144) # 12 The classes are real Ring classes with the same method names as the .NET ones \u2014 like RingQt for Qt \u2014 so code completion, inheritance and ordinary Ring syntax just work. There is no eval and no string-based dispatch in your code."}, {"u": "index.html#what-is-inside", "t": "RingDotNet", "h": "What is inside", "x": "What is inside # \ud83c\udf93 Tutorials Step-by-step lessons, grouped by topic: text, numbers, files, networking, security, databases, windows and more. Each one has a program with its real output. \ud83d\udd0c Simple syntax oObj.Method(), oObj.Property = x, enums as strings, events with setClickEvent(). \ud83d\udcda The class library Strings, dates, files, regex, networking, crypto, XML, JSON, reflection, LINQ and more. \ud83d\udd01 Callbacks and events Pass Ring code wherever .NET expects a delegate, as RingQt does with events. \ud83e\ude9f Windows Forms All controls wrapped, plus short helpers: uiForm(), uiButton(), UiCanvas for drawing. \ud83c\udfa8 WPF "}, {"u": "index.html#see-it-running", "t": "RingDotNet", "h": "See it running", "x": "See it running #"}, {"u": "index.html#how-the-documentation-is-organized", "t": "RingDotNet", "h": "How the documentation is organized", "x": "How the documentation is organized # Part What it is for Start Overview , Getting started (install, first program, the startup modes) and How it works Tutorials learning by building programs. They are grouped into chapters by topic (the basics, text and data formats, numbers and dates, collections, files, processes, networking, security, databases, Windows Forms, WPF, projects, libraries and release). Read The basics first; then take the chapter you need Guides how each area works and which functions and classes it has: the language bridge ( Core concepts first, then Advanced .NET types when a"}, {"u": "index.html#how-the-package-is-organized", "t": "RingDotNet", "h": "How the package is organized", "x": "How the package is organized # Folder Contents bin/ ring_dotnet.dll (the C extension), the managed bridge, ASP.NET and EF assemblies libraries/dotnetlib/ The Ring library: functions, the generated wrapper classes, GUI/WPF/web/data helpers extensions/ringdotnet/ C source of the extension and C# source of the bridge, web server and data layer samples/UsingDotNet/tutorials/ The program of each tutorial, named by chapter ( ch01_first_look.ring ...) samples/UsingDotNet/introduction/ Short console samples for each area samples/UsingDotNet/Forms/ and Wpf/ One small window program for each feature sam"}, {"u": "getting-started.html", "t": "Getting started", "h": "", "x": "New to Ring? The appendix has a Quick introduction to Ring (about 15 minutes); read it first if you have never written a Ring program."}, {"u": "getting-started.html#requirements", "t": "Getting started", "h": "Requirements", "x": "Requirements # You need Why Ring 1.28 or newer The language. The package is a normal Ring package. .NET 6 or newer (tested with .NET 9) The runtime that RingDotNet hosts inside the Ring process. Windows Desktop runtime Only for Windows Forms and WPF programs (it is part of the .NET Desktop runtime installer). ASP.NET Core runtime Only for web programs ( \"RingDotNet.Web\" ). .NET SDK Only to build C# libraries and to get NuGet packages ( dotnet publish ): A NuGet package . Note The package is built and tested on Windows with Ring 1.28 and .NET 9. The C extension and the managed bridge are portab"}, {"u": "getting-started.html#install", "t": "Getting started", "h": "Install", "x": "Install # Install the package with RingPM , the Ring package manager, in one step: ringpm install ringdotnet from ringpackages That is all. The package is one zip file ( extensions/ringdotnet.zip ): RingPM copies it and its setup.ring extracts it into your Ring folder, which is much faster than installing thousands of files one by one. It brings the library, the extension ( ring_dotnet.dll ), the managed assemblies, the samples and the applications, so every program can start with: load \"dotnet.ring\" You do not need to set a path or copy files. To test the installation, run a sample from the R"}, {"u": "getting-started.html#linux-and-macos", "t": "Getting started", "h": "Linux and macOS", "x": "Linux and macOS # The package works on Linux (tested on Ubuntu 24.04, x64, with .NET 9) and should work on macOS. The extension is a C library, so it is built once for your system; the managed files are the same on every system and are installed in ring/bin by the package. cd ring/extensions/ringdotnet sh buildgcc.sh # Linux (macOS: sh buildclang.sh); writes libring_dotnet.so to ring/lib The build needs the Ring headers and library ( ring/language/include and ring/lib ). Install the .NET runtime (not only the SDK) with the packages of your system or with the dotnet-install script, and for the "}, {"u": "getting-started.html#your-first-program", "t": "Getting started", "h": "Your first program", "x": "Your first program # # The first program: simple syntax, the namespaces are opened with using load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.Text\" } oSB = new StringBuilder(\"Hello\") oSB.Append(\", \").Append(\"Ring!\") ? oSB.ToString() ? oSB.Length ? new Math().Max(3,9) ? \"Running on .NET \" + new Environment().Version.ToString() Hello, Ring! 12 9 Running on .NET 9.0.15 The using { } block opens the .NET namespaces, like using System; in C#. After it, every class of those namespaces is a Ring class with the same name: new StringBuilder(\"Hello\") , oSB.Append(...) , oSB.Length , new Math().Max(3,9) . Th"}, {"u": "getting-started.html#the-startup-modes", "t": "Getting started", "h": "The startup modes", "x": "The startup modes # The first .NET call starts the runtime, and the runtime can be started in one mode only. You do not have to choose it: the using { } block looks at the names and starts the mode that the program needs. The block names... Mode that is started Use it for only System... namespaces the base runtime Console programs, files, networking, JSON, XML, reflection, LINQ System.Windows* or System.Drawing* Windows Desktop runtime, Ring thread becomes STA Windows Forms, WPF Microsoft.AspNetCore* or \"RingDotNet.Web\" ASP.NET Core runtime, loads the web server WebServer ( ASP.NET Core ) both"}, {"u": "getting-started.html#importing-namespaces", "t": "Getting started", "h": "Importing namespaces", "x": "Importing namespaces # The wrapper classes are loaded on demand , one namespace at a time, so a program only pays for what it uses. Name the namespaces in a using block, like C#. Each item must be a text; anything else is an error: using { \"System.IO\" # File, Directory, Path, StreamReader ... \"System.Text.RegularExpressions\" \"System.Windows.Forms\" # also loads the GUI helpers (uiForm, uiButton ...) } using is the same as calling dnImport(\"Namespace\") for each text; the function form is handy when the name is computed. If a method returns an object whose class belongs to a namespace you did not"}, {"u": "getting-started.html#your-first-window", "t": "Getting started", "h": "Your first window", "x": "Your first window # # A button that counts its clicks load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System.Drawing\" \"System.Windows.Forms\" } nClicks = 0 oForm = uiForm(\"Click counter\",260,130) oCount = uiLabel(oForm,\"0 clicks\",20,15,220,24) uiButton(oForm,\"Click me\",20,50,100,32,\"clicked()\") uiRun(oForm) # -------------------------------------------------------------------------- func clicked nClicks++ oCount.Text = \"\" + nClicks + \" clicks\" samples/UsingDotNet/Forms/02_button_click.ring \u2014 a form, a label and a button Run it with ring samples/UsingDotNet/Forms/02_button_click.ring (Windows). The tutorial Your fi"}, {"u": "getting-started.html#run-the-tests", "t": "Getting started", "h": "Run the tests", "x": "Run the tests # Every feature has a test, and every sample and application can test itself: sh samples/UsingDotNet/tests/run.sh ring.exe # runs tests, samples and applications (add --quick for a short run) ring samples/UsingDotNet/applications/Calculator/Calculator.ring --selftest # drives the form and prints SELFTEST OK Read Testing for how the self tests work."}, {"u": "getting-started.html#where-to-go-next", "t": "Getting started", "h": "Where to go next", "x": "Where to go next # Tutorials : lessons grouped by topic; start with the chapter The basics . The program of each lesson is in samples/UsingDotNet/tutorials . Core concepts : how .NET names, overloads, properties, enums and events look in Ring. Windows Forms and the applications . ASP.NET Core and Entity Framework Core for servers and databases. Rules and limits before you write a long program."}, {"u": "how-it-works.html", "t": "How it works", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "how-it-works.html#the-layers", "t": "How it works", "h": "The layers", "x": "The layers # Your Ring program oSB = new StringBuilder(\"Hi\") \u00b7 oForm.Text = \"Hello\" \u00b7 oBtn.setClickEvent(\"...\") dotnetlib (Ring library) generated wrapper classes \u00b7 using \u00b7 dnCallback \u00b7 gui.ring \u00b7 wpf.ring \u00b7 web.ring \u00b7 data.ring ring_dotnet.dll (C extension) starts the runtime through hostfxr \u00b7 sends requests \u00b7 runs Ring callbacks RingDotNet.Bridge.dll (C#): reflection, handle table, type conversion, delegates .NET class library WinForms \u00b7 WPF \u00b7 ASP.NET \u00b7 EF Your DLLs and NuGet packages"}, {"u": "how-it-works.html#the-extension-and-the-bridge", "t": "How it works", "h": "The extension and the bridge", "x": "The extension and the bridge # ring_dotnet.dll is a normal Ring extension written in C. When the first .NET call is made it: finds the installed .NET runtime and starts it inside the Ring process with hostfxr (no separate process, no IPC), loads RingDotNet.Bridge.dll , a small C# assembly, keeps one entry point for requests: dotnet_invoke(request) . (The extension also has the functions for callbacks and for releasing objects, below.) The bridge uses reflection. A Ring call such as oSB.Append(\", \") becomes a request call [handle, \"Append\", \", \"] ; the bridge finds the right overload from the a"}, {"u": "how-it-works.html#handles", "t": "How it works", "h": "Handles", "x": "Handles # .NET objects never leave .NET. Ring holds a handle (a number) inside a DotNetObject , and the bridge keeps the real object in a table. The generated wrapper classes are DotNetObject s with typed methods, so oSB.Append(...) and oSB.callMethod(\"Append\",[...]) do the same thing. Releasing objects # A handle keeps its object alive, so handles must be released. Each wrapper object holds a managed pointer (a Ring pointer with a function that runs when Ring drops the value). When the wrapper is dropped, the pointer queues the handle, and the queued handles are released in groups of 32, so a"}, {"u": "how-it-works.html#the-wire-format", "t": "How it works", "h": "The wire format", "x": "The wire format # Values travel as a compact tagged text: s5:Hello (string), n2:42 (number), l... (list), o3:17 (object handle), c... (callback), e... (error). Numbers that are not whole travel as the 8 bytes of the double, so there is no loss of precision ; the number becomes the type of the parameter that receives it, and for a parameter of type object a whole number is an Int32 ( Core concepts ). Binary data ( byte[] ) travels as a Ring string: a Ring text can hold any byte. A ByteBuffer object ( dnBuffer ) is the one case where Ring holds a live array, for the methods that fill an array th"}, {"u": "how-it-works.html#generated-wrapper-classes", "t": "How it works", "h": "Generated wrapper classes", "x": "Generated wrapper classes # tools/dotnettools/gendotnet.ring reads the manifest ( tools/dotnettools/dotnetmanifest.ring ) and uses reflection to write one Ring class per .NET class into libraries/dotnetlib/generated/ (two files per namespace: a loader and the classes). These files are plain Ring source with real inheritance, properties (as attributes with getX / setX ), events and enums. using { \"System.IO\" } loads one namespace on demand. A class index (a list of every class and its namespace, loaded the first time it is needed) lets the library import the right namespace automatically when a"}, {"u": "how-it-works.html#results-are-wrapped-by-their-real-type", "t": "How it works", "h": "Results are wrapped by their real type", "x": "Results are wrapped by their real type # When a method returns an object, the bridge sends the list of its type, its base types and its interfaces (the interface named after the class first, then the ones that inherit the most interfaces: a class that is not public is wrapped as its interface). dnWrapObject looks for the most specific generated class (importing its namespace if needed) and creates it. If no class exists the result is a plain DotNetObject \u2014 use callMethod and getProp on it. (A delegate type is a callback and has a class only when extension methods extend it.) A class that is no"}, {"u": "how-it-works.html#callbacks-ring-code-called-from-net", "t": "How it works", "h": "Callbacks: Ring code called from .NET", "x": "Callbacks: Ring code called from .NET # dnCallback(\"...\") passes Ring code where .NET expects a delegate. The bridge builds a delegate of the exact type, bound to a method of the bridge (a compiled expression for a delegate that a method cannot be bound to: ref and out parameters, more than 8 parameters); when .NET invokes it, the C extension runs the Ring function on the Ring thread and returns its result. Callbacks run synchronously during a call, which is why events from timers on other threads are ignored (see Rules and limits )."}, {"u": "how-it-works.html#threads", "t": "How it works", "h": "Threads", "x": "Threads # Thread What runs there The Ring thread All Ring code, all calls into .NET and all callbacks. With dnUseDesktop() it is an STA thread and runs the message loop in Application.Run() . .NET threads Kestrel connections, tasks, timers. They never call Ring directly. The web server queues requests and Ring handles them in serveOne() ."}, {"u": "how-it-works.html#runtime-modes", "t": "How it works", "h": "Runtime modes", "x": "Runtime modes # The runtime is configured with one of four runtimeconfig.json files: base ( RingDotNet.Bridge ), desktop ( RingDotNet.Desktop ), web ( RingDotNet.Web ) and all ( RingDotNet.All ). dnUseDesktop() , dnUseWeb() and dnUseAll() select the file before the runtime starts. dnUseData() only loads the Entity Framework assemblies; an AssemblyResolve handler in the bridge finds them next to it."}, {"u": "how-it-works.html#how-a-call-is-made", "t": "How it works", "h": "How a call is made", "x": "How a call is made # A call from Ring to .NET is a request that Ring packs, the extension sends to the bridge, and the bridge runs by reflection. A method that returns a text, a number, a yes/no or nothing, and a property read or write, is packed and read in C : the generated method calls dotnet_call with the handle of the object, the name of the method (a ready wire item) and the arguments, and the extension builds the request, calls the bridge and returns the answer. The bridge remembers the overload that it chose for a kind of arguments (the type; for a number, whole or not and its range; a"}, {"u": "how-it-works.html#packages", "t": "How it works", "h": "Packages", "x": "Packages # File Role ring_dotnet.dll C extension RingDotNet.Bridge.dll reflection bridge RingDotNet.Web.dll request queue and Kestrel host RingDotNet.Data.dll RingDb and RingTypedDb (Entity Framework) Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore*.dll , e_sqlite3.dll ... Entity Framework Core and SQLite"}, {"u": "how-it-works.html#where-to-go-next", "t": "How it works", "h": "Where to go next", "x": "Where to go next # Core concepts : how .NET names, overloads, properties and enums look in Ring. The basics : the first tutorial chapter, if you prefer to learn by writing programs."}, {"u": "tutorials.html", "t": "Tutorials", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tutorials.html#how-to-use-the-tutorials", "t": "Tutorials", "h": "How to use the tutorials", "x": "How to use the tutorials # Every tutorial has a program in the folder samples/UsingDotNet/tutorials . Read the explanation, run the program, change it, and do the exercises at the end. The programs are real: they are tested together with the rest of the package, so the output you see here is the output you will get. cd samples/UsingDotNet/tutorials ring ch01_first_look.ring # a console tutorial ring ch10_counter.ring # a window ring ch10_counter.ring --selftest # the same program checks itself and closes Tip You do not need to know .NET to start. If you know Ring, you can follow every tutorial"}, {"u": "tutorials.html#start-here", "t": "Tutorials", "h": "Start here", "x": "Start here # \ud83e\udded .NET for Ring programmers What .NET is, and the words you will meet: runtime, class library, assembly, namespace, class, object, static, property, method, event, struct, enum, null. \ud83d\udcd6 Reading the .NET documentation How to find a class on Microsoft Learn and turn what you read into Ring code."}, {"u": "tutorials.html#the-chapters", "t": "Tutorials", "h": "The chapters", "x": "The chapters # The tutorials are grouped by topic . Read The basics first, in order; after that, choose the chapter that you need. The pages inside a chapter lead from one to the next, and the last page of a chapter points to the next chapter. Chapter What it is about The basics Console programs that use the class library: text, numbers, collections, files, JSON and XML, errors, hashing, HTTP, tasks and processes. Text and data formats Regular expressions, formatting, Unicode and text file encodings, comparing and escaping text, URLs, Base64 and hexadecimal, domain names, MIME types, JSON in t"}, {"u": "tutorials.html#where-to-start", "t": "Tutorials", "h": "Where to start", "x": "Where to start # If you want to... Read understand the vocabulary .NET for Ring programmers , Reading the .NET documentation learn the class library step by step the chapter The basics work with text, JSON and XML Text and data formats calculate with numbers, dates and money Numbers, dates and money read, write and pack files Files and streams talk to other computers, build a web service Networking and the web store data Databases and services protect data and users Security build programs with windows Windows Forms , then WPF and Projects with windows go deeper: callbacks, reflection, your ow"}, {"u": "tutorials.html#the-basics", "t": "Tutorials", "h": "The basics", "x": "The basics # Console programs that use the class library: text, numbers, collections, files, JSON and XML, errors, hashing, HTTP, tasks and processes. Start here and read them in order. \ud83c\udf31 A first look Objects, types, properties, methods, static members, structs, collections and null. \ud83c\udf31 Working with text Ring's own text functions and what .NET adds: Join, Format, StringBuilder, regular expressions, Base64, encodings. \ud83c\udf31 Numbers, dates and times Math, parsing safely, big numbers, random numbers, DateTime, TimeSpan and cultures. \ud83c\udf31 Collections and LINQ Lists, dictionaries, sets, queues, stacks, sor"}, {"u": "tutorials.html#text-and-data-formats", "t": "Tutorials", "h": "Text and data formats", "x": "Text and data formats # Regular expressions, formatting, Unicode and text file encodings, comparing and escaping text, URLs, Base64 and hexadecimal, domain names, MIME types, JSON in two styles, XML and XSLT, Markdown, spelling, and two projects: a mail merge and a syntax highlighter. \ud83d\udd24 Regular expressions in depth Patterns, groups, replace, split, greedy and lazy, validation. \ud83d\udd24 Formatting numbers and dates Format strings: decimals, thousands, percent, patterns, alignment, dates and durations. \ud83d\udd24 Unicode and encodings Bytes, code points and letters; UTF-8, UTF-16 and mojibake. \ud83d\udd24 Text files and "}, {"u": "tutorials.html#numbers-dates-and-money", "t": "Tutorials", "h": "Numbers, dates and money", "x": "Numbers, dates and money # The number types and their limits, rounding, big numbers, number systems, exact money, vectors, randomness and statistics, then dates, time zones, calendars, working days and cultures. \ud83d\udd22 Number types Limits, checked conversion, TryParse, Int128 and floating point. \ud83d\udd22 Rounding and the Math class Round modes, Floor, Ceiling, Truncate, limits, and one rounding rule for money. \ud83d\udd22 Big numbers BigInteger for exact big whole numbers, and Complex numbers. \ud83d\udd22 Number systems and big numbers Binary and hex, bits, the 2^53 limit and BigInteger. \ud83d\udd22 Money Why 0.1 + 0.2 is not 0.3: who"}, {"u": "tutorials.html#collections-and-algorithms", "t": "Tutorials", "h": "Collections and algorithms", "x": "Collections and algorithms # Arrays, sets and other data structures, collections for many threads, enums and identifiers, undo and queues, recursion and an expression calculator. \ud83e\uddfa Arrays Typed arrays, Array.Sort, BinarySearch, Copy and two dimensions. \ud83e\uddfa Sets HashSet: union, intersection, difference and removing duplicates. \ud83e\uddfa More data structures PriorityQueue, SortedSet, SortedDictionary, LinkedList and BitArray. \ud83e\uddfa Collections for many threads Concurrent queue, stack, blocking collection and dictionary. \ud83e\uddfa Enums and flags Named choices as text, comma-separated flags and your own bit flags. \ud83e\uddfa I"}, {"u": "tutorials.html#files-and-streams", "t": "Tutorials", "h": "Files and streams", "x": "Files and streams # Paths, file information and disks, streams, binary files and random access, compression (GZip, ZIP, tar), file patterns, watching a folder, and projects that work on many files: a log analyzer, a duplicate finder, a backup tool, a file renamer, a file comparison and a checksum list. \ud83d\udcc1 Paths and the environment Path.Combine, special folders, valid names and environment variables. \ud83d\udcc1 File information FileInfo: size, times, attributes, read-only files, copying and temporary files. \ud83d\udcc1 Disks and free space DriveInfo, the sizes of a disk, will it fit, and sizes for people. \ud83d\udcc1 Stream"}, {"u": "tutorials.html#processes-and-performance", "t": "Tutorials", "h": "Processes and performance", "x": "Processes and performance # Asking the computer about itself, command-line tools, logging and tracing, starting and talking to other programs, tasks that wait, releasing resources, measuring speed and memory, and the Windows Registry. \u2699\ufe0f Asking the computer The system, processors, memory, drives and the running programs. \u2699\ufe0f A command-line tool Arguments, options, usage text and exit codes. \u2699\ufe0f Logging Levels, timestamps, a log file and rotation. \u2699\ufe0f Tracing Trace and listeners: memory and file listeners, indents, conditions and categories. \u2699\ufe0f Working with other programs Standard input, chaining,"}, {"u": "tutorials.html#networking-and-the-web", "t": "Tutorials", "h": "Networking and the web", "x": "Networking and the web # IP addresses and networks, TCP and UDP sockets, WebSockets, HttpClient and REST, web services and small web sites, route templates, a link shortener, and e-mail messages. \ud83c\udf10 Network tools IPAddress, Dns, Ping, interfaces and a port check, all on this computer. \ud83c\udf10 IP networks CIDR, IPNetwork.Contains and a private-address allow list. \ud83c\udf10 Sockets A TCP server and client that talk line by line. \ud83c\udf10 Messages on a TCP stream A length prefix, reading exactly n bytes, partial reads and a maximum length. \ud83c\udf10 UDP Datagrams between sockets, answers, time limits and lost messages. \ud83c\udf10 WebS"}, {"u": "tutorials.html#security", "t": "Tutorials", "h": "Security", "x": "Security # Hashing, encryption and signing, certificates, protecting data, logins and sessions, identities and claims, and rate limiting. \ud83d\udd12 Protecting and signing messages AES, HMAC (encrypt-then-MAC), PBKDF2 keys and RSA signatures. \ud83d\udd12 Digital signatures ECDsa keys, signing, verifying and sharing the public key. \ud83d\udd12 Certificates Self-signed X.509 certificates, PFX and PEM, signing and trust. \ud83d\udd12 Protecting data The Data Protection API: purposes, tampering, keys and expiring tokens. \ud83d\udd12 Login, sessions and cookies Registration, safe passwords, a session cookie and a protected page. \ud83d\udd12 Identities and c"}, {"u": "tutorials.html#databases-and-services", "t": "Tutorials", "h": "Databases and services", "x": "Databases and services # SQL and Entity Framework, reports, Microsoft Access, and the services of an application: settings and configuration, caching and dependency injection. \ud83d\uddc4\ufe0f Databases step by step Tables, rows, searches, relations, queries, transactions and files. \ud83d\uddc4\ufe0f Tabular data CSV, DataTable, DataView, Compute, sorting, filtering and grouping. \ud83d\uddc4\ufe0f Reports with SQL JOIN, GROUP BY, HAVING, LEFT JOIN and sub-queries. \ud83d\uddc4\ufe0f Entity objects and migrations Rows as tracked objects, navigation properties, and changing a database that has data. \ud83d\uddc4\ufe0f Microsoft Access databases An .accdb file through OD"}, {"u": "tutorials.html#windows-forms", "t": "Tutorials", "h": "Windows Forms", "x": "Windows Forms # Windows with controls, the keyboard and the mouse, layout, lists and grids, formatted text, the clipboard, drag and drop, dialogs, more than one window and a tray icon, printing, menus, timers, drawing, images and charts (Windows only). \ud83e\ude9f Your first window A form, a label, a button, an event \u2014 with and without the helper functions. \ud83e\ude9f A form with many controls Text boxes, number box, combo box, check box, list box and validation. \ud83e\ude9f More input controls Date picker, track bar, radio buttons, tool tips and the error marker. \ud83e\ude9f The keyboard in a form KeyPress filters, Enter and Escap"}, {"u": "tutorials.html#wpf", "t": "Tutorials", "h": "WPF", "x": "WPF # XAML windows, layout and styles, data binding, menus and tabs, lists and dynamic content, and animation (Windows only). \ud83c\udfa8 WPF XAML windows, finding elements, events and data binding. \ud83c\udfa8 WPF layout and styles Grid rows and columns, one Style for all buttons, a keypad. \ud83c\udfa8 WPF data binding Controls that follow each other in XAML: slider, text box, check box and list. \ud83c\udfa8 WPF menus and tabs Menu, checkable items, TabControl, ComboBox, DatePicker and Expander in XAML. \ud83c\udfa8 WPF lists and dynamic content Fill a ListBox from Ring, move and remove items, and make controls in code. \ud83c\udfa8 WPF animation Move, "}, {"u": "tutorials.html#projects-with-windows", "t": "Tutorials", "h": "Projects with windows", "x": "Projects with windows # Complete programs: a to-do list, Tic-Tac-Toe, a quiz, a command runner, an address book with a database, a sorting lab, a maze, an image filter lab and a chat (Windows only). \ud83e\udde9 Project: a to-do list A complete window application that remembers its tasks in a JSON file. \ud83e\udde9 Project: Tic-Tac-Toe Rules and state in testable functions, nine buttons from a loop. \ud83e\udde9 Project: a quiz Questions as data, radio buttons, one button with two jobs, a score and best scores. \ud83e\udde9 Project: a command runner A window around a command-line tool: output, errors, exit codes. \ud83e\udde9 Project: an address "}, {"u": "tutorials.html#libraries-and-packages", "t": "Tutorials", "h": "Libraries and packages", "x": "Libraries and packages # Giving Ring code to .NET (callbacks and events), exploring code with reflection and building it with expression trees, an object inspector, a library written in C#, and the generic functions; then NuGet packages and native code: how a package comes to Ring, a QR code, Excel files, pictures, PDF files, text to speech, sound, native DLLs, and a project that uses three packages together. \ud83d\udce6 Callbacks and events Give Ring code to .NET: function names, code texts, events, errors and the thread rule. \ud83d\udce6 Exploring .NET with reflection Ask a class what it can do: properties, met"}, {"u": "tutorials.html#testing-and-release", "t": "Tutorials", "h": "Testing and release", "x": "Testing and release # The mistakes that everybody makes first and their messages, programs that run on more than one system, testing your programs, and giving a program to other people. \ud83d\udce6 Common mistakes The seven errors of the first weeks, made on purpose: the message, what it means and the fix. \ud83d\udce6 More than one system One program on Windows and on Linux: paths, upper and lower case, line ends, folders, and keeping windows behind isWindows(). \ud83d\udce6 Testing your programs Testable functions, dnAssert, testing errors, self-testing windows. \ud83d\udce6 Giving your program to others Ring2EXE, what the other comp"}, {"u": "tut-concepts.html", "t": ".NET for Ring programmers", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-concepts.html#what-is-net", "t": ".NET for Ring programmers", "h": "What is .NET?", "x": "What is .NET? # .NET is a large collection of ready-made code from Microsoft, plus the program that runs it. You do not write that code \u2014 you use it. Instead of writing your own function to read a file, compress a folder, hash a password or talk to a web server, you ask .NET to do it. RingDotNet lets a Ring program ask. Two things make up .NET: Part What it is Analogy The runtime The engine that loads and runs .NET code. RingDotNet starts it inside your Ring program. The Ring virtual machine The class library Thousands of classes: text, numbers, files, networking, windows, databases... Ring's "}, {"u": "tut-concepts.html#classes-and-objects", "t": ".NET for Ring programmers", "h": "Classes and objects", "x": "Classes and objects # Almost everything in .NET is an object . An object has data and abilities ; the class is the description that the objects are made from \u2014 like a recipe and the cakes baked from it. using { \"System.Text\" } oText = new StringBuilder(\"Hello\") # create an object of the class StringBuilder You already know this from Ring: Ring has classes and objects too ( class , new ). The difference is that these classes were written by Microsoft, in C#, and live in .NET."}, {"u": "tut-concepts.html#properties-methods-and-events", "t": ".NET for Ring programmers", "h": "Properties, methods and events", "x": "Properties, methods and events # An object has three kinds of members: Member What it is In Ring Example Property something the object knows (a value you read or change) oObject.Name and oObject.Name = value oForm.Text = \"Hello\" Method something the object can do oObject.Name(arguments) oText.Append(\"!\") Event something that happens , to which you react oObject.setNameEvent(\"code\") oButton.setClickEvent(\"clicked()\")"}, {"u": "tut-concepts.html#static-members-no-object-needed", "t": ".NET for Ring programmers", "h": "Static members: no object needed", "x": "Static members: no object needed # Some classes are just a toolbox of functions. Their members are static : you call them on the class, without creating an object. Math is the classic example: you never create \"a math\". using { \"System\" } ? new Math().Max(3,9) # a static method (Ring creates a harmless helper object to call it) ? new Math().PI # a static property In the class reference a static member is marked static ."}, {"u": "tut-concepts.html#namespaces-and-assemblies", "t": ".NET for Ring programmers", "h": "Namespaces and assemblies", "x": "Namespaces and assemblies # .NET has thousands of classes, so they are organized: A namespace is a folder of classes with a dotted name: System.IO holds the file classes, System.Text holds the text classes, System.Windows.Forms holds the window classes. An assembly is a file (a .dll ) that contains the compiled classes. You rarely need to think about it. In Ring you bring a namespace in with using : using { \"System.IO\" \"System.Text\" } After that the classes of that namespace are available by their short name: File , Path , StringBuilder ."}, {"u": "tut-concepts.html#value-types-structs-and-enums", "t": ".NET for Ring programmers", "h": "Value types: structs and enums", "x": "Value types: structs and enums # Most types are classes. Two kinds of types are special: A struct is a small value : DateTime (a date and time), TimeSpan (a duration), Point (x and y). You use it like an object, but copying it copies the value. An enum is a list of named choices: the day of the week ( Monday , Tuesday ...), a window state ( Normal , Maximized ). In Ring an enum value is a text : oForm.WindowState = \"Maximized\" ."}, {"u": "tut-concepts.html#null", "t": ".NET for Ring programmers", "h": "null", "x": "null # null means \"no object\". A method that finds nothing may return null. An object that you got from .NET can be asked oObject.isNull() , and dnIsNull(value) asks it of any value."}, {"u": "tut-concepts.html#exceptions", "t": ".NET for Ring programmers", "h": "Exceptions", "x": "Exceptions # When something goes wrong \u2014 the file does not exist, the text is not a number \u2014 .NET raises an exception . In Ring an exception is a normal Ring error, and you catch it with try ... catch . The tutorial When things go wrong explains this."}, {"u": "tut-concepts.html#generics-and-collections", "t": ".NET for Ring programmers", "h": "Generics and collections", "x": "Generics and collections # A collection holds many items. .NET has a list ( List ), a dictionary ( Dictionary ), a set, a queue and a stack. They are generic : the same class works for any type of item ( List<string> , List<int> ). RingDotNet gives you ready names for the common ones: StringList , NumberList , StringDictionary ... The tutorial Collections and LINQ shows them."}, {"u": "tut-concepts.html#the-words-around-net", "t": ".NET for Ring programmers", "h": "The words around .NET", "x": "The words around .NET # Word Meaning C# The main language that .NET code is written in. You do not need to learn it to use .NET from Ring, but the documentation shows C# examples. SDK The software development kit : the compiler and tools for writing .NET programs. You only need it to build C# code (the tutorial A library written in C# ) and to get NuGet packages (the tutorial A NuGet package ). Runtime What is needed to run .NET programs. RingDotNet needs the runtime (and the desktop or ASP.NET runtime for windows and web). NuGet The package store of .NET. A NuGet package is a set of .dll file"}, {"u": "tut-concepts.html#how-ring-and-net-meet", "t": ".NET for Ring programmers", "h": "How Ring and .NET meet", "x": "How Ring and .NET meet # Ring and .NET are two different systems. RingDotNet is the bridge between them: A Ring text becomes a .NET string, a Ring number becomes a .NET number, a Ring list becomes an array. A .NET object comes back to Ring as a Ring object with the same name as the .NET class. A .NET enum is a text, a .NET bool is 1 or 0 . Ring code runs on one thread ; .NET may use many. Code that .NET wants to call back into Ring runs on the Ring thread. Read How it works when you want the details."}, {"u": "tut-concepts.html#next", "t": ".NET for Ring programmers", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Reading the .NET documentation shows how to find a class on Microsoft Learn and use it from Ring."}, {"u": "tut-reading-docs.html", "t": "Reading the .NET documentation", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-reading-docs.html#where-the-documentation-is", "t": "Reading the .NET documentation", "h": "Where the documentation is", "x": "Where the documentation is # The official reference is Microsoft Learn \u2192 .NET API browser (search for \"learn.microsoft.com dotnet api\"). Every class has a page. For example the page of System.Text.StringBuilder lists its constructors, properties, methods and events, with a short description and a C# example for each. RingDotNet also has its own class reference with the same information written for Ring: every member shows how to call it from Ring . Use it first; use Microsoft Learn when you want the long explanation."}, {"u": "tut-reading-docs.html#how-to-find-the-class-you-need", "t": "Reading the .NET documentation", "h": "How to find the class you need", "x": "How to find the class you need # Think about the job : \"read a file\" \u2192 files; \"split a date\" \u2192 dates; \"send a web request\" \u2192 HTTP. Search by words in the search box of this site (press / ) or on Microsoft Learn: read text file C# . The answer almost always names a class: File , StreamReader ... Look at the namespace shown on the page title: System.IO.File means the namespace is System.IO . Check that the class is wrapped : open api/System.IO.html in the class reference , or search the class name."}, {"u": "tut-reading-docs.html#reading-a-class-page", "t": "Reading the .NET documentation", "h": "Reading a class page", "x": "Reading a class page # A Microsoft page for a method looks like this (shortened): File.ReadAllText(String) \u2192 String File.ReadAllText(String, Encoding) \u2192 String and a C# example: string text = File.ReadAllText(@\"C:\\notes.txt\"); Console.WriteLine(text); From C# to Ring # In C# In Ring using System.IO; using { \"System.IO\" } var f = new FileInfo(path); oFile = new FileInfo(cPath) File.ReadAllText(path) new File().ReadAllText(cPath) (a static method is called on new File() ) text.Length (a property) oText.Length list.Add(\"x\") (a method) oList.Add(\"x\") button.Click += handler; (an event) oButton.set"}, {"u": "tut-reading-docs.html#a-complete-example", "t": "Reading the .NET documentation", "h": "A complete example", "x": "A complete example # Task: \"get the file name from a path\". Search \u2192 Path.GetFileName in System.IO . The documentation says: public static string GetFileName(string path) . It is static , so on a new Path() object, and it takes one text: using { \"System.IO\" } oPath = new Path() ? oPath.GetFileName(\"c:/work/report.txt\") # report.txt"}, {"u": "tut-reading-docs.html#when-a-class-is-not-wrapped", "t": "Reading the .NET documentation", "h": "When a class is not wrapped", "x": "When a class is not wrapped # Everything in the base library and in Windows Forms, WPF, ASP.NET and Entity Framework is wrapped. Other packages can be wrapped in one command: Your own assemblies and the lesson A NuGet package . Without wrapping, a few generic functions still reach any .NET type; they are explained in Core concepts and used in the later chapters of the tutorials."}, {"u": "tut-reading-docs.html#next", "t": "Reading the .NET documentation", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Start the first lesson: A first look ."}, {"u": "tut-01-first-look.html", "t": "A first look", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-01-first-look.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "A first look", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # how to start using .NET from Ring, what a type , an object , a property , a method and a static member look like, how a struct and a collection look."}, {"u": "tut-01-first-look.html#before-you-start", "t": "A first look", "h": "Before you start", "x": "Before you start # You need Ring and the RingDotNet package. Open a terminal in samples/UsingDotNet/tutorials . If something does not work, read Getting started . If Ring is new to you, read the Quick introduction to Ring in the appendix first."}, {"u": "tut-01-first-look.html#the-program", "t": "A first look", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # Read the program first; the explanations follow. The line using { ... } brings classes in. func main is where Ring starts. # a first look at .NET from Ring - runtime, types, objects, namespaces load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.Text\" \"System.Collections.Generic\" } func main # 1. The runtime that runs inside your Ring program ? \"Runtime : .NET \" + new Environment().Version.ToString() # 2. Everything in .NET is an object of some type (a class) oText = new StringBuilder(\"Hello\") oType = oText.GetType() ? \"Type of the object: \" + oType.FullName ? \"Namespace : \" + oType.Name"}, {"u": "tut-01-first-look.html#how-it-works-step-by-step", "t": "A first look", "h": "How it works, step by step", "x": "How it works, step by step # 1. The runtime # new Environment().Version asks the .NET runtime that runs inside your Ring program for its version ( Environment is a class of the System namespace that the using block opened). Nothing is installed \"next to\" Ring: the runtime is part of your program while it runs. 2. Everything is an object of some type # new StringBuilder(\"Hello\") creates an object. GetType() is a method that every .NET object has; it returns an object that describes the type. Its properties FullName , Namespace and IsClass tell you what you are holding: a class called System.Tex"}, {"u": "tut-01-first-look.html#what-you-learned", "t": "A first look", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # Idea Example a runtime inside your program new Environment().Version create an object new StringBuilder(\"Hello\") a property oText.Length a method oText.Append(\", .NET\") a static member new Math().Max(3,9) a struct and an enum DateTime , DayOfWeek a collection StringList no object isNull()"}, {"u": "tut-01-first-look.html#exercises", "t": "A first look", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Change the program to print the type of a DateTime object. Which namespace is it in? Create a StringBuilder and append your name five times with a loop. What is its Length ? Use new Math() to print the square root of 2 and the larger of 7 and 19 . Find Environment.MachineName in the class reference (namespace System ) and print it."}, {"u": "tut-01-first-look.html#next", "t": "A first look", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Working with text"}, {"u": "tut-02-text.html", "t": "Working with text", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-02-text.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Working with text", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # which text jobs Ring does by itself and which need .NET, String helpers ( Join , Format , Compare , IsNullOrWhiteSpace ), StringBuilder for long texts, regular expressions with Regex , Base64 and UTF-8."}, {"u": "tut-02-text.html#texts-between-ring-and-net", "t": "Working with text", "h": "Texts between Ring and .NET", "x": "Texts between Ring and .NET # A Ring text is a .NET string as soon as you pass it to a .NET method. A .NET method that returns a string gives you a Ring text. One rule is important: a .NET string is not an object in Ring , it is just a Ring text. That means a .NET instance method such as Trim() is not called on it. For those jobs Ring has its own functions: trim() , upper() , lower() , substr() , left() . Use Ring's functions for the basics , and .NET for what Ring does not include. The static methods of the class String ( Join , Format ...) work fine because they take the text as an argument."}, {"u": "tut-02-text.html#the-program", "t": "Working with text", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # working with text - Ring text functions, String helpers, StringBuilder, regular expressions, encodings load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.Text\" \"System.Text.RegularExpressions\" \"System.Globalization\" } func main # Ring already has many text functions: use them for the basics cSentence = \" The quick brown fox \" ? \"Ring trim/upper : [\" + upper(trim(cSentence)) + \"]\" aWords = str2list(substr(trim(cSentence),\" \",nl)) ? \"Ring words : \" + len(aWords) # .NET adds more helpers. String's static methods: oString = new String() ? \"Join : \" + oString.Join([ :separator = \"-\", :valu"}, {"u": "tut-02-text.html#how-it-works", "t": "Working with text", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # Ring first # upper(trim(cSentence)) is Ring. The words are found by turning spaces into new lines and reading each line as an item: str2list(substr(cSentence,\" \",nl)) . String's static helpers # Call Result oString.Join([ :separator = \"-\", :value = aWords ]) all the words with - between them oString.Format([ :format = \"{0} has {1} items\", :arg0 = \"The list\", :arg1 = 3 ]) {0} , {1} are replaced by the arguments oString.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(\" \") true for empty or blank text oString.Compare([ :strA = \"apple\", :strB = \"banana\" ]) -1 if the first sorts before the second, 0 if equal, 1 "}, {"u": "tut-02-text.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Working with text", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # Use Ring's own functions for basic text work; call String 's static helpers when you need Join , Format , Compare . StringBuilder builds long texts efficiently. Regex finds, tests and replaces patterns. Text, bytes and encodings are different things."}, {"u": "tut-02-text.html#exercises", "t": "Working with text", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Use Regex to find every number in \"I have 3 cats and 12 fish\" . (Pattern: [0-9]+ .) Use String.Format with a list of named values to print Ann is 31 years old from the values \"Ann\" and 31 . Write a function isEmail(cText) that returns true when Regex.IsMatch says the text looks like name@site.com . Encode your name to Base64 with Convert.ToBase64String , then decode it with Convert.FromBase64String ."}, {"u": "tut-02-text.html#next", "t": "Working with text", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Numbers, dates and times"}, {"u": "tut-03-numbers-dates.html", "t": "Numbers, dates and times", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-03-numbers-dates.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Numbers, dates and times", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # Math for rounding, roots and powers, TryParse : turn text into a number safely, BigInteger : numbers without a limit, Random , DateTime , TimeSpan , parsing dates and formatting in different cultures."}, {"u": "tut-03-numbers-dates.html#the-program", "t": "Numbers, dates and times", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # numbers, parsing, dates and times load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.Numerics\" \"System.Globalization\" } func main oMath = new Math() # Math: functions on numbers ? \"Round to 2 digits : \" + oMath.Round([ :value = 3.14159, :digits = 2 ]) ? \"Square root of 144: \" + oMath.Sqrt(144) ? \"Power 2^10 : \" + oMath.Pow(2,10) ? \"Absolute, floor : \" + oMath.Abs(-7.5) + \", \" + oMath.Floor(7.9) ? \"Min and Max : \" + oMath.Min(4,9) + \", \" + oMath.Max(4,9) # Converting text to numbers: TryParse never fails, it tells you whether it worked aResult = new Int32().TryParse(\"123\") ? \"TryParse "}, {"u": "tut-03-numbers-dates.html#how-it-works", "t": "Numbers, dates and times", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # Math # Math is a class of static functions: Round , Sqrt , Pow , Abs , Floor , Min , Max . Round has several versions (round to a whole number, round to some digits), so the program uses named arguments : Round([ :value = 3.14159, :digits = 2 ]) . The names come from the .NET documentation, and the order does not matter. TryParse: no crash # Int32.Parse(\"abc\") raises an error. TryParse never does: it returns a list [worked, value] . A method that has an out parameter in C# returns its results as a list in Ring: first the return value (here true or false), then the out values. aR"}, {"u": "tut-03-numbers-dates.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Numbers, dates and times", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # Math functions and named arguments, TryParse for safe conversion, BigInteger for exact big numbers, dates are values that you copy, never change, format patterns and cultures."}, {"u": "tut-03-numbers-dates.html#exercises", "t": "Numbers, dates and times", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Print how many days are left until the end of this year. Parse the text \"15/03/2024\" with ParseExact and a format of dd/MM/yyyy . Compute 100! with BigInteger and print how many digits it has. Throw a die 10 times with Random and count how many sixes you get. Print today's date in three cultures ( en-US , fr-FR , ja-JP )."}, {"u": "tut-03-numbers-dates.html#next", "t": "Numbers, dates and times", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Collections and LINQ"}, {"u": "tut-04-collections.html", "t": "Collections and LINQ", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-04-collections.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Collections and LINQ", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # the five common collections and when to use each, how to get a collection's items back into a Ring list, LINQ: filter, sort and summarize with a few calls."}, {"u": "tut-04-collections.html#which-collection", "t": "Collections and LINQ", "h": "Which collection?", "x": "Which collection? # Collection Use it when... Ring class List you need items in order, and to find them by position StringList , NumberList , ObjectList Dictionary you look items up by a key (a name, an id) StringDictionary , NumberDictionary , ObjectDictionary Set each value may appear only once StringSet Queue first in, first out (a line of customers) StringQueue Stack last in, first out (undo) StringStack Ring lists are flexible and fine for most programs. Use .NET collections when you need their special behavior (a set, a queue), speed with big data, or when a .NET method asks for one."}, {"u": "tut-04-collections.html#the-program", "t": "Collections and LINQ", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # collections and LINQ - lists, dictionaries, sets, queues, sorting and querying load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System.Collections.Generic\" \"System.Linq\" } func main # A list keeps items in order oFruit = new StringList() oFruit.Add(\"banana\") oFruit.Add(\"apple\") oFruit.Add(\"cherry\") ? \"Count : \" + oFruit.Count ? \"Second item : \" + oFruit.Item(1) oFruit.Sort(NULL) ? \"After Sort : \" + list2str(oFruit.toList()) ? \"Contains apple : \" + oFruit.Contains(\"apple\") ? \"Index of cherry : \" + oFruit.IndexOf(\"cherry\") # A dictionary maps keys to values oAges = new NumberDictionary() oAges.SetIte"}, {"u": "tut-04-collections.html#how-it-works", "t": "Collections and LINQ", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # A list # Add adds, Count counts, Item(1) reads by position (the first item is position 0 ), Sort(NULL) sorts in place, Contains and IndexOf search. toList() copies the items into a Ring list, so you can use for ... in . Note Sort(NULL) : Sort has several versions (with a comparer, with a range...). NULL means \"the version without parameters\". A dictionary # SetItem(\"Ann\",31) stores a value under a key; Item(\"Ann\") reads it. TryGetValue(\"Cy\") asks without failing when the key is missing, and returns the list [found, value] . A set # Adding \"red\" twice leaves one \"red\" . Sets also"}, {"u": "tut-04-collections.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Collections and LINQ", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # choose the collection by the question you ask of it, toList() brings a .NET collection to Ring, TryGetValue and NULL for methods with several versions, LINQ answers questions in one chain."}, {"u": "tut-04-collections.html#exercises", "t": "Collections and LINQ", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Put the numbers 5, 3, 8, 3, 1 into a NumberList , sort it, and print it. Count how many times each word appears in a sentence with a NumberDictionary . Use a StringStack to reverse the letters of a word. With LINQ: from [3,8,12,5,20] get the numbers above 4, doubled ( Select ), and their sum."}, {"u": "tut-04-collections.html#next", "t": "Collections and LINQ", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Files and folders"}, {"u": "tut-05-files.html", "t": "Files and folders", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-05-files.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Files and folders", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # why you use Path.Combine , reading a whole file, line by line, and writing and appending, information about a file, listing a folder, copying, zipping, cleaning up."}, {"u": "tut-05-files.html#the-classes", "t": "Files and folders", "h": "The classes", "x": "The classes # Class Job Path work with names of files and folders (no disk access) File read, write, copy, delete a file in one call Directory create, delete, list folders FileInfo information about one file: size, name, dates StreamReader / StreamWriter read or write a file piece by piece (big files) ZipFile ( System.IO.Compression ) make and open zip files"}, {"u": "tut-05-files.html#the-program", "t": "Files and folders", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # files and folders - Path, Directory, File, reading and writing, zip load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.IO\" \"System.IO.Compression\" } func main oPath = new Path() oFile = new File() oDirectory = new Directory() # Never build paths by joining text: Path.Combine uses the right separator cFolder = oPath.Combine([oPath.GetTempPath(),\"ringdotnet_files\"]) if oDirectory.Exists(cFolder) oDirectory.Delete([ :path = cFolder, :recursive = True ]) ok oDirectory.CreateDirectory(cFolder) ? \"Folder created : \" + oDirectory.Exists(cFolder) # Write a text file and read it back cNotes = "}, {"u": "tut-05-files.html#how-it-works", "t": "Files and folders", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # Paths # Never build a path by joining texts with / or \\ . Path.Combine([a,b]) uses the right separator for the computer, and Path.GetTempPath() gives a folder where temporary files are allowed. Other Path helpers: GetFileName , GetExtension , GetFileNameWithoutExtension , GetDirectoryName . They only work on the text of a path; the file does not have to exist. Ask before you act # Directory.Exists(cFolder) and File.Exists(cFile) return true or false. Directory.Delete([ :path = cFolder, :recursive = True ]) deletes a folder and everything in it. Delete has a version with one para"}, {"u": "tut-05-files.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Files and folders", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # build paths with Path , not with + , ask Exists before you read, use a reader for big files and always close it, FileInfo gives the facts about a file."}, {"u": "tut-05-files.html#exercises", "t": "Files and folders", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Write a program that counts the lines in every .ring file of a folder ( Directory.GetFiles([ :path = cFolder, :searchPattern = \"*.ring\" ]) ). Copy a file to the Documents folder ( Environment.GetFolderPath(\"MyDocuments\") ). Find the biggest file in a folder with FileInfo.Length . Write a log: each run appends a line with the date and time."}, {"u": "tut-05-files.html#next", "t": "Files and folders", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # JSON and XML"}, {"u": "tut-06-json-xml.html", "t": "JSON and XML", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-06-json-xml.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "JSON and XML", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # what JSON and XML are, reading a JSON document and its nested values, loading XML, looping over elements, XPath queries, adding an element."}, {"u": "tut-06-json-xml.html#json-and-xml-in-one-minute", "t": "JSON and XML", "h": "JSON and XML in one minute", "x": "JSON and XML in one minute # JSON looks like Ring lists and objects: {\"name\":\"Ring\",\"year\":2016,\"tags\":[\"fast\",\"small\"],\"author\":{\"first\":\"Ann\"}} { } an object of properties , [ ] an array, texts in quotes, numbers without. XML is a tree of elements with attributes : <book id=\"1\" year=\"1999\"><title>Learn Ring</title></book> JSON is lighter and used by web services; XML is older and used in files and configuration."}, {"u": "tut-06-json-xml.html#the-program", "t": "JSON and XML", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # JSON and XML - reading, writing and searching structured data load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System.Text.Json\" \"System.Xml\" } func main # JSON: turn Ring data into text oJson = new JsonSerializer() ? \"A Ring list as JSON : \" + oJson.Serialize([1,\"two\",[3,4]]) # JSON: read a document you received cJson = '{\"name\":\"Ring\",\"year\":2016,\"tags\":[\"fast\",\"small\"],\"author\":{\"first\":\"Ann\",\"country\":\"Egypt\"}}' oDocument = new JsonDocument().Parse(cJson) oRoot = oDocument.RootElement ? \"The name : \" + oRoot.GetProperty(\"name\").GetString() ? \"The year : \" + oRoot.GetProperty(\"year\").GetInt32() "}, {"u": "tut-06-json-xml.html#how-it-works", "t": "JSON and XML", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # JSON: write # JsonSerializer.Serialize(aList) turns a Ring list into JSON text: a list of values becomes an array , nested lists become nested arrays. (To make a JSON object from [name, value] pairs, the web library has webJson(...) : see the tutorial A web service with a database .) JSON: read # JsonDocument.Parse(text) reads a JSON text. RootElement is the top value. From an element you: Want Call a property of an object GetProperty(\"name\") an item of an array Item(0) how many items GetArrayLength() the value as text / number GetString() / GetInt32() Chain them to go deep: oRo"}, {"u": "tut-06-json-xml.html#what-you-learned", "t": "JSON and XML", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # JSON for web data, XML for documents, read JSON by following properties, XPath asks questions of XML."}, {"u": "tut-06-json-xml.html#exercises", "t": "JSON and XML", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Read a JSON text with an array of three numbers and print their sum. Find the cheapest book in the XML of the program with XPath and a loop. Save the changed XML to a file with oDoc.Save(cPath) and read it back. Turn the Ring list [1,[2,3],\"four\"] into JSON and read it back with JsonDocument ."}, {"u": "tut-06-json-xml.html#next", "t": "JSON and XML", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # When things go wrong"}, {"u": "tut-07-errors.html", "t": "When things go wrong", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-07-errors.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "When things go wrong", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # what an exception is and how Ring shows it, try / catch and reading the error text, asking first ( Exists , TryParse ) instead of catching, raising your own errors, cleaning up even when something fails."}, {"u": "tut-07-errors.html#exceptions", "t": "When things go wrong", "h": "Exceptions", "x": "Exceptions # When .NET cannot do what you asked, it throws an exception : it stops the normal flow and reports the problem. In Ring a .NET exception is a Ring error. Without try , it ends your program with a message. With try ... catch you decide what happens. try new Int32().Parse(\"abc\") catch ? cCatchError done cCatchError holds the message. It starts with DotNet Error: followed by the name of the exception ( System.FormatException ) and an explanation. The name tells you the kind of problem."}, {"u": "tut-07-errors.html#the-program", "t": "When things go wrong", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # when things go wrong - exceptions, try/catch, checking before you act load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.IO\" } func main # A .NET exception becomes a Ring error you can catch try new Int32().Parse(\"abc\") ? \"this line is never reached\" catch ? \"1. Caught the error:\" ? \" \" + cCatchError done # The text of the error names the .NET exception type: you can react to the kind of error try new File().ReadAllText(\"c:\\this\\file\\does\\not\\exist.txt\") catch if substr(cCatchError,\"FileNotFoundException\") > 0 or substr(cCatchError,\"DirectoryNotFoundException\") > 0 ? \"2. The file is m"}, {"u": "tut-07-errors.html#how-it-works", "t": "When things go wrong", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # 1. Catch the error # The first block shows the message: System.FormatException: The input string 'abc' was not in a correct format. 2. React to the kind of error # substr(cCatchError,\"FileNotFoundException\") > 0 asks if the name appears in the text. Different problems get different answers: a missing file may be told to the user; a full disk may stop the program. 3. Ask first # Catching is for the unexpected. If you can check a condition, do that: File.Exists(cName) returns false instead of raising an error. It is shorter, faster and clearer. 4. TryParse # The same idea for numb"}, {"u": "tut-07-errors.html#good-habits", "t": "When things go wrong", "h": "Good habits", "x": "Good habits # Do Do not check with Exists , TryParse , IsNullOrWhiteSpace first wrap the whole program in one try catch where you can do something about it catch and ignore show a friendly message, log the technical one show cCatchError to end users close what you open leave files open"}, {"u": "tut-07-errors.html#what-you-learned", "t": "When things go wrong", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # a .NET exception is a Ring error, and try / catch handles it, cCatchError starts with the name of the exception, which tells the kind of problem, ask first ( Exists , TryParse ) when you can, raise(\"text\") makes your own error, clean up after the try block."}, {"u": "tut-07-errors.html#exercises", "t": "When things go wrong", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Write readNumber(cText) that returns the number, or -1 if the text is not a number, using TryParse . Read a file that may not exist: print missing if it does not, the number of lines if it does. Do it twice: once with Exists , once with try . Write divide(a,b) that raises \"Division by zero\" when b is 0, and catch it. Cause a DirectoryNotFoundException : write a file into a folder that does not exist. Read the message."}, {"u": "tut-07-errors.html#next", "t": "When things go wrong", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Hashing and encryption"}, {"u": "tut-08-security.html", "t": "Hashing and encryption", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-08-security.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Hashing and encryption", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # the difference between hashing and encryption , SHA-256 hashes, HMAC and random tokens, encrypting and decrypting text with AES. Warning This is a first look at the ideas, not a security course. For real systems use the standard libraries as shown here, never invent your own scheme, and keep keys out of your source code."}, {"u": "tut-08-security.html#hashing-and-encryption", "t": "Hashing and encryption", "h": "Hashing and encryption", "x": "Hashing and encryption # Hashing Encryption Direction one way: you cannot get the data back two ways: you can decrypt with the key Result size always the same (SHA-256: 32 bytes) about the size of the data Use check that data is the same; store passwords keep data secret A hash is a fingerprint. The same input always gives the same hash; a tiny change gives a completely different hash."}, {"u": "tut-08-security.html#the-program", "t": "Hashing and encryption", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # hashing and encryption basics - checksums, HMAC, random tokens, AES load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.Security.Cryptography\" \"System.Text\" } func main oConvert = new Convert() # A hash is a fingerprint of data: the same input always gives the same hash, you can not go back ? \"SHA-256 of 'abc' : \" + lower(oConvert.ToHexString(new SHA256().HashData(\"abc\"))) ? \"MD5 of 'abc' : \" + lower(oConvert.ToHexString(new MD5().HashData(\"abc\"))) + \" (old: do not use it for security)\" ? \"Changing one letter: \" + left(lower(oConvert.ToHexString(new SHA256().HashData(\"abd\"))),16) + \".."}, {"u": "tut-08-security.html#how-it-works", "t": "Hashing and encryption", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # SHA-256 and MD5 # new SHA256().HashData(\"abc\") returns the hash bytes; Convert.ToHexString writes them as hexadecimal. SHA-256 is the modern standard. MD5 is broken for security : it is only fine for a quick checksum of a download. Passwords # Never store a password, and never store a plain hash of it either (attackers have tables of hashes of common passwords). A password needs a salted, slow hash (PBKDF2): a random salt so that two people with the same password get different hashes, and many rounds so that each guess is slow for an attacker. That needs a class whose constructo"}, {"u": "tut-08-security.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Hashing and encryption", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # hashing is one way, encryption is two ways, RandomNumberGenerator for secrets, NULL selects a method's version without parameters."}, {"u": "tut-08-security.html#exercises", "t": "Hashing and encryption", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Write checksum(cFile) that returns the SHA-256 of a file's bytes ( File.ReadAllBytes ). Hash the same text with SHA-512 ( new SHA512().HashData(...) ) and compare the length of the result with SHA-256. Compute the HMAC of a message with two different keys. Are the results different? Encrypt a file's text with AES and write the cipher as Base64 text."}, {"u": "tut-08-security.html#next", "t": "Hashing and encryption", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # HTTP"}, {"u": "tut-09-http.html", "t": "HTTP", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-09-http.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "HTTP", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # what HTTP requests and responses are, a web server whose addresses are Ring functions, a web client with HttpClient , why requests are asynchronous and what a Task is, status codes."}, {"u": "tut-09-http.html#http-in-one-minute", "t": "HTTP", "h": "HTTP in one minute", "x": "HTTP in one minute # A client sends a request : a method ( GET to read, POST to send data, DELETE to remove), an address , optional data. The server sends a response : a status code ( 200 OK, 201 Created, 404 Not Found, 500 error) and a body (text, HTML or JSON)."}, {"u": "tut-09-http.html#the-program", "t": "HTTP", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # HTTP - a web server and a web client in one program load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System.Net.Http\" \"System.Threading.Tasks\" \"RingDotNet.Web\" } oServer = \"\" oClient = \"\" cUrl = \"\" func main # 1. A web server: a Ring function answers each address oServer = new WebServer(\"http://127.0.0.1:0\") # port 0: the system picks a free port oServer.onGet(\"/hello\",\"hello\") oServer.onGet(\"/time\",\"clock\") oServer.onPost(\"/echo\",\"echo\") cUrl = oServer.url() ? \"The server listens at \" + cUrl # 2. A web client: HttpClient sends requests. Asking is asynchronous: it returns a Task oClient = new HttpC"}, {"u": "tut-09-http.html#how-it-works", "t": "HTTP", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # The server # using { ..., \"RingDotNet.Web\" } (first!) starts the ASP.NET Core runtime. new WebServer(\"http://127.0.0.1:0\") starts a server on your computer ( 127.0.0.1 is \"this computer\"); port 0 means \"any free port\", and oServer.url() tells you which one. Each address is connected to a Ring function with onGet , onPost , onDelete . The function receives the request and returns the answer : The handler returns The response a text status 200, the text as HTML [status, contentType, text] exactly that webJson(list) status 200, JSON A path that has no handler answers 404 Not found "}, {"u": "tut-09-http.html#what-you-learned", "t": "HTTP", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # requests have a method, an address and maybe a body; responses have a status and a body, handlers return text, [status, type, text] or webJson , asynchronous calls return a Task : wait, then read Result , serveOne() hands requests to Ring."}, {"u": "tut-09-http.html#exercises", "t": "HTTP", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Add a /hello variant /hello?name=Ann that returns Hello, Ann . ( oRequest.query(\"name\") .) Make /time return the real time with DateTime.Now . Add a DELETE /item handler that answers 404 for unknown items. Call a real web site: oClient.GetStringAsync(\"https://example.com\") and print the length of the answer (needs internet)."}, {"u": "tut-09-http.html#next", "t": "HTTP", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Time, tasks and other programs"}, {"u": "tut-10-tasks-processes.html", "t": "Time, tasks and other programs", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-10-tasks-processes.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Time, tasks and other programs", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # Stopwatch to measure time, tasks and Wait , Environment : the computer and its variables, Process : start another program, the one important rule about threads."}, {"u": "tut-10-tasks-processes.html#the-program", "t": "Time, tasks and other programs", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # time, tasks and other programs - Stopwatch, Task, Process, Environment load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.Diagnostics\" \"System.Threading.Tasks\" \"System.Threading\" } func main # Measuring time: Stopwatch oWatch = new Stopwatch() oWatch.Start() new Thread().Sleep(120) oWatch.Stop() ? \"Slept about 120 ms, measured at least 100: \" + (oWatch.ElapsedMilliseconds >= 100) # Tasks run work in the background; Wait() waits for the end on the Ring thread oTask = new Task().Delay(100) ? \"Task finished at once? : \" + oTask.IsCompleted oTask.Wait(NULL) ? \"Task finished after Wait : \""}, {"u": "tut-10-tasks-processes.html#how-it-works", "t": "Time, tasks and other programs", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # Stopwatch # Start() , Stop() , then ElapsedMilliseconds (or Elapsed for a TimeSpan ). Thread.Sleep(120) pauses the program for 120 ms. Measure the parts of a program that feel slow before you try to speed anything up. Tasks # A Task represents work that happens \"in the background\" or in the future. Task.Delay(100) is a task that completes after 100 ms. IsCompleted says whether it is done; Wait(NULL) blocks until it is. Task.FromResult(\"done\") is a task that is already finished, with the result done , read with its property Result . The rule: Ring runs on one thread # Note .NET c"}, {"u": "tut-10-tasks-processes.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Time, tasks and other programs", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # time measuring and waiting, tasks and their results, the Ring-thread rule, the environment and starting other programs."}, {"u": "tut-10-tasks-processes.html#exercises", "t": "Time, tasks and other programs", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Measure how long it takes to compute 100000 square roots in a Ring loop. Run cmd /c dir ( sh -c ls on Linux and macOS) and count the lines in its output. Print the three biggest environment variables by length ( GetEnvironmentVariables() ). Start notepad.exe (on Windows) with a Process , wait 3 seconds and close it with Kill() ."}, {"u": "tut-10-tasks-processes.html#where-to-go-next", "t": "Time, tasks and other programs", "h": "Where to go next", "x": "Where to go next # This was the last tutorial of the chapter The basics . The next chapter is Text and data formats : it starts with Regular expressions in depth . The Tutorials page lists all the chapters, and you can start any of them."}, {"u": "tut-29-regex.html", "t": "Regular expressions in depth", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-29-regex.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Regular expressions in depth", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # the building blocks of a pattern, finding, replacing and splitting, groups and named groups, greedy and lazy matching, validating input, escaping text that is not a pattern."}, {"u": "tut-29-regex.html#why-learn-them", "t": "Regular expressions in depth", "h": "Why learn them", "x": "Why learn them # Whenever a job says \"find the part of the text that looks like...\", a regular expression does it in one line: phone numbers, dates, e-mails, log lines, codes. The same patterns work in every language, so what you learn here is useful everywhere."}, {"u": "tut-29-regex.html#the-program", "t": "Regular expressions in depth", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # regular expressions in depth - patterns, groups, replace, split, validation load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.Text.RegularExpressions\" } func main cText = \"Ann: 555-0101, Bob: 555-0199, Cy: 555-0123 (call after 18:30 on 2024-05-17)\" # 1. Literal text matches itself ? \"1. 'Bob' found? : \" + new Regex(\"Bob\").IsMatch(cText) # 2. Character classes: \\d a digit, \\w a letter/digit/underscore, \\s a space, [abc] one of these, . any character ? \"2. First number : \" + new Regex(\"\\d+\").Match(cText).Value # 3. Quantifiers: + one or more, * zero or more, ? zero or one, {3} exactly "}, {"u": "tut-29-regex.html#the-building-blocks", "t": "Regular expressions in depth", "h": "The building blocks", "x": "The building blocks # Pattern Matches Example abc exactly those letters Bob . any one character b.b finds bob , bab \\d \\w \\s a digit, a letter/digit/underscore, a space \\d+ finds 555 [abc] [a-z] [^0-9] one of these, a range, not these [A-Z] + * ? one or more, zero or more, zero or one \\d+ {3} {2,4} exactly three, two to four \\d{3}-\\d{4} ^ $ the start, the end of the text ^Ann \\b the edge of a word \\bcat\\b a|b a or b Ann|Cy ( ) a group (ab)+ (?<name> ) a named group (?<year>\\d{4})"}, {"u": "tut-29-regex.html#how-the-program-uses-them", "t": "Regular expressions in depth", "h": "How the program uses them", "x": "How the program uses them # IsMatch answers yes or no (step 1). Match(text).Value is the first match, Matches(text) finds all of them: use Count and Item(i) (steps 2, 3). Groups pick parts of a match: Groups.Item(\"year\").Value . The pattern (?<year>\\d{4})-(?<month>\\d{2})-(?<day>\\d{2}) gives the three numbers of a date (step 5). Alternation Ann|Cy finds either name (step 6). Replace can use the groups in the replacement text ( $1 , $2 , $3 ): turning 2024-05-17 into 17/05/2024 is one call (step 7). Split cuts a text at every match of the pattern: ,\\s* is \"a comma and any spaces\" (step 8). Greed"}, {"u": "tut-29-regex.html#good-habits", "t": "Regular expressions in depth", "h": "Good habits", "x": "Good habits # Build a pattern in small steps and test each step. Put a pattern with comments in a variable with a clear name. Prefer a simple pattern plus some code over one unreadable pattern. Never use a regex to parse a language with nesting (HTML, JSON): use a parser (the tutorial JSON and XML )."}, {"u": "tut-29-regex.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Regular expressions in depth", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # classes, quantifiers, anchors, groups, alternation, IsMatch , Match , Matches , Replace , Split , greedy vs lazy, anchored patterns for validation, Escape for user text."}, {"u": "tut-29-regex.html#exercises", "t": "Regular expressions in depth", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Write a pattern for a Ring variable name that starts with n or c followed by a capital letter ( nCount , cName ) and find them all in a source file. Validate a simple e-mail address and test it with five good and five bad examples. Replace every run of spaces and tabs in a text by a single space. Extract all #hashtags from a sentence."}, {"u": "tut-29-regex.html#next", "t": "Regular expressions in depth", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Formatting numbers and dates"}, {"u": "tut-129-formatting.html", "t": "Formatting numbers and dates", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-129-formatting.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Formatting numbers and dates", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # the placeholder {0:format} of String.Format , the standard number formats F , N , E , P , C and G , whole numbers in other bases with Convert.ToString , custom patterns ( 0.00 , #,##0.00 , 0.0% ), alignment {0,10} and {0,-10} for tables, date patterns ( yyyy-MM-dd , d MMMM yyyy ) and the standard date letters, patterns for durations ( TimeSpan ), why the program uses the invariant culture . The tutorial Cultures shows that the same format gives different texts in different countries. This lesson is about the formats themselves."}, {"u": "tut-129-formatting.html#the-program", "t": "Formatting numbers and dates", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # formatting numbers and dates - format strings: fixed decimals, thousands, percent, hexadecimal, padding, alignment and custom patterns load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.Globalization\" } oString = new String() oInvariant = \"\" func main # A format string says HOW a value is written. The invariant culture is used here, so that the output is the same on every computer; # a program for people uses the culture of the user (the tutorial about cultures). oInvariant = new CultureInfo(\"en-US\").InvariantCulture # 1. Standard formats for numbers: one letter, and often a number of"}, {"u": "tut-129-formatting.html#how-it-works", "t": "Formatting numbers and dates", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # The invariant culture # Decimal separators, thousands separators, month names and money symbols depend on the culture . The program passes new CultureInfo(\"en-US\").InvariantCulture to every call, so the output is the same on every computer. For text that goes to a file or to another program (data) use the invariant culture. For text that a person reads use the culture of that person, which is the default when you leave the provider out. Placeholders # String.Format takes a text with placeholders : {0} is the first value, {1} the second. After a colon comes the format of that val"}, {"u": "tut-129-formatting.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Formatting numbers and dates", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # placeholders and the standard number formats, custom patterns and alignment, the date and duration patterns, the invariant culture for data."}, {"u": "tut-129-formatting.html#exercises", "t": "Formatting numbers and dates", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Print the numbers 1 to 5 and their squares in two columns, with the squares aligned to the right. Write money(value) that always gives two decimals and thousands separators. Write the date of today in the forms 2030-03-09 , 9 Mar 2030 and Saturday 9 March . Show a duration of 90061 seconds as 1 d 01:01:01 (the pattern d' d 'hh\\:mm\\:ss )."}, {"u": "tut-129-formatting.html#next", "t": "Formatting numbers and dates", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Unicode and encodings"}, {"u": "tut-52-unicode.html", "t": "Unicode and encodings", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-52-unicode.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Unicode and encodings", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # the difference between bytes, code points and the letters you see, UTF-8 and UTF-16 and what Encoding does, why reading with the wrong encoding gives garbage, why two texts that look the same can be different, asking .NET what kind of character something is."}, {"u": "tut-52-unicode.html#the-three-levels", "t": "Unicode and encodings", "h": "The three levels", "x": "The three levels # A byte is what is stored in a file. A code point is the number Unicode gives to a character ( \u00e9 is 233, an emoji is 128512). A text element is what you see as one letter. It can be several code points: e plus a combining accent looks like \u00e9 . An encoding turns text into bytes. UTF-8 uses 1 byte for English letters and up to 4 for others; it is the standard for files and the web. UTF-16 is what .NET uses inside. A Ring text is a sequence of bytes (the text of a program and of most files is UTF-8), so len() counts bytes ."}, {"u": "tut-52-unicode.html#the-program", "t": "Unicode and encodings", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # Unicode and text encodings - characters, code points, bytes, and why len() is not the number of letters load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.Globalization\" \"System.Text\" } func main oUtf8 = new Encoding().UTF8 oUtf16 = new Encoding().Unicode # .NET's own text format: UTF-16 oLatin1 = new Encoding().Latin1 ? \"Ring len() | UTF-8 bytes | UTF-16 bytes | letters you see | text\" aSamples = [\"Ring\",\"caf\u00e9\",\"\u041f\u0440\u0438\u0432\u0435\u0442\",\"\u0645\u0631\u062d\u0628\u0627\",\"\u65e5\u672c\u8a9e\",\"\ud83d\ude00\"] for cText in aSamples oInfo = textElements(cText) ? padRight(\"\" + len(cText),11) + \" | \" + padRight(\"\" + len(oUtf8.GetBytes(cText)),11) + \" | \" + p"}, {"u": "tut-52-unicode.html#how-it-works", "t": "Unicode and encodings", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # The table compares four counts for six texts. For Ring they are all the same; for caf\u00e9 the byte count is 5 but you see 4 letters; Russian and Arabic use two bytes per letter; Japanese three; an emoji four (two units in UTF-16). StringInfo.LengthInTextElements counts what a person sees. Its constructor that takes the text is written new StringInfo().@New([ :value = cText ]) . Step 2 turns \u00e9 into two bytes (195 and 169) and reads them again as Latin-1 : the result is \u00c3\u00a9 , the classic mojibake . The rule: the encoding that wrote the bytes must be the one that reads them . When you "}, {"u": "tut-52-unicode.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Unicode and encodings", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # bytes, code points, text elements, Encoding.UTF8 , Unicode , Latin1 , GetBytes , GetString , mojibake and how to avoid it, comparing text in a culture-aware way, CharUnicodeInfo.GetUnicodeCategory ."}, {"u": "tut-52-unicode.html#exercises", "t": "Unicode and encodings", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Write a text to a file in UTF-8 and in UTF-16 and compare the file sizes. Count the letters of your own name with len() and with StringInfo . Find out what Encoding.ASCII.GetBytes(\"\u00e9\") returns, and why."}, {"u": "tut-52-unicode.html#next", "t": "Unicode and encodings", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Text files and encodings"}, {"u": "tut-132-text-encodings.html", "t": "Text files and encodings", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-132-text-encodings.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Text files and encodings", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # the bytes of a text in four encodings, the byte order mark (BOM), what File.WriteAllText and File.ReadAllText do about encodings, reading a file that has no BOM and an old encoding (Latin-1), strict decoding with UTF8Encoding(False, True) , a simple order of tests to detect an encoding, Windows and Unix line endings. The tutorial Unicode and encodings explained characters, code points and the .NET string. This lesson is about files: the bytes on the disk."}, {"u": "tut-132-text-encodings.html#the-program", "t": "Text files and encodings", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # text files and encodings - the bytes of a text, the byte order mark, reading a file in the right encoding, and strict decoding load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.IO\" \"System.Text\" } func main oFile = new File() oPath = new Path() cFolder = oPath.Combine([ oPath.GetTempPath(), \"ringdotnet-enc-\" + oPath.GetRandomFileName() ]) new Directory().CreateDirectory(cFolder) # A file holds BYTES. An ENCODING says which bytes mean which characters. The text \"Caf\u00e9 \u20ac\" has an accent and a euro sign, which # are not in the old 7-bit ASCII table. cText = \"Caf\" + char(195) + char(169) +"}, {"u": "tut-132-text-encodings.html#how-it-works", "t": "Text files and encodings", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # Bytes and encodings # The text Caf\u00e9 \u20ac has six characters. In UTF-8 it has 9 bytes: the letters of English take 1 byte each, \u00e9 takes 2 and \u20ac takes 3. In UTF-16 (called Unicode by .NET) each character takes 2 bytes here: 12 bytes. Latin-1 has one byte for each of 256 characters, so the text takes 6 bytes, and the euro sign, which Latin-1 does not have, becomes ? . ASCII has only 128 characters, so \u00e9 and \u20ac both become ? . A byte[] is a Ring text of bytes in this package, and the function toHex shows each byte with ascii and hex . The byte order mark # A BOM is a few bytes at the st"}, {"u": "tut-132-text-encodings.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Text files and encodings", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # the bytes of a text in four encodings, the BOM and its effect on reading, the loss of data when the encoding is wrong, strict decoding and a way to detect UTF-8, the two kinds of line ending."}, {"u": "tut-132-text-encodings.html#exercises", "t": "Text files and encodings", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Write a text with the characters \u00fc and \u00df in UTF-8 and in Latin-1 and compare the sizes. Write convertFile(source, target, fromEncoding, toEncoding) that changes the encoding of a file. Make a function that counts how many replacement characters a text has, and use it to check a file after reading. Read a Windows file with ReadAllLines and write it back with Unix line endings (join the lines with char(10) )."}, {"u": "tut-132-text-encodings.html#next", "t": "Text files and encodings", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Comparing text"}, {"u": "tut-97-comparers.html", "t": "Comparing text", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-97-comparers.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Comparing text", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # ordinal comparison (the code of each character) and culture-aware comparison (the rules of a language), ignoring case, String.Compare and what its result means, StringComparer : a comparer that you give to Sort , a dictionary or a set, why \"TITLE\".ToLower() can be wrong in Turkish. The tutorial Cultures formatted numbers and dates for a culture, and the tutorial Unicode and encodings looked at characters. This lesson is about comparing text, which every search box, sort order and dictionary of names needs."}, {"u": "tut-97-comparers.html#the-program", "t": "Comparing text", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # comparing text - ordinal and culture comparison, ignoring case, StringComparer and a sorted set that ignores case load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.Globalization\" \"System.Collections.Generic\" } func main oString = new String() # 1. Two texts can be compared in different ways. Ordinal compares the code of every character: fast, exact, the same on every # computer. Culture-aware comparison follows the rules of a language (in German \"\u00e4\" is close to \"a\"). Both can ignore case. ? \"1. 'apple' = 'APPLE'\" ? \" ordinal: \" + (oString.Compare([ :strA = \"apple\", :strB = \"APPLE\", :"}, {"u": "tut-97-comparers.html#how-it-works", "t": "Comparing text", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # Ordinal and culture # Ordinal comparison looks at the number (code) of every character. It is exact and fast and gives the same answer on every computer, so it is the right choice for technical text: file names, identifiers, keys, protocols. Culture comparison follows the rules of a language: letters with accents are close to their base letters and a and A are neighbors. Use it for text that people read, such as a sorted list of names on the screen. The comparison type is an enum, so it is a text: \"Ordinal\" , \"OrdinalIgnoreCase\" , \"InvariantCulture\" , \"InvariantCultureIgnoreCase"}, {"u": "tut-97-comparers.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Comparing text", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # ordinal for technical text, culture for text that people read, the sign of the result of Compare , StringComparer for sorting, dictionaries and sets, case-insensitive lookups with OrdinalIgnoreCase , the Turkish i and why the invariant culture is safer."}, {"u": "tut-97-comparers.html#exercises", "t": "Comparing text", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Sort the file names of a folder (the tutorial Files and folders ) in ordinal order and in culture order and look at the difference. Build a case-insensitive Dictionary of settings (the tutorial Settings ). Compare \"Stra\u00dfe\" and \"STRASSE\" with the four comparison types. Write sameName(a, b) for user names that must ignore case and accents (look at CompareOptions )."}, {"u": "tut-97-comparers.html#next", "t": "Comparing text", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Text readers and writers"}, {"u": "tut-104-text-readers-writers.html", "t": "Text readers and writers", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-104-text-readers-writers.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Text readers and writers", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # StringWriter and StringReader , the base classes TextWriter and TextReader , and why a function should take one, reading line by line with ReadLine and Peek , Console.SetOut to capture output, composite formatting with String.Format ( {0} , alignment, formats). The tutorial Files and folders wrote and read files and the tutorial Streams and compression explained streams. A string can be a stream too, and that is very useful."}, {"u": "tut-104-text-readers-writers.html#the-program", "t": "Text readers and writers", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # text readers and writers - StringWriter and StringReader, TextWriter in functions, and capturing Console output load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.IO\" \"System.Text\" } func main # 1. A StringWriter is a TextWriter that writes into a string in memory. Code that writes to \"a TextWriter\" does not care whether it # is a file, the console or a string: that makes it easy to test and to reuse. oWriter = new StringWriter oWriter.WriteLine(\"first line\") oWriter.Write(\"second \") oWriter.Write(\"line\") oWriter.WriteLine(NULL) oWriter.WriteLine(\"third line\") cText = substr(oWriter.T"}, {"u": "tut-104-text-readers-writers.html#how-it-works", "t": "Text readers and writers", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # StringWriter # A StringWriter has the same methods as a StreamWriter ( Write , WriteLine ), but it collects the text in memory; ToString() gives it back. Use it to build a longer text piece by piece, and above all as a test double for a file or the console. WriteLine(NULL) ends the current line without adding text. (Windows ends a line with two characters, CR LF , and Linux with one, LF ; the program removes char(13) so the output is the same everywhere.) StringReader # StringReader reads a text like a file: ReadLine gives one line, ReadToEnd the rest. It has no EndOfStream ; Pe"}, {"u": "tut-104-text-readers-writers.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Text readers and writers", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # StringWriter and StringReader for text in memory, Peek and ReadLine to read all lines, functions that take a TextWriter can write anywhere, Console.SetOut captures output (and must be undone), String.Format with places, alignment and formats."}, {"u": "tut-104-text-readers-writers.html#exercises", "t": "Text readers and writers", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Write numberedLines(oReader, oWriter) that copies a text and puts a line number before each line. Write a test that captures the output of Console.WriteLine and checks it. Make a table with String.Format and aligned columns for a list of products and prices. Read a CSV text with StringReader and cut each line into fields with Ring ( str2list(substr(cLine,\",\",nl)) , see the tutorial Working with text )."}, {"u": "tut-104-text-readers-writers.html#next", "t": "Text readers and writers", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Escaping"}, {"u": "tut-86-escaping.html", "t": "Escaping", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-86-escaping.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Escaping", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # why text must be encoded for the place where it goes, HTML: WebUtility.HtmlEncode and HtmlEncoder , URLs: UrlEncoder , JavaScript: JavaScriptEncoder , JSON: JsonEncodedText , XML: XElement escapes for you, regular expressions: Regex.Escape , SQL: parameters, never joined text."}, {"u": "tut-86-escaping.html#the-rule", "t": "Escaping", "h": "The rule", "x": "The rule # A text has no meaning by itself; the place gives it one. <b> is just letters in a text file and a command in an HTML page. If the text of a visitor reaches a place without being encoded, the visitor can write commands for that place: a script that runs in other people's browsers ( cross-site scripting ), SQL that reads your whole database ( SQL injection ). The defense is always the same: encode for the place where the text goes , with a tool that knows that place. Place Danger Tool HTML < , > , & , quotes WebUtility.HtmlEncode , HtmlEncoder URL & , = , space, non-ASCII UrlEncoder J"}, {"u": "tut-86-escaping.html#the-program", "t": "Escaping", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # escaping - the right encoding for the right place (HTML, URL, JavaScript, JSON, XML, regular expressions and SQL) load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.Net\" \"System.Text.Encodings.Web\" \"System.Text.Json\" \"System.Text.RegularExpressions\" \"System.Xml.Linq\" \"RingDotNet.Data\" } func main # A text from a user can contain characters that mean something in the place where you put it. The rule: ENCODE FOR THE PLACE. cText = \"<script>alert('x & y')</script>\" # 1. HTML: < > & and quotes become entities, so the browser shows the text and does not run it. WebUtility is the quick tool"}, {"u": "tut-86-escaping.html#how-it-works", "t": "Escaping", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # HTML # HtmlEncode replaces < , > , & and quotes by entities ( &lt; , &amp; , \u2026). The browser then shows <script> instead of running it. Step 2 puts the same name into a page twice: without encoding, the onerror handler would run in the visitor's browser. HtmlEncoder.Default.Encode (namespace System.Text.Encodings.Web ) is the stricter encoder that ASP.NET uses; the two differ in details such as &#39; against &#x27; . HtmlDecode reverses the encoding. URL, JavaScript, JSON # UrlEncoder turns space, & , / and non-ASCII letters into %XX ; encode each value , not the whole address ("}, {"u": "tut-86-escaping.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Escaping", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # text is encoded for the place, WebUtility , HtmlEncoder , UrlEncoder , JavaScriptEncoder , JsonEncodedText , Regex.Escape , XElement escapes by itself, SQL parameters stop injection."}, {"u": "tut-86-escaping.html#exercises", "t": "Escaping", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Make the tiny web site of the tutorial A small web site safe: where does it show a visitor's text? Write a function link(cText, cAddress) that builds <a href=\"\u2026\">\u2026</a> with the right encoding for the address and for the text. Search a text file for a phrase that the user types, using Regex.Escape . Try the attack text '; DROP TABLE People; -- as a parameter and check that the table is still there."}, {"u": "tut-86-escaping.html#next", "t": "Escaping", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # URLs and encodings"}, {"u": "tut-64-urls.html", "t": "URLs and encodings", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-64-urls.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "URLs and encodings", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # the parts of a URL and the Uri class, percent-encoding of special characters ( WebUtility.UrlEncode / UrlDecode ), building and reading a query string, combining a base address with a relative path, Base64, and why it is not encryption, checking that an address is valid."}, {"u": "tut-64-urls.html#the-parts-of-a-url", "t": "URLs and encodings", "h": "The parts of a URL", "x": "The parts of a URL # https://shop.example.com:8443/products/shoes?color=red&size=42#reviews \u2514scheme\u2518 \u2514\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500host\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2518\u2514port\u2518\u2514\u2500\u2500\u2500path\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2518 \u2514\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500 query \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2518\u2514fragment\u2518 The query holds name=value pairs separated by & . The fragment (after # ) stays in the browser and is never sent to the server."}, {"u": "tut-64-urls.html#the-program", "t": "URLs and encodings", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # URLs and encodings - taking an address apart, building a query string safely, Base64 and percent-encoding load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.Net\" \"System.Text\" } func main # 1. A URL has parts. The Uri class splits them: no searching for \":\" and \"/\" yourself. oUri = new Uri(\"https://shop.example.com:8443/products/shoes?color=red&size=42#reviews\") ? \"1. scheme : \" + oUri.Scheme ? \" host : \" + oUri.Host ? \" port : \" + oUri.Port ? \" path : \" + oUri.AbsolutePath ? \" query : \" + oUri.Query ? \" fragment : \" + oUri.Fragment # 2. Default ports: no port in the text gives the po"}, {"u": "tut-64-urls.html#how-it-works", "t": "URLs and encodings", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # Uri # new Uri(text) parses the address and gives you Scheme , Host , Port , AbsolutePath , Query and Fragment . Never cut an address with substr and find : ports, user names, ? inside a fragment and many other details make it go wrong. If no port is written, the port of the scheme is used (80 for http , 443 for https ). Encoding # Some characters have a meaning in an address: & separates parameters, = joins a name and a value, ? starts the query, % starts an escape. If the text of the user contains them, they must be percent-encoded or they change the meaning: rock & roll would "}, {"u": "tut-64-urls.html#what-you-learned", "t": "URLs and encodings", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # Uri and its parts, encoding and decoding for query strings, the order: split first, decode last, relative addresses with named constructor arguments, Base64."}, {"u": "tut-64-urls.html#exercises", "t": "URLs and encodings", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Write getParameter(cUrl, cName) that returns one value of the query of an address. Build the URL of a search for the text C# & Ring and check it with Uri . Turn a small text file into a Base64 data URL ( data:text/plain;base64,... )."}, {"u": "tut-64-urls.html#next", "t": "URLs and encodings", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Bytes as text: Base64 and hexadecimal"}, {"u": "tut-138-base64-hex.html", "t": "Bytes as text: Base64 and hexadecimal", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-138-base64-hex.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Bytes as text: Base64 and hexadecimal", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # Base64 : 3 bytes become 4 characters, and = fills the end, the padding and the size of the result, sending all 256 byte values through text, the URL-safe form ( - and _ , no = ), hexadecimal with Convert.ToHexString and FromHexString , the errors of wrong input, data URIs , and a hash written in hexadecimal. The tutorial URLs and encodings used Base64 for one purpose. This lesson looks at the encodings themselves."}, {"u": "tut-138-base64-hex.html#the-program", "t": "Bytes as text: Base64 and hexadecimal", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # bytes as text - Base64, Base64 for URLs and hexadecimal: encoding and decoding, the size of the result, line breaks, and data URIs load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.Text\" \"System.Security.Cryptography\" } func main oConvert = new Convert() # Bytes can hold any value from 0 to 255, but many places accept only TEXT: an e-mail, a JSON file, a URL, an XML attribute. An # ENCODING turns bytes into letters and digits that are safe in such places, and back. The two common ones are Base64 and hexadecimal. cData = \"Ring + .NET\" # 1. Base64: 3 bytes become 4 characters from A-Z "}, {"u": "tut-138-base64-hex.html#how-it-works", "t": "Bytes as text: Base64 and hexadecimal", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # Base64 # Base64 takes the bytes in groups of three (24 bits) and writes each group as four characters of 6 bits, taken from A-Z , a-z , 0-9 , + and / . The result is a third longer than the data: 11 bytes become 16 characters. Convert.ToBase64String and FromBase64String are static methods, called through new Convert() . A byte[] is a Ring text of bytes in this package, so the text goes in and comes out as it is. Padding # When the data is not a multiple of three bytes, the last group is short and the result is filled with = : one byte gives == , two bytes give = , three give non"}, {"u": "tut-138-base64-hex.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Bytes as text: Base64 and hexadecimal", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # Base64 and its padding and size, the URL-safe form, hexadecimal with ToHexString and FromHexString , the error of wrong input, data URIs."}, {"u": "tut-138-base64-hex.html#exercises", "t": "Bytes as text: Base64 and hexadecimal", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Write toBase64Url(bytes) and fromBase64Url(text) as one function each. Encode a small PNG file and make a data URI of it ( data:image/png;base64,... ). Write a hex dump that prints 16 bytes per line with the offset (the tutorial Binary files has one). Compare the lengths of Base64 and hexadecimal for 1000 bytes."}, {"u": "tut-138-base64-hex.html#next", "t": "Bytes as text: Base64 and hexadecimal", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # International domain names"}, {"u": "tut-117-domain-names.html", "t": "International domain names", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-117-domain-names.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "International domain names", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # how names with Arabic, German, Chinese... letters travel as Punycode ( xn-- ), IdnMapping : GetAscii , GetUnicode , UseStd3AsciiRules , Uri.Host and Uri.IdnHost , what makes a name invalid, homograph attacks with look-alike letters, a check of the alphabets in each part of a name. The tutorial URLs and encodings took URLs apart and the tutorial Unicode and encodings looked at Unicode text. This lesson is where the two meet \u2014 and where it becomes a security question."}, {"u": "tut-117-domain-names.html#the-program", "t": "International domain names", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # international domain names - Punycode with IdnMapping, Uri.IdnHost, invalid names, and look-alike letters (homograph attacks) load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.Globalization\" \"System.Text\" } func main oIdn = new IdnMapping() # The system of domain names (DNS) knows only the letters a-z, the digits and the hyphen. A name with other letters is sent as # PUNYCODE: the text \"xn--\" followed by an encoding of the letters in ASCII. IdnMapping converts both ways. cGerman = \"b\" + char(195) + char(188) + \"cher.example\" # b\u00fccher.example cArabic = char(217) + char(133) + char(217"}, {"u": "tut-117-domain-names.html#how-it-works", "t": "International domain names", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # Punycode # The system of domain names (DNS) was made for the letters a to z , the digits and the hyphen. A name with other letters is sent as Punycode : the text xn-- and then the letters of the part encoded with ASCII characters only. b\u00fccher.example becomes xn--bcher-kva.example and the Arabic name becomes xn--4gbrim.example . Each part (label) of the name is converted alone, so .example stays as it is. IdnMapping.GetAscii(name) converts to Punycode, GetUnicode(name) converts back, and the round trip is exact. A name that is already plain ASCII does not change. A person sees th"}, {"u": "tut-117-domain-names.html#what-you-learned", "t": "International domain names", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # Punycode and IdnMapping , Host against IdnHost , the rules for valid names and the strict mapper, homograph attacks, checking the alphabets of each part."}, {"u": "tut-117-domain-names.html#exercises", "t": "International domain names", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Write sameSite(a, b) that compares two URLs by their IdnHost , ignoring case. Add the Hebrew range (0590\u201305FF) and the Greek look-alike \u03bf (U+03BF) to the check and test g\u03bfogle.com . Write safeLink(text, address) that warns when the visible text and the real address have different hosts. Read the e-mail address of a message (the tutorial E-mail messages ) and check the part after @ ."}, {"u": "tut-117-domain-names.html#next", "t": "International domain names", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # MIME types"}, {"u": "tut-121-mime-types.html", "t": "MIME types", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-121-mime-types.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "MIME types", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # what a MIME type (media type, content type) is, mapping file extensions to types ( FileExtensionContentTypeProvider ), adding a type of your own, reading Content-Type (type, subtype, charset ), the Accept header, quality values and wildcards, content negotiation and the answer 406 Not Acceptable . The tutorial HTTP and the tutorial HttpClient in depth used HTTP from the client side and the tutorial A tiny web framework from the server side. Whenever a body is sent, a type must say what it is. Note The content type classes ( Microsoft.AspNetCore.StaticFiles , Microsoft.Net"}, {"u": "tut-121-mime-types.html#the-program", "t": "MIME types", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # MIME types - file extensions to content types, reading a Content-Type header, and choosing what to send for an Accept header load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"Microsoft.Net.Http.Headers\" \"Microsoft.AspNetCore.StaticFiles\" \"System\" \"System.Net.Mime\" \"RingDotNet.Web\" # the content type classes of ASP.NET Core are part of its runtime: this line starts it } func main # A MIME type (media type, content type) names the KIND of a piece of data: text/html, image/png, application/json. A server sends it in # the Content-Type header so that the browser knows what to do with the bytes, and a cl"}, {"u": "tut-121-mime-types.html#how-it-works", "t": "MIME types", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # Type and subtype # A MIME type has the form type/subtype : the type is the family ( text , image , audio , video , application ) and the subtype the exact format ( html , png , json ). After a semicolon come parameters : text/html; charset=utf-8 says that the text is encoded in UTF-8 (the tutorial Unicode and encodings explains encodings). A server that sends the wrong type causes real problems: a browser that receives a script as text/plain does not run it, and one that receives a page as text/plain shows the source. From a file name # FileExtensionContentTypeProvider.TryGetCon"}, {"u": "tut-121-mime-types.html#what-you-learned", "t": "MIME types", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # type/subtype and parameters such as charset , TryGetContentType , the default application/octet-stream , and your own Mappings , the extension is not the content, the Accept list, quality and wildcards, negotiation and 406 Not Acceptable ."}, {"u": "tut-121-mime-types.html#exercises", "t": "MIME types", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Make the web server of the tutorial A tiny web framework send the right Content-Type for the files of a folder. Parse Accept-Language: ar, en;q=0.8, fr;q=0.5 with the same functions and choose a language from the list of the languages you have. Write isAllowedUpload(name) that accepts only image/png , image/jpeg and application/pdf . Add */*;q=0 to an Accept header and see how it changes the answer."}, {"u": "tut-121-mime-types.html#next", "t": "MIME types", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Writing JSON as a stream"}, {"u": "tut-100-json-writer.html", "t": "Writing JSON as a stream", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-100-json-writer.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Writing JSON as a stream", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # Utf8JsonWriter : start and end of objects and arrays, properties and values, indented and compact output ( JsonWriterOptions ), Flush and why the writer needs it, the escaping that is done for you, and the errors for invalid structure, JsonSerializer and JsonSerializerOptions for objects and collections. The tutorial JSON and XML read and wrote JSON as a tree and the tutorial JSON as a tree built it from nodes. A tree is easy to change but it keeps the whole document in memory. A writer is the other style: you say what comes next, and it goes straight to a stream (a file,"}, {"u": "tut-100-json-writer.html#the-program", "t": "Writing JSON as a stream", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # writing JSON as a stream - Utf8JsonWriter, indentation, nesting, escaping, and JsonSerializer options load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.IO\" \"System.Text\" \"System.Text.Json\" } func main # 1. Utf8JsonWriter writes a JSON text piece by piece into a stream, without building a tree in memory first. # It is the right tool for big documents and for output that must be exactly formatted. oStream = new MemoryStream oOptions = new JsonWriterOptions() oOptions.Indented = True oWriter = (new Utf8JsonWriter).@New([ :utf8Json = oStream, :options = oOptions ]) oWriter.WriteStartObje"}, {"u": "tut-100-json-writer.html#how-it-works", "t": "Writing JSON as a stream", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # Writing the document # The writer is made on a stream ( dnNew(\"Utf8JsonWriter\",[ :utf8Json = oStream, :options = oOptions ]) ). Then you call, in order, what the document contains: Call Writes WriteStartObject(NULL) / WriteEndObject() { and } (with a name, WriteStartObject(\"address\") , it is a property of the object around it) WriteStartArray(\"skills\") / WriteEndArray() [ and ] WriteString(\"name\",\"Ann\") a property with a text value WriteNumber(\"age\",31) , WriteBoolean(\"active\",True) , WriteNull(\"manager\") properties with other values WriteStringValue(\"ring\") , WriteNumberValue(4"}, {"u": "tut-100-json-writer.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Writing JSON as a stream", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # Utf8JsonWriter writes JSON into a stream, token by token, indented and compact forms, Flush and Dispose , escaping and structure checks are done for you, JsonSerializer for whole objects."}, {"u": "tut-100-json-writer.html#exercises", "t": "Writing JSON as a stream", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Write an array of 1000 objects with id and square into a file (the tutorial Files and folders ), and read it back with JsonDocument (the tutorial JSON and XML ). Write the nested folder structure of a directory as JSON with a recursive function. Make JsonSerializerOptions with PropertyNamingPolicy = JsonNamingPolicy.CamelCase and look at the names. Write a number with many decimals with WriteNumber and look at how it is written."}, {"u": "tut-100-json-writer.html#next", "t": "Writing JSON as a stream", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # JSON as a tree"}, {"u": "tut-80-json-nodes.html", "t": "JSON as a tree", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-80-json-nodes.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "JSON as a tree", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # parsing JSON into a tree you can edit ( JsonNode , JsonObject , JsonArray ), reading with Item(\"key\") and Item(index) , SetItem , Add , Remove on objects and Add on arrays, building a document from Ring values, merging two documents, DeepClone and DeepEquals . These classes are in System.Text.Json.Nodes . The tutorial JSON and XML read JSON with JsonDocument ; that class is fast but read-only. Use the nodes when you need to change JSON: configuration files, request bodies, data you assemble."}, {"u": "tut-80-json-nodes.html#the-program", "t": "JSON as a tree", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # JSON as a tree you can change - JsonNode, JsonObject and JsonArray (System.Text.Json.Nodes) load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.Text.Json\" \"System.Text.Json.Nodes\" } C_CONFIG = '{\"name\":\"shop\",\"port\":8080,\"debug\":false,\"tags\":[\"web\",\"sql\"],\"database\":{\"host\":\"localhost\",\"pool\":5}}' func main # 1. Parse text into a tree. A JsonNode is an object, an array or a value; AsObject() and AsArray() give the specific class. oRoot = new JsonNode().Parse(C_CONFIG).AsObject() ? \"1. name = \" + oRoot.Item(\"name\").ToString() + \", port + 1 = \" + (number(oRoot.Item(\"port\").ToString()) + "}, {"u": "tut-80-json-nodes.html#how-it-works", "t": "JSON as a tree", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # Reading # new JsonNode().Parse(text) gives a node; AsObject() and AsArray() give the specific class. oRoot.Item(\"name\") is the child with that key and oTags.Item(0) the first array item (indexes start at 0, as in .NET). Every node has ToString() for simple values \u2014 numbers arrive as text, so number(oRoot.Item(\"port\").ToString()) \u2014 and GetPath() , which names where the node lives ( $.tags[0] ). GetValueKind() tells Object , Array , String , Number , True , False or Null . Changing # What Call set an existing value oRoot.SetItem(\"port\",9090) add a key oRoot.Add([ :propertyName = \""}, {"u": "tut-80-json-nodes.html#what-you-learned", "t": "JSON as a tree", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # JsonNode , JsonObject , JsonArray , Item , SetItem , Add , Remove , ContainsKey , the Ring True is a number: parse true for JSON, merging with a small recursive function, DeepClone and DeepEquals ."}, {"u": "tut-80-json-nodes.html#exercises", "t": "JSON as a tree", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Read a configuration file (the tutorial Settings ) into a node, change one value and save it again. Write getPath(oRoot, \"database.host\") that follows a dotted path. Write removeNulls(oNode) that deletes all keys with null values, at every depth. Send the JSON of step 5 through the pipe of the tutorial Two programs talking ."}, {"u": "tut-80-json-nodes.html#next", "t": "JSON as a tree", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # XML in depth"}, {"u": "tut-72-xml.html", "t": "XML in depth", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-72-xml.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "XML in depth", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # the structure of an XML document: elements, attributes, text, reading with XDocument (LINQ to XML), searching with XPath , creating, changing and removing elements, saving and reading back, what happens with bad XML."}, {"u": "tut-72-xml.html#the-data", "t": "XML in depth", "h": "The data", "x": "The data # <\"t\">class=\"k\">library> <\"t\">class=\"k\">book \"t\">id=\"1\" \"t\">year=\"1937\"><\"t\">class=\"k\">title>The Hobbit</\"t\">class=\"k\">title><\"t\">class=\"k\">price>9.5</\"t\">class=\"k\">price></\"t\">class=\"k\">book> <\"t\">class=\"k\">book \"t\">id=\"2\" \"t\">year=\"1965\"><\"t\">class=\"k\">title>Dune</\"t\">class=\"k\">title><\"t\">class=\"k\">price>12</\"t\">class=\"k\">price></\"t\">class=\"k\">book> ... </\"t\">class=\"k\">library> An element has a name and can contain text and other elements; an attribute ( id=\"1\" ) belongs to an element. There is exactly one root element. The tutorial JSON and XML read XML in a simple way; here are t"}, {"u": "tut-72-xml.html#the-program", "t": "XML in depth", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # XML in depth - reading with XDocument, searching with XPath, building and changing a document load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.IO\" \"System.Xml\" \"System.Xml.Linq\" } func main cXml = '<library><book id=\"1\" year=\"1937\"><title>The Hobbit</title><price>9.5</price></book><book id=\"2\" year=\"1965\"><title>Dune</title><price>12</price></book><book id=\"3\" year=\"1984\"><title>Neuromancer</title><price>8.25</price></book></library>' # 1. XDocument (LINQ to XML) reads a text into a tree of elements. The root is the outer element. oDocument = new XDocument().Parse(cXml) ? \"1. the ro"}, {"u": "tut-72-xml.html#how-it-works", "t": "XML in depth", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # XDocument # new XDocument().Parse(text) builds the tree. oDocument.Root is the root element. Elements(NULL) gives all child elements ( NULL because the method has other forms, see Core concepts ), and .toList() makes a Ring list. For each element Attribute(\"year\").Value reads an attribute and Element(\"title\").Value the text of a child. The values are always texts : convert with number() when you need a number (step 2 adds the prices). XPath # XPath describes a path through the tree: XPath Finds /library/book all books under the root //book every book at any depth /library/book[@"}, {"u": "tut-72-xml.html#what-you-learned", "t": "XML in depth", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # elements, attributes, the root, XDocument : Root , Elements , Element , Attribute , XPath with XmlDocument , building, changing and removing, errors in bad XML."}, {"u": "tut-72-xml.html#exercises", "t": "XML in depth", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Write the books as a table: title, year and price in columns (the tutorial Tabular data ). Use XPath to find the books cheaper than 10. Add a <tags> element with several <tag> children to each book. Convert the list of books to JSON (the tutorial JSON and XML ) and back."}, {"u": "tut-72-xml.html#next", "t": "XML in depth", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # XSLT"}, {"u": "tut-77-xslt.html", "t": "XSLT", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-77-xslt.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "XSLT", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # what a stylesheet is and how XslCompiledTransform runs it, templates, for-each , sort , sum and filters, three outputs from one input: text, CSV and HTML, how this class looks from Ring."}, {"u": "tut-77-xslt.html#the-idea", "t": "XSLT", "h": "The idea", "x": "The idea # You have data in XML (the tutorial XML in depth ) and you want a report. You can walk the tree in Ring, or you can write a stylesheet : a small XML document of templates (\"when you see this part of the input, write this\"). The advantage is that the stylesheet is data : change it, and the report changes, without touching the program. <\"t\">class=\"k\">xsl:template \"t\">match=\"/library\"> <\"t\">class=\"k\">xsl:for-each \"t\">select=\"book\"> <\"t\">class=\"k\">xsl:sort \"t\">select=\"@year\" \"t\">data-type=\"number\" \"t\">order=\"descending\"/> <\"t\">class=\"k\">xsl:value-of \"t\">select=\"title\"/> (<\"t\">class=\"k\">x"}, {"u": "tut-77-xslt.html#the-program", "t": "XSLT", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # XSLT - turn XML into text, CSV and HTML with a stylesheet (System.Xml.Xsl) load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.IO\" \"System.Xml.Xsl\" } C_XML = '<library><book year=\"1937\" genre=\"fantasy\"><title>The Hobbit</title><price>9.5</price></book><book year=\"1965\" genre=\"scifi\"><title>Dune</title><price>12</price></book><book year=\"1984\" genre=\"scifi\"><title>Neuromancer</title><price>8.25</price></book></library>' # A stylesheet is XML too: a template says what to write for a part of the input. xsl:output chooses the kind of result. C_TEXT = '<xsl:stylesheet version=\"1.0\" xmlns:xs"}, {"u": "tut-77-xslt.html#how-it-works", "t": "XSLT", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # The Ring side # Everything happens in transform : it writes the stylesheet text to a file, then oTransform = new XslCompiledTransform() oTransform.@Load(cSheet) # compile the stylesheet (Load is a Ring keyword, so the method is @Load) oTransform.Transform([ :inputUri = cInput, :resultsFile = cOutput ]) # input file -> output file @Load and Transform take file names in this form; the output file is read back with File.ReadAllText . Compiling is the slow part, so a program that transforms many documents compiles the stylesheet once and calls Transform many times. Three stylesheets"}, {"u": "tut-77-xslt.html#what-you-learned", "t": "XSLT", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # XslCompiledTransform , @Load and Transform , templates, for-each , sort , sum , filters, text, CSV and HTML output, attribute value templates {@name} ."}, {"u": "tut-77-xslt.html#exercises", "t": "XSLT", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Write a stylesheet that outputs only the titles of the science fiction books. Add a <xsl:if test=\"price > 10\"> that marks expensive books with * . Make the HTML a complete page ( <html><body>\u2026 ) with a heading and the total price. Transform an XML file that you create with XDocument (the tutorial XML in depth )."}, {"u": "tut-77-xslt.html#next", "t": "XSLT", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # A project: Markdown to HTML"}, {"u": "tut-47-markdown.html", "t": "A project: Markdown to HTML", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-47-markdown.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "A project: Markdown to HTML", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # converting one text format into another, a line-by-line scan with a little state, inline formatting with regular expressions, why you escape first, a Ring feature to know: using objects during definition, x = new Class().Method(x) ."}, {"u": "tut-47-markdown.html#markdown-in-one-minute", "t": "A project: Markdown to HTML", "h": "Markdown in one minute", "x": "Markdown in one minute # Markdown is a way to write formatted text with plain characters: # Title is a heading, **bold** , *italic* , code between back-quotes, - item a list item, [text](address) a link. Many sites turn it into HTML."}, {"u": "tut-47-markdown.html#the-program", "t": "A project: Markdown to HTML", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # a project - a small Markdown to HTML converter (regular expressions, StringBuilder, files) load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.IO\" \"System.Text\" \"System.Text.RegularExpressions\" } func main cMarkdown = \"# Shopping\" + nl + \"\" + nl + \"Things to buy **today** for the *party*, see [the list](http://example.com/list).\" + nl + \"\" + nl + \"## Food\" + nl + \"- bread\" + nl + \"- cheese & `olives`\" + nl + \"- <fresh> fruit\" + nl + \"\" + nl + \"Thank you!\" ? \"---- Markdown ----\" ? cMarkdown cHtml = markdownToHtml(cMarkdown) ? nl + \"---- HTML ----\" ? cHtml # save it as a complete page cF"}, {"u": "tut-47-markdown.html#how-it-works", "t": "A project: Markdown to HTML", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # Two levels # A document has blocks (headings, paragraphs, lists) and, inside the blocks, inline parts (bold, links). The program handles them separately: markdownToHtml decides what each line is, inlineHtml formats what is inside a line. Blocks: a scan with state # The function goes through the lines. The one thing it must remember is whether it is inside a list : the first - line opens <ul> , further items continue it, and the first line that is not an item (or an empty line) closes it with </ul> . Headings are recognized by the number of # : check ### first, then ## , then # ,"}, {"u": "tut-47-markdown.html#what-you-learned", "t": "A project: Markdown to HTML", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # block-level and inline conversion are separate jobs, state for lists, StringBuilder for output, escape before formatting, lazy regexes, ordering of the steps, with x = new Class().Method(x) the variable already holds the new object (using objects during definition): use another variable."}, {"u": "tut-47-markdown.html#exercises", "t": "A project: Markdown to HTML", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Add numbered lists ( 1. item ) and <ol> . Add > quote lines as <blockquote> . Add code blocks between three backticks lines. Read a .md file from disk and write the matching .html file."}, {"u": "tut-47-markdown.html#next", "t": "A project: Markdown to HTML", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # A spell checker"}, {"u": "tut-66-spelling.html", "t": "A spell checker", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-66-spelling.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "A spell checker", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # a set ( StringSet ) for fast \"is it known?\" questions, the edit distance (Levenshtein) and the table that computes it, sorting suggestions by distance and then by name, finding words with their positions in a text, why algorithms with tables (\"dynamic programming\") are worth knowing."}, {"u": "tut-66-spelling.html#the-program", "t": "A spell checker", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # a spell checker - a set of words, edit distance (Levenshtein) and the best suggestions load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System.Collections.Generic\" \"System.Text.RegularExpressions\" } aDictionary = [\"the\",\"quick\",\"brown\",\"fox\",\"jumps\",\"over\",\"lazy\",\"dog\",\"and\",\"then\",\"there\",\"their\",\"brain\",\"crown\",\"frown\",\"jump\",\"quack\",\"quiet\",\"lake\",\"dig\",\"dot\",\"dug\"] func main # 1. A StringSet (a .NET HashSet) answers \"is this word known?\" at once, however many words it holds oWords = new StringSet() for cWord in aDictionary oWords.Add(cWord) next ? \"1. the dictionary has \" + oWords.Count + \" wor"}, {"u": "tut-66-spelling.html#how-it-works", "t": "A spell checker", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # The set # A list answers \"is this word in it?\" by looking at the items one by one: ten times more words, ten times more work. A set ( StringSet , a .NET HashSet ) answers with Contains in about the same time however big it is. A real dictionary has hundreds of thousands of words, and a set is the right place for it (the tutorial Collections and LINQ ). The edit distance # The edit distance between two words is the smallest number of single-letter changes \u2014 insert , delete or replace \u2014 that turn one into the other: kitten \u2192 sitting is 3 ( k\u2192s , e\u2192i , add g ). Words with a small d"}, {"u": "tut-66-spelling.html#what-you-learned", "t": "A spell checker", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # a set for membership tests, edit distance and its table, ranking suggestions, cheap checks first."}, {"u": "tut-66-spelling.html#exercises", "t": "A spell checker", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Add the swap of neighbors (Damerau distance) and see teh \u2192 the become 1. Read the dictionary from a text file with one word per line. Print the checked text with [brackets] around the unknown words. Suggest only words that start with the same letter, and measure how much faster it is (the tutorial Speed \u2014 measure first )."}, {"u": "tut-66-spelling.html#next", "t": "A spell checker", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # A project: mail merge"}, {"u": "tut-144-mailmerge.html", "t": "A project: mail merge", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-144-mailmerge.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "A project: mail merge", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # a template with {name} and {name:format} fields , reading CSV text into rows, and keeping empty values, Regex.Replace with a callback (a function that .NET calls for each match), groups of a match: the name and the format, reusing the formats of the tutorial Formatting numbers and dates , finding missing data before sending. The same pattern makes e-mails, invoices and reports: a template, data, and a function that fills the holes."}, {"u": "tut-144-mailmerge.html#the-program", "t": "A project: mail merge", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # a project: mail merge - a template with {fields}, rows of data, Regex.Replace with a callback, formats, missing fields and the letters load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.Globalization\" \"System.Text.RegularExpressions\" } aFields = [] # the data of the row that is being merged (the callback reads it) aMissing = [] # the names of the fields that a row did not have func main # A MAIL MERGE makes many letters from one template and a table of data. The template has FIELDS in braces: {name} is replaced by the # value of the column name, and {amount:N2} is replaced by the valu"}, {"u": "tut-144-mailmerge.html#how-it-works", "t": "A project: mail merge", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # The template and the data # The template is a text with fields in braces. A field is {name} or {name:format} : the part after the colon is a format string for a number ( N2 gives 1,234.50 ) or a date ( d MMMM yyyy gives 9 March 2030 ). The data is a CSV text: the first line has the names of the columns and each other line is one letter. readCsv makes a list of rows, and each row is a list of [name, value] pairs that is read with the name of the column: aRow[\"city\"] . splitKeep splits a line at the commas and keeps the empty values , so Cy's empty city is an empty value and not a"}, {"u": "tut-144-mailmerge.html#what-you-learned", "t": "A project: mail merge", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # fields with formats in a template, reading CSV with empty values, Regex.Replace with a Ring callback, groups of a match, checking the data before the letters are made."}, {"u": "tut-144-mailmerge.html#exercises", "t": "A project: mail merge", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Write each letter into a file named after the person ( File.WriteAllText ). Add the field {amount:C} and look at the result with a culture of your own. Read the data from a real CSV file, with a column of text that has a comma inside quotes (the tutorial Tabular data ). Count how many times each field was used in all the letters."}, {"u": "tut-144-mailmerge.html#next", "t": "A project: mail merge", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # A project: a syntax highlighter"}, {"u": "tut-152-highlighter.html", "t": "A project: a syntax highlighter", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-152-highlighter.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "A project: a syntax highlighter", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # tokens : comments, strings, numbers, words and the rest, one regular expression with named groups and the alternatives in the right order , Regex.Matches and Groups.Item(name).Success , keywords and names, HTML output with escaped text, a check: without the tags the text is the original, what a pattern like this cannot do. The tutorial Regular expressions in depth explained groups and alternatives. This project uses them to read a language."}, {"u": "tut-152-highlighter.html#the-program", "t": "A project: a syntax highlighter", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # a project: a syntax highlighter - one regular expression with named groups splits code into tokens, and each token becomes an HTML span load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.Text.RegularExpressions\" } func main # A highlighter reads code as TOKENS (comments, strings, numbers, words) and writes each kind in its own color. The idea is one # regular expression with an alternative for each kind of token: the first alternative that matches at a place wins. The order # matters: a comment must be tried before the words, so that a word inside a comment is not a keyword. cCode = \""}, {"u": "tut-152-highlighter.html#how-it-works", "t": "A project: a syntax highlighter", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # One pattern for all the tokens # The pattern is a list of alternatives separated by | , each one a named group : (?<comment>#[^\\n]*) is a # and everything to the end of the line, (?<string>\"[^\"\\n]*\") is a double quote, characters that are not a quote or a line break, and a double quote, (?<number>\\b\\d+(?:\\.\\d+)?\\b) is a whole number or a number with a decimal point, (?<word>[A-Za-z_]\\w*) is a name or a keyword, and (?<other>[\\s\\S]) is any character, including line breaks. Because the last alternative matches every character, the matches cover the whole text, and every character "}, {"u": "tut-152-highlighter.html#what-you-learned", "t": "A project: a syntax highlighter", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # tokens with named groups in one pattern, the order of the alternatives, keywords and names, escaped HTML and the check of the result."}, {"u": "tut-152-highlighter.html#exercises", "t": "A project: a syntax highlighter", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Add a group for operators ( [+\\-*/=<>]+ ) and a class for them. Add a style sheet and a page that shows the highlighted code (the tutorial A project: Markdown to HTML ). Add an alternative for strings with single quotes. Count the tokens of each kind and print a table."}, {"u": "tut-152-highlighter.html#where-to-go-next", "t": "A project: a syntax highlighter", "h": "Where to go next", "x": "Where to go next # This was the last tutorial of the chapter Text and data formats . The next chapter is Numbers, dates and money : it starts with Number types . The Tutorials page lists all the chapters, and you can start any of them."}, {"u": "tut-101-number-types.html", "t": "Number types", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-101-number-types.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Number types", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # the integer types and their limits, MinValue and MaxValue , checked conversion with Convert (an error, not a wrong number), TryParse for text that a user typed, integer division and the remainder ( Math.DivRem ), Int128 and UInt128 , Single and Double , and comparing numbers with decimals. The tutorial Numbers, dates and times used numbers and dates, the tutorial Money used Decimal for money and the tutorial Big numbers used BigInteger . This lesson is about the fixed-size types that .NET methods ask for."}, {"u": "tut-101-number-types.html#the-program", "t": "Number types", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # number types - the limits of Byte, Int16, Int32, Int64 and Int128, overflow, checked conversion, Half and Single load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" } func main # A Ring number is always a double. .NET has many number types with different sizes and limits. When a number goes to .NET, # the bridge uses the type the method asks for, and a value that does not fit raises an error: it does not wrap around. oByte = new Byte() oShort = new Int16() oInt = new Int32() oLong = new Int64() # 1. The limits of each type (MinValue and MaxValue are static fields) ? \"1. the limits:\" ? \" Byte 0"}, {"u": "tut-101-number-types.html#how-it-works", "t": "Number types", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # Many types, one Ring number # In Ring there is one number type, a double (exact for whole numbers up to 9007199254740992). .NET has Byte (0 to 255), Int16 (about plus or minus 32 thousand), Int32 (about plus or minus 2 thousand million), Int64 , Int128 and others, signed and unsigned, and Single , Double and Decimal for fractions. When Ring calls a method, the bridge converts the Ring number into the type the method asks for. A number that does not fit raises an error instead of wrapping around, so a wrong value is found at the call. Where a .NET parameter has a number type, the"}, {"u": "tut-101-number-types.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Number types", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # one Ring number, many .NET types, limits with MinValue and MaxValue , Convert.ToByte and the others raise an error when the number does not fit, TryParse for user input, DivRem , Int128 , Single against Double , compare fractions with a small difference."}, {"u": "tut-101-number-types.html#exercises", "t": "Number types", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Write readPort(cText) that returns a port number (1 to 65535) or -1, with UInt16.TryParse . Find the first number that a Single can not increase by 1 (16777216) with a loop of Convert.ToSingle . Print the limits of SByte , UInt16 , UInt32 and UInt64 . Add the amounts 0.1 ten times in a loop and compare the sum with 1 in both ways."}, {"u": "tut-101-number-types.html#next", "t": "Number types", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Rounding and the Math class"}, {"u": "tut-126-rounding.html", "t": "Rounding and the Math class", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-126-rounding.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Rounding and the Math class", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # Round and its modes : ToEven and AwayFromZero , Round with a number of digits, Floor , Ceiling and Truncate , and what they do to negative numbers, the remainder operator % , keeping a number between a minimum and a maximum, Sign , Abs , Pow , Sqrt , Cbrt and the logarithms, why money needs one rounding rule. The tutorial Numbers, dates and times used the basic functions and the tutorial Money showed why money needs care. This lesson is about the rules of rounding. Note Ring shows a number with two decimals unless you change it with decimals(n) . The program uses decimals"}, {"u": "tut-126-rounding.html#the-program", "t": "Rounding and the Math class", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # rounding and the Math class - Round with its modes, Floor, Ceiling, Truncate, DivRem, Clamp, Sign and the rounding of money load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" } func main oMath = new Math() # 1. Round has a MODE for the numbers that are exactly in the middle (x.5). \"ToEven\" (the default of .NET, called banker's rounding) goes to # the even neighbor, so that rounding many numbers does not push the total up; \"AwayFromZero\" is the rule taught at school. ? \"1. Round of x.5 with the two modes:\" ? \" \" + padRight(\"value\",8) + padRight(\"ToEven\",9) + \"AwayFromZero\" for nValue in [ 0.5,"}, {"u": "tut-126-rounding.html#how-it-works", "t": "Rounding and the Math class", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # Two modes for the middle # A number that is exactly in the middle between two whole numbers ( 2.5 ) must go one way. ToEven (the default of .NET, also called banker's rounding ) goes to the neighbor that is even: 0.5 and 2.5 go down, 1.5 and 3.5 go up. Over many numbers the errors cancel, so the sum of the rounded numbers stays close to the real sum. AwayFromZero is the rule taught at school: 2.5 becomes 3 and -2.5 becomes -3 . The mode is a text, written in the named values of the call: Round([ :value = 2.5, :mode = \"AwayFromZero\" ]) . Digits # :digits = 2 rounds to hundredths."}, {"u": "tut-126-rounding.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Rounding and the Math class", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # Round with ToEven and AwayFromZero , with and without digits, Floor , Ceiling and Truncate , the remainder of negative numbers, limits with Clamp , one rounding rule for money."}, {"u": "tut-126-rounding.html#exercises", "t": "Rounding and the Math class", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Write roundToStep(value, step) that rounds to the nearest 0.05 (the smallest coin) and test it with 1.02 and 1.03 . Write mod(n, m) that is never negative and use it for the days of a week. Add the prices 19.99 , 5.49 and 0.5 rounded to cents with both modes. Print Log2 of the powers of two from 1 to 1024 in a table."}, {"u": "tut-126-rounding.html#next", "t": "Rounding and the Math class", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Big numbers"}, {"u": "tut-90-big-numbers.html", "t": "Big numbers", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-90-big-numbers.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Big numbers", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # the limit of a Ring number and why it exists, BigInteger : Parse , Add , Multiply , Pow , Remainder , ModPow , GreatestCommonDivisor , Compare , exact factorials and Fibonacci numbers, Complex : the real and imaginary parts, Magnitude , Multiply . (The tutorial Money used Decimal for exact decimals; this lesson is about exact large whole numbers.)"}, {"u": "tut-90-big-numbers.html#the-program", "t": "Big numbers", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # numbers that do not fit - BigInteger for exact big whole numbers, Complex numbers and the limits of Ring numbers load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.Numerics\" } func main # 1. A Ring number is a double: whole numbers are exact up to 9007199254740992 (2^53). After that, the last digits are lost. ? \"1. Ring: 9007199254740992 + 1 = \" + (9007199254740992 + 1) + \" (the +1 is lost)\" # 2. BigInteger keeps every digit. It is made from a text, and its result is shown as a text. oBig = new BigInteger() oA = oBig.Parse(\"9007199254740992\") oB = oBig.Add(oA,oBig.One) ? nl + \"2. BigI"}, {"u": "tut-90-big-numbers.html#how-it-works", "t": "Big numbers", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # The limit of Ring numbers # A double stores 53 bits of precision. Whole numbers are exact up to 2^53 = 9007199254740992; above that, + 1 can be lost, as step 1 shows (it prints the same number). BigInteger # A BigInteger is a struct from System.Numerics . In Ring you create it from a text ( Parse(\"9007199254740992\") ), calculate with the static methods ( Add , Multiply , Pow , ...) and show it with ToString() . The operators of Ring work on these objects too ( oA + oB , oA * oB , oA > oB ), because the type defines them (step 2 shows + and > ); the loops use the static methods, "}, {"u": "tut-90-big-numbers.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Big numbers", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # Ring numbers are exact up to 2^53, BigInteger is created from a text and used through static methods, Pow , ModPow , GreatestCommonDivisor , Compare , Complex with Real , Imaginary and Magnitude ."}, {"u": "tut-90-big-numbers.html#exercises", "t": "Big numbers", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Print 100! and count its digits. Find the first Fibonacci number with 100 digits. Write isEven(oBig) with Remainder , and test it with 2^100 and 2^100 + 1. Multiply two complex numbers by hand and check the result with Multiply ."}, {"u": "tut-90-big-numbers.html#next", "t": "Big numbers", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Number systems and big numbers"}, {"u": "tut-67-bases.html", "t": "Number systems and big numbers", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-67-bases.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Number systems and big numbers", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # writing a number in base 2, 8, 10 and 16 and reading it back, why programmers like hexadecimal (colors, bytes), counting bits and shifting, how negative numbers look in binary, the limit of a Ring number (a double ) and how BigInteger removes it."}, {"u": "tut-67-bases.html#bases", "t": "Number systems and big numbers", "h": "Bases", "x": "Bases # The number two hundred fifty-five can be written in any base : Base Name Digits 255 is 2 binary 0 1 11111111 8 octal 0\u20137 377 10 decimal 0\u20139 255 16 hexadecimal 0\u20139 a\u2013f ff Computers store bits, so base 2 is what they use; but it is long to read. One hexadecimal digit is exactly 4 bits , so a byte is exactly two hex digits ( 00 to ff ). That is why colors ( #ff8000 ), memory addresses, hashes and byte dumps (the tutorial Binary files ) are written in hex."}, {"u": "tut-67-bases.html#the-program", "t": "Number systems and big numbers", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # number systems and big numbers - binary, hexadecimal, bits, and numbers that do not fit in a double load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.Numerics\" } func main oConvert = new Convert() # 1. The same number in different bases: 2 (binary), 8 (octal), 10 (decimal), 16 (hexadecimal) ? \"1. 255 in base 2 : \" + oConvert.Format([ :value = 255, :toBase = 2 ]) ? \" 255 in base 8 : \" + oConvert.Format([ :value = 255, :toBase = 8 ]) ? \" 255 in base 16: \" + oConvert.Format([ :value = 255, :toBase = 16 ]) # 2. And back: a text in a base to a number ? nl + \"2. 'ff' in base 16 = \" + oConv"}, {"u": "tut-67-bases.html#how-it-works", "t": "Number systems and big numbers", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # Converting # Convert.ToString(number, base) writes a number in a base and Convert.ToInt32(text, base) reads it. In Ring the two-argument forms take a list of named values: Format([ :value = 255, :toBase = 16 ]) and ToInt32([ :value = \"ff\", :fromBase = 16 ]) ( ToString with parameters is Format ). A color is three numbers: hex2 writes each as two hex digits, with a leading zero when needed, and the reverse takes each pair of digits. Bits # BitOperations.PopCount(n) counts the one-bits (255 has 8, 256 has 1) and IsPow2(n) tests for a power of two \u2014 a number that is a power of two "}, {"u": "tut-67-bases.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Number systems and big numbers", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # Convert.ToString and ToInt32 with a base, hex, bytes and colors, PopCount , IsPow2 , << , >> , two's complement, the 2\u2075\u00b3 limit and BigInteger ."}, {"u": "tut-67-bases.html#exercises", "t": "Number systems and big numbers", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Write toBinary(n) yourself with division by 2 and compare with Convert . Compute 100! and count its digits. Parse a color like #1e90ff into three numbers and back. Find the largest power of two below 10 million with PopCount or IsPow2 ."}, {"u": "tut-67-bases.html#next", "t": "Number systems and big numbers", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Money"}, {"u": "tut-57-money.html", "t": "Money", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-57-money.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Money", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # why fractions are inexact in every computer language, the cents rule: whole numbers inside, decimals only for display, rounding rules: banker's rounding and school rounding, splitting an amount fairly, taxes on the total, not on each line."}, {"u": "tut-57-money.html#the-problem", "t": "Money", "h": "The problem", "x": "The problem # Computers store numbers in binary. Just as 1/3 cannot be written exactly in decimal (0.3333\u2026), 0.1 cannot be written exactly in binary, so 0.1 + 0.2 is 0.30000000000000004 and is not equal to 0.3 . Ten additions of 0.1 do not give exactly 1. For physics this is fine; for money it is not: a shop must never be one cent off."}, {"u": "tut-57-money.html#the-program", "t": "Money", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # money - why 0.1 + 0.2 is not 0.3, cents as whole numbers, rounding rules and fair splitting load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" } func main # 1. Computers store fractions in binary: 0.1 and 0.2 cannot be written exactly, so tiny errors appear ? \"1. 0.1 + 0.2 = 0.3 ? \" + yesNo((0.1 + 0.2) = 0.3) nTotal = 0 for nI = 1 to 10 nTotal += 0.1 next ? \" ten times 0.1 equals 1 ? \" + yesNo(nTotal = 1) # 2. The rule for money: keep whole CENTS (integers) and only turn them into \"12.50\" when you show them nPriceCents = toCents(\"19.99\") nTotalCents = nPriceCents * 3 ? nl + \"2. three items of"}, {"u": "tut-57-money.html#how-it-works", "t": "Money", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # Cents as integers # Convert to whole cents when the amount comes in ( toCents(\"19.99\") \u2192 1999 ), calculate with integers (they are exact), and turn them back to text only for display ( showMoney ). Three items of 19.99 are 5997 cents: no error is possible. Note The .NET Decimal type is exact, but a decimal that crosses the bridge becomes a Ring number (a binary fraction again). In Ring, whole cents are the safe way. A database keeps money in integer columns for the same reason. Rounding # Percentages create parts of cents (7.5 % of 1999 is 149.925). You need a rule, and the rule"}, {"u": "tut-57-money.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Money", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # binary fractions are inexact, whole cents for calculation, text for display, rounding modes, fair splitting, tax on the total."}, {"u": "tut-57-money.html#exercises", "t": "Money", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Add a discount of 15 % to the invoice and decide when to round. Write showMoneyIn(nCents, cCulture) using CultureInfo (the tutorial Cultures ). Split 10.00 between 7 people and check the sum."}, {"u": "tut-57-money.html#next", "t": "Money", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Vectors and matrices"}, {"u": "tut-99-vectors.html", "t": "Vectors and matrices", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-99-vectors.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Vectors and matrices", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # Vector2 , Vector3 : a point or a direction, Length , Distance , Normalize , Add , Subtract , Multiply , Negate and Lerp , the dot product and the cross product and what they tell you, Matrix3x2 : a move, a rotation, and both together. The tutorial Drawing and animation moved shapes with Ring numbers. With vectors you describe a position as one value, and a transformation as one matrix, so the same code works for one point or for a thousand."}, {"u": "tut-99-vectors.html#the-program", "t": "Vectors and matrices", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # vectors and matrices - Vector3 (length, dot and cross product, distance), Vector2 and a rotation with Matrix3x2 load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.Numerics\" } func main # 1. A Vector3 holds three numbers (X, Y, Z): a point or a direction in space (Vector2 has two numbers, Vector4 four). # The numbers are single-precision floats, so results are shown rounded. oA = new Vector3().@New([ :x = 3, :y = 4, :z = 0 ]) oB = new Vector3().@New([ :x = 1, :y = 2, :z = 3 ]) ? \"1. a = \" + show3(oA) + \", length of a = \" + oA.Length() + \", squared length = \" + oA.LengthSquared() # 2. T"}, {"u": "tut-99-vectors.html#how-it-works", "t": "Vectors and matrices", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # Vectors # A Vector3 holds three numbers X , Y , Z . It can be a position (a point in space) or a direction with a length (a speed, a force). Vector2 has two numbers and Vector4 four. They are value types (structs): new Vector3().@New([ :x = 3, :y = 4, :z = 0 ]) makes one by names, and oV.X reads a component. The numbers are single-precision floats (about 7 digits), so a result such as 0.6 can really be 0.6000000238 ; the program rounds before printing ( roundNumber ), and so should yours when you compare. Operations # The operators of Ring work on these structs: oA + oB adds two"}, {"u": "tut-99-vectors.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Vectors and matrices", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # vectors as points and directions, length, distance, unit vectors and Lerp , the dot product (a number) and the cross product (a vector), matrices that move and rotate, and combining them, the order of transformations matters."}, {"u": "tut-99-vectors.html#exercises", "t": "Vectors and matrices", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Write angleBetween(a, b) with Dot , Length and Acos (the tutorial Numbers, dates and times ). Move a point along a straight line from A to B in 10 steps with Lerp and print the points. Rotate the four corners of a square by 45 degrees and print them. Try move first, then turn and compare the result with step 6."}, {"u": "tut-99-vectors.html#next", "t": "Vectors and matrices", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Randomness"}, {"u": "tut-69-random.html", "t": "Randomness", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-69-random.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Randomness", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # the Random class and the seed , throwing dice and checking that they are fair, shuffling with the Fisher\u2013Yates algorithm, weighted choices, Guid for unique ids, secure randomness for passwords and tokens."}, {"u": "tut-69-random.html#the-program", "t": "Randomness", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # randomness - seeds, dice, shuffling cards, weighted choices, unique ids, and when \"random\" must be secure load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.Security.Cryptography\" } func main # 1. Random with a SEED gives the same sequence every time: perfect for tests and for repeating a simulation oFirst = new Random(2024) oSecond = new Random(2024) ? \"1. two generators with the same seed: \" + oFirst.@Next(100) + \" \" + oFirst.@Next(100) + \" \" + oFirst.@Next(100) + \" and \" + oSecond.@Next(100) + \" \" + oSecond.@Next(100) + \" \" + oSecond.@Next(100) # 2. @Next(max) is 0..max-1. A die is"}, {"u": "tut-69-random.html#how-it-works", "t": "Randomness", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # Random numbers are calculated # Random does not roll dice: it follows a formula that gives numbers that look random. The starting value is the seed . The same seed gives the same numbers (step 1), which is exactly what you want for a test or a replay: a game level that is the same for the same seed, a simulation you can repeat. Without a seed, new Random(NULL) starts from the clock. @Next(100) gives 0\u202699 ( Next is a Ring keyword, so the method is called @Next ). Dice # @Next(6) + 1 is 1\u20266. With 6,000 throws every side comes up about 1,000 times; the test in the program accepts 9"}, {"u": "tut-69-random.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Randomness", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # seeds and repeatable randomness, ranges and testing random code, Fisher\u2013Yates shuffling, weighted choice, Guid , Random against RandomNumberGenerator ."}, {"u": "tut-69-random.html#exercises", "t": "Randomness", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Throw two dice 36,000 times and print how often each sum (2\u202612) appears. Write a password generator with RandomNumberGenerator.GetInt32 and a list of allowed characters. Shuffle a deck of 52 cards (numbers 1\u201352) and deal four hands of five. Estimate the probability of at least one six in four throws by simulation, then calculate it exactly."}, {"u": "tut-69-random.html#next", "t": "Randomness", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Statistics and simulation"}, {"u": "tut-44-statistics.html", "t": "Statistics and simulation", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-44-statistics.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Statistics and simulation", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # the common statistics and how to compute them, writing each formula as a function, cross-checking with LINQ, the sieve of Eratosthenes, a Monte Carlo simulation with a seeded Random ."}, {"u": "tut-44-statistics.html#the-program", "t": "Statistics and simulation", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # numbers - statistics, primes and a simulation (Monte Carlo estimate of pi) load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.Linq\" } func main aData = [4,8,15,16,23,42,8,15,8,4] # 1. The usual summaries of a set of numbers ? \"Data : \" + joinList(aData) ? \"Count, sum : \" + len(aData) + \", \" + sumOf(aData) ? \"Mean (average) : \" + mean(aData) ? \"Median (middle): \" + median(aData) ? \"Mode (common) : \" + mode(aData) ? \"Smallest, largest: \" + min(aData) + \", \" + max(aData) ? \"Standard deviation: \" + stdDev(aData) # 2. .NET gives the same answer with LINQ: a check of our own code ? nl + \"LI"}, {"u": "tut-44-statistics.html#the-statistics", "t": "Statistics and simulation", "h": "The statistics", "x": "The statistics # Name Meaning Function Mean the average: sum \u00f7 count mean Median the middle value of the sorted list (the average of the two middle ones when the count is even) median Mode the value that occurs most often mode Standard deviation how far values are from the mean, typically: the square root of the average squared distance stdDev For the data 4 8 15 16 23 42 8 15 8 4 (sorted: 4 4 8 8 8 15 15 16 23 42 ): the mean is 14.3 ; the median : there are ten values, so the middle is the pair at positions 5 and 6, which are 8 and 15 : (8 + 15) / 2 = 11.5 ; the mode is 8 (it occurs three tim"}, {"u": "tut-44-statistics.html#how-it-works", "t": "Statistics and simulation", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # One function per formula # Each statistic is a small function with one job that takes a list and returns a number. You can test each alone (see the tutorial Testing your programs ), reuse them, and the main program reads like a description of the results. The program also asks LINQ for the average ( dnQuery(aData).Average(\"\") ) and gets the same 14.3 : when two independent ways agree, you can trust the code. The histogram # A histogram counts how often each value occurs and shows it as a bar of # characters. The program collects the distinct values ( find prevents duplicates), s"}, {"u": "tut-44-statistics.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Statistics and simulation", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # mean, median, mode, standard deviation, histograms from counts, the sieve, gcd, lcm, simulation with a seeded Random , verify a result with a second method."}, {"u": "tut-44-statistics.html#exercises", "t": "Statistics and simulation", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Compute the 90th percentile of a list. Simulate 1000 throws of two dice and print the histogram of the sums. Find all perfect numbers below 10000 (a number that equals the sum of its divisors). Estimate the probability that two random people in a group of 23 share a birthday (the birthday paradox) with 10000 simulated groups."}, {"u": "tut-44-statistics.html#next", "t": "Statistics and simulation", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Dates and times"}, {"u": "tut-103-dates-and-times.html", "t": "Dates and times", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-103-dates-and-times.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Dates and times", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # DateOnly and TimeOnly : say in the type that you have only a date, or only a time, TimeSpan : the length between two moments, DateTimeOffset : a moment together with its distance from UTC, what AddMonths and AddYears do at the end of a month, counting days and computing an age, reading a date with an exact format. The tutorial Numbers, dates and times made DateTime values and the tutorial Time zones converted between time zones. This lesson adds the types that make a program clearer and safer."}, {"u": "tut-103-dates-and-times.html#the-program", "t": "Dates and times", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # dates and times - DateOnly, TimeOnly, DateTimeOffset, TimeSpan and calculations that do not break load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.Globalization\" } func main # 1. DateTime mixes a date and a time. When you only need ONE of them, DateOnly and TimeOnly say it in the type: a birthday has no # time, an opening hour has no date. oDate = makeDate(2024,2,28) ? \"1. a date: \" + oDate.Format(\"yyyy-MM-dd\") + \", \" + oDate.DayOfWeek + \", day \" + oDate.DayOfYear + \" of the year\" oNextDay = oDate.AddDays(2) ? \" 2 days later: \" + oNextDay.Format(\"yyyy-MM-dd\") + \" (2024 is a leap yea"}, {"u": "tut-103-dates-and-times.html#how-it-works", "t": "Dates and times", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # Date and time apart # A DateTime always has both a date and a time, so a birthday is midnight on that day and an opening hour is that hour on 1 January of year 1 . Neither is true. DateOnly is a day (year, month, day) and TimeOnly a time of day, so the program cannot mix them up. DateOnly knows the DayOfWeek and the DayOfYear (the 28th of February is day 59) and AddDays goes over the end of the month and over a leap day. TimeOnly.AddHours(3) after 22:30 gives 01:30: a time of day goes round the clock and has no date to move to. These structs have several constructors: new TimeOn"}, {"u": "tut-103-dates-and-times.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Dates and times", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # DateOnly and TimeOnly make the meaning clear, TimeSpan parts against totals, DateTimeOffset is a real moment, AddMonths goes to the last day that exists, how to count an age, TryParseExact for dates that people type."}, {"u": "tut-103-dates-and-times.html#exercises", "t": "Dates and times", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Write lastDayOfMonth(year, month) with DateOnly and AddMonths . Print the next 6 due dates of a bill that is due on the 31st (add months to the first date). Write daysUntil(date) that counts the days to the next birthday. Show the same moment as a DateTimeOffset in three offsets."}, {"u": "tut-103-dates-and-times.html#next", "t": "Dates and times", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Time zones"}, {"u": "tut-68-timezones.html", "t": "Time zones", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-68-timezones.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Time zones", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # UTC and local time, and why you store UTC, TimeZoneInfo and converting between zones, the effect of daylight saving time , the duration of a flight, computed in UTC, the day that has 23 hours."}, {"u": "tut-68-timezones.html#the-rule", "t": "Time zones", "h": "The rule", "x": "The rule # Note Store and calculate in UTC. Convert to local time only when you show a time to a person. UTC ( coordinated universal time ) is the same everywhere and has no daylight saving. A local time is UTC plus an offset that depends on the place and the date : New York is UTC\u22125 in winter and UTC\u22124 in summer. If you keep local times in a database you cannot even tell which of two events came first."}, {"u": "tut-68-timezones.html#the-program", "t": "Time zones", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # time zones - UTC, local time, daylight saving, a meeting for several cities and a flight load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" } func main oZones = new TimeZoneInfo() aCities = [[\"New York\",\"Eastern Standard Time\"],[\"London\",\"GMT Standard Time\"],[\"Cairo\",\"Egypt Standard Time\"],[\"Tokyo\",\"Tokyo Standard Time\"]] # 1. A meeting at 12:00 UTC. Store and compare times in UTC; convert to local time only to SHOW them. ? \"1. the meeting at 12:00 UTC on 1 July 2024:\" oSummer = new DateTime(2024,7,1).AddHours(12) for aCity in aCities oLocal = localTime(oZones,oSummer,aCity[2]) ? \" \" + padRig"}, {"u": "tut-68-timezones.html#how-it-works", "t": "Time zones", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # Zones # TimeZoneInfo.FindSystemTimeZoneById(\"Eastern Standard Time\") finds a zone by its identifier. On Windows the identifiers are names such as Eastern Standard Time , GMT Standard Time , Egypt Standard Time and Tokyo Standard Time (Linux and macOS use America/New_York ). ConvertTimeFromUtc(utc, zone) gives the local time and ConvertTimeToUtc([ :dateTime = local, :sourceTimeZone = zone ]) goes back. GetUtcOffset(time) tells the offset at that moment , so it includes daylight saving. Daylight saving # Steps 1 and 2 show the same UTC moment in July and in January. New York goes "}, {"u": "tut-68-timezones.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Time zones", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # UTC for storing, local time for showing, FindSystemTimeZoneById , ConvertTimeFromUtc , ConvertTimeToUtc , GetUtcOffset , daylight saving rules differ and change, durations are added in UTC, invalid and ambiguous times."}, {"u": "tut-68-timezones.html#exercises", "t": "Time zones", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Show the current time in four cities ( DateTime.UtcNow and ConvertTimeFromUtc ). Find the hours of the day when offices in New York and Tokyo are both open (9\u201317). Find a time that is ambiguous in New York in November 2024 ( IsAmbiguousTime ). List all the zones with TimeZoneInfo.GetSystemTimeZones and print those with a +05:30 offset."}, {"u": "tut-68-timezones.html#next", "t": "Time zones", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Calendars and dates"}, {"u": "tut-43-calendar.html", "t": "Calendars and dates", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-43-calendar.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Calendars and dates", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # DaysInMonth , DayOfWeek , DayOfYear , adding months and years safely, computing an age, business days and the next birthday, Subtract and TimeSpan ."}, {"u": "tut-43-calendar.html#why-not-do-it-yourself", "t": "Calendars and dates", "h": "Why not do it yourself", "x": "Why not do it yourself # Imagine adding one month to 31 January by hand. Is it 31 February? Does the answer depend on the year? DateTime knows all of this. The rule of this lesson: let DateTime do the calendar arithmetic ; you only decide what to ask."}, {"u": "tut-43-calendar.html#the-program", "t": "Calendars and dates", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # calendars - month tables, ages, business days and birthdays with DateTime load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" } func main # 1. A text calendar of a month: how many days it has, and on which weekday the 1st falls ? monthCalendar(2024,2) # 2. How old is somebody? Years, months and days between two dates ? \"Age of a person born on 1990-03-15, on 2024-03-14: \" + ageText(1990,3,15,2024,3,14) ? \" and on 2024-03-15 : \" + ageText(1990,3,15,2024,3,15) # 3. Days between dates, and business days (Monday to Friday) oFrom = new DateTime(2024,12,20) oTo = new DateTime(2025,1,6) ? nl + \"From "}, {"u": "tut-43-calendar.html#how-it-works", "t": "Calendars and dates", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # The month calendar # DaysInMonth(year, month) gives the number of days (29 for February 2024). DayOfWeek of the first day tells in which column it starts: the week starts on Monday, so the column number is the position of the weekday name in the list Monday ... Sunday , minus one. The program then prints the days with right(\" \" + day,2) so that the numbers line up, and starts a new line after the seventh column. Age # An age is complete years, months and days . The simple subtraction year - birthYear is wrong before the birthday. The method: subtract years, months and days separ"}, {"u": "tut-43-calendar.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Calendars and dates", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # let DateTime do the calendar work, ages need borrowing, business days need a loop and a weekday check, additions clamp to the end of the month."}, {"u": "tut-43-calendar.html#exercises", "t": "Calendars and dates", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Print the calendar of the current month ( DateTime.Now ) and mark today with [ ] . Find the next Friday the 13th after today. Add a list of holidays to businessDays and skip them. Write isLeapYear(year) by your own rule and test it against DateTime.IsLeapYear ."}, {"u": "tut-43-calendar.html#next", "t": "Calendars and dates", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Other calendars"}, {"u": "tut-76-calendars.html", "t": "Other calendars", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-76-calendars.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Other calendars", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # the Calendar classes of System.Globalization , reading a DateTime in another calendar ( GetYear , GetMonth , GetDayOfMonth ), converting a date of another calendar to a DateTime ( ToDateTime ), month lengths and why Ramadan moves through the year, month and day names in the language of a culture ( ar-SA )."}, {"u": "tut-76-calendars.html#one-moment-many-calendars", "t": "Other calendars", "h": "One moment, many calendars", "x": "One moment, many calendars # A .NET DateTime always stores a Gregorian date. A calendar object is a translator: you give it the DateTime and ask for the year, month and day as that calendar counts them . The classes you need are UmAlQuraCalendar (the Hijri calendar used in Saudi Arabia), HijriCalendar , PersianCalendar , HebrewCalendar , JapaneseCalendar , ThaiBuddhistCalendar and GregorianCalendar . All are in the namespace System.Globalization , so the program starts with using { \"System\", \"System.Globalization\" } ."}, {"u": "tut-76-calendars.html#the-program", "t": "Other calendars", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # other calendars - Hijri (Umm al-Qura), Persian, Hebrew, Japanese and Thai Buddhist dates with System.Globalization load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.Globalization\" } func main oDate = new DateTime(2024,3,11) # a DateTime always holds a Gregorian date; a Calendar object reads it in its own way # 1. The same day in several calendars. Each calendar class has GetYear, GetMonth and GetDayOfMonth. ? \"1. the day 2024-03-11 in:\" ? \" Gregorian : \" + dateText(new GregorianCalendar(),oDate) ? \" Hijri (Umm al-Qura): \" + dateText(new UmAlQuraCalendar(),oDate) ? \" Persian : \" + dat"}, {"u": "tut-76-calendars.html#how-it-works", "t": "Other calendars", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # Reading a date # Every calendar has the same three methods, so one helper ( dateText ) works for all of them. 11 March 2024 is 1 Ramadan 1445 in the Umm al-Qura calendar, 21 Esfand 1402 in the Persian calendar, and the year 2567 in the Thai Buddhist calendar. The Japanese calendar counts years within an era : 2024 is year 6 of Reiwa. Going back # calendar.ToDateTime(year, month, day, hour, minute, second, millisecond) builds the DateTime from a date in that calendar: oHijri.ToDateTime([ :year = 1445, :month = 9, :day = 1, :hour = 0, :minute = 0, :second = 0, :millisecond = 0 ]) "}, {"u": "tut-76-calendars.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Other calendars", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # calendars are translators of a DateTime , GetYear , GetMonth , GetDayOfMonth , GetDaysInMonth , ToDateTime , Arabic names and calendars with CultureInfo(\"ar-SA\") , how a calendar year differs from the Gregorian one."}, {"u": "tut-76-calendars.html#exercises", "t": "Other calendars", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Print today's date in the Hijri and the Persian calendar. Find how many days are left until the next 1 Ramadan. Use PersianCalendar to list the months of the current Persian year with their lengths. Show a date with Format in fa-IR and he-IL ."}, {"u": "tut-76-calendars.html#next", "t": "Other calendars", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Working days"}, {"u": "tut-135-workdays.html", "t": "Working days", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-135-workdays.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Working days", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # DayOfWeek and a weekend that is kept in one place , adding working days : a loop over AddDays(1) that skips weekends and holidays, counting working days between two dates, ISO week numbers and why week 1 can start in December, quarters and DaysInMonth (with leap years), an age . The tutorial Dates and times and the tutorial Calendars and dates showed the date types and the calendar of a month. This lesson is about the rules that business programs add."}, {"u": "tut-135-workdays.html#the-program", "t": "Working days", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # working days - adding business days, skipping weekends and holidays, week numbers, quarters, month ends and an age load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.Globalization\" } # the holidays of this example (fixed dates written as year-month-day) aHolidays = [ \"2030-01-01\", \"2030-12-25\", \"2030-12-26\" ] func main # 1. The day of the week. DayOfWeek is a text: \"Monday\" ... \"Sunday\". Many countries have another weekend (Friday and Saturday), so the # weekend is a list in one place. oDate = makeDate(2030,3,9) ? \"1. \" + oDate.Format(\"yyyy-MM-dd\") + \" is a \" + oDate.DayOfWeek + \", we"}, {"u": "tut-135-workdays.html#how-it-works", "t": "Working days", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # The weekend and the holidays # DayOfWeek gives the name of the day as a text. The function isWeekend compares it with \"Saturday\" and \"Sunday\" ; some countries have a weekend on Friday and Saturday, and then only this function changes. The holidays are a Ring list of dates written as year-month-day , and isHoliday looks for the date in it. Real programs keep the holidays in a file or a table for each country and year (and some holidays, like Easter, move: they come from a rule, not from a fixed date). Adding working days # There is no formula that handles weekends and holidays, s"}, {"u": "tut-135-workdays.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Working days", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # the weekend and the holidays as data in one place, adding and counting working days, the ISO week and its year, quarters, month lengths and ages."}, {"u": "tut-135-workdays.html#exercises", "t": "Working days", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Write previousWorkday(date) and nextWorkday(date) . Write lastWorkdayOfMonth(year, month) . Add Easter Monday: compute Easter Sunday with the Gregorian algorithm and add one day. Print a calendar for one ISO week: the seven dates and which are working days."}, {"u": "tut-135-workdays.html#next", "t": "Working days", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Cultures"}, {"u": "tut-33-cultures.html", "t": "Cultures", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-33-cultures.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Cultures", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # what a culture is, formatting numbers, money and dates for other cultures, reading numbers with the right culture, the invariant culture for data, comparing and changing the case of text per language."}, {"u": "tut-33-cultures.html#what-is-a-culture", "t": "Cultures", "h": "What is a culture?", "x": "What is a culture? # A culture is a language plus, optionally, a country: en-US (English in the United States), de-DE (German in Germany), ar-EG (Arabic in Egypt). It decides: the symbols: the decimal separator ( . or , ), the thousands separator, the currency sign, the order and names of dates: 7/4/2024 , 04.07.2024 , the names of days and months, the rules for sorting and for upper and lower case. Every ToString and Parse that you call without saying a culture uses the culture of the computer's user . That is exactly right for text that people read, and exactly wrong for data that programs e"}, {"u": "tut-33-cultures.html#the-program", "t": "Cultures", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # cultures - the same number, date and text written, read and sorted in different languages load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.Globalization\" } func main oString = new String() oConvert = new Convert() aCultures = [\"en-US\",\"de-DE\",\"fr-FR\",\"ar-EG\",\"ja-JP\"] # A culture (language + country) decides how numbers, money and dates are written ? \"A number, money and a date in five cultures:\" oDate = new DateTime(2024,7,4) for cName in aCultures oCulture = new CultureInfo(cName) cNumber = oString.Format([ :provider = oCulture, :format = \"{0:N2}\", :arg0 = 1234567.891 ]) cMoney = o"}, {"u": "tut-33-cultures.html#how-it-works", "t": "Cultures", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # Writing # String.Format(culture, \"{0:N2}\", value) formats with a format code : N2 is a number with two decimals and a thousands separator, C is currency, D (on a date) the long date. The same value 1234567.891 becomes 1,234,567.89 in English, 1.234.567,89 in German, 1 234 567,89 in French (with a special space) and uses Arabic digits for ar-EG . Reading # Double.Parse(text, culture) reads a number written in that culture . The text 1.234,5 is 1234.5 for the German culture, but an error or a different number for the English culture, where the comma means \"thousands\". TryParse avo"}, {"u": "tut-33-cultures.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Cultures", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # a culture is a set of writing rules, say the culture when you format or parse, invariant for data, user culture for people, sorting and case depend on the language."}, {"u": "tut-33-cultures.html#exercises", "t": "Cultures", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Print today's date in a short format in six cultures of your choice. Parse \"3,5\" with fr-FR and with en-US : which works? Sort the words zebra apple \u00c4rger \u00d6l ananas in German and in Swedish. Write a function toData(nNumber) that formats a number with the invariant culture, and fromData(cText) that reads it back. Test both."}, {"u": "tut-33-cultures.html#where-to-go-next", "t": "Cultures", "h": "Where to go next", "x": "Where to go next # This was the last tutorial of the chapter Numbers, dates and money . The next chapter is Collections and algorithms : it starts with Arrays . The Tutorials page lists all the chapters, and you can start any of them."}, {"u": "tut-106-arrays.html", "t": "Arrays", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-106-arrays.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Arrays", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # how a .NET array differs from a Ring list, making a typed array ( List<T>.ToArray() ), toList() to read an array in Ring, the Array methods: Sort , Reverse , IndexOf , BinarySearch , Copy , Clear , zero-based positions, an array with two dimensions. The tutorial Collections and LINQ used lists and dictionaries, the tutorial Binary files used arrays of bytes and the tutorial More data structures other collections. Arrays are the base of all of them."}, {"u": "tut-106-arrays.html#the-program", "t": "Arrays", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # arrays - Ring lists and .NET arrays, Array.Sort, Reverse, IndexOf, BinarySearch, Copy and Clear, and arrays with two dimensions load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" } func main oArray = new Array() # 1. A .NET array has a fixed size and one type. A Ring list becomes an array when a method asks for one: the bridge makes an int[], # string[] or double[] from the values of the list. An array that comes back is a DotNetObject: toList() makes a Ring list of it. aNumbers = [42,7,19,3,25] new Array().Sort(aNumbers) ? \"1. a list given to Array.Sort comes back unchanged: \" + list2text(aN"}, {"u": "tut-106-arrays.html#how-it-works", "t": "Arrays", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # Arrays and lists # A .NET array has a fixed length and one type ( int[] , string[] , double[] ), so it is compact and fast and an array of numbers never holds a text. A Ring list can change size and hold any mix. When a method wants an array and you pass a list, the bridge makes an array from the values (an int[] for whole numbers, a string[] for texts) for that call only . The array that the method sorts or changes is a copy : in step 1 the Ring list is unchanged after Array.Sort . To work on an array you must have the array, as a .NET object. Making an array # The easy way is "}, {"u": "tut-106-arrays.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Arrays", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # an array has a fixed size and one type; a list given to .NET becomes an array copy, typed arrays from List<T>.ToArray() and back with toList() , Sort , Reverse , IndexOf , BinarySearch , Copy , Clear , positions start at 0, BinarySearch needs a sorted array and answers a negative number for \"not found\", two dimensions with CreateInstance and @GetLength ."}, {"u": "tut-106-arrays.html#exercises", "t": "Arrays", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Write contains(oArray, value) with IndexOf . Fill an array with 100 000 random numbers (the tutorial Randomness ), sort it and compare IndexOf and BinarySearch with a Stopwatch . Use BinarySearch to insert a value into a sorted list at the right place. Fill a 9 x 9 Double array with the multiplication table and print it."}, {"u": "tut-106-arrays.html#next", "t": "Arrays", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Sets"}, {"u": "tut-107-sets.html", "t": "Sets", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-107-sets.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Sets", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # what a set is and why it is fast, HashSet : Add , Remove , Count , UnionWith , IntersectWith , ExceptWith , SymmetricExceptWith , IsSubsetOf , IsSupersetOf , Overlaps , SetEquals , a set with a comparer (case ignored), removing duplicates from a list. The tutorial More data structures showed SortedSet , a set that keeps its values in order. HashSet does not keep an order, and it is faster."}, {"u": "tut-107-sets.html#the-program", "t": "Sets", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # sets - HashSet, union, intersection, difference, subsets, and how a set removes duplicates and finds common items fast load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" } func main cText = \"System.String\" # A set holds each value ONCE and has no order. Adding a value that is already there changes nothing. Looking for a value is # very fast, even with a million values, because a set uses a hash table (a list has to look at every item). oSetA = makeSet([\"ring\",\"dotnet\",\"linq\",\"json\",\"web\"]) oSetB = makeSet([\"web\",\"json\",\"tcp\",\"udp\",\"ring\",\"ring\"]) ? \"1. A has \" + oSetA.Count + \" items: \" + sor"}, {"u": "tut-107-sets.html#how-it-works", "t": "Sets", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # One copy of each value # A set holds a value once . The list for set B has six words, ring twice, and the set has five items. Add answers whether the value was new ( true ) or already there ( false ); Remove takes a value away. There is no order : the items come out in an order that you must not depend on, so the program sorts them before printing. The set is stored in a hash table : a value is turned into a number that points at the place where it lives, so Contains takes the same short time for ten values and for ten million, while a list has to look at every item. When a prog"}, {"u": "tut-107-sets.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Sets", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # a set keeps one copy of each value and finds values fast, Add tells whether the value was new, union, intersection, difference and symmetric difference change the set, so copy first, IsSubsetOf , IsSupersetOf , Overlaps and SetEquals , comparers and removing duplicates."}, {"u": "tut-107-sets.html#exercises", "t": "Sets", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Find the words that two text files have in common (the tutorial Files and folders ). Check that a user has all the permissions of a list with IsSupersetOf (the tutorial Identities and claims ). Count the different words of a text (the tutorial Working with text ). Compare Contains in a HashSet and in a Ring list with 100 000 items and a Stopwatch ."}, {"u": "tut-107-sets.html#next", "t": "Sets", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # More data structures"}, {"u": "tut-92-data-structures.html", "t": "More data structures", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-92-data-structures.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "More data structures", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # PriorityQueue : always the most urgent item first, SortedSet : no duplicates and always sorted, SortedDictionary : keys in order, LinkedList : cheap changes at both ends and next to a node, BitArray : a million yes/no values in 125 KB, with logic operations, the Ring words that start with @ ( @Get , @And , @Or ). The tutorial Collections and LINQ used List and Dictionary , and the tutorial Collections for many threads the collections for many threads."}, {"u": "tut-92-data-structures.html#the-program", "t": "More data structures", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # more data structures - PriorityQueue, SortedSet, SortedDictionary, LinkedList and BitArray load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.Collections\" \"System.Collections.Generic\" } func main cInt = \"System.Int32\" cText = \"System.String\" # 1. PriorityQueue<element, priority>: Dequeue always gives the element with the SMALLEST priority, whatever the order of Enqueue. oTasks = new PriorityQueue([ :typeArguments = [cText,cInt] ]) oTasks.Enqueue(\"write the report\",3) oTasks.Enqueue(\"fix the server\",1) oTasks.Enqueue(\"answer the mail\",5) oTasks.Enqueue(\"call the customer\",2) ? \"1. \" + "}, {"u": "tut-92-data-structures.html#how-it-works", "t": "More data structures", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # Closed generic types # All of them but BitArray are generic: dnNewGeneric(type, [type arguments], [constructor values]) makes a closed type such as PriorityQueue<string, int> . Most have no Ring class, so those objects are used in the plain style: callMethod(\"Add\", [value]) , getProp(\"Count\") , and toList() to copy a collection into a Ring list ( Core concepts ). PriorityQueue # Enqueue(element, priority) adds; Dequeue() returns the element with the smallest priority number , whatever the order of adding. It is the right tool for a to-do list by urgency, for events by time, and "}, {"u": "tut-92-data-structures.html#what-you-learned", "t": "More data structures", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # five collections and what each one is for, the plain style ( callMethod , getProp , toList ) for closed generic types, BitArray operations change their own array, the few Ring words that start with @ ."}, {"u": "tut-92-data-structures.html#exercises", "t": "More data structures", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Make a task list with PriorityQueue where two tasks have the same priority: in which order do they come out? (Do not rely on it.) Use a SortedSet of words to print the words of a text without duplicates in alphabetical order. Find all primes below 1000 with a BitArray (the sieve of Eratosthenes). Build an undo list of the last 5 actions with a LinkedList ."}, {"u": "tut-92-data-structures.html#next", "t": "More data structures", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Collections for many threads"}, {"u": "tut-89-concurrent-collections.html", "t": "Collections for many threads", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-89-concurrent-collections.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Collections for many threads", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # why an ordinary list is not safe for several threads, ConcurrentQueue and ConcurrentStack , BlockingCollection : Add , Take , CompleteAdding , a bounded size, ConcurrentDictionary : TryAdd , TryUpdate , TryRemove , the Try... methods that return a list ( [ success, value ] ), generic types made with dnNewGeneric and used with callMethod and getProp ."}, {"u": "tut-89-concurrent-collections.html#why", "t": "Collections for many threads", "h": "Why", "x": "Why # If two threads add to one List at the same moment, the list can lose an item or break. The concurrent collections do each step (add, remove, \"add if missing\") as one indivisible step . They are the tool for a producer that adds work and a consumer that takes it. The program in this lesson uses one thread, so that its output is always the same; the calls are the same as in a program with many threads. (The tutorial Tasks that wait shows tasks.)"}, {"u": "tut-89-concurrent-collections.html#the-program", "t": "Collections for many threads", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # collections for many threads - ConcurrentQueue, ConcurrentStack, BlockingCollection and ConcurrentDictionary load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.Collections.Concurrent\" } func main # The collections of System.Collections.Concurrent are safe when several threads use them at the same time: the 'is it # there? then change it' steps are done as ONE step. They are generic, so they are made with dnNewGeneric (a type name, # the type arguments, the constructor values). cInt = \"System.Int32\" cText = \"System.String\" # 1. ConcurrentQueue: first in, first out. TryDequeue answers ["}, {"u": "tut-89-concurrent-collections.html#how-it-works", "t": "Collections for many threads", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # Generic types # These classes are generic ( ConcurrentQueue<T> ), so a Ring program creates a closed type with dnNewGeneric(type, [type arguments], [constructor values]) . Some closed types have a Ring class and work like any object (the queue of names in step 1: oQueue.Enqueue(...) , oQueue.Count ); for the others the object is a plain DotNetObject and you use callMethod(\"Name\", [arguments]) and getProp(\"Name\") ( Core concepts ). The result is the same, only the way of writing differs. A dictionary item is read with callMethod(\"get_Item\", [key]) . Try methods # TryDequeue , Try"}, {"u": "tut-89-concurrent-collections.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Collections for many threads", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # concurrent collections do each step as one step, the Try methods return [ success, value ] , BlockingCollection and CompleteAdding , bounded collections, dnNewGeneric with callMethod and getProp ."}, {"u": "tut-89-concurrent-collections.html#exercises", "t": "Collections for many threads", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Count the letters of a text in a ConcurrentDictionary with TryAdd and TryUpdate . Write a function drain(oQueue) that returns all the items of a ConcurrentQueue as a Ring list. Fill a bounded collection (size 3) with TryAdd and print what was refused. Use ConcurrentBag (an unordered collection) and compare it with the queue."}, {"u": "tut-89-concurrent-collections.html#next", "t": "Collections for many threads", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Enums and flags"}, {"u": "tut-65-enums.html", "t": "Enums and flags", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-65-enums.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Enums and flags", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # what an enum is and how it looks in Ring (a text), listing the names, testing a name, parsing a text, flags : several choices at once ( ReadOnly, Hidden ), your own flags with the bit operators & , | and ~ ."}, {"u": "tut-65-enums.html#enums", "t": "Enums and flags", "h": "Enums", "x": "Enums # An enum is a type with a fixed set of named values: DayOfWeek ( Sunday \u2026 Saturday ), FileAttributes , StringComparison , Environment.SpecialFolder . In C# you write DayOfWeek.Friday ; behind the name there is a number. In Ring an enum value is simply the text of its name : you read oDate.DayOfWeek and get \"Friday\" , and when a method wants an enum you pass \"Friday\" . You never need the numbers."}, {"u": "tut-65-enums.html#the-program", "t": "Enums and flags", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # enums and flags - named choices from .NET as text, checking and parsing them, and combining options with bits load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.IO\" } C_READ = 1 # the options of the permission bits in step 5 C_WRITE = 2 C_RUN = 4 func main oEnum = new Enum() oDays = dnType(\"System.DayOfWeek\") # 1. An enum is a fixed list of names. In Ring an enum value is a TEXT with the name: there is no number to remember. oDate = new DateTime(2024,5,17) ? \"1. 2024-05-17 is a \" + oDate.DayOfWeek + \". Is it a Friday? \" + yesNo(oDate.DayOfWeek = \"Friday\") # 2. All the names of an enum"}, {"u": "tut-65-enums.html#how-it-works", "t": "Enums and flags", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # Comparing # Step 1 compares the value with a text: oDate.DayOfWeek = \"Friday\" . It reads like English and cannot be confused with another number. Names, IsDefined and Parse # Enum.GetNames(type) lists all the names (the tutorial Exploring .NET with reflection explains dnType ). IsDefined asks whether a text is a valid name; use IsDefined([ :enumType = type, :value = text ]) (the two-argument form). Enum.Parse checks and converts: a wrong name raises an error that says Requested value 'Funday' was not found . When the text comes from a user or a file, either test it first or try "}, {"u": "tut-65-enums.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Enums and flags", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # enum values are texts in Ring, GetNames , IsDefined , Parse , flag enums are comma-separated texts, bit flags with | , & and ~ ."}, {"u": "tut-65-enums.html#exercises", "t": "Enums and flags", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Print all the names of FileAttributes , then read the attributes of a folder with File.GetAttributes and decide whether it is a directory. Add a C_DELETE = 8 option and a function parse(\"read,write\") that turns a text into the number. Use StringComparison ( \"OrdinalIgnoreCase\" ) with String.Equals to compare two texts without regard to case."}, {"u": "tut-65-enums.html#next", "t": "Enums and flags", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Identifiers and versions"}, {"u": "tut-93-identifiers.html", "t": "Identifiers and versions", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-93-identifiers.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Identifiers and versions", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # Guid : a new identifier, its text formats, parsing, Empty , why Guids can be made anywhere without a clash, Version : its parts and its comparison, sorting a list of versions correctly, the version of the running .NET."}, {"u": "tut-93-identifiers.html#the-program", "t": "Identifiers and versions", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # identifiers and versions - Guid, Version, and sorting version numbers by number and not as text load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.Collections.Generic\" } func main oGuid = new Guid() # 1. A Guid is a 128-bit number that is unique for practical purposes: two programs on two computers can make one each and # never meet the same value. It is shown as 32 hex digits in 5 groups. NewGuid is random: only its shape is the same. cNew = oGuid.NewGuid().ToString() ? \"1. a new Guid has \" + len(cNew) + \" characters, with dashes at 9, 14, 19 and 24: \" + (cNew[9] = \"-\" and cNew[14] ="}, {"u": "tut-93-identifiers.html#how-it-works", "t": "Identifiers and versions", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # Guid # A Guid is a 128-bit number. A new one is random, with so many possible values that two programs on two computers never make the same one in practice, so no server or counter has to hand them out. That makes a Guid a good key for a record that is created offline, a name for a temporary file and an id in a message. NewGuid() gives a new one; the output of step 1 shows only its shape (36 characters with dashes in fixed places), because the value is different at every run. Parse reads the usual text; Format(\"N\") writes 32 digits without dashes, \"B\" writes with braces and \"D\" "}, {"u": "tut-93-identifiers.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Identifiers and versions", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # Guid : new, parse, formats, Empty , TryParse , a Guid is unique but not secret, Version compares numbers, not text, sorting versions with a List<Version> ."}, {"u": "tut-93-identifiers.html#exercises", "t": "Identifiers and versions", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Make 100000 Guids and check that all are different (use a HashSet ). Write isNewer(cA, cB) for two version texts. Read the version of the bridge assembly ( RingDotNet.Bridge ) with reflection. Name a file with a Guid and write a function that finds such files again by their name pattern."}, {"u": "tut-93-identifiers.html#next", "t": "Identifiers and versions", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Undo, redo and queues"}, {"u": "tut-58-undo.html", "t": "Undo, redo and queues", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-58-undo.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Undo, redo and queues", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # stack : last in, first out; queue : first in, first out, Push , Pop , Peek , Enqueue , Dequeue , Count , building undo and redo with two stacks, what happens when you take from an empty collection."}, {"u": "tut-58-undo.html#two-ways-to-wait", "t": "Undo, redo and queues", "h": "Two ways to wait", "x": "Two ways to wait # A stack is a pile of plates: you put on top and take from the top. A queue is a line at a shop: the first to arrive is served first. Stack Queue add Push(x) Enqueue(x) take Pop() Dequeue() look Peek() Peek() size Count Count used for undo, the Back button, parsing, the call stack print jobs, tasks to do, messages The ready-made Ring classes are StringStack and StringQueue (see Collections )."}, {"u": "tut-58-undo.html#the-program", "t": "Undo, redo and queues", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # stacks and queues - undo/redo with two stacks, and a first-come-first-served job queue load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System.Collections.Generic\" } func main # A STACK: last in, first out (a pile of plates). A QUEUE: first in, first out (people waiting in a line). # 1. Stack basics oStack = new StringStack() oStack.Push(\"a\") oStack.Push(\"b\") oStack.Push(\"c\") ? \"1. Peek (look at the top): \" + oStack.Peek() + \", Pop (take it): \" + oStack.Pop() + \", now \" + oStack.Count + \" items, the top is \" + oStack.Peek() # 2. Undo and redo with two stacks: every change pushes the old text on the"}, {"u": "tut-58-undo.html#how-it-works", "t": "Undo, redo and queues", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # Undo and redo # TextHistory keeps the current text and two stacks. Each change pushes the old text on the undo stack . undo pushes the current text on the redo stack and pops the previous text from the undo stack. redo does the reverse. The most recent change is always on top, which is exactly the order a user expects. A new change after an undo clears the redo stack ( Clear() ): the old future no longer exists. The last line of step 2 shows can redo now? no . The same idea works for a drawing program (push each shape), a browser (the Back and Forward buttons are two stacks), or"}, {"u": "tut-58-undo.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Undo, redo and queues", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # stacks and queues and when to use each, undo and redo with two stacks, always check Count ."}, {"u": "tut-58-undo.html#exercises", "t": "Undo, redo and queues", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Add a limit of 100 undo steps: when the undo stack is full, drop the oldest entry (hint: use a list). Make a job queue with priorities : urgent jobs first (hint: two queues). Check that parentheses in (a + [b]) * c are balanced, using a stack."}, {"u": "tut-58-undo.html#next", "t": "Undo, redo and queues", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Recursion"}, {"u": "tut-75-recursion.html", "t": "Recursion", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-75-recursion.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Recursion", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # what recursion is and the two parts every recursive function needs, how the calls pile up (depth), classic problems that become short with recursion, walking a tree (folders), the danger of recursion without an end."}, {"u": "tut-75-recursion.html#the-idea", "t": "Recursion", "h": "The idea", "x": "The idea # Russian dolls: to open a doll you open the outer doll, and then you do the same job for the doll inside, until you reach the smallest doll that cannot be opened. A recursive function has the same two parts: the stop (the base case ): a case that is answered directly \u2014 factorial(1) is 1; the step : a call of itself with a smaller problem \u2014 factorial(n) is n * factorial(n \u2212 1) . If the step does not get closer to the stop, the function calls itself until the program runs out of memory."}, {"u": "tut-75-recursion.html#the-program", "t": "Recursion", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # recursion - a function that calls itself: factorial, the Hanoi towers, permutations, a folder tree and a flood fill load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.IO\" } nMoves = 0 func main # 1. Recursion = a function solves a big problem by solving a smaller one with itself. It needs a STOP: a case that is answered directly. ? \"1. factorial(5) = \" + factorial(5) + \" (5 * factorial(4), ..., and factorial(1) = 1 stops it)\" ? \" sum of the numbers 1..100 = \" + sumTo(100) # 2. The danger: a recursion without a stop (or too deep) never ends. Count the depth to see how the calls pile up"}, {"u": "tut-75-recursion.html#how-it-works", "t": "Recursion", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # 1\u20132. Factorial and the depth # factorial(5) waits for factorial(4) , which waits for factorial(3) \u2026 until factorial(1) returns 1; then every call finishes in reverse order. Step 2 prints one line for each call, indented by its depth: the calls pile up like a stack of plates (the tutorial Undo, redo and queues ) and are removed in the opposite order. 3. Towers of Hanoi # To move n discs from A to C: move n \u2212 1 discs from A to B (using C), move the biggest disc to C, then move the n \u2212 1 discs from B to C. That is the whole solution; you never have to think about the individual mov"}, {"u": "tut-75-recursion.html#when-not-to-use-recursion", "t": "Recursion", "h": "When not to use recursion", "x": "When not to use recursion # Ring (like most languages) keeps each call in memory; a depth of hundreds of thousands can exhaust it. A loop uses no extra memory. A recursion that repeats the same work (the naive Fibonacci in the tutorial Speed \u2014 measure first ) needs a cache. Use recursion when the problem has the shape of a tree or \"the same problem, but smaller\" ; use a loop for simple repetition."}, {"u": "tut-75-recursion.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Recursion", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # stop and step, depth and the call stack, Hanoi, permutations, folder trees, flood fill, when a loop is better."}, {"u": "tut-75-recursion.html#exercises", "t": "Recursion", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Write power(base, exponent) recursively. Print all subsets of [1,2,3] . Count the files of each extension below a folder with a recursive function. Write reverse(text) recursively (the last letter + the reverse of the rest)."}, {"u": "tut-75-recursion.html#next", "t": "Recursion", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # An expression calculator"}, {"u": "tut-54-expression.html", "t": "An expression calculator", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-54-expression.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "An expression calculator", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # the two steps of every language tool: tokens , then grammar , how a parser respects precedence ( * before + ) and parentheses, writing a grammar as a few short rules and one function per rule, error messages that say what is wrong, using the .NET Math class for functions like sqrt ."}, {"u": "tut-54-expression.html#the-grammar", "t": "An expression calculator", "h": "The grammar", "x": "The grammar # Each rule becomes one function. A rule only calls the rules below it, and that is how precedence works: the rule for + and - calls the rule for * and / to get its operands, so the multiplications are done first. expression = term { (\"+\" | \"-\") term } term = power { (\"*\" | \"/\") power } power = unary [ \"^\" power ] unary = \"-\" unary | primary primary = number | \"(\" expression \")\" | name \"(\" expression { \",\" expression } \")\" { ... } means \"zero or more times\", [ ... ] \"optional\". Parentheses return to the top rule, so (1 + 2) * 3 works. power calls itself on the right side, which mak"}, {"u": "tut-54-expression.html#the-program", "t": "An expression calculator", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # an expression calculator - tokens, a recursive-descent parser, precedence and clear errors load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.Text.RegularExpressions\" } func main aTests = [\"1 + 2 * 3\",\"(1 + 2) * 3\",\"10 / 4 - 1\",\"2 ^ 3 ^ 2\",\"-(2 + 3) * 4\",\"sqrt(16) + max(3, 9)\",\"2 * (3 + \",\"4 $ 4\",\"1 / 0\"] for cText in aTests oCalc = new Evaluator() ? padRight(cText,24) + \" = \" + oCalc.calculate(cText) next func padRight cText, nWidth while len(cText) < nWidth cText += \" \" end return cText # Step 1: the text becomes tokens (numbers, names, operators). Step 2: the parser reads the token"}, {"u": "tut-54-expression.html#how-it-works", "t": "An expression calculator", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # Tokens # tokenize uses one regular expression, \\s*(\\d+\\.?\\d*|[A-Za-z]+|[-+*/^(),]|\\S) : skip spaces, then take a number, a name, an operator \u2014 or any other character, which is reported as unknown character '$' . Regex.Matches gives all the pieces; Groups.Item(1).Value is the piece without the spaces. The parser # aTokens is the list and nPos the position. peek() looks at the next token without taking it; the functions expression , term , power , unary and primary follow the grammar one to one. expect(\")\") checks that the closing parenthesis is there. Errors # When something is w"}, {"u": "tut-54-expression.html#what-you-learned", "t": "An expression calculator", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # tokens and grammar, one function per grammar rule, precedence through the order of the rules, clear error messages, Math functions from a parser."}, {"u": "tut-54-expression.html#exercises", "t": "An expression calculator", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Add % (remainder) at the same level as * and / . Add variables: x = 5 stores a value and later expressions can use x . Show the position of the error (the number of the token) in each message."}, {"u": "tut-54-expression.html#where-to-go-next", "t": "An expression calculator", "h": "Where to go next", "x": "Where to go next # This was the last tutorial of the chapter Collections and algorithms . The next chapter is Files and streams : it starts with Paths and the environment . The Tutorials page lists all the chapters, and you can start any of them."}, {"u": "tut-59-paths.html", "t": "Paths and the environment", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-59-paths.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Paths and the environment", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # building and taking apart paths with the Path class, relative and absolute paths, cleaning a text so that it is a valid file name, special folders (documents, temporary files), environment variables with a default value, how .NET null arrives in Ring."}, {"u": "tut-59-paths.html#why-not-add-strings", "t": "Paths and the environment", "h": "Why not add strings?", "x": "Why not add strings? # \"reports\" + \"\\\" + \"2024\" works on your computer only: other systems use / , and an extra separator or a missing one breaks the path. Path.Combine chooses the separator of the system and never doubles it."}, {"u": "tut-59-paths.html#the-program", "t": "Paths and the environment", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # paths and the environment - building paths safely, splitting them, special folders and environment variables load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.IO\" } func main oPath = new Path() # 1. Build a path with Combine, never with + \"\\\": Combine uses the right separator for the system and never doubles it cFile = oPath.Combine([\"reports\",\"2024\",\"january.txt\"]) ? \"1. Combine: \" + cFile ? \" Combine with a rooted second part restarts the path: \" + oPath.Combine([\"reports\",\"/etc/hosts\"]) # 2. Take a path apart cFull = \"C:\\data\\reports\\2024\\summary.final.txt\" ? nl + \"2. file name : "}, {"u": "tut-59-paths.html#how-it-works", "t": "Paths and the environment", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # Combine and split # Path.Combine joins parts: give a list of the parts ( Combine([a, b, c]) ) or name them ( Combine([ :path1 = a, :path2 = b ]) ). Careful: if a later part is rooted (starts with a separator or a drive), the earlier parts are dropped. That is correct behavior but a classic surprise when the part comes from user input. GetFileName , GetFileNameWithoutExtension , GetExtension , GetDirectoryName and ChangeExtension take a path apart or change one piece. Use them instead of searching for dots and slashes yourself: summary.final.txt has two dots, and only the last on"}, {"u": "tut-59-paths.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Paths and the environment", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # Path.Combine and the other Path methods, relative or absolute, valid file names, GetFolderPath , GetTempPath , environment variables and the null object."}, {"u": "tut-59-paths.html#exercises", "t": "Paths and the environment", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Write uniqueName(cFolder, cName) that adds (2) , (3) to the name until the file does not exist. Read a variable MYAPP_FOLDER for the data folder, with the documents folder as the default. List the extensions found in a folder with how many files each has."}, {"u": "tut-59-paths.html#next", "t": "Paths and the environment", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # File information"}, {"u": "tut-123-file-info.html", "t": "File information", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-123-file-info.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "File information", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # FileInfo : name, extension, size and whether the file exists, the times of a file and Refresh() , attributes and the read-only flag, the errors of a read-only file and of copying over an existing file, temporary files ( GetTempFileName ), DirectoryInfo and the sizes of the files in a folder. The tutorial Files and folders read and wrote the content of files. Here the file is the thing : how big it is, when it was written, whether it can be changed."}, {"u": "tut-123-file-info.html#the-program", "t": "File information", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # file information - size, attributes, times, read-only files, temporary files and copying with File and FileInfo load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.IO\" } func main oFile = new File() oPath = new Path() oDirectory = new Directory() # A work folder with a random name; the program deletes it at the end. The names of the folders are not printed (they differ on each computer). cFolder = oPath.Combine([ oPath.GetTempPath(), \"ringdotnet-info-\" + oPath.GetRandomFileName() ]) oDirectory.CreateDirectory(cFolder) cFile = cFolder + \"/report.txt\" oFile.WriteAllText([ :path = cFile, "}, {"u": "tut-123-file-info.html#how-it-works", "t": "File information", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # File and FileInfo # File has static methods that take a path ( File.Exists , File.Copy , File.Delete ...). FileInfo is an object made from a path: it gives Name , Extension , Length (bytes), Exists , IsReadOnly , the times and more, and it has the same actions as methods. FileInfo reads the information once and keeps it: when the file changes, Refresh() reads it again (step 2 needs it after the time was set). Times # A file has a creation time, a last write time and a last access time (each also in UTC). File.SetLastWriteTime(path, time) changes one; the program sets a known mom"}, {"u": "tut-123-file-info.html#what-you-learned", "t": "File information", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # FileInfo and DirectoryInfo , times, Refresh() and the attributes, read-only files and the exceptions of files, temporary files and folders."}, {"u": "tut-123-file-info.html#exercises", "t": "File information", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Write isOlderThan(cFile, nDays) and use it to list the files of a folder that were not changed for a month. Set the Hidden attribute and find the hidden files of a folder ( GetFiles and Attributes ). Copy a file and compare the last write times of the two. Write totalSize(cFolder) for a folder and all its subfolders."}, {"u": "tut-123-file-info.html#next", "t": "File information", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Disks and free space"}, {"u": "tut-124-disks.html", "t": "Disks and free space", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-124-disks.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Disks and free space", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # GetDrives() and the drive of a path, TotalSize , TotalFreeSpace and AvailableFreeSpace , IsReady and DriveType , a check will it fit? before a big write, a function that shows bytes as KB, MB, GB and TB, why this program prints yes and no for facts about the computer. The tutorial Paths and the environment gave the special folders and the tutorial File information the size of one file. This lesson is about the disk that holds them."}, {"u": "tut-124-disks.html#the-program", "t": "Disks and free space", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # disks and free space - DriveInfo: the drives of the computer, size, free space, and a check before a big write load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.IO\" } func main # Drive names, sizes and formats are different on each computer, so this program prints what is true on every computer (counts, yes/no) # and a few sizes that come from its own functions. # 1. Sizes for people. A number of bytes such as 1610612736 is hard to read: 1.5 GB is easy. Each step divides by 1024. ? \"1. formatSize:\" for nBytes in [ 0, 512, 1024, 1536, 1048576, 1610612736, 5497558138880 ] ? \" \" + padRi"}, {"u": "tut-124-disks.html#how-it-works", "t": "Disks and free space", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # Sizes for people # formatSize divides by 1024 until the number is smaller than 1024 and writes it with one decimal and a unit: 1610612736 bytes is 1.5 GB . It is a good function to keep in a library of your own: every program that shows a file size or a disk size needs it. (Disk makers count 1000 per step and operating systems 1024; this program uses 1024.) The drives # GetDrives() returns one DriveInfo for each drive: the letters of Windows ( C:\\ ), or the mounted file systems of Linux and macOS. It is a static method, called through any object of the class (the program makes o"}, {"u": "tut-124-disks.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Disks and free space", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # listing drives and finding the drive of a path, the three sizes and IsReady , checking the space before writing, a size in KB, MB, GB and TB."}, {"u": "tut-124-disks.html#exercises", "t": "Disks and free space", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Print a table of the ready drives with the type, the size and the free space in a readable form. Add a bar of ten characters that shows how full each drive is. Write waitForSpace(oDrive, nBytes, nSeconds) that checks again each second. Use formatSize for the sizes of the files in the tutorial File information ."}, {"u": "tut-124-disks.html#next", "t": "Disks and free space", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Streams and compression"}, {"u": "tut-56-streams.html", "t": "Streams and compression", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-56-streams.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Streams and compression", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # what a stream is and why .NET uses it everywhere, MemoryStream , StreamWriter and StreamReader , Flush , Position and Dispose , compressing and decompressing with GZipStream , the same code for memory and for files."}, {"u": "tut-56-streams.html#streams-in-one-minute", "t": "Streams and compression", "h": "Streams in one minute", "x": "Streams in one minute # A stream is like a pipe: bytes go in at one end and come out at the other. The same few classes work with every kind of pipe: Class Source or target MemoryStream memory FileStream a file ( File.Create , File.OpenRead ) NetworkStream a socket (the tutorial Sockets ) GZipStream wraps another stream and compresses or decompresses what passes StreamWriter / StreamReader turn text into bytes and back Because they wrap each other you can stack them: text \u2192 StreamWriter \u2192 GZipStream \u2192 FileStream \u2192 disk."}, {"u": "tut-56-streams.html#the-program", "t": "Streams and compression", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # streams and compression - the same data in memory, in a file, and squeezed with GZip load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.IO\" \"System.IO.Compression\" \"System.Text\" } func main oUtf8 = new Encoding().UTF8 # 1. A stream is a flow of bytes. A MemoryStream keeps them in memory: no file is needed to learn the pattern. oMemory = new MemoryStream() oWriter = new StreamWriter(oMemory) oWriter.WriteLine(\"first line\") oWriter.WriteLine(\"second line\") oWriter.Flush() # the writer keeps a buffer: Flush sends it to the stream ? \"1. bytes in the memory stream: \" + oMemory.Length # 2. "}, {"u": "tut-56-streams.html#how-it-works", "t": "Streams and compression", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # Write, rewind, read # Step 1 writes two lines into a MemoryStream . A StreamWriter keeps a buffer, so Flush() sends it to the stream before you look at Length . Step 2 sets Position = 0 to go back to the start (after writing, the position is at the end) and reads the lines with a StreamReader . Compression # The text of step 3 repeats the same sentence 200 times: 9,000 bytes become 120. Compression removes repetition, so text compresses very well and already compressed data (a zip, a JPEG) does not. A GZipStream created with \"Compress\" compresses what you write into it; with \"De"}, {"u": "tut-56-streams.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Streams and compression", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # streams as pipes that can be stacked, Flush , Position , Dispose , GZipStream with \"Compress\" and \"Decompress\" , dnNew(\"GZipStream\",[ :stream = ..., :mode = ..., :leaveOpen = True ]) for a constructor with three values."}, {"u": "tut-56-streams.html#exercises", "t": "Streams and compression", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Compress a text file to .gz and decompress it to a new file, then compare the two with a hash (the tutorial Hashing and encryption ). Try ZipArchive to put several files in one zip. Compress a text of 200 different lines and compare the size with the repeated text."}, {"u": "tut-56-streams.html#next", "t": "Streams and compression", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Binary files"}, {"u": "tut-45-binary.html", "t": "Binary files", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-45-binary.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Binary files", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # the difference between text and binary files, FileStream , BinaryWriter , BinaryReader , designing a small file format with a magic number, a hex dump, bytes, little endian and the type that you pass, closing files in every case."}, {"u": "tut-45-binary.html#text-and-binary", "t": "Binary files", "h": "Text and binary", "x": "Text and binary # A text file is a list of characters (encoded as bytes). A binary file is a list of bytes with a meaning that only the format tells: the first four bytes are a number, the next ones a name... A program that does not know the format sees garbage. A binary format is smaller and faster to read, and exact (a number is stored as a number, not as the text 21.5 )."}, {"u": "tut-45-binary.html#the-program", "t": "Binary files", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # binary files - bytes, BinaryWriter and BinaryReader, a file format, and a hex dump load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.IO\" \"System.Text\" } func main cFile = new Path().Combine([new Path().GetTempPath(),\"ringdotnet_binary.dat\"]) # 1. A binary file stores values as bytes, not as readable text. We invent a tiny format: # the text \"RDN1\" (a \"magic number\": it says what kind of file this is), then # a 32-bit whole number (the count), a text (the title), and that many decimal numbers. # Note: Write(21.5) writes a decimal number (8 bytes); Write(23) would write a whole number "}, {"u": "tut-45-binary.html#how-it-works", "t": "Binary files", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # The format # We invented it: first the text RDN1 , then the count (3) as a 32-bit whole number, the title as a text, then that many decimal numbers. Any reader must follow exactly the same order . The BinaryWriter writes each value in a fixed, compact way, and the BinaryReader reads them back with matching calls: ReadString , ReadInt32 , ReadDouble . Reading the hex dump # Each line of the dump shows an offset (position), eight bytes in hexadecimal, and the same bytes as characters ( . for unprintable ones). Read the real file: 0000 04 52 44 4e 31 03 00 00 .RDN1... 04 is the len"}, {"u": "tut-45-binary.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Binary files", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # binary files are bytes with a format, BinaryWriter / BinaryReader read and write in the same order, a hex dump shows the truth, little endian, magic numbers, always close."}, {"u": "tut-45-binary.html#exercises", "t": "Binary files", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Add a version number to the format, and make the reader accept versions 1 and 2. Write a list of names (count, then each name) and read it back. Extend hexDump to take a start offset and a length. Read the first 8 bytes of a PNG file and check that they are 89 50 4E 47 0D 0A 1A 0A ."}, {"u": "tut-45-binary.html#next", "t": "Binary files", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Random access and file locks"}, {"u": "tut-130-random-access.html", "t": "Random access and file locks", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-130-random-access.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Random access and file locks", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # fixed-size records and the position of record n , FileStream with a mode, an access and a share, Seek , Position , Length and SetLength , changing one record in place and adding one at the end, Flush , locking a file with FileShare and the IOException of a file that is in use. The tutorial Binary files wrote and read a file from the first byte to the last. This lesson jumps to a place in the file."}, {"u": "tut-130-random-access.html#the-program", "t": "Random access and file locks", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # random access files - FileStream: records of a fixed size, Seek, changing one record in place, SetLength and locking a file with FileShare load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.IO\" \"System.Text\" } nSize = 20 # every record has 20 bytes: the name in 12, the quantity in 4, and 4 spaces func main oPath = new Path() cFolder = oPath.Combine([ oPath.GetTempPath(), \"ringdotnet-records-\" + oPath.GetRandomFileName() ]) new Directory().CreateDirectory(cFolder) cFile = cFolder + \"/stock.dat\" # A text file is read from the first byte to the last. A file of RECORDS with the same size "}, {"u": "tut-130-random-access.html#how-it-works", "t": "Random access and file locks", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # Records of a fixed size # Every record of the file has 20 bytes: the name in 12 characters (spaces fill the rest), the quantity in 4 digits (zeros in front) and 4 spaces. With a fixed size, record number n (counted from 1) starts at byte (n - 1) * 20 . The program needs no index and does not read the records before it. Real formats of this kind use binary numbers (the tutorial Binary files ); text fields are used here so that the file can be read by eye. Opening a FileStream # dnNew(\"FileStream\",[ :path = ..., :mode = \"Create\", :access = \"ReadWrite\", :share = \"None\" ]) has three"}, {"u": "tut-130-random-access.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Random access and file locks", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # fixed-size records and the position of a record, FileStream modes, access and share, Seek , Position , Flush and SetLength , changing a record in place and adding one, locking a file against other writers."}, {"u": "tut-130-random-access.html#exercises", "t": "Random access and file locks", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Write findRecord(oStream, cName) that reads the records one by one until it finds the name. Add a function deleteRecord(n) that copies the last record over record n and cuts the file by one record. Make the record size 24 and add a price of 8 characters, and write a program that converts the old file. Open the file with FileShare.None in one program and try to open it in a second program (the tutorial Two programs talking )."}, {"u": "tut-130-random-access.html#next", "t": "Random access and file locks", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Copying a big file with progress"}, {"u": "tut-157-copy-progress.html", "t": "Copying a big file with progress", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-157-copy-progress.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Copying a big file with progress", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # reading and writing in pieces with FileStream.Read and Write , a byte buffer ( dnBuffer ) that is filled by Read and sent on by Write , why Read returns a number and 0 means the end, a progress report and a cancel that removes the half copy, checking the copy with the size and a SHA-256 checksum, the size of the buffer and the number of calls. The tutorial Binary files introduced the byte buffer. Here the buffer does the work of a copy."}, {"u": "tut-157-copy-progress.html#the-program", "t": "Copying a big file with progress", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # copying a big file with progress - reading and writing in pieces with a byte buffer, a percentage, cancelling, and checking the copy load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.IO\" \"System.Security.Cryptography\" } func main oPath = new Path() oFile = new File() cFolder = oPath.Combine([ oPath.GetTempPath(), \"ringdotnet-copy-\" + oPath.GetRandomFileName() ]) new Directory().CreateDirectory(cFolder) cSource = cFolder + \"/source.bin\" cTarget = cFolder + \"/target.bin\" # A source file of 3,000,000 bytes: a pattern of all 256 byte values, repeated (the program makes it, so that the ou"}, {"u": "tut-157-copy-progress.html#how-it-works", "t": "Copying a big file with progress", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # The loop # copyFile opens the source for reading and the target for writing ( Create makes a new file), and makes one buffer of 64 KB. The loop is always the same: Read fills the buffer and answers how many bytes it wrote, Write sends those bytes on, and the loop ends when Read answers 0 , the end of the file. The same buffer is used again and again, so the program needs 64 KB of memory for a file of any size. Read gives a number # Read(buffer, 0, size) may write fewer bytes than the buffer holds: the last piece of a file is almost always smaller (the file here has 3,000,000 byt"}, {"u": "tut-157-copy-progress.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Copying a big file with progress", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # the read and write loop with one buffer, Read answers how many bytes it wrote, and 0 at the end, progress and cancel, and deleting a half copy, checking a copy, the size of the buffer."}, {"u": "tut-157-copy-progress.html#exercises", "t": "Copying a big file with progress", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Show the speed in megabytes per second (a Stopwatch , the tutorial Speed: measure first ). Copy two files at once by taking one piece from each in turn. Make moveFile that copies and deletes the source only after the checksums are equal. Resume an interrupted copy: open the target with Append , seek the source to the size of the target, and go on."}, {"u": "tut-157-copy-progress.html#next", "t": "Copying a big file with progress", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # What kind of file is it?"}, {"u": "tut-159-file-types.html", "t": "What kind of file is it?", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-159-file-types.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "What kind of file is it?", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # magic numbers : the first bytes of PNG, GIF, JPEG, ZIP, GZip, PDF and Windows programs, reading the first n bytes of a file into a byte buffer, a signature table and the function that uses it, telling text from binary data, comparing what the name says with what the bytes say, why an upload check needs the bytes."}, {"u": "tut-159-file-types.html#the-program", "t": "What kind of file is it?", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # finding out what a file is - the first bytes (magic numbers), a signature table, text or binary, and a file whose name tells a lie load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.IO\" \"System.Text\" } aSignatures = [] # [ name, mime type, the first bytes ] func main oFile = new File() oPath = new Path() cFolder = oPath.Combine([ oPath.GetTempPath(), \"ringdotnet-types-\" + oPath.GetRandomFileName() ]) new Directory().CreateDirectory(cFolder) # The NAME of a file is only a habit: anybody can call a picture \"notes.txt\". Many file formats start with a few fixed bytes, the # MAGIC NUMBER ("}, {"u": "tut-159-file-types.html#how-it-works", "t": "What kind of file is it?", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # The first bytes # A PNG picture always starts with the bytes 137 80 78 71 13 10 26 10 (the second to the fourth are the letters PNG ), a GIF with GIF8 , a JPEG with 255 216 255 , a ZIP file with PK and the codes 3 and 4, a GZip file with 31 139 , a PDF with %PDF- and a Windows program with MZ . firstBytes opens the file with a FileStream , reads up to 16 bytes into a byte buffer and returns them as a Ring text; it does not read the rest of the file, so it is fast for any size. A file that is shorter than 16 bytes gives fewer bytes, and an empty file gives none. The table # aSign"}, {"u": "tut-159-file-types.html#what-you-learned", "t": "What kind of file is it?", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # the first bytes of common formats, reading the start of a file into a buffer, a signature table and text or binary, names that tell a lie and the upload check."}, {"u": "tut-159-file-types.html#exercises", "t": "What kind of file is it?", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Add the signatures of BMP ( BM ), RAR and 7z (look up their first bytes). Make detectAll(folder) that prints the type of every file of a folder and lists the files whose name does not fit. Detect a text file with a byte order mark and tell UTF-8 from UTF-16 (the tutorial Text files and encodings ). Read the first 4 bytes of a ZIP file made by ZipFile.CreateFromDirectory and check PK ."}, {"u": "tut-159-file-types.html#next", "t": "What kind of file is it?", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Compression"}, {"u": "tut-88-compression.html", "t": "Compression", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-88-compression.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Compression", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # how a compression stream wraps another stream, GZipStream , DeflateStream and BrotliStream , compressing and decompressing bytes in a MemoryStream , why Dispose is needed to finish the data, why random data does not shrink, writing and reading a .gz file with StreamWriter and StreamReader . The tutorial Files and folders made a ZIP archive of files. This lesson compresses data (one stream of bytes). GZip is the format of .gz files and of Content-Encoding: gzip on the web."}, {"u": "tut-88-compression.html#the-program", "t": "Compression", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # compression - GZip, Deflate and Brotli on streams, sizes, a round trip and a compressed file load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.IO\" \"System.IO.Compression\" \"System.Text\" } func main # Compression looks for repetition. A text with many repeated parts becomes small; random data does not. cText = \"\" for i = 1 to 200 cText += \"The quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog. \" next oUtf8 = new UTF8Encoding() aBytes = oUtf8.GetBytes(cText) # a byte[] is a Ring text of bytes in this package ? \"1. original text: \" + len(aBytes) + \" bytes\" # 2. The three formats. Each one is a str"}, {"u": "tut-88-compression.html#how-it-works", "t": "Compression", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # Streams that wrap streams # A compression stream does not hold data itself: it is built on top of another stream and a mode ( Compress or Decompress ). Bytes written to a compressor are packed and written to the stream below it; bytes read from a decompressor come from the stream below it, unpacked. The stream below can be a MemoryStream (bytes in memory) or a FileStream (a file). The three classes have the same members, so the function packer is the only place that knows the format. It is made with new GZipStream(oStream, \"Compress\") . The mode is an enum, so it is a text. Comp"}, {"u": "tut-88-compression.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Compression", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # a compression stream wraps another stream, Dispose finishes the compressed data, the three formats have the same members, repeated text shrinks, random or already compressed data does not, a compressed file is a StreamWriter on a GZipStream on a FileStream ."}, {"u": "tut-88-compression.html#exercises", "t": "Compression", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Compress the text of a real file (a log from the tutorial A project: analyze a log file ) and print the ratio for each format. Add CompressionLevel and compare \"Fastest\" with \"SmallestSize\" with a Stopwatch . Write a function gzipFile(source, target) that uses CopyTo between two file streams. Compress the same bytes twice and look at the size of the second result."}, {"u": "tut-88-compression.html#next", "t": "Compression", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # ZIP archives in memory"}, {"u": "tut-108-zip-in-memory.html", "t": "ZIP archives in memory", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-108-zip-in-memory.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "ZIP archives in memory", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # ZipArchive on a MemoryStream (modes Create , Read , Update ), CreateEntry , Entries , GetEntry , Delete , why Dispose is needed, compression levels, checking the names of entries. The tutorial Files and folders made a ZIP file from a folder, the tutorial Compression compressed streams and the tutorial Tar archives made tar archives. This lesson works with the entries of an archive one by one."}, {"u": "tut-108-zip-in-memory.html#the-program", "t": "ZIP archives in memory", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # ZIP archives in memory - ZipArchive on a MemoryStream, entries, reading one file, compression levels, and updating an archive load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.IO\" \"System.IO.Compression\" \"System.Text\" } func main oUtf8 = new UTF8Encoding() # 1. A ZIP archive does not have to be a file. ZipArchive works on any stream: a MemoryStream keeps the whole archive in memory, which is # what a web program does when it sends a download, or a test that does not want to leave files on the disk. oMemory = new MemoryStream oZip = (new ZipArchive).@New([ :stream = oMemory, :mode = \""}, {"u": "tut-108-zip-in-memory.html#how-it-works", "t": "ZIP archives in memory", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # The stream # ZipArchive is made on a stream and a mode: dnNew(\"ZipArchive\",[ :stream = oMemory, :mode = \"Create\", :leaveOpen = True ]) . leaveOpen = True keeps the MemoryStream open when the archive is closed, so the program can read the bytes afterwards ( ToArray() ), send them or open the archive again. Without it, closing the archive also closes the stream. The mode is an enum, so it is a text. Creating entries # CreateEntry(\"data/names.txt\") makes an entry (a file inside the archive; folders are just a / in the name) and Open() gives a stream to write its content, here a Str"}, {"u": "tut-108-zip-in-memory.html#what-you-learned", "t": "ZIP archives in memory", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # ZipArchive on a MemoryStream with leaveOpen , CreateEntry , Entries , GetEntry , Open , Delete , the archive must be disposed to be complete, compression levels, and small files that grow, check entry names before you unpack."}, {"u": "tut-108-zip-in-memory.html#exercises", "t": "ZIP archives in memory", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Write zipFiles(aNames) that returns the bytes of an archive and send them in the web server of the tutorial A tiny web framework . Write listArchive(oStream) that returns the entry names as a Ring list. Compare the five levels for a real text file and a JPEG picture. Add a comment to an entry ( Comment ) and read it back."}, {"u": "tut-108-zip-in-memory.html#next", "t": "ZIP archives in memory", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Tar archives"}, {"u": "tut-96-tar.html", "t": "Tar archives", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-96-tar.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Tar archives", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # what a tar file is and how it differs from ZIP and GZip, making a tar file from a folder ( TarFile.CreateFromDirectory ), listing the entries with TarReader , reading one file without unpacking the rest, unpacking ( TarFile.ExtractToDirectory )."}, {"u": "tut-96-tar.html#zip-gzip-and-tar", "t": "Tar archives", "h": "ZIP, GZip and tar", "x": "ZIP, GZip and tar # Format What it does ZIP (the tutorial Files and folders ) collects files and compresses each one GZip (the tutorial Compression ) compresses one stream of data tar collects files and folders into one file, without compressing Linux programs combine the last two: a .tar.gz (or .tgz ) file is a tar archive compressed with GZip. Tar also keeps things that ZIP does not always keep, such as Unix permissions and links."}, {"u": "tut-96-tar.html#the-program", "t": "Tar archives", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # tar archives - make a .tar from a folder, list it, read one file from it and unpack it (System.Formats.Tar) load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.IO\" \"System.Formats.Tar\" } func main oPath = new Path() cBase = oPath.Combine([oPath.GetTempPath(),\"ringdotnet_tar\"]) cSource = oPath.Combine([cBase,\"source\"]) cTar = oPath.Combine([cBase,\"backup.tar\"]) cTarget = oPath.Combine([cBase,\"unpacked\"]) oDirectory = new Directory() oFile = new File() if oDirectory.Exists(cBase) oDirectory.Delete([ :path = cBase, :recursive = True ]) ok oDirectory.CreateDirectory(oPath.Combine([cSource,"}, {"u": "tut-96-tar.html#how-it-works", "t": "Tar archives", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # Making the archive # TarFile.CreateFromDirectory(source folder, tar file, include the folder itself) packs a whole folder. It is a static method of a static class, so the program calls it on new TarFile() with the arguments in order. Here the folder itself is not included, so the entries are readme.txt and notes/a.txt , not source/readme.txt . A tar file is built from blocks of 512 bytes, which step 1 checks: the size is a multiple of 512. Reading the entries # A TarReader is made on a stream ( new TarReader(oStream) ). GetNextEntry gives the next TarEntry , and null at the end,"}, {"u": "tut-96-tar.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Tar archives", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # tar collects files without compressing them, CreateFromDirectory and ExtractToDirectory as static methods of TarFile , TarReader , GetNextEntry , EntryType , DataStream , reading is sequential, the danger of names that leave the target folder."}, {"u": "tut-96-tar.html#exercises", "t": "Tar archives", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Make a .tar.gz : create the tar file, then compress it with GZipStream (the tutorial Compression ). Write listTar(cFile) that returns the names of the entries as a Ring list. Write a function that adds one more file to an archive with TarWriter . Make an archive with an empty folder and look at how it is listed."}, {"u": "tut-96-tar.html#next", "t": "Tar archives", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # File patterns (globbing)"}, {"u": "tut-112-globbing.html", "t": "File patterns (globbing)", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-112-globbing.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "File patterns (globbing)", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # the glob wildcards * , ** and ? , Matcher : AddInclude and AddExclude , finding files in a folder ( GetResultsInFullPath ), Match on a list of paths that are not on a disk, the case rule of a matcher, extension methods and dnStatic . The tutorial Files and folders listed the files of a folder and the tutorial Regular expressions in depth used regular expressions. A glob is simpler than a regular expression and made for paths, so it is the language of file selection: build files, ignore files, backup rules, \"which files changed\". Note Microsoft.Extensions.FileSystemGlobbin"}, {"u": "tut-112-globbing.html#the-program", "t": "File patterns (globbing)", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # file patterns (globbing) - \"**/*.ring\", include and exclude rules, matching a list of paths, and finding files in a folder tree load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"Microsoft.Extensions.FileSystemGlobbing\" \"System\" \"System.IO\" \"RingDotNet.Web\" # Microsoft.Extensions.FileSystemGlobbing is part of the ASP.NET Core runtime: this line starts it } func main # A glob pattern describes a set of files with wildcards, like the patterns in a .gitignore file or a build tool: # * any characters inside one name *.ring a.ring, b.ring # ** any number of folders (also none) **/*.ring src/a.ring, src/su"}, {"u": "tut-112-globbing.html#how-it-works", "t": "File patterns (globbing)", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # The wildcards # Pattern Matches *.ring any file name that ends with .ring , in one folder ? exactly one character: file?.txt is file1.txt and fileA.txt ** any number of folders, also none : **/*.ring is a.ring , src/a.ring and src/util/a.ring src/*.ring files in src , not in its sub-folders docs/**/*.md every .md file below docs , at any depth The separator is always / , on every system. Include and exclude # A Matcher collects include rules ( AddInclude ) and exclude rules ( AddExclude ). A file is in the result when at least one include rule matches it and no exclude rule does"}, {"u": "tut-112-globbing.html#what-you-learned", "t": "File patterns (globbing)", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # * , ** and ? , and why ** also matches no folder, include rules and exclude rules, GetResultsInFullPath for folders, Match for lists of names, sorted output for a stable result, the \".\" root and the List<string> input."}, {"u": "tut-112-globbing.html#exercises", "t": "File patterns (globbing)", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Write findFiles(cFolder, aInclude, aExclude) that returns the relative names as a Ring list. Read the patterns from a settings file (the tutorial Configuration ) and use them for a backup program (the tutorial A project: a backup tool ). Make a matcher that finds the images ( **/*.png , **/*.jpg ) but not those in a folder thumbs . Test your patterns on a list of names with Match before you run them on the disk."}, {"u": "tut-112-globbing.html#next", "t": "File patterns (globbing)", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Watching a folder"}, {"u": "tut-122-watch-folder.html", "t": "Watching a folder", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-122-watch-folder.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Watching a folder", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # what a FileSystemWatcher reports: Created , Changed , Renamed and Deleted , the folder, the filter ( *.txt ) and the subfolders, WaitForChanged with a time limit, why a change that happens while the program is not waiting is not reported, a child program that makes the changes (the pattern of the tutorial Two programs talking ). Programs that watch folders are everywhere: a build tool that starts when a file is saved, an editor that notices that a file was changed by another program, a program that processes the files that other programs drop into an inbox folder."}, {"u": "tut-122-watch-folder.html#the-program", "t": "Watching a folder", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # Watching a folder - FileSystemWatcher: waiting for files to be created, changed, renamed and deleted # ring ch05_watch_folder.ring the parent: it watches a folder and starts a second copy of this program (the child) that changes it # ring ch05_watch_folder.ring --child the child (the parent starts it, you do not) load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.IO\" \"System.Diagnostics\" \"System.Threading.Tasks\" } func main if find(sysargv,\"--child\") > 0 child(sysargv[len(sysargv)]) return ok # A FileSystemWatcher tells a program that something happened in a folder: a file was created"}, {"u": "tut-122-watch-folder.html#how-it-works", "t": "Watching a folder", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # Two programs # A watcher has nothing to report if nobody changes the folder, and the program that waits cannot change it at the same moment. So the program starts a second copy of itself with --child and the name of the folder. The child waits a second, then creates note.txt , appends to it, renames it to done.txt , writes a file that the filter does not match, and deletes done.txt , with a pause between the steps. The parent only watches. The watcher # new FileSystemWatcher().@New([ :path = cFolder, :filter = \"*.txt\" ]) watches one folder. The filter is a file name pattern: onl"}, {"u": "tut-122-watch-folder.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Watching a folder", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # a watcher reports created, changed, renamed and deleted files, the filter and the subfolders, WaitForChanged and its time limit, one change can raise several events, a child program is a good way to test a watcher."}, {"u": "tut-122-watch-folder.html#exercises", "t": "Watching a folder", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Remove the filter ( * ) and look at the extra event for ignored.log . Set IncludeSubdirectories = True and make the child write into a subfolder. Make a loop that copies each new .txt file of an inbox folder to an archive folder. Count the Changed events for one AppendAllText call."}, {"u": "tut-122-watch-folder.html#next", "t": "Watching a folder", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # A project: analyze a log file"}, {"u": "tut-21-log-analyzer.html", "t": "A project: analyze a log file", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-21-log-analyzer.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "A project: analyze a log file", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # how to plan a small data-processing program, combining File , Regex , NumberDictionary , LINQ and JSON, writing helper functions for the things you do many times."}, {"u": "tut-21-log-analyzer.html#the-task", "t": "A project: analyze a log file", "h": "The task", "x": "The task # A server writes lines like these to a log file: 2024-05-17 08:15:02 ERROR [db] connection lost 2024-05-17 08:16:10 INFO [web] request done We want to know: how many lines of each level, which parts have errors, which error texts occur, and to save a short report."}, {"u": "tut-21-log-analyzer.html#the-plan", "t": "A project: analyze a log file", "h": "The plan", "x": "The plan # Read the file line by line. Cut each line into date, time, level, part, text with a regular expression. Lines that do not match are skipped. Count with a dictionary: lines per level, errors per part. Collect the error texts and ask LINQ for the distinct ones, sorted. Write a JSON report. The program makes its own sample log first, so that you can run it anywhere."}, {"u": "tut-21-log-analyzer.html#the-program", "t": "A project: analyze a log file", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # a project - analyze a log file (files, regular expressions, dictionaries, LINQ, JSON) load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.IO\" \"System.Text.RegularExpressions\" \"System.Collections.Generic\" \"System.Linq\" \"System.Text.Json\" } func main # STEP 1. Make a sample log file (a real program would read an existing file) cLog = new Path().Combine([new Path().GetTempPath(),\"ringdotnet_log_analyzer.log\"]) makeLog(cLog) ? \"STEP 1: log written, \" + len(new File().ReadAllLines(cLog).toList()) + \" lines\" # STEP 2. Read it and pick the parts of each line with a regular expression. # a lin"}, {"u": "tut-21-log-analyzer.html#how-it-works", "t": "A project: analyze a log file", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # The regular expression # ^(?<date>\\d{4}-\\d{2}-\\d{2}) (?<time>\\d{2}:\\d{2}:\\d{2}) (?<level>[A-Z]+) \\[(?<part>\\w+)\\] (?<text>.+)$ Read it left to right: four digits, a dash, two digits... then a space, a time, a word in capital letters (the level), a word in square brackets (the part), and the rest of the line. (?<level>...) is a named group : after a match, oMatch.Groups.Item(\"level\").Value reads it. oMatch.Success says whether the line matched at all; the last line of the sample file does not, so it is skipped (30 of 31 lines were understood). Counting with a dictionary # A dicti"}, {"u": "tut-21-log-analyzer.html#what-you-learned", "t": "A project: analyze a log file", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # plan: read \u2192 parse \u2192 count \u2192 summarize \u2192 write, named regex groups make parsing readable, dictionaries count, small helper functions keep main short."}, {"u": "tut-21-log-analyzer.html#exercises", "t": "A project: analyze a log file", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Count the errors per day : put the date in a dictionary. Print the busiest hour (use the first two characters of the time). Take the file name from the command line ( sysargv[3] ) and report an error if it does not exist. Write the report with webJson instead of the helper (with \"RingDotNet.Web\" in the using block)."}, {"u": "tut-21-log-analyzer.html#next", "t": "A project: analyze a log file", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # A project: find duplicate files"}, {"u": "tut-31-duplicates.html", "t": "A project: find duplicate files", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-31-duplicates.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "A project: find duplicate files", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # recursive folder listing, content hashes as fingerprints, grouping with a dictionary, a report that is useful, cleaning up after a demo."}, {"u": "tut-31-duplicates.html#the-idea", "t": "A project: find duplicate files", "h": "The idea", "x": "The idea # Two files are the same when their content is the same, whatever their names. Comparing every pair of files byte by byte is slow. A hash (SHA-256) is a short fingerprint of the content: the same content always gives the same hash and different content (practically) never does. So: compute the hash of every file, put the files in a dictionary keyed by the hash , every key with more than one file is a set of duplicates."}, {"u": "tut-31-duplicates.html#the-program", "t": "A project: find duplicate files", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # a project - find duplicate files (folders, hashes, dictionaries) load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.IO\" \"System.Security.Cryptography\" \"System.Collections.Generic\" } func main # STEP 1. A folder with some files: three of them have the same content, two others as well cFolder = new Path().Combine([new Path().GetTempPath(),\"ringdotnet_duplicates\"]) if new Directory().Exists(cFolder) new Directory().Delete([ :path = cFolder, :recursive = True ]) ok new Directory().CreateDirectory(cFolder) new Directory().CreateDirectory(new Path().Combine([cFolder,\"old\"])) makeFile(cFolde"}, {"u": "tut-31-duplicates.html#how-it-works", "t": "A project: find duplicate files", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # Step 1: make test data # The program creates its own folder with files: three with the same text (one in a subfolder, one with a different name), two with the same other text, and one unique file. A demo that makes its own data runs anywhere and can be checked: the answer is known. Step 2: list recursively # Directory.GetFiles([ :path = folder, :searchPattern = \"*.txt\", :searchOption = \"AllDirectories\" ]) returns the files of the folder and of every subfolder. \"TopDirectoryOnly\" would skip the subfolders. The third parameter is an enum, written as a text. Step 3: hash and group "}, {"u": "tut-31-duplicates.html#what-you-learned", "t": "A project: find duplicate files", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # a hash identifies content, a dictionary groups by key, recursive listing, tests with data that you made yourself."}, {"u": "tut-31-duplicates.html#exercises", "t": "A project: find duplicate files", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Group by file size first; hash only the files whose size occurs more than once. Also compare the first 1 MB only for a quick check of very big files. Add an option to move duplicates to a duplicates folder instead of just reporting. Make it a window: a folder box, a Scan button, and the result in a TreeView (the tutorial Layout and richer controls )."}, {"u": "tut-31-duplicates.html#next", "t": "A project: find duplicate files", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # A project: a backup tool"}, {"u": "tut-46-backup.html", "t": "A project: a backup tool", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-46-backup.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "A project: a backup tool", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # zipping and unzipping folders, names that sort in time order, keeping the newest N files (retention), restoring and verifying, the shape of a program that is safe to run again and again."}, {"u": "tut-46-backup.html#the-design", "t": "A project: a backup tool", "h": "The design", "x": "The design # Need Decision a backup must not overwrite the previous one every backup has its own file: backup-<date and time>.zip the files should be easy to find and to sort the stamp is year-month-day_hourminute : sorting the names sorts by time disk space is limited keep only the newest N (here 3) and delete the older ones a backup that was never restored is not a backup the program restores the newest one and checks it"}, {"u": "tut-46-backup.html#the-program", "t": "A project: a backup tool", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # a project - a backup tool (zip a folder, name by date, keep the newest few, restore) load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.IO\" \"System.IO.Compression\" } func main oPath = new Path() oDirectory = new Directory() oFile = new File() cWork = oPath.Combine([oPath.GetTempPath(),\"ringdotnet_backup\"]) if oDirectory.Exists(cWork) oDirectory.Delete([ :path = cWork, :recursive = True ]) ok cSource = oPath.Combine([cWork,\"documents\"]) cBackups = oPath.Combine([cWork,\"backups\"]) oDirectory.CreateDirectory(oPath.Combine([cSource,\"notes\"])) oDirectory.CreateDirectory(cBackups) oFile.Wri"}, {"u": "tut-46-backup.html#how-it-works", "t": "A project: a backup tool", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # Names that sort # 2024-05-03_0900 sorts correctly as text because the biggest unit comes first and every part has a fixed width ( 05 , not 5 ). That is why sort(list of names) puts the oldest backup first. A stamp like 3-5-2024 would sort wrongly. A real program makes the stamp from the clock: new DateTime().Now.Format(\"yyyy-MM-dd_HHmm\") ; the tutorial passes fixed stamps so that the output is repeatable. Making a backup # ZipFile.CreateFromDirectory([ :sourceDirectoryName = folder, :destinationArchiveFileName = zipFile ]) compresses the folder, with its subfolders, into a zip. "}, {"u": "tut-46-backup.html#what-you-learned", "t": "A project: a backup tool", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # zip with ZipFile , fixed-width date stamps sort as text, retention by deleting from the front of a sorted list, restore and verify."}, {"u": "tut-46-backup.html#exercises", "t": "A project: a backup tool", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Use the real clock for the stamp and run the program twice. Skip a backup when no file changed since the last one (compare file dates or hashes: the tutorial A project: find duplicate files ). Put the list of folders to back up in a settings file (the tutorial Settings ). Add a window with a Back up now button and a list of the backups."}, {"u": "tut-46-backup.html#next", "t": "A project: a backup tool", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # A project: rename many files"}, {"u": "tut-141-renamer.html", "t": "A project: rename many files", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-141-renamer.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "A project: rename many files", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # a rule as data and one function that applies a list of rules, splitting a name from its extension , a preview before any change, conflict checks: the same new name for two files, an empty name, File.Move to rename, and a log that makes undo possible, why the folder order of files must be sorted, a project made of small functions that a test can call. The tutorial A project: a backup tool did something to files that is easy to repeat. Renaming is different: a wrong rule damages the names, so the tool works in careful steps."}, {"u": "tut-141-renamer.html#the-program", "t": "A project: rename many files", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # a project: rename many files - rules (replace, regular expression, case, numbering), a preview, conflict checks and undo load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.IO\" \"System.Text.RegularExpressions\" } func main oFile = new File() oPath = new Path() oDirectory = new Directory() # A work folder with some files with untidy names (it is removed at the end) cFolder = oPath.Combine([ oPath.GetTempPath(), \"ringdotnet-rename-\" + oPath.GetRandomFileName() ]) oDirectory.CreateDirectory(cFolder) for cName in [ \"IMG 0042 holiday.JPG\", \"IMG 0043 holiday.JPG\", \"IMG 0050 Beach Trip.jpg\", \""}, {"u": "tut-141-renamer.html#how-it-works", "t": "A project: rename many files", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # The four steps # The program lists the names, makes the new names with a rule, shows them without renaming anything (the preview), checks the plan for problems, and only then renames. Each step is a function: fileNames , makePlan , findConflicts and applyPlan . The plan is a Ring list of [old, new] pairs, so it can be printed, checked, saved and undone. Rules are data # A rule is a small list: [ \"replace\", \" \", \"_\" ] , [ \"regex\", \"\\s*final\", \"\" ] , [ \"lower\" ] or [ \"number\", \"holiday-\", \"jpg\", 2 ] . A rule set is a list of rules that are applied in order to each name, so replace"}, {"u": "tut-141-renamer.html#what-you-learned", "t": "A project: rename many files", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # rules as data and a function that applies them, the preview, the conflict check and the log, renaming with File.Move , and undo, sorting the names of a folder."}, {"u": "tut-141-renamer.html#exercises", "t": "A project: rename many files", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Add the rule extension that changes only the extension. Add a rule that puts the date of the file ( LastWriteTime ) in front of the name. Save the log as a text file and write undoFromFile(path) . Add a dry run switch that prints the plan and does not call applyPlan ."}, {"u": "tut-141-renamer.html#next", "t": "A project: rename many files", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # A project: compare two files"}, {"u": "tut-145-diff.html", "t": "A project: compare two files", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-145-diff.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "A project: compare two files", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # the longest common subsequence (LCS) and how a table finds it, building a diff from the table: keep, delete ( - ), add ( + ), reading a text file as a list of lines ( ReadAllLines ), showing only the changes with context lines, a similarity in percent, what a moved line looks like in a diff. Version control, code review and the compare command of an editor all show a diff. The method is a classic algorithm, written in plain Ring."}, {"u": "tut-145-diff.html#the-program", "t": "A project: compare two files", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # a project: compare two text files - the longest common subsequence of lines, a diff with + and -, changes with context, and a similarity load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.IO\" } func main oFile = new File() oPath = new Path() cFolder = oPath.Combine([ oPath.GetTempPath(), \"ringdotnet-diff-\" + oPath.GetRandomFileName() ]) new Directory().CreateDirectory(cFolder) # Two versions of a small text file (the program writes them, so that the output is always the same) cOld = \"title: Tea\" + nl + \"water: 200 ml\" + nl + \"tea: 2 g\" + nl + \"heat: 90 C\" + nl + \"wait: 3 min\" + nl + \""}, {"u": "tut-145-diff.html#how-it-works", "t": "A project: compare two files", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # Keep, delete, add # To change the old file into the new one, each old line is either kept , or deleted , and some new lines are added . Many answers are possible (delete everything and add everything), so the diff looks for the one that keeps as many lines as possible. The lines that are kept form a list that is in both files in the same order: the longest common subsequence . The table # lcsTable makes a table in which cell [i][j] is the length of the LCS of the old lines from i on and the new lines from j on. It is filled from the end: when the two lines are the same, the cell"}, {"u": "tut-145-diff.html#what-you-learned", "t": "A project: compare two files", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # the longest common subsequence, a diff from the table, the changes with context, the similarity of two files."}, {"u": "tut-145-diff.html#exercises", "t": "A project: compare two files", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Compare two real files and print only the changes with two lines of context. Add the option ignore case : compare lower(a) with lower(b) but print the original lines. Compare the words of two sentences instead of the lines. Write the diff in the unified format, with @@ -start,count +start,count @@ before each group."}, {"u": "tut-145-diff.html#next", "t": "A project: compare two files", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # A project: check that files did not change"}, {"u": "tut-147-checksums.html", "t": "A project: check that files did not change", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-147-checksums.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "A project: check that files did not change", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # a checksum : the SHA-256 hash of the bytes of a file, a list in the format of the programs sha256sum and shasum , finding all the files of a folder and its subfolders, with relative paths, verify : OK , CHANGED , MISSING and NEW , why the list itself needs a checksum that is kept somewhere else. The tutorial A project: find duplicate files used hashes to find files with the same content. Here the hash proves that a file is the same as it was ."}, {"u": "tut-147-checksums.html#the-program", "t": "A project: check that files did not change", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # a project: check that files did not change - a checksum list in the format of sha256sum, and a verify that finds changed, missing and new files load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.IO\" \"System.Security.Cryptography\" } func main oFile = new File() oPath = new Path() oDirectory = new Directory() # A work folder with a few files, some of them in a subfolder (it is removed at the end) cFolder = oPath.Combine([ oPath.GetTempPath(), \"ringdotnet-sums-\" + oPath.GetRandomFileName() ]) oDirectory.CreateDirectory(cFolder + \"/docs\") oFile.WriteAllText([ :path = cFolder + \"/readme.tx"}, {"u": "tut-147-checksums.html#how-it-works", "t": "A project: check that files did not change", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # The checksum # SHA256.ComputeHash(bytes) gives 32 bytes, and Convert.ToHexString writes them as 64 hexadecimal characters (the tutorial Bytes as text: Base64 and hexadecimal ). The program writes them in lower case, as sha256sum does. A change of one bit anywhere in the file gives a completely different checksum, and nobody can find another file with the same checksum by chance. The checksum of a file that is read as bytes does not depend on the encoding or the line endings: it is the file as it is. The list # Each line has the checksum, two spaces and the path with / , relative"}, {"u": "tut-147-checksums.html#what-you-learned", "t": "A project: check that files did not change", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # SHA-256 checksums of files, the sha256sum list format, listing files with relative paths, verify with changed, missing and new files, protecting the list."}, {"u": "tut-147-checksums.html#exercises", "t": "A project: check that files did not change", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Write the list for a folder, copy the folder, and verify the copy. Print the percentage of files that are OK and the total size of the changed files. Make updateList that adds the new files to the list and removes the missing ones, and shows what it did. Use HMACSHA256 with a secret key instead of SHA256 (the tutorial Protecting and signing messages ) so that only the owner of the key can make a valid list."}, {"u": "tut-147-checksums.html#where-to-go-next", "t": "A project: check that files did not change", "h": "Where to go next", "x": "Where to go next # This was the last tutorial of the chapter Files and streams . The next chapter is Processes and performance : it starts with Asking the computer . The Tutorials page lists all the chapters, and you can start any of them."}, {"u": "tut-74-system.html", "t": "Asking the computer", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-74-system.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Asking the computer", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # the operating system and the .NET runtime, processors, memory, drives and the user, the current process and the list of all processes, the .NET memory manager ( GC ), writing a program that behaves differently on Windows and on other systems."}, {"u": "tut-74-system.html#the-program", "t": "Asking the computer", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # asking the computer - the operating system, the runtime, processors, memory, drives and running programs load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.IO\" \"System.Diagnostics\" \"System.Text\" \"System.Runtime.InteropServices\" } func main oEnvironment = new Environment() oRuntime = new RuntimeInformation() # 1. Which system runs the program? A program that must behave differently on Windows, Linux and macOS asks first. ? \"1. platform : \" + oEnvironment.OSVersion.Platform ? \" runtime : \" + oRuntime.FrameworkDescription ? \" process type : \" + oRuntime.ProcessArchitecture + \", 64-bit sy"}, {"u": "tut-74-system.html#how-it-works", "t": "Asking the computer", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # The system and the runtime # Environment.OSVersion.Platform is Win32NT on Windows and Unix on Linux and macOS. RuntimeInformation.FrameworkDescription is the .NET version and ProcessArchitecture the type of the processor ( X64 , Arm64 ...). A program that needs a different path or command on each system asks first and then chooses (the tutorial Paths and the environment shows paths that work everywhere). The machine and the user # ProcessorCount is the number of logical processors; it tells you how many tasks can really run at the same time (the tutorial Time, tasks and other pr"}, {"u": "tut-74-system.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Asking the computer", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # Environment , RuntimeInformation , Process.GetCurrentProcess and GetProcesses , GC.GetTotalMemory , testing values that differ between computers."}, {"u": "tut-74-system.html#exercises", "t": "Asking the computer", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Print the memory used by your program (in MB) before and after you fill a list with a million numbers. Show the 10 programs that use the most memory (name and MB) \u2014 a text task manager. Read the environment variable PATH and print each folder on its own line (the tutorial Paths and the environment )."}, {"u": "tut-74-system.html#next", "t": "Asking the computer", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # A command-line tool"}, {"u": "tut-50-cli.html", "t": "A command-line tool", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-50-cli.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "A command-line tool", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # reading the arguments of a program ( sysargv ), options with and without values ( --top 5 , --help ), printing a usage text and clear error messages, exit codes: 0 success, non-zero a problem, keeping the logic in a function that is easy to test."}, {"u": "tut-50-cli.html#the-program", "t": "A command-line tool", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # a command-line tool - arguments, options, usage text and results # ring ch06_cli.ring runs a demonstration of several command lines # ring ch06_cli.ring file.txt --top 5 is a real use of the tool: the 5 most frequent words of file.txt load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System.IO\" \"System.Text.RegularExpressions\" } func main # the arguments after the program name: sysargv = [ring, program, arg1, arg2, ...] (the first two are not ours) aArguments = [] for nIndex = 3 to len(sysargv) aArguments + sysargv[nIndex] next if len(aArguments) > 0 nCode = run(aArguments) ? \"(exit code \" + nCode +"}, {"u": "tut-50-cli.html#how-it-works", "t": "A command-line tool", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # sysargv holds [ring, program, arg1, arg2, ...] , so your arguments start at item 3. run(aArguments) does everything and returns an exit code instead of stopping the program: the demonstration calls it with several lists of arguments, a real use passes the real ones. This is the trick that makes a command-line tool easy to test. parseArguments walks the list. A word that starts with -- is an option; --top and --min take the next word as their value, and it is checked with a regular expression ( ^[0-9]+$ ). Any other word is the file. A mistake prints one clear line ( error: --top"}, {"u": "tut-50-cli.html#what-you-learned", "t": "A command-line tool", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # arguments, options and values, usage text and error lines, exit codes, a run function that is easy to test."}, {"u": "tut-50-cli.html#exercises", "t": "A command-line tool", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Add --ignore-case false to count The and the separately. Add --sort alpha to sort by word instead of by count. Read the text from the keyboard when no file is given."}, {"u": "tut-50-cli.html#next", "t": "A command-line tool", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Logging"}, {"u": "tut-51-logging.html", "t": "Logging", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-51-logging.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Logging", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # what to log and at which level, appending lines to a file, the minimum level (development versus production), logging an error you caught, rotation: a log must not grow forever."}, {"u": "tut-51-logging.html#the-program", "t": "Logging", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # logging - levels, timestamps, a file, rotation, and what to write load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.IO\" } func main cFolder = new Path().Combine([new Path().GetTempPath(),\"ringdotnet_logging\"]) if new Directory().Exists(cFolder) new Directory().Delete([ :path = cFolder, :recursive = True ]) ok new Directory().CreateDirectory(cFolder) cFile = new Path().Combine([cFolder,\"app.log\"]) # 1. A logger writes lines: time, level, message. The level says how important a line is. oLog = new Logger(cFile) oLog.setClock(\"2024-05-17 08:00:00\") # a fixed time: the output of the tuto"}, {"u": "tut-51-logging.html#how-it-works", "t": "Logging", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # A line has a time, a level and a message. The level says how important it is: DEBUG (details for the developer), INFO (normal events), WARN (something is wrong but the program goes on), ERROR (an operation failed). The logger has a minimum level : lines below it are not written, so step 1 skips the debug line, and step 2 shows everything and then only errors. File.AppendAllText adds a line at the end of the file. The clock can be fixed with setClock so that the output of the tutorial is the same every run; a real logger leaves it empty and uses the real time. Step 3 shows the ma"}, {"u": "tut-51-logging.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Logging", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # levels and a minimum level, AppendAllText , logging caught errors, rotation with FileInfo and File.Move ."}, {"u": "tut-51-logging.html#exercises", "t": "Logging", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Keep three old files ( .1 , .2 , .3 ). Add a fatal level and a --verbose option like in the tutorial A command-line tool . Write a log line for each request of the web server in the tutorial A small web site ."}, {"u": "tut-51-logging.html#next", "t": "Logging", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Tracing"}, {"u": "tut-136-tracing.html", "t": "Tracing", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-136-tracing.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Tracing", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # Trace.WriteLine and the listeners of Trace , a listener that collects messages in memory ( StringWriter ) and one that writes a file, Indent and Unindent , WriteLineIf and a category , adding and removing listeners without changing the code that writes messages, how tracing differs from logging. The tutorial Logging wrote a log that the program owns. Tracing is for diagnosis: the program sends messages to a standard place, and what happens to them is decided outside the code that writes them."}, {"u": "tut-136-tracing.html#the-program", "t": "Tracing", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # tracing - Trace and listeners: messages with an indent, a listener that collects them in memory, a listener that writes a file, and levels load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.IO\" \"System.Diagnostics\" \"System.Threading.Tasks\" } func main # TRACING writes messages about what a program does, for the person who looks for a problem. A program sends a message to the class # Trace, and the LISTENERS that were added to Trace decide where it goes: the console, a file, a window, a log service. The program # that writes the messages does not know where they go, and a listener can "}, {"u": "tut-136-tracing.html#how-it-works", "t": "Tracing", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # Trace and listeners # Trace is a static class, so its members are called through new Trace() : oTrace.WriteLine(text) sends a message to every listener in oTrace.Listeners . A TextWriterTraceListener writes into any text writer: a StringWriter (memory), a file or the console. The listener is added with Listeners.Add(listener) . AutoFlush = True makes each message go to the listeners at once; without it messages wait in a buffer until Flush or the end of the program. Memory and files # The memory listener is the best choice for a test, because the test can read the messages back:"}, {"u": "tut-136-tracing.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Tracing", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # the listeners of Trace and where messages go, memory and file listeners, indented messages, conditions and categories, adding and removing listeners without changing the code."}, {"u": "tut-136-tracing.html#exercises", "t": "Tracing", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Write a function traced(name, code) that writes enter name , runs the code, and writes leave name with the time, with Indent and Unindent around it. Add a ConsoleTraceListener and look at the messages on the screen. Write each message with the date and time in front of it. Make the file listener append to a file, run the program twice, and read the file."}, {"u": "tut-136-tracing.html#next", "t": "Tracing", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Working with other programs"}, {"u": "tut-85-feed-programs.html", "t": "Working with other programs", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-85-feed-programs.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Working with other programs", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # the three streams of a program: standard input , standard output and standard error , sending lines to a program and closing its input, chaining programs: the output of one is the input of another, environment variables and the working folder of the new program, exit codes: 0 means success, WaitForExit(ms) as a time limit and Kill . The tutorial Time, tasks and other programs started programs and the tutorial A project: a command runner showed their output in a window. This lesson adds the input side and the control of the new program. Note The program chooses the command"}, {"u": "tut-85-feed-programs.html#the-program", "t": "Working with other programs", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # working with other programs - feeding standard input, environment variables, errors and exit codes, a time limit and Kill # (the commands are chosen for the system: sort, find and cmd on Windows; sort, grep and sh on Linux and macOS) load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.IO\" \"System.Diagnostics\" } # the shell of this system (set in main, used by shellArgs) cShell = \"\" cShellOption = \"\" func main # the program that keeps the lines with an \"e\", and the shell, for this system cFilter = \"grep\" cFilterArgs = \"e\" cShell = \"sh\" cShellOption = \"-c\" cDirError = \"ls this_folder_doe"}, {"u": "tut-85-feed-programs.html#how-it-works", "t": "Working with other programs", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # Starting a program with redirected streams # startProgram makes a ProcessStartInfo (the file, the arguments) and sets three things: UseShellExecute = False (needed to redirect), CreateNoWindow (no console window) and, when asked, RedirectStandardInput , RedirectStandardOutput and RedirectStandardError . Process.Start(info) starts it and gives a Process . After that oProcess.StandardInput , StandardOutput and StandardError are stream writers and readers: your program is now the keyboard and the screen of the other program. Input # sort reads lines until its input ends, then print"}, {"u": "tut-85-feed-programs.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Working with other programs", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # redirecting the three streams, the write, close, read, wait pattern, environment variables and WorkingDirectory , exit codes and standard error, WaitForExit(ms) and Kill ."}, {"u": "tut-85-feed-programs.html#exercises", "t": "Working with other programs", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Write a function runAndCapture(cFile, cArguments) that returns [exit code, output, error] . Run ping with a time limit of two seconds and stop it if it is slower. Write a little program that reads lines from its input and prints them in upper case (the child of the tutorial Two programs talking ) and feed it from the parent. Chain three programs: sort, remove duplicates ( uniq or sort /unique ), count lines."}, {"u": "tut-85-feed-programs.html#next", "t": "Working with other programs", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Two programs talking"}, {"u": "tut-79-processes-talk.html", "t": "Two programs talking", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-79-processes-talk.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Two programs talking", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # starting another program with ProcessStartInfo and Process , and reading what it prints, named pipes : NamedPipeServerStream and NamedPipeClientStream , a line-based protocol on top of a stream ( StreamReader / StreamWriter ), a named mutex ( Mutex ) that the whole computer shares: only one copy of a program, the same program as parent and child ( --child )."}, {"u": "tut-79-processes-talk.html#pipes-and-mutexes", "t": "Two programs talking", "h": "Pipes and mutexes", "x": "Pipes and mutexes # A named pipe is a channel between two processes on one computer. One side is the server : it creates the pipe with a name and waits. The other side is the client : it connects to the name. Both sides get a stream and use it like a file or a socket (the tutorial Streams and compression , the tutorial Sockets ). Pipes are fast and need no network and no port number. A named mutex is a lock with a name. The first program that gets it holds it; another program that asks for the same name does not get it. Typical use: a program that must not run twice (a tray application, a data"}, {"u": "tut-79-processes-talk.html#the-program", "t": "Two programs talking", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # two programs talking - a named pipe between processes, and a mutex that allows only one copy # ring ch06_processes_talk.ring the parent: it starts a second copy of this program (the child) and talks to it # ring ch06_processes_talk.ring --child the child (the parent starts it, you do not) load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.IO\" \"System.IO.Pipes\" \"System.Diagnostics\" \"System.Threading\" } C_PIPE = \"ringdotnet_talk\" C_MUTEX = \"ringdotnet_talk_single_copy\" func main if find(sysargv,\"--child\") > 0 runChild() return ok runParent() # -------------------------------------------"}, {"u": "tut-79-processes-talk.html#how-it-works", "t": "Two programs talking", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # One file, two roles # The program starts itself a second time with the argument --child ( exefilename() is the Ring executable, filename() this file). main looks at sysargv : with --child it is the child, otherwise the parent. This keeps the tutorial in one file; in real life the two programs would be different files. The parent # The lock. new Mutex().@New([ :initiallyOwned = False, :name = ... ]) opens (or creates) the mutex; WaitOne(0) means try to take it, do not wait . The parent gets it. The pipe. NamedPipeServerStream creates the pipe. The constructor has many forms, so i"}, {"u": "tut-79-processes-talk.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Two programs talking", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # Process.Start with redirected output, named pipes between processes, a named mutex for a single-instance program, a line protocol over a stream."}, {"u": "tut-79-processes-talk.html#exercises", "t": "Two programs talking", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Make the child answer time with the current time. Let the parent start two children one after the other, each using the pipe in turn. Make a program that exits with a message when it cannot get the mutex, and start it twice from two terminals. Send a list as a line of JSON (the tutorial JSON as a tree ) and parse it on the other side."}, {"u": "tut-79-processes-talk.html#next", "t": "Two programs talking", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Shared memory"}, {"u": "tut-82-shared-memory.html", "t": "Shared memory", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-82-shared-memory.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Shared memory", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # what a memory-mapped file is, MemoryMappedFile.CreateNew and OpenExisting (a named block that two programs share), a view accessor for numbers ( Write , ReadInt32 , ReadDouble ) and a view stream for text, why a text read from the memory ends at the first zero byte, mapping a real file ( CreateFromFile ) and changing it in place."}, {"u": "tut-82-shared-memory.html#the-idea", "t": "Shared memory", "h": "The idea", "x": "The idea # A normal file is read and written through calls. A memory-mapped file is a file (or just a block of memory with a name) that the system shows to your program as ordinary memory: what one program writes into it, another program that mapped the same name sees at once. It is the fastest way for programs on one computer to share data, and it is also a comfortable way to work with a very big file without loading it. Compare the tools of the last tutorials: a named pipe (the tutorial Two programs talking ) sends messages one after another; shared memory is a place both programs can read a"}, {"u": "tut-82-shared-memory.html#the-program", "t": "Shared memory", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # shared memory - a memory-mapped file seen by two programs, numbers and text in it, and a file that is edited in place # ring ch06_shared_memory.ring the parent: it makes the memory and starts the child # ring ch06_shared_memory.ring --child the child (the parent starts it) load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.IO\" \"System.Diagnostics\" \"System.IO.MemoryMappedFiles\" \"System.Threading\" } C_NAME = \"ringdotnet_shared_memory\" func main if find(sysargv,\"--child\") > 0 runChild() return ok # a NAMED memory map is a Windows feature (on Linux and macOS .NET maps files only); the fil"}, {"u": "tut-82-shared-memory.html#how-it-works", "t": "Shared memory", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # One name, two programs # The parent calls MemoryMappedFile.CreateNew([ :mapName = \"...\", :capacity = 4096 ]) : a block of 4,096 bytes with a name. The child (a second copy of the same program, started with --child as in Two programs talking ) calls OpenExisting(name) and gets the same block . Nothing is copied. Numbers # CreateViewAccessor(NULL) gives a view with methods that work at a position (a byte number): Write(position, value) , ReadInt32(position) , ReadDouble(position) , ReadBoolean(position) . The two programs must agree on the layout \u2014 here: position 0 holds an intege"}, {"u": "tut-82-shared-memory.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Shared memory", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # named shared memory and OpenExisting , CreateViewAccessor for numbers and CreateViewStream for text, zero-filled memory and cutting a text, CreateFromFile for editing a file in place, the need for a layout and for locking."}, {"u": "tut-82-shared-memory.html#exercises", "t": "Shared memory", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Put a \"ready\" flag at position 200 and let the child wait ( Thread.Sleep in a loop) until the parent sets it. Keep a counter at position 0 that both programs increase, protected by a Mutex . Map a text file and replace one character of it in place. Store a text as length first : four bytes for the length, then the text."}, {"u": "tut-82-shared-memory.html#next", "t": "Shared memory", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Tasks that wait"}, {"u": "tut-83-tasks-waiting.html", "t": "Tasks that wait", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-83-tasks-waiting.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Tasks that wait", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # what a Task is and what Wait does, Task.Delay : a wait that does not block a thread, WhenAll and WhenAny , a time limit with Wait(milliseconds) , canceling with CancellationTokenSource and a token, why many waits together take the time of the longest, not the sum."}, {"u": "tut-83-tasks-waiting.html#tasks-and-ring", "t": "Tasks that wait", "h": "Tasks and Ring", "x": "Tasks and Ring # In .NET a task is a promise: a piece of work that is running or waiting and will be finished , failed or canceled . HttpClient.GetAsync , File.ReadAllTextAsync and Process.WaitForExitAsync all return tasks. Ring runs your code on one thread, and Ring code cannot run on other threads ( Core concepts ), so you do not write the work of a task in Ring. You use the tasks that .NET provides, start several, and wait for them in Ring. Task.Delay is the simplest such task: it finishes after a time and does nothing else, which makes the rules visible."}, {"u": "tut-83-tasks-waiting.html#the-program", "t": "Tasks that wait", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # tasks that wait - Task.Delay, WhenAll, WhenAny, time limits and cancellation (System.Threading.Tasks) load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.Diagnostics\" \"System.Threading\" \"System.Threading.Tasks\" } func main oTasks = new Task() # 1. A Task is \"something that finishes later\". Task.Delay(ms) is a task that finishes after that time, without using a thread to sleep. oWatch = new Stopwatch() oWatch.Start() oOne = oTasks.Delay(300) ? \"1. the task was made at once (under 100 ms): \" + yesNo(oWatch.ElapsedMilliseconds < 100) + \", finished already? \" + yesNo(oOne.IsCompleted) oOne"}, {"u": "tut-83-tasks-waiting.html#how-it-works", "t": "Tasks that wait", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # Delay and Wait # Task.Delay(300) returns at once with a task that is not finished yet ( IsCompleted is false). Wait(NULL) blocks the Ring program until the task is finished; its Status is then RanToCompletion . Making the task and waiting for it are two separate steps \u2014 that separation is what lets you start many tasks first. WhenAll and WhenAny # Task.WhenAll([t1, t2, t3]) is a new task that finishes when all of them did. Three delays of 300 ms started together are over after about 300 ms, not 900. The same idea turns a hundred slow downloads into one wait. Task.WhenAny([\u2026]) fi"}, {"u": "tut-83-tasks-waiting.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Tasks that wait", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # Task.Delay , Wait , Status , IsCompleted , WhenAll and WhenAny with a Ring list of tasks, Wait(ms) as a time limit, CancellationTokenSource , CancelAfter and the token, tasks wait together."}, {"u": "tut-83-tasks-waiting.html#exercises", "t": "Tasks that wait", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Start three HttpClient requests (the tutorial HttpClient in depth ) and wait for all with WhenAll ; compare the time with doing them one after another. Print the elapsed milliseconds for each step and see how close they are to the delays. Use WhenAny with a delay as a timeout : whichever of a request and a 2-second delay ends first wins. Cancel a Process.WaitForExitAsync of a program that runs for a minute."}, {"u": "tut-83-tasks-waiting.html#next", "t": "Tasks that wait", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Releasing resources"}, {"u": "tut-71-dispose.html", "t": "Releasing resources", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-71-dispose.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Releasing resources", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # what a resource is and why programs must release them, seeing a file stay locked, Dispose (and Close ), clean-up that runs after an error, which classes need it."}, {"u": "tut-71-dispose.html#resources", "t": "Releasing resources", "h": "Resources", "x": "Resources # Memory is cleaned automatically, but some things are lent by the operating system: open files, network connections, database connections, window handles. While you hold one, others may be unable to use it \u2014 a file that is open for writing cannot be deleted or moved, a connection counts against the limit of the server. You must give them back. In .NET the method is Dispose() (many classes also have Close() , which does the same)."}, {"u": "tut-71-dispose.html#the-program", "t": "Releasing resources", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # releasing resources - files that stay locked, Dispose, and cleaning up even when an error happens load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.IO\" } func main cFile = new Path().Combine([new Path().GetTempPath(),\"ringdotnet_dispose.txt\"]) new File().WriteAllText([ :path = cFile, :contents = \"some text\" ]) # 1. A resource is something the operating system lends to your program: a file, a socket, a database connection. # While you hold it, others may not be able to use it. Open the file for writing and try to delete it. oStream = new File().Open([ :path = cFile, :mode = \"Open\" ]) "}, {"u": "tut-71-dispose.html#how-it-works", "t": "Releasing resources", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # Locked while open # Step 1 opens the file and tries to delete it: no , the system refuses, because the file is in use. Step 2 calls Dispose() and now the delete works. A program that forgets this leaves files locked until it ends, and a long-running program (a service, a window) can run out of handles. An error skips the clean-up # Step 3 is the typical bug. The function opens a reader, then does some work that fails (here a number that cannot be read), and the line oReader.Dispose() after the failing call is never reached: the file is still locked after the error. Note the comm"}, {"u": "tut-71-dispose.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Releasing resources", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # resources and locks, Dispose and Close , an error can skip a clean-up, the clean-up goes after the try block, the .NET object outlives the Ring variable."}, {"u": "tut-71-dispose.html#exercises", "t": "Releasing resources", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Write readFirstLine(cFile) that opens a StreamReader , reads a line, disposes it and returns the line \u2014 even if the file is empty. Open the same file twice for writing and see the error message. Write a function that copies a file with two streams and releases both even if the copy fails."}, {"u": "tut-71-dispose.html#next", "t": "Releasing resources", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Speed \u2014 measure first"}, {"u": "tut-53-speed.html", "t": "Speed \u2014 measure first", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-53-speed.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Speed \u2014 measure first", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # measuring time with Stopwatch , why you must measure instead of assuming, a cache ( memoization ) that turns a slow function into a fast one, the cost of a call between Ring and .NET, and how to make fewer calls."}, {"u": "tut-53-speed.html#the-rules-of-speed", "t": "Speed \u2014 measure first", "h": "The rules of speed", "x": "The rules of speed # Make it work, then make it right, then make it fast \u2014 and only if it is too slow. Measure. The slow part is rarely the part you suspect. The biggest wins come from doing less work (a cache, a better algorithm, fewer calls), not from clever tricks."}, {"u": "tut-53-speed.html#the-program", "t": "Speed \u2014 measure first", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # speed - measure first, then choose: Stopwatch, StringBuilder, a cache (memoization) and fewer calls load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.Diagnostics\" \"System.Text\" \"System.Collections.Generic\" } nCalls = 0 nCachedCalls = 0 aCache = [] func main # 1. Measure with a Stopwatch. A guess about speed is often wrong. oWatch = new Stopwatch() oWatch.Start() nSum = 0 for nI = 1 to 200000 nSum += nI next oWatch.Stop() ? \"1. A loop of 200000 additions gives \" + nSum + \" (measured: \" + (oWatch.ElapsedMilliseconds >= 0) + \")\" # 2. Building a big text: Ring's own += against the .NET S"}, {"u": "tut-53-speed.html#how-it-works", "t": "Speed \u2014 measure first", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # Stopwatch # new Stopwatch() , Start() , Stop() , and then ElapsedMilliseconds or Elapsed.TotalMilliseconds . The tutorial prints comparisons (\"which is faster?\") and not the times, so that the output is the same on every computer; when you try your own code, print the numbers. Ring's += against StringBuilder # In C# a StringBuilder is the standard answer to building a long text. In Ring the answer is different: the += of Ring is already efficient, and each Append of a StringBuilder is a call across the bridge . Here the plain Ring version wins. This is the reason for rule 2: adv"}, {"u": "tut-53-speed.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Speed \u2014 measure first", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # Stopwatch and measuring, the result may be the opposite of what you expect, a cache saves repeated work, one call for many items beats many calls."}, {"u": "tut-53-speed.html#exercises", "t": "Speed \u2014 measure first", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Measure File.ReadAllLines against StreamReader.ReadLine in a loop on a 10,000-line file. Add a time limit to fibCached 's cache: forget answers older than a minute. Time new Regex(pattern) created inside a loop and created once before the loop."}, {"u": "tut-53-speed.html#next", "t": "Speed \u2014 measure first", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Memory and the garbage collector"}, {"u": "tut-98-memory.html", "t": "Memory and the garbage collector", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-98-memory.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Memory and the garbage collector", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # what the garbage collector (GC) does and why you rarely call it, GC.GetTotalMemory , GC.Collect , GC.CollectionCount , WeakReference : a pointer that does not keep an object alive, generations 0, 1 and 2, handles: how Ring frees .NET objects for you ( dnFlush() and release() )."}, {"u": "tut-98-memory.html#the-program", "t": "Memory and the garbage collector", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # memory and the garbage collector - how much memory .NET uses, GC.Collect, WeakReference, generations and a big array load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.IO\" \"System.Text\" } func main oGC = new GC() # 1. .NET frees the memory of objects that nothing uses any more: the garbage collector (GC). The numbers depend on the computer and # the moment, so the program prints only what is always true. nBefore = oGC.GetTotalMemory(True) ? \"1. the program uses memory: \" + (nBefore > 0) ? \" the number of generations: \" + (oGC.MaxGeneration + 1) + \" (0 = new objects, 1 = in between, 2 "}, {"u": "tut-98-memory.html#how-it-works", "t": "Memory and the garbage collector", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # The garbage collector # In .NET you never free an object by hand. The GC finds the objects that no variable can reach any more and frees them. You do not choose the moment: it runs when memory gets short, or when the runtime decides. GC.Collect() forces a collection, and GC.GetTotalMemory(True) collects first and then returns the number of bytes in use. Both are useful for learning and testing ; in a real program calling Collect is almost always wrong, because the GC knows better than you when to run (and a forced collection can slow the program). The numbers (bytes in use, how "}, {"u": "tut-98-memory.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Memory and the garbage collector", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # the GC frees unreachable objects; you do not choose when, GetTotalMemory , Collect and CollectionCount for learning and tests, Ring frees the handle of a dropped object for you; Dispose() frees resources, callgc() with dnFlush() , or release() , make it immediate, WeakReference for caches, generations: young objects are collected often."}, {"u": "tut-98-memory.html#exercises", "t": "Memory and the garbage collector", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Make 100 000 StringBuilder objects with a capacity of 4000 in a loop and look at GC.GetTotalMemory after the loop, with and without callgc() and dnFlush() . Build a small cache of results with WeakReference objects in a Ring list. Find out how many collections of generation 0, 1 and 2 happened in your program ( CollectionCount ). Read about GCSettings.IsServerGC and GCSettings.LatencyMode and print them."}, {"u": "tut-98-memory.html#next", "t": "Memory and the garbage collector", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # The Windows Registry"}, {"u": "tut-118-registry.html", "t": "The Windows Registry", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-118-registry.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "The Windows Registry", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # the Registry as a tree of keys with values , HKEY_CURRENT_USER and why it is the safe place to write, CreateSubKey , OpenSubKey , SetValue , GetValue , the value kinds: String , DWord , QWord , MultiString , ExpandString , Binary , listing values and sub-keys, deleting them, the rules for a program that writes to the Registry. The tutorial Settings saved settings in a file. On Windows, programs often use the Registry for the same job, and a program that works with Windows (file types, start-up, system settings) must."}, {"u": "tut-118-registry.html#the-program", "t": "The Windows Registry", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # the Windows Registry - keys and values in HKEY_CURRENT_USER, value types, listing, a settings class, and deleting what you made # (Windows only: anywhere else the program says so and stops) load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"Microsoft.Win32\" } C_PATH = \"Software\\RingDotNetTutorial\" func main if not iswindows() ? \"The Registry exists only on Windows.\" return ok # The Registry is a tree of KEYS (like folders) that hold VALUES (a name and data with a type). Windows and many programs keep their # settings in it. The part HKEY_CURRENT_USER (HKCU) belongs to the user who is logged "}, {"u": "tut-118-registry.html#how-it-works", "t": "The Windows Registry", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # Keys and values # The Registry looks like a file system: keys are the folders, and each key holds values , a name with data and a type. The big branches are hives : HKEY_CURRENT_USER (HKCU) belongs to the user who is logged in, HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE (HKLM) is for the whole computer, HKEY_CLASSES_ROOT holds the file types. The program writes only in HKCU, under its own key Software\\RingDotNetTutorial , because that needs no administrator rights and does not affect other users. HKLM needs an administrator to write. Registry.CurrentUser is a static property of the class, read through "}, {"u": "tut-118-registry.html#rules-for-programs-that-use-the-registry", "t": "The Windows Registry", "h": "Rules for programs that use the Registry", "x": "Rules for programs that use the Registry # Write only under HKCU\\Software\\<your company>\\<your program> . Never change keys of other programs or of Windows. Do not store secrets in plain text: any program of the same user can read HKCU. Use the Data Protection API (the tutorial Protecting data ) or the Windows credential store. Read with a default and handle an invalid value; users and other tools edit the Registry. Do not put data there that is big (a few kilobytes is fine, megabytes are not): use a file (the tutorial Files and folders ). Prefer a settings file when the program must also run "}, {"u": "tut-118-registry.html#what-you-learned", "t": "The Windows Registry", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # the Registry is a tree of keys and values; HKCU is yours, CreateSubKey , OpenSubKey , SetValue , GetValue , GetValueKind , the value kinds and how they come back in Ring, listing and deleting, and cleaning up, the rules for a program that writes there."}, {"u": "tut-118-registry.html#exercises", "t": "The Windows Registry", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Write a small class Settings with load() and save() that keeps a list of values (name, value) in a key. Read the version of Windows from HKLM\\SOFTWARE\\Microsoft\\Windows NT\\CurrentVersion ( ProductName , CurrentBuild ); it is read-only for you. Count how many sub-keys HKCU\\Software has. Write the position and size of a window of the tutorial Your first window when it closes and restore them at the next start."}, {"u": "tut-118-registry.html#where-to-go-next", "t": "The Windows Registry", "h": "Where to go next", "x": "Where to go next # This was the last tutorial of the chapter Processes and performance . The next chapter is Networking and the web : it starts with Network tools . The Tutorials page lists all the chapters, and you can start any of them."}, {"u": "tut-81-network-tools.html", "t": "Network tools", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-81-network-tools.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Network tools", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # IPAddress : parsing, families (IPv4, IPv6), loopback, TryParse and other methods with out parameters, IPEndPoint : an address and a port, Dns.GetHostEntry , Ping , NetworkInterface , checking whether a port accepts connections, with a time limit."}, {"u": "tut-81-network-tools.html#the-program", "t": "Network tools", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # network tools - IP addresses, DNS, ping, network interfaces and checking whether a port is open (all on this computer) load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.Net\" \"System.Net.Sockets\" \"System.Net.NetworkInformation\" } func main # 1. IPAddress parses an address and knows its family. A method with an \"out\" parameter returns a list: [success, the address]. oParser = new IPAddress() oAddress = oParser.Parse(\"192.168.1.20\") cBytes = oAddress.GetAddressBytes() # the 4 bytes of an IPv4 address (a byte[] is a Ring text) ? \"1. \" + oAddress.ToString() + \" is \" + oAddress.AddressFami"}, {"u": "tut-81-network-tools.html#how-it-works", "t": "Network tools", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # Addresses and out parameters # IPAddress.Parse(\"192.168.1.20\") gives an object. GetAddressBytes() returns the four bytes as a Ring text (a byte[] is a text in this package), so ascii(cBytes[1]) is 192. AddressFamily is InterNetwork for IPv4 and InterNetworkV6 for IPv6, and IsLoopback tests for 127.0.0.1 and ::1 . IPAddress.TryParse(text, out address) answers yes or no without raising an error. In .NET the address comes back through an out parameter; in Ring a method with out parameters returns a list : [ success, the address ] . So aGood = oParser.TryParse(\"10.0.0.7\") gives aGoo"}, {"u": "tut-81-network-tools.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Network tools", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # IPAddress , IPEndPoint , out parameters come back as a list, Dns , Ping , NetworkInterface , a connection test with a time limit."}, {"u": "tut-81-network-tools.html#exercises", "t": "Network tools", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Write waitForPort(address, port, seconds) that tries every 200 ms and returns when the port opens. List the open ports of 127.0.0.1 between 8000 and 8010 after starting two listeners. Ping 127.0.0.1 five times and print the average round trip. Resolve a real host name with Dns.GetHostEntry and handle the error when there is no network."}, {"u": "tut-81-network-tools.html#next", "t": "Network tools", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # IP networks"}, {"u": "tut-102-ip-networks.html", "t": "IP networks", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-102-ip-networks.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "IP networks", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # the CIDR notation ( 192.168.1.0/24 ), IPNetwork : TryParse , BaseAddress , PrefixLength , Contains , how many addresses a network has, what a wrong network looks like, an allow list for private addresses (RFC 1918), IPv6. The tutorial Network tools used IPAddress and IPEndPoint . This lesson goes one level up: a range of addresses. A web program (the tutorial A web service with a database ) uses it to allow or refuse a client by its address."}, {"u": "tut-102-ip-networks.html#the-program", "t": "IP networks", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # IP networks - CIDR notation, the addresses of a subnet, \"is this address in that network?\" and a small allow list load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.Net\" } func main # A network is written in CIDR form: an address and the number of bits that are the same for the whole network. # 192.168.1.0/24 means: the first 24 bits (192.168.1) are fixed, the last 8 bits (0 to 255) are free. oNetworkClass = new IPNetwork() aParsed = oNetworkClass.TryParse(\"192.168.1.0/24\") oNetwork = aParsed[2] ? \"1. network: \" + oNetwork.ToString() ? \" base address: \" + oNetwork.BaseAddress.ToString"}, {"u": "tut-102-ip-networks.html#how-it-works", "t": "IP networks", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # CIDR notation # An IPv4 address has 32 bits. A network is written as an address and a prefix length : the number of bits at the start that are the same for every address of the network. In 192.168.1.0/24 the first 24 bits ( 192.168.1 ) are fixed and the last 8 bits are free, so the network holds the addresses 192.168.1.0 to 192.168.1.255 . IPNetwork.TryParse(text) answers [ success, network ] (the tutorial Network tools ); the network has BaseAddress , PrefixLength and ToString() . Contains # oNetwork.Contains(address) tells whether an IPAddress is inside the range. The first an"}, {"u": "tut-102-ip-networks.html#what-you-learned", "t": "IP networks", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # CIDR: an address and a prefix length, IPNetwork.TryParse , BaseAddress , PrefixLength , Contains , the size of a network, 2 to the power of (bits \u2212 prefix), the base address rule, an allow list in a Ring list."}, {"u": "tut-102-ip-networks.html#exercises", "t": "IP networks", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Write networkOf(cAddress, nPrefix) that returns the network that contains an address. Write an allow list that reads its networks from a settings file (the tutorial Settings ). Use the list in the tutorial A tiny web framework so that only addresses of 127.0.0.0/8 can see the page. Print the first and last address of 192.168.1.64/26 ."}, {"u": "tut-102-ip-networks.html#next", "t": "IP networks", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Sockets"}, {"u": "tut-25-sockets.html", "t": "Sockets", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-25-sockets.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Sockets", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # what an address, a port, TCP and a socket are, TcpListener (the server) and TcpClient (the client), streams, readers and writers over a connection, the order in which a conversation happens."}, {"u": "tut-25-sockets.html#words", "t": "Sockets", "h": "Words", "x": "Words # Word Meaning IP address the address of a computer: 127.0.0.1 means this computer port a number (0\u201365535) that says which program on that computer TCP a reliable connection: bytes arrive in order, none lost socket one end of a connection listener a program that waits for others to connect"}, {"u": "tut-25-sockets.html#the-program", "t": "Sockets", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # sockets - a TCP server and client in one program load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System.IO\" \"System.Net\" \"System.Net.Sockets\" } func main # STEP 1. The server: a listener waits for connections on a port of this computer. oListener = new TcpListener(new IPAddress().Loopback,0) # port 0: any free port oListener.Start(NULL) nPort = oListener.LocalEndpoint.Port ? \"STEP 1: the server listens on 127.0.0.1, port is chosen: \" + (nPort > 0) # STEP 2. The client connects. The connection is accepted by the operating system at once. oClient = new TcpClient() oClient.Connect([ :hostname = \"127."}, {"u": "tut-25-sockets.html#how-it-works", "t": "Sockets", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # The server side # new TcpListener(new IPAddress().Loopback, 0) prepares a listener on the loopback address ( 127.0.0.1 , reachable only from this computer) and port 0 , which means \"any free port\". Start(NULL) starts it, and LocalEndpoint.Port tells which port was chosen. The client side # new TcpClient() is the client; Connect([ :hostname = \"127.0.0.1\", :port = nPort ]) connects. The operating system accepts the connection at once, even before the program calls AcceptTcpClient ; this is why client and server can live in one program, without threads. Accepting # oListener.Accept"}, {"u": "tut-25-sockets.html#real-servers", "t": "Sockets", "h": "Real servers", "x": "Real servers # In a real system the server and the clients are different programs. A server loops: accept a client, talk, accept the next. Ring code runs on one thread, so a server that serves many clients at once checks oListener.Pending() and polls its clients in turn; for a web server use the ASP.NET WebServer (the tutorial HTTP ), which handles the threading for you."}, {"u": "tut-25-sockets.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Sockets", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # an address and a port identify the other side, listener/accept on the server, connect on the client, streams, readers and writers, each read needs a matching write."}, {"u": "tut-25-sockets.html#exercises", "t": "Sockets", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Make the server answer pong to ping and unknown to anything else. Let the client send a number; the server answers its square. Try a port that nobody listens on and catch the error from Connect . Print the address the client sees: oClient.Client.RemoteEndPoint.ToString() ."}, {"u": "tut-25-sockets.html#next", "t": "Sockets", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Messages on a TCP stream"}, {"u": "tut-158-framing.html", "t": "Messages on a TCP stream", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-158-framing.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Messages on a TCP stream", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # why TCP has no message boundaries , a length prefix : four bytes in network order (big endian), Read that returns fewer bytes than asked, and reading exactly n bytes in a loop, sending text, binary, empty and long messages, several messages written together and read one by one, a maximum length that protects the receiver. The tutorial Sockets sent a few lines over TCP. Real protocols (chat, file transfer, a database) send many messages of different sizes."}, {"u": "tut-158-framing.html#the-program", "t": "Messages on a TCP stream", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # messages on a TCP stream - TCP has no message boundaries: a length prefix, reading exactly n bytes, partial reads and a limit load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.Net\" \"System.Net.Sockets\" } func main # TCP is a STREAM of bytes, not a list of messages. If a program writes \"ab\" and then \"cd\", the other side may read \"abcd\" in one call, # or \"a\" and \"bcd\", or ... The sizes of the pieces that Read returns have nothing to do with the sizes of the writes. A protocol that # sends MESSAGES must say where each one ends: here every message has a LENGTH PREFIX, four bytes with the"}, {"u": "tut-158-framing.html#how-it-works", "t": "Messages on a TCP stream", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # A stream, not a list of messages # If a program writes ab and then cd , the other side may read abcd at once, or a and bcd : the operating system splits and joins the bytes as it likes, and a read gives what has arrived so far . A program that assumes one write is one read works on the test computer and fails on a slow network or with a long message. The receiver needs to be told where a message ends. The length prefix # Each message starts with its length in four bytes, biggest byte first (the order of the network, called big endian ): 300 is 00 00 01 2C (1 \u00d7 256 + 44). The rec"}, {"u": "tut-158-framing.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Messages on a TCP stream", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # TCP is a stream and needs a protocol for messages, a length prefix in big endian, reading exactly n bytes into a buffer, with partial reads, empty and binary messages, a maximum length."}, {"u": "tut-158-framing.html#exercises", "t": "Messages on a TCP stream", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Add a message type (one byte) after the length: T for text and B for binary. Write writeMessage so that it sends the length and the message in one write (join them first). Make the receiver close the connection after a refused length and tell the sender. Add a checksum (four bytes of CRC32 or the start of a SHA-256) after each message and check it."}, {"u": "tut-158-framing.html#next", "t": "Messages on a TCP stream", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # UDP"}, {"u": "tut-91-udp.html", "t": "UDP", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-91-udp.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "UDP", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # the difference between UDP (messages) and TCP (a stream), UdpClient : Send , Receive , ReceiveAsync , Available , binding to a port, and reading the port the system chose, answering the sender, a time limit with Task.Wait(ms) , why a program that uses UDP must expect lost messages. The tutorial Sockets used TCP: a connection, then a stream of bytes. UDP has no connection. Everything in this lesson runs on 127.0.0.1 , so it works without a network."}, {"u": "tut-91-udp.html#the-program", "t": "UDP", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # UDP - datagrams between two sockets of this computer, Receive with a time limit, broadcast-style replies and lost data load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.Net\" \"System.Net.Sockets\" \"System.Text\" } func main oUtf8 = new UTF8Encoding() oLoop = new IPAddress().Loopback # 1. UDP sends separate messages (datagrams) without a connection: no Accept, no stream. A socket that listens binds to a port # (0 = the system chooses a free one); LocalEndPoint tells which. oServer = new UdpClient().@New([ :port = 0 ]) nPort = oServer.Client.LocalEndPoint.Port ? \"1. the server socket is b"}, {"u": "tut-91-udp.html#how-it-works", "t": "UDP", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # Datagrams # A UDP message is called a datagram . It is sent in one piece and received in one piece: three Send calls give three Receive results of 3, 12 and 5 bytes, never a joined or a half message (a TCP stream has no such borders). There is no Accept and no stream; a socket that wants to receive only needs a port. new UdpClient([ :port = 0 ]) binds to a port that the system chooses, and Client.LocalEndPoint.Port tells which one. Texts are sent as bytes ( UTF8Encoding.GetBytes ) and read with GetString . Sending # Send has several forms, so it takes names: the bytes ( :dgram )"}, {"u": "tut-91-udp.html#what-you-learned", "t": "UDP", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # UDP sends messages; TCP sends a stream, UdpClient.Send , Receive , ReceiveAsync , Available , answering to the sender's endpoint, a time limit for a receive, UDP gives no promise of delivery."}, {"u": "tut-91-udp.html#exercises", "t": "UDP", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Write a small time server : it answers every datagram time with the current time. Send 100 numbered datagrams quickly to a socket that is slow to read and count how many arrive (on one computer the answer is usually all of them: why is that no proof for UDP in general?). Add a resend: the client sends, waits 200 ms for an answer and tries three times. Send a datagram larger than 1500 bytes and look at what Receive returns."}, {"u": "tut-91-udp.html#next", "t": "UDP", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # WebSockets"}, {"u": "tut-156-websockets.html", "t": "WebSockets", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-156-websockets.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "WebSockets", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # what a WebSocket is and how it starts (an HTTP request that asks to upgrade ), a server with HttpListener and AcceptWebSocketAsync , a client with ClientWebSocket and ConnectAsync , text and binary messages with SendAsync and ReceiveAsync , how bytes go through the bridge: an ArraySegment that .NET fills, messages that arrive in parts ( EndOfMessage ), the close handshake . The tutorials Sockets and UDP sent bytes over TCP and UDP. A WebSocket adds messages on top of TCP, and a browser can use it."}, {"u": "tut-156-websockets.html#the-program", "t": "WebSockets", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # WebSockets - a connection that stays open: a client and a server in one program, text and binary messages, long messages in parts, closing load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.Net\" \"System.Net.Sockets\" \"System.Net.WebSockets\" \"System.Threading\" } oToken = \"\" # a token that cancels everything after 20 seconds, so that a mistake cannot hang the program func main # HTTP is a question and an answer. A WEBSOCKET is a connection that stays open after the first HTTP request: both sides can send a # message whenever they like, which is what a chat, a live price list or a game ne"}, {"u": "tut-156-websockets.html#how-it-works", "t": "WebSockets", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # The upgrade # A WebSocket starts as a normal HTTP request with the headers Upgrade: websocket and Connection: Upgrade . The server answers 101 Switching Protocols , and from then on the connection is not HTTP any more: it carries messages in both directions until it is closed. In .NET the server side is an HttpListener : GetContextAsync() waits for a request, Request.IsWebSocketRequest says whether it asks for an upgrade, and AcceptWebSocketAsync does the upgrade and gives a WebSocket . (The WebServer of this package serves HTTP only; HttpListener is the .NET class for this job."}, {"u": "tut-156-websockets.html#what-you-learned", "t": "WebSockets", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # a WebSocket as a connection that stays open, the server with HttpListener and the client with ClientWebSocket , sending and receiving text and binary messages through an ArraySegment , reading long messages in parts, the close handshake."}, {"u": "tut-156-websockets.html#exercises", "t": "WebSockets", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Make the server answer each message with its length. Send a message of 10,000 bytes with a buffer of 1,024 and count the parts. Let the client send a message after the server closed, and read the error. Send a JSON text (the tutorial JSON as a tree ) and parse it on the other side."}, {"u": "tut-156-websockets.html#next", "t": "WebSockets", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # HttpClient in depth"}, {"u": "tut-84-http-client.html", "t": "HttpClient in depth", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-84-http-client.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "HttpClient in depth", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # one HttpClient for the whole program, and DefaultRequestHeaders , reading response headers and the content type, status codes: an answer of 404 is not an error, EnsureSuccessStatusCode when \"not OK\" must stop the program, canceling a request that takes too long, saving a download with CopyToAsync . The tutorial A REST client made a first REST client; the tutorial HTTP explained HTTP. This lesson adds what you need for real services."}, {"u": "tut-84-http-client.html#the-program", "t": "HttpClient in depth", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # The service is a small WebServer that runs in the same program (the client code is the same for a real service; only the address differs). # HttpClient in depth - headers both ways, status codes, errors, time limits, cancelling and saving a download # (a small server runs inside the program, so no internet is needed; the client code is what you write for a real service) load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.IO\" \"System.Net.Http\" \"System.Threading\" \"RingDotNet.Web\" } oServer = \"\" oClient = \"\" func main oServer = new WebServer(\"http://127.0.0.1:0\") oServer.onGet(\"/echo\",\"echo"}, {"u": "tut-84-http-client.html#how-it-works", "t": "HttpClient in depth", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # One client # Create one HttpClient and use it for all requests: it keeps connections open and is meant to be shared. Creating a client for each request wastes connections. DefaultRequestHeaders.Add(\"X-Client\", \u2026) adds headers that go with every request (a user agent, an API key, Accept ). The timeout of ten seconds protects the program from a server that never answers. Headers # Step 1 shows both directions. The request carried X-Client ; the page read it with oRequest.header(\"X-Client\") . The page set its own header with oRequest.setHeader(\"X-Server\",\"ring\") , and the client re"}, {"u": "tut-84-http-client.html#what-you-learned", "t": "HttpClient in depth", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # one shared HttpClient , default headers, request and response headers, ContentType , StatusCode , ReasonPhrase , IsSuccessStatusCode , EnsureSuccessStatusCode , canceling with a token, CopyToAsync to a file."}, {"u": "tut-84-http-client.html#exercises", "t": "HttpClient in depth", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Add a header Authorization with a fake token and let the server answer 401 when it is missing. Download the file with ResponseHeadersRead and print the ContentLength from the headers before copying. Send three requests together and wait with WhenAll (the tutorial Tasks that wait ). Retry a request three times with a pause when the status is 503 ."}, {"u": "tut-84-http-client.html#next", "t": "HttpClient in depth", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # A REST client"}, {"u": "tut-39-rest-client.html", "t": "A REST client", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-39-rest-client.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "A REST client", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # what REST means in practice, sending GET and POST requests with a JSON body, reading the status and the JSON answer, timeouts and errors, keeping the request code in one helper function."}, {"u": "tut-39-rest-client.html#rest-in-one-minute", "t": "A REST client", "h": "REST in one minute", "x": "REST in one minute # A REST service gives you things ( resources ) at addresses and lets you use the HTTP methods on them: Request Meaning GET /api/books the list of books GET /api/book?id=2 one book POST /api/books + JSON add a book PUT / DELETE change / remove The answers are JSON, and the status code tells whether it worked: 200 OK, 201 Created, 404 Not Found, 500 server error. A good client always looks at the status before it trusts the body."}, {"u": "tut-39-rest-client.html#the-program", "t": "A REST client", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # For this tutorial the service is started in the same program, so that it needs no internet. The client code is exactly what you would write for a real service: only cUrl would be different. # a REST client - call a JSON service, read the answer, handle the errors # (the service is started in the same program so that the tutorial needs no internet) load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System.Net.Http\" \"System.Text\" \"System.Text.Json\" \"RingDotNet.Web\" } oServer = \"\" oClient = \"\" cUrl = \"\" aBooks = [[1,\"The Hobbit\",1937],[2,\"Dune\",1965],[3,\"Neuromancer\",1984]] func main # --- the service (a "}, {"u": "tut-39-rest-client.html#how-it-works", "t": "A REST client", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # One helper for all requests # request(method, path, body) builds the request, sends it, waits and returns [status, body] . Everything else calls it. A request is an HttpRequestMessage created with named arguments : [ :method = new HttpMethod(cMethod), :requestUri = cUrl + cPath ] . For a POST the body is a StringContent with the text, the encoding ( UTF8 ) and the media type application/json , which tells the server how to read it. The Task pattern # SendAsync returns a Task at once; Wait(NULL) waits for the answer; .Result is the HttpResponseMessage . Reading the body is again "}, {"u": "tut-39-rest-client.html#what-you-learned", "t": "A REST client", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # GET and POST with JSON, status before body, Task + Wait + Result , timeouts and catching network errors, one request helper."}, {"u": "tut-39-rest-client.html#exercises", "t": "A REST client", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Add deleteBook(nId) using the DELETE method (and a handler on the service). Retry a failed request up to three times with a pause ( Thread.Sleep ). Add an Accept header ( oClient.DefaultRequestHeaders.Add(...) ). Call a real public API (for example a free JSON placeholder service) and print three fields. You need internet for this one."}, {"u": "tut-39-rest-client.html#next", "t": "A REST client", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # A web service with a database"}, {"u": "tut-16-web-service.html", "t": "A web service with a database", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-16-web-service.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "A web service with a database", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # the shape of a small web API ( GET list, POST add, DELETE remove), storing data with RingDatabase from a web handler, answering with JSON and the right status codes, validating a request."}, {"u": "tut-16-web-service.html#the-design", "t": "A web service with a database", "h": "The design", "x": "The design # A REST-style API uses the HTTP method to say what to do and the address to say what to do it with: Request Meaning Answer GET /notes list the notes 200 and a JSON array POST /notes (the text is the body) add a note 201 and the new id, or 400 if the text is empty DELETE /notes?id=1 delete a note 200 , or 404 if there is no such note"}, {"u": "tut-16-web-service.html#the-program", "t": "A web service with a database", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # a web service with a database - JSON over HTTP, stored with Entity Framework load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System.Net.Http\" \"RingDotNet.Web\" \"RingDotNet.Data\" } oServer = \"\" oClient = \"\" cUrl = \"\" oDb = \"\" func main # 1. The database: one table, kept in memory for this tutorial (use \"Data Source=notes.db\" for a file) oDb = new RingDatabase(\"Data Source=:memory:\",[ [\"Notes\",[[\"Id\",\"int\",1],[\"Text\",\"string\",0,[[\"required\",1],[\"maxlength\",200]]],[\"Done\",\"int\",0,[[\"default\",0]]]]] ]) # 2. The web service: each address is a Ring function oServer = new WebServer(\"http://127.0.0.1:0\") o"}, {"u": "tut-16-web-service.html#how-it-works", "t": "A web service with a database", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # Two start-up calls # \"RingDotNet.Web\" in the using block starts the web runtime and \"RingDotNet.Data\" loads Entity Framework. Both before any other .NET call. The database # A RingDatabase with a table Notes : a numbered key Id , a required Text (at most 200 characters), and a Done flag with a default of 0. \"Data Source=:memory:\" keeps the data in memory, which is perfect for a tutorial; use \"Data Source=notes.db\" for a file. See the tutorial Databases step by step . The handlers # Each handler is a Ring function that receives the request and returns the response. listNotes read"}, {"u": "tut-16-web-service.html#what-you-learned", "t": "A web service with a database", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # methods + addresses + status codes make an API, handlers use the database and return JSON, validate input and answer with the right status, globals for what handlers need."}, {"u": "tut-16-web-service.html#exercises", "t": "A web service with a database", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Add PUT /notes?id=1 that marks a note as done ( update ). Add GET /notes?id=2 that returns one note, or 404 . Make the server serve an HTML page at / that lists the notes, as the WebApi application does. Switch to a file database and restart the program: are the notes still there?"}, {"u": "tut-16-web-service.html#next", "t": "A web service with a database", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # A small web site"}, {"u": "tut-26-website.html", "t": "A small web site", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-26-website.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "A small web site", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # serving HTML pages that share a frame, an HTML form and reading its fields, redirecting the work by status code ( 400 for bad input), cross-site scripting and why you escape text, running the site for real."}, {"u": "tut-26-website.html#the-site", "t": "A small web site", "h": "The site", "x": "The site # Address Page GET / the home page GET /about an about page GET /guestbook the entries, and a form to add one POST /guestbook the form sends its fields here"}, {"u": "tut-26-website.html#the-program", "t": "A small web site", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # a small web site - HTML pages, a form, links and a guestbook (ASP.NET Core) load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System.Net.Http\" \"System.Text\" \"RingDotNet.Web\" } oServer = \"\" oClient = \"\" cUrl = \"\" aEntries = [] # the guestbook: [name, message] # Run it as a web site: ring ch07_website.ring --serve then open http://localhost:5090/ func main cAddress = \"http://127.0.0.1:0\" if find(sysargv,\"--serve\") > 0 cAddress = \"http://localhost:5090\" ok oServer = new WebServer(cAddress) oServer.onGet(\"/\",\"homePage\") oServer.onGet(\"/guestbook\",\"guestbookPage\") oServer.onPost(\"/guestbook\",\"signGuestbo"}, {"u": "tut-26-website.html#how-it-works", "t": "A small web site", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # One frame for all pages # The function page(cTitle, cBody) builds the whole HTML text: a title, a few lines of style, a navigation bar and the body. Every handler calls it, so every page looks the same and a change of design is made in one place. A handler returns a text ; the server sends it as text/html . The form # <\"t\">class=\"k\">form method='post' action='/guestbook'> <\"t\">class=\"k\">input name='name' placeholder='Your name'> <\"t\">class=\"k\">textarea name='message' ...></\"t\">class=\"k\">textarea> <\"t\">class=\"k\">button type='submit'>Send</\"t\">class=\"k\">button> </\"t\">class=\"k\">for"}, {"u": "tut-26-website.html#what-you-learned", "t": "A small web site", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # pages are texts; a function can build the common frame, forms send fields by POST; oRequest.form(\"name\") reads them, answer errors with the right status, always escape what visitors write."}, {"u": "tut-26-website.html#exercises", "t": "A small web site", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Add a page /stats that shows the number of entries and the longest message. Show the newest entry first. Limit a message to 200 characters and refuse longer ones with a 400 . Store the entries in a SQLite file with RingDatabase , so that they survive a restart."}, {"u": "tut-26-website.html#next", "t": "A small web site", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # A tiny web framework"}, {"u": "tut-55-miniweb.html", "t": "A tiny web framework", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-55-miniweb.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "A tiny web framework", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # routes with parameters : /users/:name/posts/:id , one catch-all handler ( notFound ) that decides with a router, a template with {{name}} places and automatic escaping, calling a function by its name ( call ), testing the whole thing in one program."}, {"u": "tut-55-miniweb.html#the-idea", "t": "A tiny web framework", "h": "The idea", "x": "The idea # WebServer maps a fixed address to a handler. A real site has addresses with changing parts: /users/ali , /users/sara/posts/7 . Instead of one route for each user, a framework keeps a pattern ( /users/:name ) and, for each request, finds the pattern that fits and extracts the parameters ( name = ali )."}, {"u": "tut-55-miniweb.html#the-program", "t": "A tiny web framework", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # a tiny web framework - routes with parameters (/users/:id), templates ({{name}}) and one catch-all handler load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System.Net\" \"System.Net.Http\" \"RingDotNet.Web\" } oServer = \"\" oClient = \"\" oRouter = \"\" func main oServer = new WebServer(\"http://127.0.0.1:0\") oRouter = new Router() oRouter.add(\"GET\",\"/\",\"homePage\") oRouter.add(\"GET\",\"/users/:name\",\"userPage\") oRouter.add(\"GET\",\"/users/:name/posts/:id\",\"postPage\") oServer.notFound(\"dispatch\") # every request that has no ordinary route comes here: the router decides oClient = new HttpClient() oClient.Timeout = "}, {"u": "tut-55-miniweb.html#how-it-works", "t": "A tiny web framework", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # The router # Router.add(method, pattern, handlerName) stores the pattern split into parts. find(method, path) splits the real path too, and compares part by part: a part that starts with : matches anything and is saved as a parameter, any other part must be equal. A path with a different number of parts can never match. The result is [handler, parameters] \u2014 or an empty list. One handler for everything # oServer.notFound(\"dispatch\") registers a handler for every request that has no ordinary route. Here that is every request, so dispatch is the only entry of the application: it as"}, {"u": "tut-55-miniweb.html#what-you-learned", "t": "A tiny web framework", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # patterns with :parameters , a router that finds the page and the values, notFound as the single entry point, templates with escaping, call for a function name."}, {"u": "tut-55-miniweb.html#exercises", "t": "A tiny web framework", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Add a route POST /users/:name for a form, and look at oRequest.form(\"field\") . Support a * part that matches the rest of the path ( /files/* ). Put the templates in files in a views folder and read them with File.ReadAllText ."}, {"u": "tut-55-miniweb.html#next", "t": "A tiny web framework", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Route templates"}, {"u": "tut-120-routes.html", "t": "Route templates", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-120-routes.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Route templates", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # the syntax of a route template , required parts, optional parts ( ? ), default values ( = ), catch-all parts ( * ), TemplateParser and TemplateMatcher , reading the values of the parts, why the order of routes matters, what a template does not do. The tutorial A web service with a database and the tutorial A tiny web framework made small web servers with simple routes. Real programs have many routes with parts that change ( /users/42 , /users/43 ). Templates are the standard way to write them, and the same classes work in your own Ring program. Note The routing classes ar"}, {"u": "tut-120-routes.html#the-program", "t": "Route templates", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # route templates - \"users/{id}/orders/{orderId?}\", optional and default values, catch-all parameters, and choosing a route load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"Microsoft.AspNetCore.Routing.Template\" \"Microsoft.AspNetCore.Routing\" \"System\" \"RingDotNet.Web\" # the routing classes are part of the ASP.NET Core runtime: this line starts it } func main # A web program decides what to do from the PATH of the address: /users/42 shows user 42, /files/docs/a/b.txt shows a file. A ROUTE # TEMPLATE describes such a path with named parts in braces. ASP.NET Core uses them for every route; the same clas"}, {"u": "tut-120-routes.html#how-it-works", "t": "Route templates", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # The syntax # A template is a path whose parts are either literal text ( users , orders : matched without regard to case) or parameters in braces: Written Meaning {id} required: one part of the path {orderId?} optional : it may be missing at the end {page=1} has a default value : it may be missing at the end, and then the value is 1 {*path} catch-all : everything that is left, with its slashes {id:int} has a constraint (here: a whole number) TemplateParser.Parse(text) reads a template into an object whose Parameters list each part with its Name , IsOptional , IsCatchAll and Defau"}, {"u": "tut-120-routes.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Route templates", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # templates with literal and parameter parts, optional ( ? ), default ( = ) and catch-all ( * ) parameters, TemplateParser , TemplateMatcher and RouteValueDictionary , values are text and must be checked, the first matching route wins, so the order matters."}, {"u": "tut-120-routes.html#exercises", "t": "Route templates", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Make the table of routes for a small shop ( / , /products , /products/{id} , /cart , /checkout ) and test ten paths. Add a check to the {id:int} route: refuse the match when the value is not a number. Put the routes in the web server of the tutorial A tiny web framework and answer 404 when none matches. Write isSafePath(value) for a catch-all {*path} and test ../secret.txt ."}, {"u": "tut-120-routes.html#next", "t": "Route templates", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # A project: a link shortener"}, {"u": "tut-150-shortener.html", "t": "A project: a link shortener", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-150-shortener.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "A project: a link shortener", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # a web service with POST /shorten , GET /stats and a catch-all handler ( notFound ), base 62 codes made from a counter, the redirect : status 302 Found and the header Location , a client that does not follow redirects, so a test can see them, answering 400 and 404 , why a redirecting service must check where it redirects. The tutorial A web service with a database and the tutorial Route templates made services and routes. This project is a small complete one."}, {"u": "tut-150-shortener.html#the-program", "t": "A project: a link shortener", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # a project: a link shortener - a web service that makes short codes, redirects with 302, counts the visits and refuses bad addresses load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.Net.Http\" \"System.Text\" \"RingDotNet.Web\" } oServer = \"\" oClient = \"\" cUrl = \"\" aLinks = [] # [code, address, visits] nCounter = 1000 # the next number to turn into a code func main oServer = new WebServer(\"http://127.0.0.1:0\") oServer.onPost(\"/shorten\",\"shorten\") oServer.onGet(\"/stats\",\"statsOverview\") oServer.notFound(\"visit\") # any other address is a short code cUrl = oServer.url() # The client does NOT"}, {"u": "tut-150-shortener.html#how-it-works", "t": "A project: a link shortener", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # The routes # onPost(\"/shorten\", \"shorten\") makes a short link, onGet(\"/stats\", \"statsOverview\") lists the visits, and notFound(\"visit\") handles every other path: the text after the first / is a code. The handler of an unknown path is the way to make a service whose addresses are data , not a fixed list. (The status names in the output, Created , Found , NotFound and BadRequest , are the names of the status codes 201, 302, 404 and 400.) Codes # toCode writes a number in base 62 with the digits 0-9 , a-z and A-Z : 62 symbols that are safe in a URL and short. The counter starts at "}, {"u": "tut-150-shortener.html#what-you-learned", "t": "A project: a link shortener", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # a service with a catch-all handler, base 62 codes, the 302 redirect and the Location header, testing redirects with a client that does not follow them, checking the address before redirecting."}, {"u": "tut-150-shortener.html#exercises", "t": "A project: a link shortener", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Add GET /stats/CODE that shows the visits of one link as JSON ( webJson ). Let the caller choose the code ( code=docs ), and refuse a code that is already used. Add the time of the last visit and show it in the statistics. Make the codes random and check that a code was not used before."}, {"u": "tut-150-shortener.html#next", "t": "A project: a link shortener", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # E-mail messages"}, {"u": "tut-94-mail-messages.html", "t": "E-mail messages", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-94-mail-messages.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "E-mail messages", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # MailAddress : reading Name <user@host> and using it as a check, MailMessage : sender, recipients ( To , CC ), subject, body, properties named like Ring words ( From , To ), AlternateView : the same message as text and as HTML, where sending would happen ( SmtpClient ) and why it is not in the lesson."}, {"u": "tut-94-mail-messages.html#the-program", "t": "E-mail messages", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # e-mail messages - addresses, a MailMessage with several recipients, an attachment, a body in HTML and in text (nothing is sent) load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.IO\" \"System.Net.Mail\" \"System.Net.Mime\" \"System.Text\" } func main # 1. MailAddress reads \"Name <user@host>\" and splits it. A wrong address raises an error, so it is a check too. oAddress = new MailAddress(\"Ann Lee <ann.lee@example.com>\") ? \"1. display name: \" + oAddress.DisplayName ? \" user: \" + oAddress.User + \", host: \" + oAddress.Host + \", address: \" + oAddress.Address for cText in [\"bob@example.com\",\"bob@"}, {"u": "tut-94-mail-messages.html#how-it-works", "t": "E-mail messages", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # Addresses # new MailAddress(\"Ann Lee <ann.lee@example.com>\") splits the text into DisplayName , User , Host and Address . A text that is not an address raises an error, so the function isAddress uses try / catch (the tutorial When things go wrong ) to turn the error into a yes or no. The check is about the form ( user@host ); it cannot know whether the mailbox exists. bob@ and @example.com are refused, bob@example.com is accepted. Note .NET is generous with display names: bob example@x.com is read as the name bob and the address example@x.com . If your program needs exactly one "}, {"u": "tut-94-mail-messages.html#what-you-learned", "t": "E-mail messages", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # MailAddress splits and checks an address, MailMessage has sender, recipients, subject and body, From and To work as normal properties, alternate views give text and HTML in one mail, sending is a separate step that needs a server."}, {"u": "tut-94-mail-messages.html#exercises", "t": "E-mail messages", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Write addressList(cText) that splits a@x.com; b@y.com into a list of MailAddress objects and skips the wrong ones. Add a Bcc recipient and print all the recipients of the mail. Add an attachment ( Attachments.Add ) made from a file of the tutorial Files and folders and print its name. Read about MailMessage.Headers and add the header X-Mailer ."}, {"u": "tut-94-mail-messages.html#where-to-go-next", "t": "E-mail messages", "h": "Where to go next", "x": "Where to go next # This was the last tutorial of the chapter Networking and the web . The next chapter is Security : it starts with Protecting and signing messages . The Tutorials page lists all the chapters, and you can start any of them."}, {"u": "tut-78-crypto.html", "t": "Protecting and signing messages", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-78-crypto.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Protecting and signing messages", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # turning a password into keys with PBKDF2 ( Rfc2898DeriveBytes ), AES encryption with a random IV, an HMAC that proves that the message was not changed, why you check the HMAC before you decrypt, digital signatures with RSA , how these classes are called from Ring (byte arrays are Ring texts). This builds on the tutorial Hashing and encryption (hashes and basic encryption)."}, {"u": "tut-78-crypto.html#two-different-problems", "t": "Protecting and signing messages", "h": "Two different problems", "x": "Two different problems # Problem Tool Key Nobody should read the message encryption (AES) one secret key , shared by sender and receiver Nobody should change it, and I want to know it is genuine HMAC (shared key) or a signature (RSA) HMAC: the same shared key. Signature: a private key signs, the public key verifies. Use both for a protected message: encrypt first, then compute the HMAC over the encrypted bytes ( encrypt-then-MAC ). The receiver checks the HMAC first, so a forged or damaged package is refused before any decryption work happens."}, {"u": "tut-78-crypto.html#the-program", "t": "Protecting and signing messages", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # protecting and signing messages - AES, HMAC (encrypt-then-MAC), a password-derived key and RSA signatures load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.Text\" \"System.Security.Cryptography\" } func main oRandom = new RandomNumberGenerator() # 1. A key from a password: PBKDF2 stretches it (many rounds, a random salt) into as many bytes as we ask for. # 64 bytes: the first 32 are the AES key, the last 32 the HMAC key (never use one key for two jobs). cSalt = oRandom.GetBytes(16) cKeys = deriveKeys(\"correct horse battery staple\",cSalt) cEncryptionKey = left(cKeys,32) cMacKey = right(c"}, {"u": "tut-78-crypto.html#how-it-works", "t": "Protecting and signing messages", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # Keys from a password # A password is short and predictable; a key must be 32 random-looking bytes. Rfc2898DeriveBytes (PBKDF2) repeats a hash 100,000 times with a random salt and returns as many bytes as you ask for. 64 bytes are split in two: one key for encryption and a different one for the HMAC \u2014 never use one key for two jobs. The salt is not secret: store it next to the package. The constructor has several forms, so it is made with named arguments: dnNew(\"Rfc2898DeriveBytes\",[ :password = ..., :salt = ..., :iterations = ..., :hashAlgorithm = ... ]) . Encrypting # Aes (made"}, {"u": "tut-78-crypto.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Protecting and signing messages", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # PBKDF2 keys and salts, AES with a fresh IV, encrypt-then-MAC and verifying first, a comparison that takes the same time, RSA signatures with a public key, byte arrays as Ring texts."}, {"u": "tut-78-crypto.html#exercises", "t": "Protecting and signing messages", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Put the salt in front of the package so that it can be opened with only the password. Save the public key XML in a file and verify a signature in a second program. Sign a file's SHA-256 hash instead of the whole file with SignHash . Time deriveKeys and change the number of rounds; how many are comfortable for your computer?"}, {"u": "tut-78-crypto.html#next", "t": "Protecting and signing messages", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Digital signatures"}, {"u": "tut-105-signatures.html", "t": "Digital signatures", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-105-signatures.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Digital signatures", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # what a signature proves, and what it does not, private and public keys, ECDsa : making a key, SignData , VerifyData , why a changed message or a different key fails, sharing the public key and nothing else. The tutorial Hashing and encryption used hashes and encryption and the tutorial Protecting and signing messages used RSA and more. A signature adds the answer to \"who?\" ."}, {"u": "tut-105-signatures.html#the-program", "t": "Digital signatures", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # digital signatures - ECDsa keys, signing a message, verifying it, and what happens when the message or the key changes load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.Security.Cryptography\" \"System.Text\" } func main oUtf8 = new UTF8Encoding() # A digital signature proves two things about a message: who sent it (only the owner of the PRIVATE key can make the signature) and # that it was not changed (the signature fits only this exact message). Everybody with the PUBLIC key can check it. # ECDsa uses elliptic curves: small keys and fast signatures. (the tutorial \"Protecting and signi"}, {"u": "tut-105-signatures.html#how-it-works", "t": "Digital signatures", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # What a signature proves # A key pair has two parts that fit each other. The private key stays with its owner and makes signatures. The public key can be given to everybody and only checks signatures. A valid signature proves two things: the message was signed by the owner of the private key , and the message is exactly the signed one \u2014 changing a single character makes the check fail. It does not hide the message (that is encryption) and it does not prove that the owner is the person you think; for that you need a certificate from somebody you trust. ECDsa # ECDsa uses elliptic "}, {"u": "tut-105-signatures.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Digital signatures", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # a signature proves the owner and the integrity, not secrecy, the private key signs and the public key verifies, ECCurve.CreateFromFriendlyName , ECDsa.Create , SignData , VerifyData , a changed message or another key fails, export the public parameters and keep the private key."}, {"u": "tut-105-signatures.html#exercises", "t": "Digital signatures", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Sign a file (the tutorial Files and folders ) and write the signature to another file; verify both later. Write signedMessage(text) that returns the text and its signature as Base64 in a JSON object (the tutorial Writing JSON as a stream ). Change one byte of the signature and check that verification fails. Try the curve nistP384 and look at the size of the signature."}, {"u": "tut-105-signatures.html#next", "t": "Digital signatures", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Certificates"}, {"u": "tut-116-certificates.html", "t": "Certificates", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-116-certificates.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Certificates", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # what a certificate is: public key, subject, issuer, validity, thumbprint, making a self-signed certificate ( CertificateRequest , CreateSelfSigned ), extensions such as basic constraints, exporting: PFX (with the private key and a password) and PEM / .cer (public only), signing with the private key and verifying with the public certificate, trust and the chain . The tutorial Digital signatures signed data with a bare key pair. A certificate adds the answer to \"whose key is this?\" ."}, {"u": "tut-116-certificates.html#the-program", "t": "Certificates", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # certificates - a self-signed X.509 certificate, what it contains, export with and without the private key, and signing with it load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.Text\" \"System.Security.Cryptography\" \"System.Security.Cryptography.X509Certificates\" } func main oUtf8 = new UTF8Encoding() # A certificate is a public key together with a name (the SUBJECT), a time of validity, and a signature of whoever vouches for it (the # ISSUER). Web sites use certificates for HTTPS, programs use them to sign code and documents, and services use them to prove who they # are. Here we make"}, {"u": "tut-116-certificates.html#how-it-works", "t": "Certificates", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # What a certificate says # A certificate is a small document, signed by an issuer , that says: the public key below belongs to the subject named here, and this is true from this date to that date . The subject is a distinguished name such as CN=Demo Server, O=RingDotNet, C=EG (common name, organization, country). The thumbprint is the SHA-1 hash of the whole certificate: a short identifier that you compare to be sure it is this certificate and not another one with the same name. In the real world the issuer is a certificate authority (CA) that your computer trusts, and it checks "}, {"u": "tut-116-certificates.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Certificates", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # subject, issuer, validity and thumbprint, CertificateRequest and CreateSelfSigned , PFX with a password for the private key, PEM and .cer for the public part, signing with the private key, verifying with the public certificate, why a self-signed certificate is not trusted."}, {"u": "tut-116-certificates.html#exercises", "t": "Certificates", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Add a subject alternative name ( SubjectAlternativeNameBuilder ) for localhost and 127.0.0.1 . Write the PEM to a file (the tutorial Files and folders ) and read it back with X509Certificate2.CreateFromPem . Make a second certificate with a validity of one second and look at NotAfter and the check in isValidNow after a pause. Find the certificates of your own computer: open the store Root with X509Store and print the subjects of the first five (the tutorial The Windows Registry shows another store of Windows)."}, {"u": "tut-116-certificates.html#next", "t": "Certificates", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Protecting data"}, {"u": "tut-113-data-protection.html", "t": "Protecting data", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-113-data-protection.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Protecting data", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # what the Data Protection API does and when to use it, Protect and Unprotect , purposes : tokens for one use cannot be used for another, why a changed token is refused, keys in a folder, and what happens with other keys, time-limited tokens. The tutorial Hashing and encryption and the tutorial Protecting and signing messages used the cryptography classes directly: you choose the algorithm, the key and the mode, and every choice is a chance for a mistake. The Data Protection API is the high-level tool: you give it a text, it gives you a token. Note The API is part of the AS"}, {"u": "tut-113-data-protection.html#the-program", "t": "Protecting data", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # protecting data - the Data Protection API: purposes, tampering, keys on disk, and values that expire load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"Microsoft.AspNetCore.DataProtection\" \"System\" \"System.Threading\" \"System.IO\" \"RingDotNet.Web\" # the Data Protection API is part of the ASP.NET Core runtime: this line starts it } func main # The Data Protection API encrypts and signs small pieces of data (a cookie, a token, a \"remember me\" value, a link in an e-mail) # without you handling keys, algorithms or initialization vectors. It makes keys, keeps them in a folder, renews them, # and encrypts wi"}, {"u": "tut-113-data-protection.html#how-it-works", "t": "Protecting data", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # A provider and a protector # DataProtectionProvider.Create(folder) makes a provider whose keys are kept in the folder as files (it makes the first key itself and renews keys before they expire). CreateProtector(\"purpose\") makes a protector for a purpose. Protect(text) returns a token : encrypted with AES and signed, so nobody can read it and nobody can change it. Unprotect(token) returns the text. Protect and Unprotect for texts are extension methods, so they are called with dnStatic(\"...DataProtectionCommonExtensions\",\"Protect\",[protector,text]) (the tutorial Caching ); CreateP"}, {"u": "tut-113-data-protection.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Protecting data", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # Protect and Unprotect encrypt and sign small values, a purpose keeps uses apart, a changed token is refused, the key folder is the secret and can be shared, time-limited tokens expire by themselves."}, {"u": "tut-113-data-protection.html#exercises", "t": "Protecting data", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Make a \"remember me\" cookie: protect the user id and the time, and write a function that reads it back or answers not valid . Make a password-reset link that is valid for 15 minutes (the tutorial A chat over TCP shows a small server to send it from). Look at the key file in the folder: it is XML. Which parts are readable and which are not? Protect a long text (10 000 characters) and look at the size of the token."}, {"u": "tut-113-data-protection.html#next", "t": "Protecting data", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Login, sessions and cookies"}, {"u": "tut-49-login.html", "t": "Login, sessions and cookies", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-49-login.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Login, sessions and cookies", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # why web servers need sessions, storing users with salted password hashes, creating and checking a session token, reading and setting cookies, the right status codes: 401 , 409 , 400 , security details that matter."}, {"u": "tut-49-login.html#the-problem", "t": "Login, sessions and cookies", "h": "The problem", "x": "The problem # Every HTTP request stands alone: the server does not know that the request for /profile comes from the person who logged in a minute ago. The solution is a session : the user logs in with a name and password, the server creates a long random token and remembers token \u2192 user , it sends the token to the browser in a cookie ( Set-Cookie ), the browser sends the cookie back with every later request ( Cookie ), the server finds the user by the token."}, {"u": "tut-49-login.html#the-program", "t": "Login, sessions and cookies", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # login, sessions and cookies - a web site that knows who you are load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.Net.Http\" \"System.Security.Cryptography\" \"System.Text\" \"RingDotNet.Web\" \"RingDotNet.Data\" } oServer = \"\" oClient = \"\" cUrl = \"\" oDb = \"\" aSessions = [] # [token, user name]: who is logged in func main # THE USERS: a table; the password is never stored, only a salted slow hash of it (the tutorial \"Hashing and encryption\") oDb = new RingDatabase(\"Data Source=:memory:\",[ [\"Users\",[[\"Id\",\"int\",1],[\"Name\",\"string\",0,[[\"required\",1],[\"unique\",1]]],[\"Salt\",\"string\",0],[\"Hash\",\"s"}, {"u": "tut-49-login.html#how-it-works", "t": "Login, sessions and cookies", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # Registering # registerUser reads the form fields. It refuses a missing name or a short password with 400 , creates a random salt , and stores the name, the salt and a hash of the password ( PBKDF2 , the tutorial Hashing and encryption ) \u2014 never the password itself. The unique option of the name column makes the database refuse a duplicate, which the handler turns into 409 Conflict . Logging in # loginUser looks up the name, hashes the typed password with that user's salt and compares it with the stored hash. If the name does not exist or the password is wrong, the answer is the "}, {"u": "tut-49-login.html#security-notes", "t": "Login, sessions and cookies", "h": "Security notes", "x": "Security notes # Do Because hash passwords with a salt and many rounds a stolen database must not reveal passwords long random tokens from RandomNumberGenerator a guessable token is a stolen account the same answer for a wrong name and a wrong password do not reveal who has an account HttpOnly cookies scripts cannot steal them use HTTPS (not shown here) and add Secure to the cookie on plain HTTP the cookie travels readable expire sessions after some time a forgotten login should not live forever escape text put into pages (the tutorial A small web site ) otherwise a script can act as the user "}, {"u": "tut-49-login.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Login, sessions and cookies", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # sessions turn separate requests into a conversation, token \u2192 user, token in a cookie, store hashes, never passwords, 401 / 409 / 400 , the cookie header in both directions."}, {"u": "tut-49-login.html#exercises", "t": "Login, sessions and cookies", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Add an expiry time to each session (a date) and refuse expired ones. Limit failed logins: after five wrong passwords for a name, answer 429 for a minute. Add a /change-password route that needs the old password. Store the sessions in the database so that they survive a restart."}, {"u": "tut-49-login.html#next", "t": "Login, sessions and cookies", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Identities and claims"}, {"u": "tut-95-claims.html", "t": "Identities and claims", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-95-claims.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Identities and claims", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # a claim : a type and a value, ClaimsIdentity (claims from one source) and ClaimsPrincipal (the user), FindFirst , FindAll and HasClaim , roles and IsInRole , a permission check where the rules are data. ASP.NET Core uses exactly these classes for every login, so what you learn here is what a web program (the tutorial A web service with a database ) receives after the user signed in. The tutorial Login, sessions and cookies made a login with a password hash; this lesson is about what happens after the login."}, {"u": "tut-95-claims.html#the-program", "t": "Identities and claims", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # identities and claims - ClaimsIdentity, ClaimsPrincipal, roles, claim types and a simple permission check load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.Security.Claims\" } func main # A claim is a statement about a user: a type and a value (\"Role\" = \"Admin\", \"Email\" = \"ann@example.com\"). # An identity is a list of claims from one source; a principal holds identities. ASP.NET uses these classes for every login. oIdentity = new ClaimsIdentity().@New([ :authenticationType = \"Password\" ]) oIdentity.AddClaim((new Claim).@New([ :type = \"Name\", :value = \"Ann\" ])) oIdentity.AddClaim((new "}, {"u": "tut-95-claims.html#how-it-works", "t": "Identities and claims", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # Claims, identities, principals # A claim is one statement about a user: Name = Ann , Email = ann@example.com , Role = Editor . The type is just a text; .NET has standard names ( ClaimTypes.Email is a long address-like text) but your own short names work in your own program. A user can have several claims of the same type, like the two roles here. An identity is a list of claims that came from one source (a password login, a certificate, a token) together with the name of that source ( AuthenticationType ). A principal is the user as the program sees them, and can hold several id"}, {"u": "tut-95-claims.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Identities and claims", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # claims, identities and principals, FindFirst , FindAll , HasClaim and IsInRole , roleType and nameType , a permission check driven by a list of rules."}, {"u": "tut-95-claims.html#exercises", "t": "Identities and claims", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Add the claim Department = Sales and write a rule that allows delete only for the Sales department. Make hasAllRoles(oPrincipal, aRoles) . Serialize the claims of a user to JSON (the tutorial JSON as a tree ) and build the identity again from the JSON. Make an identity with no authentication type and look at IsAuthenticated ."}, {"u": "tut-95-claims.html#next", "t": "Identities and claims", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Rate limiting"}, {"u": "tut-114-rate-limiting.html", "t": "Rate limiting", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-114-rate-limiting.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Rate limiting", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # why a service needs a rate limiter , the token bucket : a burst is allowed, the long-run pace is limited, the concurrency limiter and leases, AttemptAcquire and IsAcquired , statistics and the retry time, one limiter for each client. A web program (the tutorial A web service with a database ) uses limiters to answer 429 Too Many Requests , a client that calls an API (the tutorial HttpClient in depth ) uses one to stay below the limit of the server, and a program that sends mail uses one so that it is not marked as spam. Note System.Threading.RateLimiting is part of the AS"}, {"u": "tut-114-rate-limiting.html#the-program", "t": "Rate limiting", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # rate limiting - a token bucket, a concurrency limiter with leases, statistics, and one limiter for each client load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System.Threading.RateLimiting\" \"System\" \"System.Threading\" \"RingDotNet.Web\" # System.Threading.RateLimiting is part of the ASP.NET Core runtime: this line starts it } func main # A rate limiter protects a service from too many requests: a client that asks 1000 times a second, a script with a bug, an attack. # It says YES or NO to each request at once (AttemptAcquire), without waiting. The program decides what NO means (an error 429 # \"Too Ma"}, {"u": "tut-114-rate-limiting.html#how-it-works", "t": "Rate limiting", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # Yes or no at once # AttemptAcquire(1) asks for one permit and answers immediately with a lease ; IsAcquired says whether the permit was given. The limiter never waits in this call: what a no means is the program's decision (an error to the client, a pause, a message). ( AcquireAsync can wait in a queue; with QueueLimit = 0 , as here, nothing waits.) The token bucket # The bucket holds up to TokenLimit tokens (3). Each request takes one. Tokens come back at a fixed pace: TokensPerPeriod tokens every ReplenishmentPeriod . So a client can make a burst \u2014 here 3 requests at once \u2014 an"}, {"u": "tut-114-rate-limiting.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Rate limiting", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # limiters answer yes or no at once with AttemptAcquire , a token bucket allows bursts and limits the pace, a concurrency limiter uses leases that must be disposed, statistics and the retry time, one limiter for each client."}, {"u": "tut-114-rate-limiting.html#exercises", "t": "Rate limiting", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Write handleRequest(cClient) that returns 200 or 429 and the retry seconds, using one bucket for each client name. Use a FixedWindowRateLimiter (a window of 1 second, 5 permits) and make 10 requests with short pauses. Combine a concurrency limiter with a token bucket: a request must pass both. Print the statistics of every client after 100 random requests (the tutorial Randomness )."}, {"u": "tut-114-rate-limiting.html#where-to-go-next", "t": "Rate limiting", "h": "Where to go next", "x": "Where to go next # This was the last tutorial of the chapter Security . The next chapter is Databases and services : it starts with Databases step by step . The Tutorials page lists all the chapters, and you can start any of them."}, {"u": "tut-17-databases.html", "t": "Databases step by step", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-17-databases.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Databases step by step", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # what a database, a table, a row and a key are, describing tables as data and creating them, insert, read, update, delete, searching safely with SQL parameters, relations between tables, queries, aggregates, transactions and file databases."}, {"u": "tut-17-databases.html#database-words", "t": "Databases step by step", "h": "Database words", "x": "Database words # Word Meaning table a list of rows of the same shape (like a spreadsheet sheet) column one field of every row ( Title , Year ) row one record key a column that identifies a row ( Id ); the database numbers it for you foreign key a column that holds the key of a row in another table ( AuthorId ) SQL the language of databases; you only need a little of it RingDotNet uses Entity Framework Core with SQLite , a database in one file (or in memory). You describe the tables as Ring data; there is no SQL to write for normal work."}, {"u": "tut-17-databases.html#part-1-tables-rows-searches", "t": "Databases step by step", "h": "Part 1: tables, rows, searches", "x": "Part 1: tables, rows, searches # # databases step by step - a library catalog with Entity Framework Core load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"RingDotNet.Data\" } func main # STEP 1. Describe the tables: [table name, [ [column, type, isKey, options], ... ]] aSchema = [ [\"Authors\",[ [\"Id\",\"int\",1], # a key of type int is numbered by the database [\"Name\",\"string\",0,[[\"required\",1],[\"unique\",1]]] ]], [\"Books\",[ [\"Id\",\"int\",1], [\"Title\",\"string\",0,[[\"required\",1],[\"maxlength\",80]]], [\"Year\",\"int\",0,[[\"nullable\",1]]], [\"Copies\",\"int\",0,[[\"default\",1]]], [\"AuthorId\",\"int\",0,[[\"references\",\"Authors\"],[\"ondelete"}, {"u": "tut-17-databases.html#part-2-relations-queries-transactions-files", "t": "Databases step by step", "h": "Part 2: relations, queries, transactions, files", "x": "Part 2: relations, queries, transactions, files # # the tutorial \"Databases step by step\", part 2: relations, queries, transactions and a file database load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System.IO\" \"RingDotNet.Data\" } func main cFile = new Path().Combine([new Path().GetTempPath(),\"ringdotnet_library.db\"]) if new File().Exists(cFile) new File().Delete(cFile) ok aSchema = [ [\"Authors\",[[\"Id\",\"int\",1],[\"Name\",\"string\",0,[[\"required\",1]]]]], [\"Books\",[[\"Id\",\"int\",1],[\"Title\",\"string\",0],[\"Year\",\"int\",0],[\"Stars\",\"double\",0], [\"AuthorId\",\"int\",0,[[\"references\",\"Authors\"],[\"ondelete\",\"cascade\"]]]]] ] oDb ="}, {"u": "tut-17-databases.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Databases step by step", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # describe the schema as data, let the database enforce the rules, parameters, never pasted text, relations with foreign keys, queries, aggregates, transactions, files."}, {"u": "tut-17-databases.html#exercises", "t": "Databases step by step", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Add a Members table and a Loans table (a member borrows a book) and list who has which book. Write a function that lists the authors with no books. Use migrate() after adding a Pages column to the schema ( Entity Framework ). Use RingEntityDb for the same data and compare ( Entity Framework )."}, {"u": "tut-17-databases.html#next", "t": "Databases step by step", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Tabular data"}, {"u": "tut-30-tables.html", "t": "Tabular data", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-30-tables.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Tabular data", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # what CSV is and how to read it, DataTable , DataRow , DataView , Compute for sums, averages, counts, sorting and filtering without changing the data, grouping by hand and writing CSV."}, {"u": "tut-30-tables.html#csv", "t": "Tabular data", "h": "CSV", "x": "CSV # CSV (comma separated values) is the simplest table format: a header line with the column names, then one line per row with the values separated by commas. Region,Seller,Amount North,Ann,1200 South,Bob,800 Spreadsheets, databases and many programs read and write it. Note Real CSV files can contain commas inside quoted values ( \"Smith, John\" ). This tutorial's data has none, so splitting on commas is enough. For messy files use a CSV library (a NuGet package, the tutorial A library written in C# ) or a regular expression."}, {"u": "tut-30-tables.html#the-program", "t": "Tabular data", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # tabular data - read a CSV text, use a DataTable, filter, sort, summarise, write a CSV load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.Data\" \"System.IO\" } func main # STEP 1. The data: a CSV text (comma separated values): a header line and one line per row cCsv = \"Region,Seller,Amount\" + nl + \"North,Ann,1200\" + nl + \"South,Bob,800\" + nl + \"North,Cy,450\" + nl + \"East,Dee,2000\" + nl + \"South,Eve,300\" + nl + \"North,Ann,700\" # STEP 2. A DataTable has typed columns and rows oTable = new DataTable() oTable.Columns.Add([ :columnName = \"Region\", :type = \"System.String\" ]) oTable.Columns.Add"}, {"u": "tut-30-tables.html#how-it-works", "t": "Tabular data", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # Step 2: the table # A DataTable has typed columns : Region and Seller are texts, Amount is an integer ( System.Int32 ). Types matter: sums and sorting treat numbers as numbers, not as text (as text, 800 would sort after 2000 ). Rows.Add([...]) adds a row; the values are in column order. The program turns the lines into rows: str2list splits the text into lines, substr(line,\",\",nl) turns the commas into line breaks, and number() converts the amount. Step 3: Compute # Compute(expression, filter) evaluates an aggregate over the rows that pass the filter: Expression Meaning SUM(Amou"}, {"u": "tut-30-tables.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Tabular data", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # tables have typed columns, Compute summarises, DataView sorts and filters, group by with a loop, reading and writing CSV is a text job."}, {"u": "tut-30-tables.html#exercises", "t": "Tabular data", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Add the average sale per seller. Sort by region, then by amount descending ( oView.Sort = \"Region, Amount DESC\" ). Write only the rows of one region to a new CSV file. Add a calculated column Commission = Amount * 0.1 ( Columns.Add with an Expression )."}, {"u": "tut-30-tables.html#next", "t": "Tabular data", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Reports with SQL"}, {"u": "tut-73-sql-reports.html", "t": "Reports with SQL", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-73-sql-reports.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Reports with SQL", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # joining two tables with JOIN \u2026 ON , totals per group with GROUP BY and the aggregates COUNT , SUM , AVG , MIN , MAX , the difference between WHERE and HAVING , LEFT JOIN to keep rows that have no partner, a sub-query, parameters instead of pasted values."}, {"u": "tut-73-sql-reports.html#the-data", "t": "Reports with SQL", "h": "The data", "x": "The data # Two tables (the tutorial Databases step by step explains how to create them): Customers (Ann and Cy in Cairo, Bob in Oslo, Dee in Tokyo) and Orders with a CustomerId that points to the customer, a product, an amount and a month. Dee has never ordered anything \u2014 that matters in step 4."}, {"u": "tut-73-sql-reports.html#the-program", "t": "Reports with SQL", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # reports with SQL - JOIN, GROUP BY, HAVING, LEFT JOIN, sub-queries and parameters load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"RingDotNet.Data\" } func main aSchema = [ [\"Customers\",[[\"Id\",\"int\",1],[\"Name\",\"string\",0],[\"City\",\"string\",0]]], [\"Orders\",[[\"Id\",\"int\",1],[\"CustomerId\",\"int\",0,[[\"references\",\"Customers\"]]],[\"Product\",\"string\",0],[\"Amount\",\"int\",0],[\"Month\",\"string\",0]]] ] oDb = new RingDatabase(\"Data Source=:memory:\",aSchema) nAnn = oDb.insert(\"Customers\",[[\"Name\",\"Ann\"],[\"City\",\"Cairo\"]]) nBob = oDb.insert(\"Customers\",[[\"Name\",\"Bob\"],[\"City\",\"Oslo\"]]) nCy = oDb.insert(\"Customers\",[[\"N"}, {"u": "tut-73-sql-reports.html#how-it-works", "t": "Reports with SQL", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # oDb.sqlRows(sql, parameters) runs any SELECT and returns the rows as lists of [column, value] pairs; dbValue(row, \"Total\") reads one column. The show helper prints a few columns as a small table. 1. JOIN # FROM Orders o JOIN Customers c ON c.Id = o.CustomerId puts together each order and the customer whose Id equals its CustomerId . o and c are short aliases of the tables. The result has one row per order, with the customer's name next to it. 2. GROUP BY # GROUP BY c.Name makes one row per customer ; COUNT(o.Id) and SUM(o.Amount) are calculated over the orders of that customer. "}, {"u": "tut-73-sql-reports.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Reports with SQL", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # JOIN , LEFT JOIN , GROUP BY with aggregates, WHERE against HAVING , sub-queries and scalar , parameters against injection."}, {"u": "tut-73-sql-reports.html#exercises", "t": "Reports with SQL", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Show the average order per customer, rounded. List the products that were never ordered by a customer from Cairo. Find the month with the highest total. Add a Products table with prices and join it to the orders."}, {"u": "tut-73-sql-reports.html#next", "t": "Reports with SQL", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Entity objects and migrations"}, {"u": "tut-27-entities.html", "t": "Entity objects and migrations", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-27-entities.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Entity objects and migrations", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # the object style of Entity Framework: RingEntityDb , navigation properties and include , tracked objects and save() , migrations: add columns, tables and indexes to an existing file, named, run-once changes with apply ."}, {"u": "tut-27-entities.html#two-styles-one-schema", "t": "Entity objects and migrations", "h": "Two styles, one schema", "x": "Two styles, one schema # The tutorial Databases step by step used RingDatabase : a row is a list of [column, value] pairs. RingEntityDb uses the same schema but turns each table into a real .NET class. A row is an object; related rows are properties of the object. RingDatabase RingEntityDb a row [[\"Name\",\"Ann\"],[\"Goals\",5]] an object: oPlayer.getProp(\"Name\") a related row parent(...) , children(...) oPlayer.getProp(\"Team\") , oTeam.getProp(\"Players\") changing update(table, key, values) oPlayer.setProp(\"Goals\",8) then save() best for scripts, quick tools programs that work with objects for a whi"}, {"u": "tut-27-entities.html#part-1-objects", "t": "Entity objects and migrations", "h": "Part 1: objects", "x": "Part 1: objects # # the tutorial \"Entity objects and migrations\", part 1: entity objects - the rows of a table as real objects with navigation properties load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"RingDotNet.Data\" } func main # The schema is the same as with RingDatabase. RingEntityDb turns every table into a .NET class. oDb = new RingEntityDb(\"Data Source=:memory:\",[ [\"Teams\",[[\"Id\",\"int\",1],[\"Name\",\"string\",0,[[\"required\",1]]]]], [\"Players\",[[\"Id\",\"int\",1],[\"Name\",\"string\",0],[\"Goals\",\"int\",0,[[\"default\",0]]],[\"TeamId\",\"int\",0,[[\"references\",\"Teams\"]]]]] ]) # The generated class has a property for every co"}, {"u": "tut-27-entities.html#part-2-migrations", "t": "Entity objects and migrations", "h": "Part 2: migrations", "x": "Part 2: migrations # You shipped version 1 of a program. Users have data. In version 2 you need a Phone column and a Notes table. If you only change the schema in the program, the old file still has the old tables : new code meets old data. A migration brings the file up to date. # the tutorial \"Entity objects and migrations\", part 2: changing a database that already has data - migrations load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System.IO\" \"RingDotNet.Data\" } func main cFile = new Path().Combine([new Path().GetTempPath(),\"ringdotnet_migrations.db\"]) if new File().Exists(cFile) new File().Delete(cFile) ok c"}, {"u": "tut-27-entities.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Entity objects and migrations", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # objects and navigation properties, include loads related objects, change a property, save() , migrate() for schema changes, apply() for one-time data changes."}, {"u": "tut-27-entities.html#exercises", "t": "Entity objects and migrations", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Add a Matches table (two teams, a score) and print every match with both team names. Move a player to another team by changing the Team navigation and saving. Add a Email column in version 3 and write the migration steps. Count the goals of each team with a for loop over query(\"Teams\", [[\"include\",[\"Players\"]]]) ."}, {"u": "tut-27-entities.html#next", "t": "Entity objects and migrations", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Microsoft Access databases"}, {"u": "tut-115-access.html", "t": "Microsoft Access databases", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-115-access.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Microsoft Access databases", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # what is needed to use Access from Ring (and why it works only on Windows), making a new .accdb file (ADOX) and connecting with OdbcConnection , the Access column types and SQL dialect, parameters ( ? ) instead of text joined into a statement, reading rows with a reader, joins, GROUP BY , TOP , LIKE , dates, transactions, closing, the limits of Access and when to choose another database. The tutorial Databases step by step used SQLite and the tutorial Reports with SQL wrote reports with SQL. The ideas are the same; only the driver and a few details of the SQL change."}, {"u": "tut-115-access.html#what-you-need", "t": "Microsoft Access databases", "h": "What you need", "x": "What you need # Need Why Windows the Access drivers exist only there the Microsoft Access Database Engine , 64-bit it brings the ODBC driver Microsoft Access Driver (\\ .mdb, \\ .accdb) and the OLEDB provider. It is installed with Microsoft Access or Microsoft 365, or from the free \"Access Database Engine Redistributable\" System.Data.Odbc.dll the .NET classes for ODBC. They are a NuGet package, not part of the runtime; the RingDotNet package has the file in its bin folder and the program loads it with dnLoad The bitness must match. Ring and the .NET runtime here are 64-bit, so the driver must be"}, {"u": "tut-115-access.html#the-program", "t": "Microsoft Access databases", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # Microsoft Access databases - an .accdb file through ODBC: tables, parameters, joins, totals, dates, transactions # (Windows only: it needs the Microsoft Access Database Engine; anywhere else the program says why it stops) load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System.Data.Odbc\" \"Microsoft.Win32\" \"System\" \"System.IO\" } func main # 0. What the program needs. Without it, it stops with a message and no error. cDriver = \"Microsoft Access Driver (*.mdb, *.accdb)\" if not iswindows() ? \"This tutorial needs Windows: the Microsoft Access drivers exist only there.\" return but not driverInstalled(cDr"}, {"u": "tut-115-access.html#how-it-works", "t": "Microsoft Access databases", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # A new file # The ODBC driver opens Access files but cannot create one. ADOX can: it is a COM component that comes with Access, and Catalog.Create(connection string) makes an empty .accdb . The program reaches it through reflection: Type.GetTypeFromProgID(\"ADOX.Catalog\") , Activator.CreateInstance , and Type.InvokeMember(\"Create\", ...) with the connection string of the OLEDB provider ( Provider=Microsoft.ACE.OLEDB.16.0;Data Source=file ). If you already have an Access file, skip this step and just open it. The connection # Driver={Microsoft Access Driver (*.mdb, *.accdb)};Dbq=C:\\"}, {"u": "tut-115-access.html#limits-and-advice", "t": "Microsoft Access databases", "h": "Limits and advice", "x": "Limits and advice # Size and users. An Access file is limited to 2 GB and is meant for a few users on a fast local disk; there is no server, every program opens the file itself. It is not a good choice for a web site with many visitors or for a file on a slow network share. Backups. The file is the database: copy it when no program has it open (or after compact and repair , which the JRO.JetEngine COM object does). Old files. .mdb files of Access 97 or 2000 may need the older Jet engine, which has no 64-bit version; open them in Access and save them as .accdb . Moving on. When the data grows o"}, {"u": "tut-115-access.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Microsoft Access databases", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # Access needs Windows, the 64-bit Access Database Engine and System.Data.Odbc.dll , ADOX makes a new file; ODBC opens it, the Access column types and the small differences of its SQL, parameters instead of text in statements, readers, joins, grouping, dates and transactions, the limits of Access."}, {"u": "tut-115-access.html#exercises", "t": "Microsoft Access databases", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Add a table Products and a table OrderLines and make a report of the quantity sold for each product. Write insertMany(oConnection, cSql, aRows) that inserts a list of rows inside one transaction, and compare its time with single inserts for 1000 rows (the tutorial Speed \u2014 measure first ). Write export(oConnection, cTable, cFile) that writes a table as JSON (the tutorial Writing JSON as a stream ). Open an existing .accdb file of your own and list its tables and columns."}, {"u": "tut-115-access.html#next", "t": "Microsoft Access databases", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Settings"}, {"u": "tut-32-settings.html", "t": "Settings", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-32-settings.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Settings", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # the special folders of the user, defaults, loading and saving settings, a Ring class that hides the details, what to do when the file is missing or damaged."}, {"u": "tut-32-settings.html#where-do-settings-live", "t": "Settings", "h": "Where do settings live?", "x": "Where do settings live? # The system has a folder for every kind of file; .NET finds them with Environment.GetFolderPath : Name For MyDocuments files the user creates ApplicationData settings that follow the user to other computers LocalApplicationData settings and caches of this computer Desktop the desktop A program normally keeps its settings in a file inside its own folder under ApplicationData , for example \u2026\\ApplicationData\\MyProgram\\settings.json . The tutorial uses the temp folder so that it leaves nothing behind."}, {"u": "tut-32-settings.html#the-design", "t": "Settings", "h": "The design", "x": "The design # Rule Why every setting has a default the first run, and a missing key, work the file is JSON , readable by people users and support can read and edit it loading never fails the program a damaged file must not stop the application saving happens when the user changes something a crash does not lose the choices"}, {"u": "tut-32-settings.html#the-program", "t": "Settings", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # settings - defaults, a JSON file, loading, saving, and special folders load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.IO\" \"System.Text.Json\" } func main # Where do programs keep their files? .NET knows the special folders of the user. oEnvironment = new Environment() ? \"Special folders exist:\" for cFolder in [\"MyDocuments\",\"ApplicationData\",\"LocalApplicationData\",\"Desktop\"] ? \" \" + cFolder + \" : \" + (len(oEnvironment.GetFolderPath(cFolder)) > 0) next # For this tutorial the file is in the temp folder cFile = new Path().Combine([new Path().GetTempPath(),\"ringdotnet_settings-setting"}, {"u": "tut-32-settings.html#how-it-works", "t": "Settings", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # A class for settings # Everything about settings is in one Ring class, Settings , so the rest of the program only says oSettings.getValue(\"name\") and oSettings.setValue(\"fontSize\",14) . The class keeps a list of [key, value] pairs: its starting content is the list of defaults , so the keys that exist are defined in one place. Saving # save writes a JSON object by hand: texts in quotes, numbers without. For real data with arbitrary text use JsonSerializer (the tutorial JSON and XML ), which also escapes quotes and line breaks. Loading # readFile first checks that the file exists."}, {"u": "tut-32-settings.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Settings", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # the user's special folders, defaults first, then the file on top, unknown and missing keys are fine, loading must never crash the program."}, {"u": "tut-32-settings.html#exercises", "t": "Settings", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Move the file to ApplicationData\\TutorialApp\\settings.json (create the folder if needed with Directory.CreateDirectory ). Add a recentFiles setting that holds a list of up to five names. Make setValue refuse an unknown key with an error. Use the settings in a window: remember its position and size, and restore them when it opens (the tutorial A small text editor )."}, {"u": "tut-32-settings.html#next", "t": "Settings", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Configuration"}, {"u": "tut-111-configuration.html", "t": "Configuration", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-111-configuration.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Configuration", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # the idea of layered configuration (the last source wins), keys as paths: App:Port , ConfigurationBuilder and the sources: JSON file, memory, environment variables, GetValue with a type and a default, sections ( GetSection , GetChildren ), where secrets belong. The tutorial Settings saved the settings of a program in a file. This is the system that ASP.NET Core and most .NET programs use, and it works the same way for a web service and for a console tool. Note The configuration providers (JSON, environment variables) are part of the ASP.NET Core runtime, so the program has"}, {"u": "tut-111-configuration.html#the-program", "t": "Configuration", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # configuration - settings from a JSON file, from memory and from environment variables, layered so that the last one wins load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.IO\" \"Microsoft.Extensions.Configuration\" \"RingDotNet.Web\" # the configuration providers are part of the ASP.NET Core runtime: this line starts it } func main # A program needs settings that are not in its code: a port, a name, a path, the address of a database. The configuration system of # .NET reads them from SEVERAL sources and puts them into one tree of keys. Sources added later override sources added earlier: #"}, {"u": "tut-111-configuration.html#how-it-works", "t": "Configuration", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # Layers # A ConfigurationBuilder collects sources ; Build() reads them and gives the configuration. The sources are added in order and later sources override earlier ones . So the usual setup is: the defaults in a JSON file that comes with the program, then the settings of one computer or one environment (testing or production), then environment variables, then the command line. The code that reads a value does not know or care which source gave it. Every source is added by an extension method that the builder has as a method (the tutorial Caching ). Each has several forms, so th"}, {"u": "tut-111-configuration.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Configuration", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # configuration comes from layers, the last one wins, keys are paths with : , values are text, AddJsonFile , AddInMemoryCollection and AddEnvironmentVariables , GetValue with a type and a default, sections and children, secrets do not belong in the code."}, {"u": "tut-111-configuration.html#exercises", "t": "Configuration", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Add a second JSON file with the name settings.local.json that overrides some keys, and add it after the first. Print the whole tree (every key and value) with a recursive function that uses GetChildren . Write setting(cKey, xDefault) that reads a value and keeps the default when the key is missing. Read the command line ( AddCommandLine with sysargv , the tutorial A command-line tool ) as the last source."}, {"u": "tut-111-configuration.html#next", "t": "Configuration", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Caching"}, {"u": "tut-109-memory-cache.html", "t": "Caching", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-109-memory-cache.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Caching", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # what a cache is and when it helps, MemoryCache : Set , TryGetValue , Remove , Clear , Count , absolute and sliding expiration, GetOrCreate : make the value only when it is missing, extension methods, which are methods of the cache, the rules that keep a cache safe. The tutorial Speed \u2014 measure first measured how fast calls are. The fastest call is the one you do not make: a result that was already calculated, fetched or read can be reused."}, {"u": "tut-109-memory-cache.html#the-program", "t": "Caching", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # caching - MemoryCache, absolute and sliding expiration, remove and update, and when a cache is a bad idea load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.Threading\" \"Microsoft.Extensions.Caching.Memory\" } nRuns = 0 func main # A cache keeps the result of slow work (a database query, a web request, a big calculation) for a while, so the next request # gets it at once. MemoryCache keeps the values in memory, with a key for each one and rules for when a value must go. oCache = new MemoryCache(new MemoryCacheOptions()) # 1. Set stores a value under a key. TryGetValue answers [ found, v"}, {"u": "tut-109-memory-cache.html#how-it-works", "t": "Caching", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # The cache # MemoryCache (namespace Microsoft.Extensions.Caching.Memory ) is a dictionary with rules. Each value has a key (a text such as \"user:1\" ) and may have an expiration. TryGetValue(key) answers [ found, value ] (the tutorial Network tools explains the out value). A cache is made with options ( MemoryCacheOptions ), for example a size limit; the defaults are fine for this lesson. Extension methods # Set and GetOrCreate are extension methods of the static class CacheExtensions , not methods of MemoryCache itself, but the class MemoryCache has them, so they are called on th"}, {"u": "tut-109-memory-cache.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Caching", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # a cache is a dictionary with expiration rules, Set and GetOrCreate are extension methods, called as methods of the cache, absolute expiration for stale data, sliding expiration for data in use, GetOrCreate with a Ring function as the factory, remove entries when the data changes, and know what not to cache."}, {"u": "tut-109-memory-cache.html#exercises", "t": "Caching", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Cache the result of a slow function (the tutorial Speed \u2014 measure first ) and measure the time of the first and the second call. Combine a sliding and an absolute expiration for a session. Count how often GetOrCreate found the value (a hit ) and how often it made it (a miss ) and print the hit rate. Set a size limit ( MemoryCacheOptions.SizeLimit ) and see what happens when entries are added without a size."}, {"u": "tut-109-memory-cache.html#next", "t": "Caching", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Dependency injection"}, {"u": "tut-110-dependency-injection.html", "t": "Dependency injection", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-110-dependency-injection.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Dependency injection", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # what dependency injection (DI) is and why programs use it, ServiceCollection and the service provider, the three lifetimes : singleton, transient, scoped, scopes ( CreateScope ) and what one request means, factories that ask the provider for other services, the rules that avoid mistakes. Every ASP.NET Core program is built on this container, so what you learn here is what a web program (the tutorial A web service with a database ) is using inside."}, {"u": "tut-110-dependency-injection.html#the-program", "t": "Dependency injection", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # dependency injection - a service container, the three lifetimes (singleton, transient, scoped) and services that need other services load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.Collections\" \"System.IO\" \"Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection\" } nSingletons = 0 nTransients = 0 nScoped = 0 func main # Dependency injection (DI) means: a part of a program does not create the things it needs, it ASKS for them. A container makes them, # and decides how long each one lives. That makes the parts easy to replace and to test, and it is how every ASP.NET program is built. # The containe"}, {"u": "tut-110-dependency-injection.html#how-it-works", "t": "Dependency injection", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # The idea # Without DI, a function that sends mail creates its own mail client, and a test of that function sends real mail. With DI, the function asks the container for a mail client; the program registers the real one, and a test registers a fake one. The code that uses a service does not know how it is made, and the code that makes it does not know who uses it. The container is a ServiceCollection (the list of registrations); BuildServiceProvider turns it into a provider that makes the objects. A service is asked for with its type: oProvider.GetService(type) . The keys are .NE"}, {"u": "tut-110-dependency-injection.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Dependency injection", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # DI: a part asks for what it needs, a container makes it, ServiceCollection , BuildServiceProvider , GetService , singleton, transient and scoped lifetimes and scopes, Ring functions as factories, a factory can ask the provider for other services, the captive dependency mistake."}, {"u": "tut-110-dependency-injection.html#exercises", "t": "Dependency injection", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Register a fourth service and print how many times its factory ran for 10 requests (10 scopes). Make a scope, get a scoped MemoryStream from it, write to it and dispose the scope. Register the same type twice and see which one GetService returns (the last one). Look up GetServices (many) and GetRequiredService (an error when missing) in the class reference ."}, {"u": "tut-110-dependency-injection.html#where-to-go-next", "t": "Dependency injection", "h": "Where to go next", "x": "Where to go next # This was the last tutorial of the chapter Databases and services . The next chapter is Windows Forms : it starts with Your first window . The Tutorials page lists all the chapters, and you can start any of them."}, {"u": "tut-11-first-window.html", "t": "Your first window", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-11-first-window.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Your first window", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # the parts of a Windows Forms program: runtime, form, controls, events, message loop, the helper functions uiForm , uiLabel , uiButton , uiRun , why event code can only see global variables, what the helpers do, by writing the program without them."}, {"u": "tut-11-first-window.html#windows-forms-in-one-minute", "t": "Your first window", "h": "Windows Forms in one minute", "x": "Windows Forms in one minute # Windows Forms is the classic way to build desktop windows in .NET. A window is a Form . Things in it \u2014 labels, buttons, text boxes \u2014 are controls . A control is an object with properties ( Text , Location , Size ) and events ( Click ). The program runs a message loop : it waits for the user to do something, runs your code, and waits again until the window closes."}, {"u": "tut-11-first-window.html#step-1-the-smallest-window", "t": "Your first window", "h": "Step 1: the smallest window", "x": "Step 1: the smallest window # # Your first window, step 1: the smallest window load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System.Drawing\" \"System.Windows.Forms\" } oForm = uiForm(\"My first window\",320,120) uiRun(oForm) # show the window and wait until the user closes it Step 1: a window with a title The using { } block comes first . Because it names System.Windows.Forms , it starts the Windows desktop runtime and makes the Ring thread suitable for windows (this is what dnUseDesktop() does). uiForm(\"My first window\",320,120) makes a form with a title and a client area of 320 \u00d7 120 pixels, centered on the scree"}, {"u": "tut-11-first-window.html#step-2-a-label-and-a-button", "t": "Your first window", "h": "Step 2: a label and a button", "x": "Step 2: a label and a button # # Your first window, step 2: a label and a button load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System.Drawing\" \"System.Windows.Forms\" } oForm = uiForm(\"Click counter\",320,120) oLabel = uiLabel(oForm,\"The button was not clicked yet\",20,15,280,24) # parent, text, x, y, width, height oButton = uiButton(oForm,\"Click me\",20,55,120,32,\"\") # no code yet for the click uiRun(oForm) Step 2: controls uiLabel(parent, text, x, y, width, height) and uiButton(parent, text, x, y, width, height, code) create a control and put it into the form. The position x, y is measured in pixels from the top-"}, {"u": "tut-11-first-window.html#step-3-the-button-does-something", "t": "Your first window", "h": "Step 3: the button does something", "x": "Step 3: the button does something # # Your first window, step 3: the button does something - events and global variables load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System.Drawing\" \"System.Windows.Forms\" } # The code of an event runs later, outside this part of the program: it can only see GLOBAL variables. # So the controls and the counter are global variables, created here at the top. oForm = \"\" oLabel = \"\" nClicks = 0 func main oForm = uiForm(\"Click counter\",320,120) oLabel = uiLabel(oForm,\"The button was not clicked yet\",20,15,280,24) uiButton(oForm,\"Click me\",20,55,120,32,\"clicked()\") # the click calls t"}, {"u": "tut-11-first-window.html#step-4-without-the-helpers", "t": "Your first window", "h": "Step 4: without the helpers", "x": "Step 4: without the helpers # The helpers are only short-cuts. This program builds the same window with the real classes, so you can see what is going on: # Your first window, step 4: the same window without the helper functions - what uiForm, uiLabel and uiButton do load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System.Drawing\" \"System.Windows.Forms\" } oForm = \"\" oLabel = \"\" oButton = \"\" nClicks = 0 func main # every control is an object of a class, configured with properties inside braces oForm = new Form() { Text = \"Click counter (no helpers)\" ClientSize = new Size(320,120) StartPosition = \"CenterScreen\" # an"}, {"u": "tut-11-first-window.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Your first window", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # Idea Example start the desktop runtime first using { \"System.Windows.Forms\" } a form and controls uiForm , uiLabel , uiButton positions and sizes in pixels 20, 15, 280, 24 an event is Ring code in a text \"clicked()\" events see only globals oLabel , nClicks at the top the same without helpers new Form() { ... }"}, {"u": "tut-11-first-window.html#exercises", "t": "Your first window", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Add a third button, Add 10 , that adds ten to the counter. Make the label red when the counter is above 5 ( oLabel.ForeColor = new Color().Red ). Add a second label that shows even or odd . Change the form so that it cannot be resized ( FormBorderStyle = \"FixedDialog\" , MaximizeBox = False )."}, {"u": "tut-11-first-window.html#next", "t": "Your first window", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # A form with many controls"}, {"u": "tut-12-controls.html", "t": "A form with many controls", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-12-controls.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "A form with many controls", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # the common controls and their main properties, reading and changing what the user entered, validating input and giving feedback, finding controls in Controls for tests."}, {"u": "tut-12-controls.html#the-common-controls", "t": "A form with many controls", "h": "The common controls", "x": "The common controls # Control The user... Main property Label reads text Text TextBox types text Text NumericUpDown picks a number Value , Minimum , Maximum ComboBox picks one item of a drop-down list SelectedIndex , Text CheckBox switches something on or off Checked ListBox sees (and picks from) a list Items , SelectedIndex Button clicks Text , event Click"}, {"u": "tut-12-controls.html#the-program", "t": "A form with many controls", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # a form with many controls - text boxes, check box, combo box, list box, validation load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System.Drawing\" \"System.Windows.Forms\" } # controls that the event code uses are global variables oForm = \"\" oName = \"\" oAge = \"\" oCountry = \"\" oNewsletter = \"\" oResult = \"\" oPeople = \"\" func main oForm = uiForm(\"Registration\",400,330) uiLabel(oForm,\"Name\",20,20,80,22) oName = uiTextBox(oForm,110,18,260,24) uiLabel(oForm,\"Age\",20,56,80,22) oAge = new NumericUpDown() { Location = new Point(110,54) Size = new Size(80,24) Minimum = 1 Maximum = 120 Value = 30 } oForm.Contr"}, {"u": "tut-12-controls.html#how-it-works", "t": "A form with many controls", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # Creating the controls # The helpers uiTextBox , uiComboBox , uiCheckBox and uiListBox create the control and add it to the form. uiComboBox(oForm, x, y, width, aItems) fills the drop-down list from a Ring list. A NumericUpDown has no helper, so it is created with the class and configured in braces: oAge = new NumericUpDown() { Location = new Point(110,54) Size = new Size(80,24) Minimum = 1 Maximum = 120 Value = 30 } oForm.Controls.Add(oAge) The control only appears in the window after oForm.Controls.Add(...) . Reading what was entered # Question Code what was typed? oName.Text w"}, {"u": "tut-12-controls.html#what-you-learned", "t": "A form with many controls", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # controls are objects; read and write their properties, Items , Checked , Value , SelectedIndex for the common ones, validate first, then act, PerformClick() lets a test press a button."}, {"u": "tut-12-controls.html#exercises", "t": "A form with many controls", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Clear the age and country after a registration. Refuse ages under 18 with a message. Add a RadioButton pair ( Male / Female ) in a GroupBox and add the choice to the line. Add a Remove button that deletes the selected person ( oPeople.Items.RemoveAt(...) )."}, {"u": "tut-12-controls.html#next", "t": "A form with many controls", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # More input controls"}, {"u": "tut-128-inputs.html", "t": "More input controls", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-128-inputs.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "More input controls", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # DateTimePicker : the date, the limits and the format, TrackBar : a slider with whole numbers and its change event, RadioButton and GroupBox : one choice out of a few, ToolTip : text that appears when the mouse rests on a control, ErrorProvider : an error icon with a message next to a control, keeping the price in one function that the controls call. The tutorial A form with many controls used text boxes, a combo box and check boxes. These are the other controls that you find in most forms."}, {"u": "tut-128-inputs.html#the-program", "t": "More input controls", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # more input controls - a date picker, a track bar, radio buttons, tool tips and an error marker (a booking form) load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.Drawing\" \"System.Windows.Forms\" } # controls that the event code uses are global variables oForm = \"\" oName = \"\" oDate = \"\" oNights = \"\" oNightsLabel = \"\" oSingle = \"\" oDouble = \"\" oSuite = \"\" oPrice = \"\" oErrors = \"\" oTips = \"\" oResult = \"\" oBook = \"\" func main oForm = uiForm(\"Booking\",420,330) uiLabel(oForm,\"Name\",20,20,80,22) oName = uiTextBox(oForm,110,18,270,24) # a date picker: Value is a DateTime. MinDate and MaxDate "}, {"u": "tut-128-inputs.html#how-it-works", "t": "More input controls", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # The date picker # DateTimePicker.Value is a DateTime . MinDate and MaxDate limit what the user can choose (here the year 2030), and the value is always kept inside them. Format = \"Short\" shows the date in the short form of the user's culture; Long , Time and Custom (with CustomFormat ) are the others. To make a date the program uses new DateTime(2030,5,10) , and to show it Value.Format(\"d MMMM yyyy\") (the tutorial Dates and times ). The track bar # A TrackBar is a slider: Minimum , Maximum , Value and TickFrequency (the distance between the small marks). setValueChangedEvent(\"sh"}, {"u": "tut-128-inputs.html#what-you-learned", "t": "More input controls", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # the date picker with limits and formats, the track bar and its change event, radio buttons in a group box, tool tips and the error marker, a price that is calculated in one function."}, {"u": "tut-128-inputs.html#exercises", "t": "More input controls", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Add a check box Breakfast that adds 8 per night. Make the date picker choose the departure and show the number of nights from the arrival date. Show a tool tip on each radio button with the price of the room. Disable Book while the name is empty (the event TextChanged of the text box)."}, {"u": "tut-128-inputs.html#next", "t": "More input controls", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # The keyboard in a form"}, {"u": "tut-146-keyboard.html", "t": "The keyboard in a form", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-146-keyboard.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "The keyboard in a form", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # the KeyPress event: KeyChar and Handled , MaxLength , AcceptButton and CancelButton : the buttons of the Enter and Escape keys, the tab order ( TabIndex ), access keys : the & in a label, selecting all the text when a box gets the focus ( Enter event, SelectAll ), typing keys in a test with uiSendKeys . The tutorial Drawing and animation used KeyDown for a game. A data form uses the keyboard in other ways."}, {"u": "tut-146-keyboard.html#the-program", "t": "The keyboard in a form", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # the keyboard in a form - KeyPress to allow only digits, the Enter and Escape buttons, the tab order, the access keys and select-all load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System.Drawing\" \"System.Windows.Forms\" } # controls and state that the event code uses are global variables oForm = \"\" oName = \"\" oAge = \"\" oOk = \"\" oCancel = \"\" oResult = \"\" nRejected = 0 # how many keys the age box refused lClosed = False func main oForm = uiForm(\"Keyboard\",360,230) # An ACCESS KEY: the & in front of a letter underlines it, and Alt + that letter moves the focus to the NEXT control in the tab order # (a"}, {"u": "tut-146-keyboard.html#how-it-works", "t": "The keyboard in a form", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # Only digits # KeyPress runs for each character that the user types, before it reaches the box. The event argument ( aDotNetArgs[2] , passed to filterKey ) has KeyChar , the character, and Handled : when the function sets Handled = True the character is thrown away. The function accepts the digits (codes 48 to 57) and the Backspace key (code 8) and refuses all the rest. This filter works for typing; text that is pasted does not go through KeyPress , so a real program also checks the value at the end (the Validating event, or the check in the OK button). MaxLength # MaxLength = 3 "}, {"u": "tut-146-keyboard.html#what-you-learned", "t": "The keyboard in a form", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # filtering the characters of a box, Enter and Escape buttons, the tab order and access keys, select-all on entering, testing with real key presses."}, {"u": "tut-146-keyboard.html#exercises", "t": "The keyboard in a form", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Allow one decimal point in a box for prices, but only one. Change the access keys: &Name and N&ame are different, and two labels with the same key move through the controls in turn. Make the OK button disabled until both boxes have text. Use the Validating event of the age box to refuse a value over 150."}, {"u": "tut-146-keyboard.html#next", "t": "The keyboard in a form", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Layout and richer controls"}, {"u": "tut-23-layout.html", "t": "Layout and richer controls", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-23-layout.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Layout and richer controls", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # the layout tools: Dock , SplitContainer , TabControl , TableLayoutPanel , DataGridView showing a DataTable through a DataView , connecting controls with events, two layout details that are worth knowing before you start."}, {"u": "tut-23-layout.html#layout-in-windows-forms", "t": "Layout and richer controls", "h": "Layout in Windows Forms", "x": "Layout in Windows Forms # Placing controls with x and y numbers (as in Your first window ) is fine for a small fixed window. A window that the user can resize needs layout containers : Tool What it does Dock glue a control to an edge ( Top , Bottom , Left , Right ) or fill the rest ( Fill ) SplitContainer two panels with a bar the user can drag TabControl / TabPage pages with tabs TableLayoutPanel a grid of rows and columns FlowLayoutPanel controls placed one after the other, wrapping Anchor keep the distance to the edges when the window grows"}, {"u": "tut-23-layout.html#the-program", "t": "Layout and richer controls", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # layout and richer controls - SplitContainer, TabControl, TableLayoutPanel, DataGridView with a filter load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System.Data\" \"System.Drawing\" \"System.Windows.Forms\" } # the controls that event code uses oForm = \"\" oList = \"\" oNameValue = \"\" oCityValue = \"\" oCountValue = \"\" oGrid = \"\" oView = \"\" aCustomers = [[\"Ann\",\"Cairo\"],[\"Bob\",\"Oslo\"],[\"Cy\",\"Tokyo\"]] func main oForm = uiForm(\"Customers\",620,300) # A SplitContainer has two panels with a bar between them that the user can drag. oSplit = new SplitContainer() { Dock = \"Fill\" } oForm.Controls.Add(oSplit) oSplit"}, {"u": "tut-23-layout.html#how-it-works", "t": "Layout and richer controls", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # SplitContainer # A SplitContainer has Panel1 (left) and Panel2 (right). You add controls to the panels: oSplit.Panel1.Controls.Add(oList) . Give each child Dock = \"Fill\" so that it follows its panel. Warning Set SplitterDistance after the container was added to the form. Before that, the container has its default size (200 pixels) and the position is relative to that: the bar jumps when the form's real size arrives. Tabs # Create a TabPage for each tab, set its Text (the title on the tab), and add the pages to oTabs.TabPages . The page is a container: add its controls to oDetail"}, {"u": "tut-23-layout.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Layout and richer controls", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # containers instead of pixel positions, Dock , SplitContainer , TabControl , TableLayoutPanel , DataTable \u2192 DataView \u2192 DataGridView , set the splitter after adding it, ask the view for counts."}, {"u": "tut-23-layout.html#exercises", "t": "Layout and richer controls", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Add a FlowLayoutPanel at the bottom with three buttons that wrap when the window is narrow. Sort the grid by quantity when the user clicks a button ( oView.Sort = \"Quantity DESC\" ). Add a text box that filters the customer list by what the user types. Add a third tab with a PictureBox that shows the picture made in the tutorial Images ."}, {"u": "tut-23-layout.html#next", "t": "Layout and richer controls", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # A list with columns"}, {"u": "tut-134-listview.html", "t": "A list with columns", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-134-listview.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "A list with columns", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # ListView in the details view, with Columns , rows ( ListViewItem ) and their cells ( SubItems ), FullRowSelect , GridLines and CheckBoxes , the event of a click on a column header and its arguments, sorting: the data is a Ring list, the view only shows it, comparing text with strcmp , removing the checked rows. The tutorial A folder browser filled a ListView with files. This lesson makes the list work: sorting, checking and removing."}, {"u": "tut-134-listview.html#the-program", "t": "A list with columns", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # a list with columns - ListView in details view: columns, check boxes, sorting by a column header, removing the checked rows load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System.Drawing\" \"System.Windows.Forms\" } # controls and data that the event code uses are global variables oForm = \"\" oList = \"\" oStatus = \"\" aTasks = [ [\"Write the report\",2,\"2030-03-14\"], [\"Call the bank\",1,\"2030-03-11\"], [\"Buy tea\",3,\"2030-03-12\"], [\"Book the room\",1,\"2030-03-20\"], [\"Back up the files\",2,\"2030-03-09\"] ] nSortColumn = -1 # no column yet lAscending = True func main oForm = uiForm(\"Tasks\",520,330) oList = new Li"}, {"u": "tut-134-listview.html#how-it-works", "t": "A list with columns", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # Columns and rows # View = \"Details\" shows the columns. Columns.Add([ :text = \"Task\", :width = 220 ]) adds a column with a title. A row is new ListViewItem().@New([ :text = cName ]) : its text is the first cell, and SubItems.Add(text) adds the others. Items.Add(item) puts the row into the list. Items.Item(0) is the first row (counted from 0) and SubItems.Item(2) its third cell. CheckBoxes = True puts a check box in front of each row, and Checked is true when it is on. The data is the list # The program keeps the tasks in a Ring list aTasks , and fillList clears the ListView and c"}, {"u": "tut-134-listview.html#what-you-learned", "t": "A list with columns", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # the details view with columns, cells and check boxes, the header click event and aDotNetArgs , a Ring list as the data and a view that only shows it, sorting with strcmp , removing the checked rows."}, {"u": "tut-134-listview.html#exercises", "t": "A list with columns", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Show a small arrow in the title of the sorted column ( oList.Columns.Item(0).Text = \"Task \u25b2\" ). Add a text box that filters the rows: only tasks that contain the typed text are shown (keep the full list in aTasks and fill the view from a filtered copy). Show a message with the selected task when the user double-clicks a row (the event DoubleClick and SelectedItems ). Use sort(aTasks, 2) of Ring for the sort by priority and compare the code with sortBy ."}, {"u": "tut-134-listview.html#next", "t": "A list with columns", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Editing a grid"}, {"u": "tut-142-grid.html", "t": "Editing a grid", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-142-grid.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Editing a grid", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # columns with types : DataGridViewTextBoxColumn , ...CheckBoxColumn , ...ComboBoxColumn , adding rows with the values of the cells ( Rows.Add ), reading and writing a cell: Rows.Item(row).Cells.Item(column).Value , the CellValueChanged event and RowIndex , committing a check box or a combo box change at once, a read-only column that the program fills, a number that arrives as text, and a text that is not a number. The tutorial Layout and richer controls showed a grid that is bound to a DataTable . Here the grid is unbound : it holds its own cells, and the program works wit"}, {"u": "tut-142-grid.html#the-program", "t": "Editing a grid", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # editing a grid - DataGridView with typed columns (text, check box, combo box), a read-only total, cell events and a grand total load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.Drawing\" \"System.Windows.Forms\" } # controls that the event code uses are global variables oForm = \"\" oGrid = \"\" oSum = \"\" func main oForm = uiForm(\"Order\",560,320) oGrid = new DataGridView() oGrid.Location = new Point(15,15) oGrid.Size = new Size(520,200) oGrid.AllowUserToAddRows = False oGrid.RowHeadersVisible = False # The columns have TYPES: a text column, a check box column, a combo box column. The user "}, {"u": "tut-142-grid.html#how-it-works", "t": "Editing a grid", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # Columns with types # Each column is an object that is added to oGrid.Columns . A text box column lets the user type; a check box column shows a check box in each cell; a combo box column shows a drop-down list with the items you add to the column ( Items.Add ). Name is how the program finds the column, HeaderText is the title, and ReadOnly = True makes the column not editable: the Total column is only filled by the program. Rows and cells # Rows.Add([ \"Pens\", 10, 2.5, False, \"Small\", \"\" ]) adds a row, with one value for each column in the order of the columns. A cell is found by"}, {"u": "tut-142-grid.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Editing a grid", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # typed columns and read-only columns, adding rows, reading and writing cells, CellValueChanged and committing edits, converting cell values safely, a grand total that follows the changes."}, {"u": "tut-142-grid.html#exercises", "t": "Editing a grid", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Add a button Delete row that removes the selected row ( Rows.RemoveAt ) and updates the sum. Add a column Discount (a combo box with 0, 5 and 10 percent) and use it in the total. Show a red background for a row whose quantity is not a number ( DefaultCellStyle.BackColor ). Sort by the Total column when the user clicks its header ( SortMode )."}, {"u": "tut-142-grid.html#next", "t": "Editing a grid", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # A small text editor"}, {"u": "tut-13-editor.html", "t": "A small text editor", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-13-editor.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "A small text editor", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # menus with uiMenu , a multi-line TextBox that fills the window ( Dock ), a StatusStrip , the open and save dialogs, reacting to a change with an event, a layout rule: add the filling control first."}, {"u": "tut-13-editor.html#the-program", "t": "A small text editor", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # a small text editor - menus, a status bar, files and a message when you quit with unsaved text load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.IO\" \"System.Drawing\" \"System.Windows.Forms\" } oForm = \"\" oEditor = \"\" oStatus = \"\" cFileName = \"\" lChanged = False func main oForm = uiForm(\"Tiny editor\",560,360) # The editor fills the window. Add the control that fills FIRST: Windows Forms docks the controls added later # (the menu and the status bar) before it, so the editor gets what is left. oEditor = uiMemo(oForm,0,0,560,338) oEditor { Dock = \"Fill\" Font = new Font(\"Consolas\",11) setTe"}, {"u": "tut-13-editor.html#how-it-works", "t": "A small text editor", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # Menus # uiMenu(oForm, aMenus) builds the menu bar from a list: [title, [ [item, code], ... ]] . Each item has the Ring code to run when it is chosen; \"-\" is a separator line. Filling the window: Dock # Dock = \"Fill\" makes a control take all the free space of its parent; other values are \"Top\" , \"Bottom\" , \"Left\" , \"Right\" . When the window is resized the control follows. Warning Order matters. Windows Forms places docked controls starting with the last added . If you add the menu first and the filling editor after it, the editor is placed first and fills everything \u2014 under the m"}, {"u": "tut-13-editor.html#what-you-learned", "t": "A small text editor", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # Idea Example a menu from a list uiMenu(oForm, [...]) fill the window Dock = \"Fill\" add the filling control first editor, then menu, then status bar status information StatusStrip file dialogs uiOpenFileName , uiSaveFileName react to changes setTextChangedEvent"}, {"u": "tut-13-editor.html#exercises", "t": "A small text editor", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Warn before New or Exit when the text is not saved ( uiAsk ). Add Edit \u2192 Select all ( oEditor.SelectAll() ) and Copy ( oEditor.Copy() ). Show the cursor line number in the status bar ( oEditor.GetLineFromCharIndex(oEditor.SelectionStart) ). Add a Font menu item that opens a FontDialog and changes oEditor.Font . The full-size version of this program is the Notepad application."}, {"u": "tut-13-editor.html#next", "t": "A small text editor", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Formatted text"}, {"u": "tut-133-richtext.html", "t": "Formatted text", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-133-richtext.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Formatted text", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # the selection : SelectionStart , SelectionLength , SelectedText and Select , formatting the selection: SelectionFont and SelectionColor , Find and marking every match with SelectionBackColor , the difference between Text and Rtf , SaveFile and LoadFile with the format RichText . The tutorial A small text editor used a plain text box, where all the text has the same font. A RichTextBox keeps a format for each part of the text."}, {"u": "tut-133-richtext.html#the-program", "t": "Formatted text", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # formatted text - RichTextBox: bold and colored words, searching, saving as RTF and loading it again load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.IO\" \"System.Drawing\" \"System.Windows.Forms\" } # controls that the event code uses are global variables oForm = \"\" oRich = \"\" oStatus = \"\" oBold = \"\" oFind = \"\" func main oForm = uiForm(\"Formatted text\",520,360) oBold = uiButton(oForm,\"Bold\",15,12,70,28,\"toggleBold()\") uiButton(oForm,\"Red\",92,12,70,28,\"makeRed()\") uiButton(oForm,\"Plain\",169,12,70,28,\"makePlain()\") oFind = uiTextBox(oForm,260,14,110,24) oFind.Text = \"ring\" uiButton(oForm,"}, {"u": "tut-133-richtext.html#how-it-works", "t": "Formatted text", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # The selection # Formatting always applies to the selection . Select([ :start = 5, :length = 4 ]) selects four characters from the position 5 (positions count from 0), SelectedText is the text of the selection and SelectionLength its size. When nothing is selected the format applies to the text that is typed next. The function selectWord finds a word and selects it. Font and color # SelectionFont is a Font ; it has the family, the size and the style ( Bold , Italic ...). A font object does not change: to make the selection bold the program makes a new font ( uiFont(\"Segoe UI\", 11"}, {"u": "tut-133-richtext.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Formatted text", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # the selection and its formatting, fonts as objects that are replaced, not changed, finding and marking text, Text and Rtf , and saving as RTF."}, {"u": "tut-133-richtext.html#exercises", "t": "Formatted text", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Add buttons Italic and Underline with the font styles Italic and Underline . Add a ColorDialog ( new ColorDialog() ) so that the user chooses the color. Change findAll to match the case, with a check box Match case . Add a button that removes all the marks ( SelectionBackColor to white for the whole text, after SelectAll() )."}, {"u": "tut-133-richtext.html#next", "t": "Formatted text", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # The clipboard"}, {"u": "tut-127-clipboard.html", "t": "The clipboard", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-127-clipboard.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "The clipboard", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # Clipboard.SetText , GetText and ContainsText , a table that a spreadsheet pastes as cells (tabs and new lines), SetImage , ContainsImage and GetImage , Clear , why a program that tests the clipboard puts back what it found. The clipboard belongs to the whole computer: every program can read and replace it. The tutorial A small text editor used the edit commands of a text box; this lesson uses the clipboard itself, which also copies to and from other programs."}, {"u": "tut-127-clipboard.html#the-program", "t": "The clipboard", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # the clipboard - text, a table for a spreadsheet, a picture, and leaving the clipboard as the user had it load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System.Drawing\" \"System.Threading.Tasks\" \"System.Windows.Forms\" } # controls and state that the event code uses are global variables oForm = \"\" oEdit = \"\" oStatus = \"\" oPicture = \"\" aTable = [ [\"Item\",\"Price\"], [\"Tea\",\"2.50\"], [\"Coffee\",\"3.20\"], [\"Juice\",\"4.00\"] ] cBefore = \"\" func main oForm = uiForm(\"Clipboard\",420,300) oEdit = uiTextBox(oForm,15,15,380,26) oEdit.Text = \"Copied from Ring\" uiButton(oForm,\"Copy text\",15,55,110,30,\"copyText()\") uiB"}, {"u": "tut-127-clipboard.html#how-it-works", "t": "The clipboard", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # Text # SetText(text) puts a text on the clipboard. GetText(NULL) reads the text (the method has more than one form, so it takes NULL when there is nothing to choose). Before reading, ContainsText(NULL) says whether there is text at all: the clipboard can hold a picture, a list of files or nothing. The program shows The clipboard has no text when pasting is not possible instead of showing an empty box. The clipboard belongs to the whole computer, and another program (a clipboard manager, for example) can have it open for a moment, so a read can answer nothing although there is te"}, {"u": "tut-127-clipboard.html#what-you-learned", "t": "The clipboard", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # reading and writing text on the clipboard, a table for spreadsheets, pictures on the clipboard, Clear , and leaving the clipboard as it was."}, {"u": "tut-127-clipboard.html#exercises", "t": "The clipboard", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Add a button Copy as CSV that puts the table on the clipboard with commas, and quotes the cells that have a comma. Add a button that pastes a table from a spreadsheet into a ListBox (split the rows at the new lines and the cells at the tabs). Show the length of the clipboard text in the status line each second (a timer; the tutorial Timers and progress ). Clear the clipboard 20 seconds after copying a text."}, {"u": "tut-127-clipboard.html#next", "t": "The clipboard", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Drag and drop"}, {"u": "tut-154-dragdrop.html", "t": "Drag and drop", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-154-dragdrop.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Drag and drop", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # the source side: MouseDown and DoDragDrop , the target side: AllowDrop , DragEnter , DragOver and DragDrop , the data ( DataObject ): a format and a value, the effect : Copy , Move or None , and the Ctrl key, dropping files from the explorer (the format FileDrop ), making the event arguments yourself to test a drop."}, {"u": "tut-154-dragdrop.html#the-program", "t": "Drag and drop", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # drag and drop - moving items between two lists, Ctrl for copy, dropping files on a window, and testing the drop without a mouse load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.IO\" \"System.Drawing\" \"System.Windows.Forms\" } # controls and state that the event code uses are global variables oForm = \"\" oSource = \"\" oBasket = \"\" oStatus = \"\" oFilesList = \"\" func main oForm = uiForm(\"Drag and drop\",520,360) uiLabel(oForm,\"Fruits (drag from here)\",15,12,230,22) oSource = uiListBox(oForm,15,36,230,130) uiLabel(oForm,\"Basket (drop here)\",270,12,230,22) oBasket = uiListBox(oForm,270,36,230,1"}, {"u": "tut-154-dragdrop.html#how-it-works", "t": "Drag and drop", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # The source starts the drag # The MouseDown event of the fruit list runs beginDrag(x, y) . IndexFromPoint finds the item under the mouse, and DoDragDrop([ :data = item, :allowedEffects = \"Copy, Move\" ]) starts the operation: the mouse now carries the data, and the call waits until the user drops or cancels. Its result is the effect that the target chose. When it is Move the item is taken out of the source; for Copy it stays. The target decides # A control accepts drops only when AllowDrop = True . Then it gets DragEnter when the mouse with the data comes in, DragOver while it mov"}, {"u": "tut-154-dragdrop.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Drag and drop", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # starting a drag and the meaning of its result, accepting a drop and choosing the effect, Ctrl for copy and the bit test & 8 , dropping files, building the data and the arguments for a test."}, {"u": "tut-154-dragdrop.html#exercises", "t": "Drag and drop", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Allow dragging from the basket back to the fruit list. Show the drop position in the status ( e.X and e.Y , converted with PointToClient ). Accept only files with the extension .txt and refuse the others in DragEnter . Reorder the items of one list by dragging inside it (use IndexFromPoint on the drop point)."}, {"u": "tut-154-dragdrop.html#next", "t": "Drag and drop", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Your own dialog"}, {"u": "tut-40-dialog.html", "t": "Your own dialog", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-40-dialog.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Your own dialog", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # what modal means and how ShowDialog works, building a dialog form with OK and Cancel , AcceptButton , CancelButton and DialogResult , validating input and keeping the dialog open, returning the answer to the caller, testing a modal dialog with a timer."}, {"u": "tut-40-dialog.html#modal-and-modeless", "t": "Your own dialog", "h": "Modal and modeless", "x": "Modal and modeless # A modal window blocks the window behind it until it is closed: the user must answer first. That is what you want when the program needs an answer now \u2014 a file name, a choice, a few settings. form.ShowDialog() shows a window modally and waits : the line after it runs only after the dialog closed, and it returns how it was closed ( \"OK\" or \"Cancel\" ). A modeless window ( Show() ) lets the user continue working elsewhere."}, {"u": "tut-40-dialog.html#the-program", "t": "Your own dialog", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # your own dialog - a modal form that asks for data and gives it back load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System.Drawing\" \"System.Windows.Forms\" } # the main window oForm = \"\" oShow = \"\" cName = \"Ann\" nAge = 31 # the dialog and its controls (the dialog code is run by events, so they are global too) oDialog = \"\" oNameBox = \"\" oAgeBox = \"\" oError = \"\" oOk = \"\" oTimer = \"\" nStep = 0 # which step of the test is next cNextName = \"\" func main oForm = uiForm(\"Profile\",340,150) oShow = uiLabel(oForm,\"\",20,20,300,50) oShow.Font = new Font(\"Segoe UI\",12) uiButton(oForm,\"Edit profile...\",20,90,130,"}, {"u": "tut-40-dialog.html#how-it-works", "t": "Your own dialog", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # A function that asks # askProfile(name, age) builds the dialog, shows it, and returns [name, age] or \"\" . The rest of the program does not know that a window was involved: editProfile calls it, checks islist(aAnswer) , and uses the answer. A dialog function hides the window behind a plain call, which also makes the logic easy to follow and to test. Making it look like a dialog # Property Effect FormBorderStyle = \"FixedDialog\" a thin border, not resizable MaximizeBox = False , MinimizeBox = False no maximize or minimize buttons StartPosition = \"CenterParent\" opens in the middle o"}, {"u": "tut-40-dialog.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Your own dialog", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # ShowDialog is modal: it waits and returns the result, AcceptButton , CancelButton , DialogResult , validate before closing with OK, a dialog function that returns data or \"\" , a timer drives a modal dialog in a test."}, {"u": "tut-40-dialog.html#exercises", "t": "Your own dialog", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Add a Country combo box to the dialog. Show the current profile in the dialog title ( Edit profile - Ann ). Add an Apply button that changes the main window without closing the dialog. Use the standard dialogs for a change: ColorDialog , FontDialog , FolderBrowserDialog (they also use ShowDialog and return \"OK\" )."}, {"u": "tut-40-dialog.html#next", "t": "Your own dialog", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Closing a window"}, {"u": "tut-137-closing.html", "t": "Closing a window", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-137-closing.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Closing a window", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # the FormClosing event, which runs before the window closes, the event argument Cancel that keeps the window open, a flag for changes that are not saved ( TextChanged ), the three answers of the question: Yes (save), No (do not save), Cancel, the FormClosed event, which runs after the close, a test that answers the question itself. Every editor, every form that has data, asks before it closes. This is the pattern."}, {"u": "tut-137-closing.html#the-program", "t": "Closing a window", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # closing a window - the FormClosing event: asking to save changes, cancelling the close, and what runs after the form closed load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System.IO\" \"System.Drawing\" \"System.Windows.Forms\" } # controls and state that the event code uses are global variables oForm = \"\" oEdit = \"\" oStatus = \"\" cFile = \"\" lChanged = False # are there changes that are not saved? cTestAnswer = \"\" # a test sets the answer of the question; empty = ask the user with a message box nCloseAttempts = 0 lClosed = False func main cFile = new Path().Combine([ new Path().GetTempPath(), \"ringdotne"}, {"u": "tut-137-closing.html#how-it-works", "t": "Closing a window", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # Remember the changes # The text box has the event TextChanged ; its code sets the global lChanged to true and writes a message in the status label. saveNow writes the file and sets lChanged to false. The program asks about changes only when lChanged is true, so a user who did not change anything is never asked. FormClosing # setFormClosingEvent(\"onClosing()\") runs onClosing each time the form is about to close: the user clicked the X, pressed Alt+F4, or the program called Close() . The event arguments are aDotNetArgs[2] , and aDotNetArgs[2].Cancel = True stops the close . The fu"}, {"u": "tut-137-closing.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Closing a window", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # FormClosing and Cancel , a flag for unsaved changes, the Yes, No and Cancel answers, FormClosed for the clean up, a question that a test can answer."}, {"u": "tut-137-closing.html#exercises", "t": "Closing a window", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Show the changes flag in the title of the window ( Notes * ) while there are changes, as editors do. Add a New button that asks the same question before it clears the text. Remember the position and the size of the window in the FormClosing event (the tutorial Settings ) and restore them at the start. Ask the question with the text of the changes in it (how many characters were added)."}, {"u": "tut-137-closing.html#next", "t": "Closing a window", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # More than one window"}, {"u": "tut-149-windows.html", "t": "More than one window", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-149-windows.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "More than one window", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # the difference between a modal window ( ShowDialog ) and a window that stays open ( Show ), Show(owner) : the tool window belongs to the main window, opening one copy and bringing it to the front ( Activate ), two windows that talk: a function that changes the controls of the other, Application.OpenForms , why an event must not destroy its own window object. The tutorial Your own dialog used a modal dialog: the main window waits until it is closed. A tool window is modeless : both windows work."}, {"u": "tut-149-windows.html#the-program", "t": "More than one window", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # more than one window - a tool window next to the main window: Show with an owner, only one copy, talking between windows, closing together load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System.Drawing\" \"System.Windows.Forms\" } # the windows and the state are global variables oForm = \"\" # the main window (uiTest closes the window in the variable oForm) oShown = \"\" # the label of the main window that the tool window changes oTool = \"\" # the tool window (the last one that was made) lToolOpen = False # is the tool window open now? oToolBox = \"\" oToolStatus = \"\" nToolOpened = 0 # how many times a tool"}, {"u": "tut-149-windows.html#how-it-works", "t": "More than one window", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # Show with an owner # oTool.Show(oForm) opens the tool window and returns at once , so the main window can still be used. The argument is the owner : an owned window always stays in front of its owner, is minimized and restored with it, and is closed with it. Without an owner ( Show(NULL) ) the two windows are separate. The test reads oTool.Owner.Text and gets the title of the main window. One copy # Clicking Open the tool window twice must not make two tool windows. openTool first looks at lToolOpen : when the window is open it only calls Activate() , which brings it to the fron"}, {"u": "tut-149-windows.html#what-you-learned", "t": "More than one window", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # Show(owner) for a window that stays open, opening one copy, windows that call functions that change each other's controls, OpenForms , closing and the clean up of a window object."}, {"u": "tut-149-windows.html#exercises", "t": "More than one window", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Make the tool window remember its place: write Left and Top in the closed event (the tutorial Settings ) and use them when it opens again. Add a button Close the tool to the main window. Open a modal dialog from the tool window with ShowDialog and see which windows can be used. Make a second kind of tool window (a Colors window) and keep one copy of each."}, {"u": "tut-149-windows.html#next", "t": "More than one window", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # A tray icon"}, {"u": "tut-155-tray.html", "t": "A tray icon", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-155-tray.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "A tray icon", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # NotifyIcon : the icon, the tip ( Text ) and Visible , the icon ( SystemIcons ), a ContextMenuStrip with Open and Quit , Hide() and Show() for the window, the balloon message ( ShowBalloonTip ), removing the icon when the program ends. The tutorial Closing a window and the tutorial More than one window used Close , Hide and Show . A tray program hides its window instead of closing it."}, {"u": "tut-155-tray.html#the-program", "t": "A tray icon", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # a tray icon - NotifyIcon: a program that lives in the notification area, a context menu, hiding the window, and a balloon message load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.Drawing\" \"System.Windows.Forms\" } # controls and state that the event code uses are global variables oForm = \"\" oTray = \"\" oMenu = \"\" oStatus = \"\" nShown = 0 # how many times the window was brought back from the tray func main oForm = uiForm(\"Tray demo\",380,160) oStatus = uiLabel(oForm,\"The window is visible\",20,20,330,24) uiButton(oForm,\"Hide to the tray\",20,60,160,32,\"hideToTray()\") # The tray icon needs "}, {"u": "tut-155-tray.html#how-it-works", "t": "A tray icon", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # The icon # A NotifyIcon needs an Icon : a small picture. new SystemIcons().Application is the standard application icon; a program with its own icon loads it from an .ico file. Text is the tip that appears when the mouse rests on the icon (at most 63 characters), and Visible = True puts the icon in the tray. An icon that is not visible is not shown at all. The menu # The right click shows the ContextMenuStrip of the icon. Items.Add(\"Open\") adds an item and returns it, and setClickEvent gives it code. The double click on the icon has its own event, DoubleClick , and both call sho"}, {"u": "tut-155-tray.html#what-you-learned", "t": "A tray icon", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # a tray icon with a tip and a menu, hiding a window and bringing it back, the balloon message, removing the icon at the end."}, {"u": "tut-155-tray.html#exercises", "t": "A tray icon", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Hide the window instead of closing it when the user clicks the X (the event FormClosing with Cancel ; the tutorial Closing a window ), and quit only from the menu. Change the icon (draw one with Graphics on a 16 by 16 Bitmap and make an Icon of it). Add a menu item that shows the time in a balloon. Make the program start hidden in the tray."}, {"u": "tut-155-tray.html#next", "t": "A tray icon", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Menus, toolbars and shortcuts"}, {"u": "tut-41-menus.html", "t": "Menus, toolbars and shortcuts", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-41-menus.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Menus, toolbars and shortcuts", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # the menu bar, toolbar, context menu and status bar controls, shortcut keys, a menu item that can be ticked, the rule that keeps a program tidy: write each action once ."}, {"u": "tut-41-menus.html#the-principle", "t": "Menus, toolbars and shortcuts", "h": "The principle", "x": "The principle # A user may reach \"make upper case\" from the Text menu, a toolbar button, the right-click menu, or Ctrl+U . If each of those had its own code, a fix would have to be made four times. Instead each action is one function ( upperCase , newText ), and every control only calls it."}, {"u": "tut-41-menus.html#the-program", "t": "Menus, toolbars and shortcuts", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # menus, toolbars, context menus and shortcuts - one action, several ways to reach it load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System.Drawing\" \"System.Windows.Forms\" } oForm = \"\" oText = \"\" oStatus = \"\" oWrapItem = \"\" nActions = 0 func main oForm = uiForm(\"Menus\",480,300) # The text area first (it fills the window: see the tutorial \"A small text editor\") oText = uiMemo(oForm,0,0,480,260) oText { Dock = \"Fill\" Font = new Font(\"Consolas\",11) Text = \"Select some text, then use the menu, the toolbar, the right mouse button or a shortcut key.\" } # 1. A toolbar: buttons for the same actions. Contro"}, {"u": "tut-41-menus.html#how-it-works", "t": "Menus, toolbars and shortcuts", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # The menu bar # uiMenu builds the bar from a list: [title, [ [item, code, shortcut], ... ]] . The code is the Ring code to run (a function call), \"-\" is a separator, and the optional shortcut is the .NET text of the Keys enum: \"Control, N\" , \"Control, A\" , \"Alt, F4\" . The shortcut also appears next to the item in the menu, and works even when the menu is closed. A tick in the menu # Word wrap is a ToolStripMenuItem created by hand with CheckOnClick = True : each click toggles Checked and raises CheckedChanged . The handler copies the state to the text box ( oText.WordWrap = oWrap"}, {"u": "tut-41-menus.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Menus, toolbars and shortcuts", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # write each action once, menu items, toolbar buttons, context menus all call it, shortcuts are enum texts, CheckOnClick for ticks, docking order decides the layout."}, {"u": "tut-41-menus.html#exercises", "t": "Menus, toolbars and shortcuts", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Add Redo and Undo ( oText.Undo() ) with Ctrl+Z . Enable or disable Cut and Copy depending on whether text is selected ( Enabled , SelectionChanged ). Add a recent-files list that grows as the user opens files. Add a toolbar button with an image ( oButton.Image = new Bitmap(...) , the tutorial Images )."}, {"u": "tut-41-menus.html#next", "t": "Menus, toolbars and shortcuts", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Timers and progress"}, {"u": "tut-34-timers.html", "t": "Timers and progress", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-34-timers.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Timers and progress", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # the Windows Forms Timer and why it is safe, measuring time with Stopwatch , the progress bar, why a long job freezes a window, and the fix: work in small steps, how to test code that depends on time."}, {"u": "tut-34-timers.html#the-message-loop-and-freezing", "t": "Timers and progress", "h": "The message loop and freezing", "x": "The message loop and freezing # A window program lives in a message loop : it waits for something to happen (a click, a key, a repaint), runs your code, and returns to waiting. While your code runs, nothing else happens : the window is not repainted, buttons do not react. A job that takes five seconds, run in one go, freezes the window for five seconds, and Windows may mark it \"not responding\". In languages with threads the usual answer is \"do the work on another thread\". Ring code runs on one thread , so the answer here is different: cut the job into small parts and do one part each time a ti"}, {"u": "tut-34-timers.html#the-program", "t": "Timers and progress", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # timers and progress - a stopwatch and a long job that keeps the window alive load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.Diagnostics\" \"System.Threading\" \"System.Drawing\" \"System.Windows.Forms\" } oForm = \"\" oTime = \"\" oBar = \"\" oInfo = \"\" oClock = \"\" # the timer that updates the time every 50 ms oJob = \"\" # the timer that does one small part of the job every 20 ms oWatch = \"\" # a Stopwatch object nDone = 0 # how many parts of the job are done nTotal = 100 lRunning = False func main oWatch = new Stopwatch() oForm = uiForm(\"Timers\",360,200) oTime = uiLabel(oForm,\"0:00.0\",20,10,320"}, {"u": "tut-34-timers.html#how-it-works", "t": "Timers and progress", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # The timer # uiTimer(50,\"showTime()\") creates a Windows Forms Timer that calls showTime() every 50 milliseconds once it is started. This timer raises its Tick event on the Ring thread, inside the message loop , so the code can use Ring variables and controls freely. (The System.Threading.Timer of .NET ticks on another thread: Ring code cannot run there.) The stopwatch # A Stopwatch measures time exactly: Start() , Stop() , Reset() , IsRunning and ElapsedMilliseconds . The function showTime splits the milliseconds into minutes, seconds and tenths and writes them into the label. Th"}, {"u": "tut-34-timers.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Timers and progress", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # never run a long job in one go inside an event, a timer calls a small step again and again, Stopwatch measures, Timer displays, put the work in functions so tests can call them."}, {"u": "tut-34-timers.html#exercises", "t": "Timers and progress", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Add a Cancel button that stops the job. Make the step size depend on how long the last step took. Add a countdown: a text box with seconds, a Start button, and a message when it reaches zero. Show the estimated time remaining next to the progress bar."}, {"u": "tut-34-timers.html#next", "t": "Timers and progress", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Drawing and animation"}, {"u": "tut-14-animation.html", "t": "Drawing and animation", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-14-animation.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Drawing and animation", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # drawing with UiCanvas , animation with a timer and a \"game loop\", keyboard events and why a form needs KeyPreview , keeping the program state in global variables."}, {"u": "tut-14-animation.html#the-ingredients-of-a-game", "t": "Drawing and animation", "h": "The ingredients of a game", "x": "The ingredients of a game # State : numbers that describe the world (position, speed, score). Update : a function that changes the state a little (move the ball). Draw : a function that shows the state. A timer that calls update and draw, again and again. Input : keys change the state."}, {"u": "tut-14-animation.html#the-program", "t": "Drawing and animation", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # drawing and animation - a canvas, a timer and the keyboard load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System.Drawing\" \"System.Windows.Forms\" } C_WIDTH = 400 C_HEIGHT = 260 oForm = \"\" oPicture = \"\" oCanvas = \"\" oTimer = \"\" nX = 60 nY = 40 nDX = 4 nDY = 3 nPaddle = 160 nScore = 0 func main oForm = uiForm(\"Bounce\",C_WIDTH,C_HEIGHT) oPicture = uiPictureBox(oForm,0,0,C_WIDTH,C_HEIGHT) oCanvas = new UiCanvas(oPicture) # a canvas draws on the picture box draw() # the form (not a button) must receive the keys: arrows move the paddle oForm.KeyPreview = True oForm.setKeyDownEvent(\"keyPressed(aDotNetArg"}, {"u": "tut-14-animation.html#how-it-works", "t": "Drawing and animation", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # The canvas # uiPictureBox(oForm, x, y, w, h) creates a picture box, and new UiCanvas(oPicture) gives it a bitmap and simple drawing calls: Call Draws clear(color) fills everything fillCircle(x,y,radius,color) a filled circle fillRect(x,y,w,h,color) a filled rectangle text(text,x,y,color,size) text setPixel(x,y,red,green,blue) one pixel from its red, green and blue parts (0 to 255) setPixels(x,y,width,height,rgb) a rectangle of pixels from a text of 3 bytes for each pixel, much faster than one setPixel for each show() puts the finished picture on the screen A color is a name ( \"O"}, {"u": "tut-14-animation.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Drawing and animation", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # state, update, draw, timer, input, UiCanvas for drawing, show() to display, KeyPreview and KeyCode , stop the timer in tests and call the update by hand."}, {"u": "tut-14-animation.html#exercises", "t": "Drawing and animation", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Make the ball faster each time it hits the paddle. Add three lives: when the ball falls, take one life and show it; at zero show Game over . Add the Space key to pause ( oTimer.Stop() / Start() ). Draw a row of bricks at the top and remove the one the ball hits. The Pong and Snake applications show complete games."}, {"u": "tut-14-animation.html#next", "t": "Drawing and animation", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Drawing with the mouse"}, {"u": "tut-139-paint.html", "t": "Drawing with the mouse", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-139-paint.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Drawing with the mouse", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # the events MouseDown , MouseMove and MouseUp , the event argument: X , Y (pixels from the top left of the control), a stroke as three steps and a flag that says the button is down, drawing on a Bitmap that a PictureBox shows, a round pen, and why the first point of a stroke is a circle, testing a mouse program by reading pixels. The tutorial Drawing and animation drew with a timer and the keyboard. Here the mouse draws."}, {"u": "tut-139-paint.html#the-program", "t": "Drawing with the mouse", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # drawing with the mouse - MouseDown, MouseMove and MouseUp: a small paint program with colors, a pen size, clear and save load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.IO\" \"System.Drawing\" \"System.Drawing.Imaging\" \"System.Windows.Forms\" } # controls and state that the event code uses are global variables oForm = \"\" oPicture = \"\" oBitmap = \"\" oStatus = \"\" cColor = \"Black\" nPenSize = 4 lDrawing = False # is the mouse button down? nLastX = 0 nLastY = 0 nStrokes = 0 func main oForm = uiForm(\"Paint\",460,380) uiButton(oForm,\"Black\",10,10,70,28,\"setColor('Black')\") uiButton(oForm,\"Red\",8"}, {"u": "tut-139-paint.html#how-it-works", "t": "Drawing with the mouse", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # The three events # setMouseDownEvent , setMouseMoveEvent and setMouseUpEvent of the picture box run Ring code. The code has the event arguments in aDotNetArgs[2] : aDotNetArgs[2].X and .Y are the place of the pointer in pixels from the top left corner of the picture box. The code passes them to functions ( mouseDown(x, y) ), and the functions do the work. This is the whole design: the events only translate, so a test can call mouseDown(50, 100) without a mouse. A stroke # A stroke has a start, a middle and an end. mouseDown sets lDrawing to true and remembers the place; mouseMov"}, {"u": "tut-139-paint.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Drawing with the mouse", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # the three mouse events and their arguments, a stroke with a flag, drawing on a bitmap and showing it in a picture box, a test that calls the functions of the events and reads pixels."}, {"u": "tut-139-paint.html#exercises", "t": "Drawing with the mouse", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Add a button Eraser that draws in white with a thick pen. Add the color Green and a button that opens a ColorDialog . Draw a straight line from the first place to the current one while the button is down (a rubber band ): keep a copy of the bitmap and draw on the copy. Add Undo : keep a list of bitmap copies, one for each stroke."}, {"u": "tut-139-paint.html#next", "t": "Drawing with the mouse", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Images"}, {"u": "tut-22-images.html", "t": "Images", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-22-images.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Images", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # bitmaps and pixels, Graphics , brushes and pens, GetPixel and SetPixel , saving and loading image files, when to use a different way to call a method (positional arguments and objects)."}, {"u": "tut-22-images.html#concepts", "t": "Images", "h": "Concepts", "x": "Concepts # An image is a grid of pixels ; each pixel has a color made of red, green, blue (0\u2013255) and an alpha value for transparency. A Bitmap is such a grid in memory. To draw on it you use a Graphics object created from the bitmap. A Brush fills areas, a Pen draws lines, a Font draws text. Note System.Drawing is part of the desktop runtime, so the using block with System.Drawing starts the desktop runtime even though the program shows no window."}, {"u": "tut-22-images.html#the-program", "t": "Images", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # images - create, draw, read pixels, resize, convert to gray and save (no window needed) # ring ch10_images.ring [output folder] load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.IO\" \"System.Drawing\" \"System.Drawing.Imaging\" } func main cFolder = new Path().GetTempPath() if len(sysargv) >= 3 and left(sysargv[3],2) != \"--\" # an option such as --selftest is not a folder cFolder = sysargv[3] new Directory().CreateDirectory(cFolder) ok # STEP 1. A bitmap is a rectangle of pixels. Graphics draws on it. oBitmap = new Bitmap(240,160) oGraphics = new Graphics().FromImage(oBitmap) oGraphics.Cl"}, {"u": "tut-22-images.html#how-it-works", "t": "Images", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # Drawing # new Bitmap(240,160) is an empty picture; new Graphics().FromImage(oBitmap) gives the drawing tool; Clear(color) fills the background. Then: Call Draws FillRectangle(brush, new Rectangle(x,y,w,h)) a filled rectangle FillEllipse(brush, new Rectangle(x,y,w,h)) a filled ellipse (a circle if w = h) DrawLine(pen, new Point(x1,y1), new Point(x2,y2)) a line DrawString(text, font, brush, new PointF(x,y)) text Colors are new Color().SteelBlue , new Color().Black ... (the names of the .NET Color properties). A pen's width is a property: new Pen(color) { Width = 3 } . Tip These dr"}, {"u": "tut-22-images.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Images", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # bitmap, pixel, color, Graphics , brushes and pens, GetPixel / SetPixel loops, save, load, resize, Dispose() when finished."}, {"u": "tut-22-images.html#exercises", "t": "Images", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Draw a 100 \u00d7 100 chessboard with FillRectangle . Turn the picture into a negative (255 \u2212 each channel). Make a thumbnail that keeps the proportions of any picture: width 100, height 100 \u00d7 h / w . Save the same bitmap as a JPEG and compare the file sizes. Show the picture in a window with a PictureBox ( oPicture.Image = oBitmap )."}, {"u": "tut-22-images.html#next", "t": "Images", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Screens and pictures of windows"}, {"u": "tut-125-screenshot.html", "t": "Screens and pictures of windows", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-125-screenshot.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Screens and pictures of windows", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # the Screen class: the primary screen, its size and its working area, taking a picture of a part of the screen with Graphics.CopyFromScreen , taking a picture of a control with DrawToBitmap , reading pixels with GetPixel to check a window, why the program prints yes and no for the facts of the screen. The tutorial Images made pictures from nothing. Here the pictures come from the screen and from windows. These classes are part of Windows Forms, so the lesson needs Windows."}, {"u": "tut-125-screenshot.html#the-program", "t": "Screens and pictures of windows", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # screens and pictures of windows - Screen, taking a picture of the screen, and a picture of a form (Windows Forms) load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.IO\" \"System.Drawing\" \"System.Drawing.Imaging\" \"System.Windows.Forms\" } func main # 1. Screen describes the monitors. The size of a screen depends on the computer, so the program prints only what is always true. oScreens = new Screen() oPrimary = oScreens.PrimaryScreen nWidth = oPrimary.Bounds.Width nHeight = oPrimary.Bounds.Height ? \"1. the primary screen has a size: \" + yesNo(nWidth > 0 and nHeight > 0) ? \" the working ar"}, {"u": "tut-125-screenshot.html#how-it-works", "t": "Screens and pictures of windows", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # Screens # Screen.PrimaryScreen is the main monitor and Screen.AllScreens all of them. Bounds is the size of a screen and WorkingArea the part that windows use (the screen without the taskbar). The values depend on the computer, so the program checks relations that are true everywhere: the working area fits in the screen. A picture of the screen # A Bitmap holds the picture, and a Graphics made from it ( FromImage ) draws on it. CopyFromScreen copies a part of the screen into the bitmap: the place on the screen, the place in the bitmap and the size of the part. Save writes a PNG "}, {"u": "tut-125-screenshot.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Screens and pictures of windows", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # Screen , Bounds and WorkingArea , CopyFromScreen and DrawToBitmap , checking a window by reading its pixels, Dispose for pictures."}, {"u": "tut-125-screenshot.html#exercises", "t": "Screens and pictures of windows", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Make a function screenshot(cFile) that saves the whole primary screen. Take a picture of a panel that has a button and check the pixel in the middle of the button. Write a test that changes the BackColor of a panel and checks that the pixels changed. Take a picture of the panel before and after a click and count the pixels that differ."}, {"u": "tut-125-screenshot.html#next", "t": "Screens and pictures of windows", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Printing"}, {"u": "tut-153-printing.html", "t": "Printing", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-153-printing.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Printing", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # PrintDocument and the PrintPage event, drawing a page on e.Graphics inside the margins ( e.MarginBounds ), paging : how many rows fit, and e.HasMorePages , paper size, margins and landscape ( DefaultPageSettings ), the preview controller : pages as pictures, no printer needed, why a page of the preview is a metafile , and how to look at its pixels. Printing is drawing: the tutorial Images used Graphics to draw on a bitmap, and here the same calls draw on a page. These classes are part of Windows Forms and need Windows."}, {"u": "tut-153-printing.html#the-program", "t": "Printing", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # printing - PrintDocument and the PrintPage event: a report on several pages, margins, page numbers, landscape, and a preview without a printer load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.IO\" \"System.Drawing\" \"System.Drawing.Imaging\" \"System.Drawing.Printing\" \"System.Windows.Forms\" # (it also loads the helpers uiFont and uiColor) } aRows = [] # the report: [number, name, price] nPage = 0 # the page that is being printed nNextRow = 1 # the first row that has not been printed yet aOnPage = [] # how many rows each page got func main # 1. A report with 100 rows for i = 1 to 100 aRow"}, {"u": "tut-153-printing.html#how-it-works", "t": "Printing", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # A document and its event # A PrintDocument does not draw by itself. Print() starts the job, and for each page the document raises PrintPage ; the program draws on e.Graphics and, if the page is full, sets e.HasMorePages = True . The document then asks for the next page, and so on until the program answers False . The state of the job (the number of the page, the next row to print) is kept in global variables, because each call of the event is separate. The page # The unit of the page is a hundredth of an inch. e.MarginBounds is the rectangle inside the margins ( Left , Top , Rig"}, {"u": "tut-153-printing.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Printing", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # the PrintPage event and HasMorePages , drawing inside MarginBounds and paging by the height of a line, paper, margins and landscape, the preview controller and looking at a page as a bitmap."}, {"u": "tut-153-printing.html#exercises", "t": "Printing", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Print a table with lines between the rows and the columns ( DrawLine ). Print the number of the page as Page 2 of 3 : first count the pages with the preview controller, then print. Add a button to a form that prints the report with PrintDialog . Print a picture on its own page and scale it to fit inside the margins."}, {"u": "tut-153-printing.html#next", "t": "Printing", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # A chart"}, {"u": "tut-36-chart.html", "t": "A chart", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-36-chart.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "A chart", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # the arithmetic of a chart: scaling values to pixels, a \"nice\" axis maximum, drawing a grid, bars, numbers and labels, saving the picture, testing a drawing by reading its pixels."}, {"u": "tut-36-chart.html#the-plan", "t": "A chart", "h": "The plan", "x": "The plan # A chart is a picture of numbers, and every point of the picture is computed: a plot area : left margin for the numbers, top margin for the title, bottom margin for the labels, a scale : the biggest value (rounded up to a nice number) fills the height of the plot area, for each value a bar : its height is plotHeight \u00d7 value / scaleMax , its left edge is left + slot \u00d7 index , the text : title, numbers on the axis, value above each bar, label below."}, {"u": "tut-36-chart.html#the-program", "t": "A chart", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # a chart - turn numbers into a bar chart with UiCanvas, show it in a window and save it as a PNG load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.IO\" \"System.Drawing\" \"System.Drawing.Imaging\" \"System.Windows.Forms\" } C_WIDTH = 480 C_HEIGHT = 300 aLabels = [\"Jan\",\"Feb\",\"Mar\",\"Apr\",\"May\",\"Jun\"] aValues = [12,19,8,25,17,22] aColors = [\"SteelBlue\",\"Orange\",\"SeaGreen\",\"Crimson\",\"MediumPurple\",\"Goldenrod\"] oForm = \"\" oCanvas = \"\" func main oForm = uiForm(\"Sales chart\",C_WIDTH,C_HEIGHT + 36) oPicture = uiPictureBox(oForm,0,0,C_WIDTH,C_HEIGHT) oCanvas = new UiCanvas(oPicture) drawChart() uiB"}, {"u": "tut-36-chart.html#how-it-works", "t": "A chart", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # The scale # The biggest value is 25. If 25 touched the top of the chart the axis would read 0, 6.25, 12.5... Ugly. niceMax finds a round number at or above the maximum: 25 \u2192 30, 8 \u2192 8, 120 \u2192 150. It takes the power of ten below the value ( 10 for 25), tries the factors 1, 1.5, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 8, 10 and returns the first product that is big enough. The axis then runs 0, 7.5, 15, 22.5, 30. (Ring prints 7.5 as 7.50 ; use rounding or a format if you want 7.5 .) Pixels from values # For a bar: nHeight = (nBottom - nTop) * value / nScaleMax . A value of nScaleMax fills the whole plot a"}, {"u": "tut-36-chart.html#what-you-learned", "t": "A chart", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # chart = scale + bars + text, y grows downwards, a round axis maximum, tests can read pixels."}, {"u": "tut-36-chart.html#exercises", "t": "A chart", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Draw a line chart : points joined by oCanvas.line . Add a legend that lists the months with their colors. Read the data from a CSV file (the tutorial Tabular data ). Draw a pie chart with FillPie ( Graphics ), using the percentage of each value."}, {"u": "tut-36-chart.html#next", "t": "A chart", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # A folder browser"}, {"u": "tut-42-browser.html", "t": "A folder browser", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-42-browser.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "A folder browser", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # TreeView and its nodes, and the Tag property, ListView in details mode with columns, building a tree from folders recursively, reacting to the user's choice, formatting file sizes."}, {"u": "tut-42-browser.html#the-controls", "t": "A folder browser", "h": "The controls", "x": "The controls # Control Shows You use TreeView a hierarchy Nodes , Node.Nodes , SelectedNode , event AfterSelect ListView a list with columns View = \"Details\" , Columns , Items , ListViewItem.SubItems"}, {"u": "tut-42-browser.html#the-program", "t": "A folder browser", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # a folder browser - a TreeView of folders, a ListView of files, and the details of a file load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.IO\" \"System.Drawing\" \"System.Windows.Forms\" } oForm = \"\" oTree = \"\" oList = \"\" oStatus = \"\" cRoot = \"\" func main # The folder to browse: this program makes a small demo folder (give another folder as the first argument to browse it) cRoot = makeDemoFolder() if len(sysargv) >= 3 and sysargv[3] != \"--selftest\" cRoot = sysargv[3] ok oForm = uiForm(\"Folder browser\",640,360) # The pieces: a SplitContainer with the folders on the left and the files on t"}, {"u": "tut-42-browser.html#how-it-works", "t": "A folder browser", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # A demo folder # makeDemoFolder creates folders and files in the temp folder, so the tutorial can show a result and be tested anywhere. Give the program a real folder as its first argument to browse it: ring ch10_browser.ring \"C:\\Users\\you\\Documents\" . Building the tree # addFolderNode(nodes, folder) makes one node for the folder and then calls itself for every subfolder: that is recursion , the natural way to walk a tree. Folders are sorted so the order is stable. The important line: oNode.Tag = cFolder Tag is a free property that can hold anything : here the full path of the fo"}, {"u": "tut-42-browser.html#what-you-learned", "t": "A folder browser", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # recursion builds a tree, Tag carries data on a node, ListView details mode, selection events fill the other control."}, {"u": "tut-42-browser.html#exercises", "t": "A folder browser", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Show an icon for folders and files (an ImageList ). Sort the list when the user clicks a column header ( ColumnClick ). Open a file with a double-click ( Process.Start with UseShellExecute = True ). Add a Refresh button that reloads the tree. The full-size version is the FileExplorer application."}, {"u": "tut-42-browser.html#where-to-go-next", "t": "A folder browser", "h": "Where to go next", "x": "Where to go next # This was the last tutorial of the chapter Windows Forms . The next chapter is WPF : it starts with WPF . The Tutorials page lists all the chapters, and you can start any of them."}, {"u": "tut-15-wpf.html", "t": "WPF", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-15-wpf.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "WPF", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # the difference between WPF and Windows Forms, XAML, finding elements by name, events, data binding."}, {"u": "tut-15-wpf.html#wpf-and-windows-forms", "t": "WPF", "h": "WPF and Windows Forms", "x": "WPF and Windows Forms # Windows Forms WPF Layout positions in pixels panels that arrange their children ( StackPanel , Grid ) Description code only XAML text or code Look classic Windows controls any look: shapes, brushes, animation Best for simple tools, quick results modern, scalable UIs Both work from Ring with the same ideas: objects, properties, events."}, {"u": "tut-15-wpf.html#the-program", "t": "WPF", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # WPF - describe the window in XAML, find elements by name, bind a slider to a text load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System.Windows\" \"System.Windows.Controls\" \"System.Windows.Controls.Primitives\" \"System.Windows.Media\" \"System.Windows.Shapes\" \"System.Windows.Data\" \"System.Windows.Threading\" \"System\" } oWindow = \"\" oSlider = \"\" oShape = \"\" oValue = \"\" oGreet = \"\" oNameBox = \"\" func main # 1. The window is described in XAML: a text format of elements and attributes cXaml = '<Window xmlns=\"http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation\" xmlns:x=\"http://schemas.microsoft.com/wi"}, {"u": "tut-15-wpf.html#how-it-works", "t": "WPF", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # XAML # XAML is XML where each element is a WPF object and each attribute is a property: <\"t\">class=\"k\">Button \"t\">x:Name=\"greet\" \"t\">Content=\"Greet\" \"t\">Margin=\"0,8,0,0\"/> means \"a Button , named greet , with the content Greet , and a margin on top\". <StackPanel> is a panel that stacks its children vertically. The Window element is the window. The program builds the XAML text and calls wpfXaml(text) , which creates all the objects. Note WPF has classes with the same names as Windows Forms ones. In RingDotNet the WPF version of a clashing name starts with Wpf : WpfButton , WpfTex"}, {"u": "tut-15-wpf.html#what-you-learned", "t": "WPF", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # WPF windows can be described in XAML, wpfXaml , wpfFind , wpfRun , events work as before, bindings keep elements in sync without code."}, {"u": "tut-15-wpf.html#exercises", "t": "WPF", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Add a CheckBox named shout and make the greeting upper case when it is ticked. Add a ProgressBar and bind its Value to the slider as well. Change the circle's color with a second slider ( Brush ). See the WPF samples for shapes, grids, menus, tabs and timers."}, {"u": "tut-15-wpf.html#next", "t": "WPF", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # WPF layout and styles"}, {"u": "tut-38-wpf-layout.html", "t": "WPF layout and styles", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-38-wpf-layout.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "WPF layout and styles", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # the Grid and its row and column definitions ( Auto , * ), Grid.Row , Grid.Column , Grid.ColumnSpan , resources and styles, building XAML with a loop, many events with one function."}, {"u": "tut-38-wpf-layout.html#layout-in-wpf", "t": "WPF layout and styles", "h": "Layout in WPF", "x": "Layout in WPF # WPF does not use pixel positions. A panel arranges its children: Panel Arrangement StackPanel one after another, vertically or horizontally Grid rows and columns DockPanel edges and a rest WrapPanel like text: wraps to the next line Canvas exact positions The window can be resized and everything follows. The Grid is the most useful: you divide the space and put each element into a cell."}, {"u": "tut-38-wpf-layout.html#the-program", "t": "WPF layout and styles", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # WPF layout and styles - Grid, resources, a Style for all buttons, a keypad load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System.Windows\" \"System.Windows.Controls\" \"System.Windows.Controls.Primitives\" \"System.Windows.Media\" \"System.Windows.Data\" \"System\" } oWindow = \"\" oDisplay = \"\" cEntered = \"\" func main # The window: a Grid with a display row on top and a keypad below. # - Grid.RowDefinitions / ColumnDefinitions divide the space: \"Auto\" = as big as the content, \"*\" = share what is left # - Window.Resources holds a Style: every Button inside gets the same look without repeating it cXaml = '<Win"}, {"u": "tut-38-wpf-layout.html#how-it-works", "t": "WPF layout and styles", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # The grid # <\"t\">class=\"k\">Grid.RowDefinitions> <\"t\">class=\"k\">RowDefinition \"t\">Height=\"Auto\"/> <!-- the display: as high as its content --> <\"t\">class=\"k\">RowDefinition \"t\">Height=\"*\"/> <!-- four rows that share the rest equally --> ... </\"t\">class=\"k\">Grid.RowDefinitions> <\"t\">class=\"k\">Grid.ColumnDefinitions> <\"t\">class=\"k\">ColumnDefinition \"t\">Width=\"*\"/> ... three equal columns </\"t\">class=\"k\">Grid.ColumnDefinitions> Auto is as big as the content needs, * takes a share of what is left ( 2* would take double). A fixed number is pixels. The keypad's four rows therefore grow a"}, {"u": "tut-38-wpf-layout.html#what-you-learned", "t": "WPF layout and styles", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # Grid rows and columns with Auto and * , Grid.Row , Grid.Column , Grid.ColumnSpan , one Style for all controls of a type, XAML assembled by a loop, many events with one function."}, {"u": "tut-38-wpf-layout.html#exercises", "t": "WPF layout and styles", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Make the equal row heights 2* for the display and * for the keys. Add a style for TextBlock with a bigger font. Add a . key and a + key that appends the symbol (use another row in the grid). Give the window a minimum size ( MinWidth , MinHeight ) and resize it to see the layout follow."}, {"u": "tut-38-wpf-layout.html#next", "t": "WPF layout and styles", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # WPF data binding"}, {"u": "tut-131-wpf-binding.html", "t": "WPF data binding", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-131-wpf-binding.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "WPF data binding", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # a binding : a property that follows another property, ElementName and Path , Mode=TwoWay and UpdateSourceTrigger=PropertyChanged , StringFormat and FallbackValue , binding to IsChecked and to SelectedItem.Content , testing a window by changing a value and reading another. The tutorial WPF made one binding in code, and the tutorial WPF layout and styles wrote XAML. Here the bindings are in the XAML, so most of the work is done by WPF and the Ring code is short."}, {"u": "tut-131-wpf-binding.html#the-program", "t": "WPF data binding", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # WPF data binding - controls that follow each other: a slider, a text box, a progress bar, a check box and a list, all in XAML load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System.Windows\" \"System.Windows.Controls\" \"System\" } oWindow = \"\" func main # A BINDING connects a property of one control to a property of another: when one changes, the other follows, and no Ring code runs. # {Binding ElementName=amount, Path=Value} the property Value of the element named \"amount\" # Mode=TwoWay the change goes both ways (a text box writes back to the slider) # UpdateSourceTrigger=PropertyChanged write back a"}, {"u": "tut-131-wpf-binding.html#how-it-works", "t": "WPF data binding", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # A binding # {Binding ElementName=amount, Path=Value} means: take the property Value of the element named amount . It is written as the value of a property in XAML. The element needs an x:Name , and the property that has the binding follows it from then on, at the start and at each change. The Ring program only creates the window; moving the slider changes the text box, the progress bar and the text without any Ring event. Two directions # A binding to a property such as ProgressBar.Value goes one way: the slider is the source and the bar the target . A text box is also an input,"}, {"u": "tut-131-wpf-binding.html#what-you-learned", "t": "WPF data binding", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # the binding syntax with ElementName and Path , one-way and two-way bindings, StringFormat and FallbackValue , bindings between a check box and a button, and between a list and a text, a test that reads the result of the bindings."}, {"u": "tut-131-wpf-binding.html#exercises", "t": "WPF data binding", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Add a Slider for the width of a Rectangle and bind Width to its Value . Bind the Text of a TextBlock to the Text of the text box, so that it shows what is typed. Make a second slider and bind its Value to the first one with Mode=TwoWay . Add a ComboBox instead of the list and bind to SelectedItem.Content ."}, {"u": "tut-131-wpf-binding.html#next", "t": "WPF data binding", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # WPF menus and tabs"}, {"u": "tut-140-wpf-menu.html", "t": "WPF menus and tabs", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-140-wpf-menu.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "WPF menus and tabs", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # a DockPanel with a Menu at the top and a StatusBar at the bottom, MenuItem : Header (with _ for the key), separators and InputGestureText , a checkable menu item and its Checked and Unchecked events, TabControl , TabItem and the SelectionChanged event, ComboBox , DatePicker and Expander , Visibility : Visible and Collapsed , clicking a menu item in a test by raising its Click event. The tutorial WPF layout and styles used a Grid ; this lesson uses the controls of a normal application window."}, {"u": "tut-140-wpf-menu.html#the-program", "t": "WPF menus and tabs", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # WPF menus and tabs - Menu and MenuItem, a checkable item, a TabControl, an Expander, a ComboBox and a DatePicker in XAML load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System.Windows\" \"System.Windows.Controls\" \"System\" } oWindow = \"\" nNew = 0 func main # XAML describes the window as a tree: a DockPanel puts the menu at the top, a StatusBar at the bottom and the rest in the middle. # Every element that the program uses has an x:Name, and wpfFind(oWindow,\"name\") finds it. cXaml = '<Window xmlns=\"http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation\" xmlns:x=\"http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2"}, {"u": "tut-140-wpf-menu.html#how-it-works", "t": "WPF menus and tabs", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # The layout # A DockPanel places each child on a side of the window: the menu goes to the Top and the status bar to the Bottom ( DockPanel.Dock ), and the last child (the tab control) fills what is left. Everything that the program uses has an x:Name , so wpfFind(oWindow, \"name\") finds it. Menus # In Header=\"_File\" the underscore marks the access key: the user presses Alt+F. A MenuItem inside another is a submenu entry; <Separator/> draws a line; InputGestureText=\"Ctrl+N\" shows the shortcut text (a shortcut itself needs a command , which is written with an InputBindings element i"}, {"u": "tut-140-wpf-menu.html#what-you-learned", "t": "WPF menus and tabs", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # a window with a menu, tabs and a status bar in XAML, menu items, separators and a checkable item, tabs, combo boxes, date pickers and expanders, Visibility , testing menu items by raising Click ."}, {"u": "tut-140-wpf-menu.html#exercises", "t": "WPF menus and tabs", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Add a menu Edit with Copy and Paste that use the text of a TextBox . Add a context menu to the text block of the About tab ( ContextMenu inside the element). Add a third tab with a ListBox and show the number of items in the status bar. Make Exit close the window ( oWindow.Close() )."}, {"u": "tut-140-wpf-menu.html#next", "t": "WPF menus and tabs", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # WPF lists and dynamic content"}, {"u": "tut-148-wpf-lists.html", "t": "WPF lists and dynamic content", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-148-wpf-lists.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "WPF lists and dynamic content", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # the Items collection: Add , Insert , RemoveAt , Clear , Count , SelectedIndex (and -1 for nothing), moving an item: take it out and put it in at a new place, creating controls in code and adding them to a panel ( Children ), keeping the window in step with the data with one refresh function, clicking buttons in a test. The tutorial WPF menus and tabs wrote a fixed window in XAML. Here the XAML has an empty list and an empty panel, and the program fills them."}, {"u": "tut-148-wpf-lists.html#the-program", "t": "WPF lists and dynamic content", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # WPF lists and dynamic content - a ListBox that the program fills, selection, moving and removing items, and controls made in code load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System.Windows\" \"System.Windows.Controls\" \"System\" } oWindow = \"\" func main # The window has a text box to type into, buttons, a ListBox and a WrapPanel. The ListBox is empty in the XAML: the program fills it. cXaml = '<Window xmlns=\"http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation\" xmlns:x=\"http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml\" Title=\"Lists\" Width=\"420\" Height=\"420\" Background=\"#F4F2FB\">' cXaml += '<Stack"}, {"u": "tut-148-wpf-lists.html#how-it-works", "t": "WPF lists and dynamic content", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # The Items collection # listBox().Items is the list of the items of a ListBox . Add(text) adds an item at the end, Insert(index, item) puts one at a place, RemoveAt(index) removes one, Clear() removes all, and Count and Item(index) read it. The items here are texts, but a list can hold any object. Insert has two values in a fixed order, so it is called with two plain values and not with names. The selection # SelectedIndex is the number of the selected item, counted from 0, and -1 when nothing is selected. removeItem and moveItem start by looking at it, and do nothing when there "}, {"u": "tut-148-wpf-lists.html#what-you-learned", "t": "WPF lists and dynamic content", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # adding, inserting, removing and clearing items, the selection and its -1 , one function that keeps the window in step with the data, controls that the program makes and adds to a panel."}, {"u": "tut-148-wpf-lists.html#exercises", "t": "WPF lists and dynamic content", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Add a button that sorts the items (read them into a Ring list, sort it, fill the list again). Show the number of the selected item in the count text when the selection changes ( setSelectionChangedEvent ). Make each chip remove its item when it is clicked. Add an Undo button that remembers the last removed item."}, {"u": "tut-148-wpf-lists.html#next", "t": "WPF lists and dynamic content", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # WPF animation"}, {"u": "tut-70-wpf-animation.html", "t": "WPF animation", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-70-wpf-animation.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "WPF animation", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # the idea of an animation : a property, a start, an end and a duration, DoubleAnimation , Duration , AutoReverse and RepeatBehavior , easing , so that movements start and stop smoothly, animating a position ( Canvas.Left ), Opacity and a rotation Angle , stopping an animation, testing an animated window."}, {"u": "tut-70-wpf-animation.html#timer-or-animation", "t": "WPF animation", "h": "Timer or animation?", "x": "Timer or animation? # In the tutorial Drawing and animation a timer changes the position 50 times a second and the program redraws. In WPF the rendering system knows about time: you describe the change once and WPF calculates every frame, uses the graphics card and keeps the window responsive. You animate dependency properties : Opacity , Width , Canvas.Left , Angle , colors..."}, {"u": "tut-70-wpf-animation.html#the-program", "t": "WPF animation", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # WPF animation - move, fade and rotate with animations that WPF runs for you load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.Windows\" \"System.Windows.Controls\" \"System.Windows.Media\" \"System.Windows.Media.Animation\" \"System.Windows.Shapes\" \"System.Windows.Threading\" } oWindow = \"\" oBall = \"\" oBox = \"\" oSpin = \"\" oStatus = \"\" oCheck = \"\" func main # 1. The window: a canvas (free positions) with three shapes, and two buttons cXaml = '<Window xmlns=\"http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation\" xmlns:x=\"http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml\" Title=\"Animation\" Width="}, {"u": "tut-70-wpf-animation.html#how-it-works", "t": "WPF animation", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # The window # The window is described in XAML (the tutorial WPF ): a Canvas for free positions with an ellipse, a rectangle and a second rectangle that has a RotateTransform named spin as its RenderTransform ; and a row with two buttons and a text. One animation # oMove = new DoubleAnimation() oMove.SetProp(\"From\",10) oMove.SetProp(\"To\",340) oMove.Duration = new Duration().@New([ :timeSpan = new TimeSpan().FromSeconds(2) ]) oMove.AutoReverse = True oMove.RepeatBehavior = new RepeatBehavior().Forever oBall.BeginAnimation(new Canvas().LeftProperty,oMove) A DoubleAnimation changes a"}, {"u": "tut-70-wpf-animation.html#what-you-learned", "t": "WPF animation", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # DoubleAnimation with From , To , Duration , AutoReverse and RepeatBehavior , easing, BeginAnimation and stopping with dnNull() , testing with a timer and the current values."}, {"u": "tut-70-wpf-animation.html#exercises", "t": "WPF animation", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Make the ball bounce with BounceEase and EasingMode = \"EaseOut\" . Animate the Width of the blue box. Animate a color: ColorAnimation on Fill.Color of the ball. Add a slider that changes the speed (the Duration ) of the ball."}, {"u": "tut-70-wpf-animation.html#where-to-go-next", "t": "WPF animation", "h": "Where to go next", "x": "Where to go next # This was the last tutorial of the chapter WPF . The next chapter is Projects with windows : it starts with A project: a to-do list that remembers . The Tutorials page lists all the chapters, and you can start any of them."}, {"u": "tut-24-todo.html", "t": "A project: a to-do list that remembers", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-24-todo.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "A project: a to-do list that remembers", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # planning a small application: data, window, behavior, saving, a CheckedListBox , saving to JSON and loading it again, keeping a window and its data in step."}, {"u": "tut-24-todo.html#the-design", "t": "A project: a to-do list that remembers", "h": "The design", "x": "The design # Part Decision Data the tasks live in the list control itself; each has a text and a done flag Window a text box and Add / Remove buttons on top, the list in the middle, a status line at the bottom Behavior Add ignores blank text; clicking an item ticks it; the status says \"n of m done\" Saving after every change the tasks are written to a JSON file; at start-up the file is read"}, {"u": "tut-24-todo.html#the-program", "t": "A project: a to-do list that remembers", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # a project - a to-do list that remembers its tasks (Windows Forms + JSON + files) load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.IO\" \"System.Text.Json\" \"System.Drawing\" \"System.Windows.Forms\" } oForm = \"\" oNew = \"\" oTasks = \"\" oStatus = \"\" cFile = \"\" lLoading = False func main # the tasks are saved here (a test uses a temporary file) cFile = new Path().Combine([new Path().GetTempPath(),\"ringdotnet_todo-tasks.json\"]) if uiSelfTest() cFile = new Path().Combine([new Path().GetTempPath(),\"ringdotnet_todo-test.json\"]) if new File().Exists(cFile) new File().Delete(cFile) ok ok oForm = ui"}, {"u": "tut-24-todo.html#how-it-works", "t": "A project: a to-do list that remembers", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # The window # Remember the tutorial A small text editor : the control that fills the window is added first , then the top panel and the status label (the helpers uiPanel , uiLabel and Dock do the rest). CheckOnClick = True makes one click tick or untick an item. Add and remove # addTask trims the text, ignores a blank one, adds Items.Add([ :item = text, :isChecked = False ]) (the second parameter is the initial checked state), clears the text box, saves and refreshes the status. removeTask removes the selected item ( SelectedIndex is -1 when nothing is selected). Reacting to a ti"}, {"u": "tut-24-todo.html#what-you-learned", "t": "A project: a to-do list that remembers", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # design first: data, window, behavior, saving, CheckedListBox , events after a click, JSON for saving the tasks as a list of [done, text] pairs, a flag to avoid saving while loading, tests that use their own file."}, {"u": "tut-24-todo.html#exercises", "t": "A project: a to-do list that remembers", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Show the done tasks in gray (use DrawItem , or simply move done tasks to the bottom). Add Clear done that removes all ticked tasks. Save to the user's documents folder ( Environment.GetFolderPath(\"MyDocuments\") ). Add a due date to each task and sort by it. The full-size version is the TodoList application."}, {"u": "tut-24-todo.html#next", "t": "A project: a to-do list that remembers", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # A project: Tic-Tac-Toe"}, {"u": "tut-35-tictactoe.html", "t": "A project: Tic-Tac-Toe", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-35-tictactoe.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "A project: Tic-Tac-Toe", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # separating the rules from the window , representing a game as data, creating controls in a loop, testing a game without clicking."}, {"u": "tut-35-tictactoe.html#the-structure", "t": "A project: Tic-Tac-Toe", "h": "The structure", "x": "The structure # Layer Contains Knows about windows? State aBoard (nine cells), cTurn , lOver no Rules winnerOf(aBoard) , boardFull(aBoard) no Moves play(nCell) , newGame() yes: they update the buttons and the status Window nine buttons, a label, New game yes The rules take the board as a parameter and return an answer. They do not read or write buttons. That makes them easy to test and easy to reuse: the same winnerOf could drive a computer opponent."}, {"u": "tut-35-tictactoe.html#the-program", "t": "A project: Tic-Tac-Toe", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # a project - Tic-Tac-Toe (game logic in functions, nine buttons, a status line) load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System.Drawing\" \"System.Windows.Forms\" } # THE STATE: nine cells, \"\" (empty), \"X\" or \"O\"; whose turn it is; is the game over aBoard = [\"\",\"\",\"\",\"\",\"\",\"\",\"\",\"\",\"\"] cTurn = \"X\" lOver = False # THE WINDOW oForm = \"\" oStatus = \"\" aButtons = [] func main oForm = uiForm(\"Tic-Tac-Toe\",300,376) oStatus = uiLabel(oForm,\"X to play\",10,10,280,26) oStatus.Font = new Font(\"Segoe UI\",14) for nCell = 1 to 9 nRow = floor((nCell - 1) / 3) nColumn = (nCell - 1) % 3 oButton = uiButton(oForm,"}, {"u": "tut-35-tictactoe.html#how-it-works", "t": "A project: Tic-Tac-Toe", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # The board as data # A list of nine texts, \"\" for an empty cell, \"X\" or \"O\" . Cell numbers go 1\u20139 across and down: 1 2 3 4 5 6 7 8 9 The rules # There are only eight ways to win: three rows, three columns, two diagonals. winnerOf keeps them as a list of eight lists of three cell numbers. For each line it asks: is the first cell not empty, and are the other two the same? The first line where that is true gives the winner. Compare this with eight nested if s: the data describes the game, the code is short. Nine buttons in a loop # for nCell = 1 to 9 nRow = floor((nCell - 1) / 3) nC"}, {"u": "tut-35-tictactoe.html#what-you-learned", "t": "A project: Tic-Tac-Toe", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # state, rules, moves, window: four layers, data that describes the game (the eight lines), controls from a loop, with click code built as a text, tests that play."}, {"u": "tut-35-tictactoe.html#exercises", "t": "A project: Tic-Tac-Toe", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Highlight the three winning buttons. Add a score: how many games X and O have won. Write bestMove(aBoard, cPlayer) for a computer opponent: win if you can, block if you must, otherwise take the center. Make the board size 4 \u00d7 4 with four in a row. The TicTacToe application is the full-size version."}, {"u": "tut-35-tictactoe.html#next", "t": "A project: Tic-Tac-Toe", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # A project: a quiz"}, {"u": "tut-143-quiz.html", "t": "A project: a quiz", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-143-quiz.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "A project: a quiz", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # questions as data : a list of [question, answers, right answer] , an array of RadioButton controls made in a loop, a button with two jobs ( Check and then Next ) and a state flag, feedback that shows the right answer, a score screen and a saved list of best scores , a test that plays the quiz. This is the pattern of any wizard or exam program: a list of steps, a current step, a state, and a button that moves on."}, {"u": "tut-143-quiz.html#the-program", "t": "A project: a quiz", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # a project: a quiz - questions in data, radio buttons, a score, feedback, a result screen and a saved list of best scores load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.IO\" \"System.Drawing\" \"System.Windows.Forms\" } # The questions are DATA: each is [ the question, [ the four answers ], the number of the right answer ]. A new question is a new line in # the list, and the program does not change. aQuestions = [ [ \"Which language is used with this package?\", [ \"Ring\",\"Pascal\",\"Lua\",\"Go\" ], 1 ], [ \"What does a byte[] become in Ring?\", [ \"A list\",\"A text of bytes\",\"A number\",\"An object\""}, {"u": "tut-143-quiz.html#how-it-works", "t": "A project: a quiz", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # The questions are data # aQuestions is a list at the top of the program. Each entry has the text of the question, a list of four answers and the number of the right answer. Adding a question means adding a line; the program does not change, and the list could be read from a file or a JSON text (the tutorial JSON and XML ). Radio buttons in a loop # The four RadioButton controls are made in a loop and kept in the list aAnswers , so the code can say aAnswers[i].Text = ... for all of them. Radio buttons that have the same parent are one group, so choosing one turns the others off. "}, {"u": "tut-143-quiz.html#what-you-learned", "t": "A project: a quiz", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # questions as data, radio buttons made in a loop, one button with a state, saving scores in a file, a test that plays through the whole quiz."}, {"u": "tut-143-quiz.html#exercises", "t": "A project: a quiz", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Show the questions in a random order ( random in a loop, or shuffle the list first). Add a time limit for each question with a timer (the tutorial Timers and progress ). Read the questions from a JSON file and write a second file with more questions. Show the list of all the scores, not only the best three, with the date of each."}, {"u": "tut-143-quiz.html#next", "t": "A project: a quiz", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # A project: a command runner"}, {"u": "tut-37-runner.html", "t": "A project: a command runner", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-37-runner.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "A project: a command runner", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # starting another program from a window application, reading its output and error output, exit codes, handling failure with try / catch , a read-only, console-style text area."}, {"u": "tut-37-runner.html#the-idea", "t": "A project: a command runner", "h": "The idea", "x": "The idea # Almost every tool has a command-line version: git , ping , dotnet , ffmpeg , robocopy . A Ring program starts it with a Process , collects what it prints, and shows it. The same pattern is how you would build a front-end for any such tool: gather the options in controls, build the command line from them, run, show the result."}, {"u": "tut-37-runner.html#the-program", "t": "A project: a command runner", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # a project - a command runner (a window that starts a program and shows what it prints) load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.Diagnostics\" \"System.Drawing\" \"System.Windows.Forms\" } oForm = \"\" oCommand = \"\" oOutput = \"\" oStatus = \"\" func main oForm = uiForm(\"Command runner\",560,380) uiLabel(oForm,\"Command:\",10,12,70,22) oCommand = uiTextBox(oForm,84,10,380,24) oCommand.Text = \"echo hello from the command line\" uiButton(oForm,\"Run\",472,8,76,28,\"runCommand()\") oOutput = uiMemo(oForm,10,46,538,300) oOutput { Font = new Font(\"Consolas\",10) ReadOnly = True BackColor = new Color("}, {"u": "tut-37-runner.html#how-it-works", "t": "A project: a command runner", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # Describing the program to start # A ProcessStartInfo says what to run: Property Meaning FileName = \"cmd\" the program: the Windows command interpreter Arguments = \"/c \" + cCommand /c means \"run this command and then exit\" RedirectStandardOutput = True capture what it prints RedirectStandardError = True capture its error messages (a different stream) UseShellExecute = False start it directly (needed for redirecting) CreateNoWindow = True do not open a console window Using cmd /c lets the user type anything that the command prompt understands, including && to chain commands and bui"}, {"u": "tut-37-runner.html#what-you-learned", "t": "A project: a command runner", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # ProcessStartInfo + Process run any program, output and error output are separate streams, the exit code tells success or failure, a window around a command-line tool."}, {"u": "tut-37-runner.html#exercises", "t": "A project: a command runner", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Add a Stop button that kills a long-running command ( oProcess.Kill() ; the process must be a global variable). Keep a history: a ComboBox with the last ten commands. Make a ping window: a host box, a Ping button and the result in plain text. Run a command in a given folder ( WorkingDirectory )."}, {"u": "tut-37-runner.html#next", "t": "A project: a command runner", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # A project: an address book with a database"}, {"u": "tut-48-addressbook.html", "t": "A project: an address book with a database", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-48-addressbook.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "A project: an address book with a database", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # connecting a database to a window, a DataGridView as a view of the database (not the data itself), searching with SQL parameters while typing, keeping the grid, the fields and the database consistent, tests for a complete application."}, {"u": "tut-48-addressbook.html#the-design", "t": "A project: an address book with a database", "h": "The design", "x": "The design # Three layers, as in the tutorial A project: Tic-Tac-Toe : Layer Holds Database ( oDb ) the truth: the contacts, stored in a SQLite file Grid data ( oTable ) a copy of the rows that match the search, for display Window the grid, the search box, the fields and the buttons The golden rule: the database changes first, then the grid is reloaded . The grid is never edited directly, so it can never disagree with the database."}, {"u": "tut-48-addressbook.html#the-program", "t": "A project: an address book with a database", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # a project - an address book with a database (Windows Forms + Entity Framework + a data grid) load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System.Data\" \"System.Drawing\" \"System.Windows.Forms\" \"RingDotNet.Data\" } oForm = \"\" oDb = \"\" oTable = \"\" # a DataTable: what the grid shows, filled from the database oGrid = \"\" oName = \"\" oPhone = \"\" oCity = \"\" oSearch = \"\" oStatus = \"\" func main # THE DATABASE: a file next to the program (a test uses memory, so that it leaves nothing behind) cConnection = \"Data Source=addressbook.db\" if uiSelfTest() cConnection = \"Data Source=:memory:\" ok oDb = new RingDatab"}, {"u": "tut-48-addressbook.html#how-it-works", "t": "A project: an address book with a database", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # The database # A table Contacts with a required Name (at most 60 characters), Phone and City . The program uses a file addressbook.db next to it, so the contacts are there the next time; a test uses memory ( :memory: ) so it leaves nothing behind. (The tutorial Databases step by step explains the schema.) The grid and reload # DataGridView.DataSource = oTable shows a DataTable . reload() is the heart of the program: it clears the table, asks the database for the rows that match the search text, and adds one row per contact. It is called at the start and after every change. The s"}, {"u": "tut-48-addressbook.html#what-you-learned", "t": "A project: an address book with a database", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # the database is the truth, the grid is a view, one reload() after every change, search with LIKE and parameters, DataBindingComplete for column settings, test through the same controls the user uses."}, {"u": "tut-48-addressbook.html#exercises", "t": "A project: an address book with a database", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Sort the contacts by name ( [\"orderby\",...] with query , the tutorial Databases step by step ). After an update, select the row with the same key again. Add a Notes table with several notes per contact and show them in a second grid (the tutorial Entity objects and migrations ). Export the contacts to CSV (the tutorial Tabular data )."}, {"u": "tut-48-addressbook.html#next", "t": "A project: an address book with a database", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Watching sorting algorithms"}, {"u": "tut-60-sorting.html", "t": "Watching sorting algorithms", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-60-sorting.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Watching sorting algorithms", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # how bubble sort, selection sort and insertion sort work, recording work as a list of steps and replaying it with a timer, drawing bars on a canvas and highlighting the two that are touched, counting comparisons and swaps to compare algorithms, a generator of random numbers with a fixed start."}, {"u": "tut-60-sorting.html#the-program", "t": "Watching sorting algorithms", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # watching sorting algorithms - an animation, a timer and counting the work load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System.Drawing\" \"System.Windows.Forms\" } C_COUNT = 36 C_BAR = 12 C_HEIGHT = 300 oForm = \"\" oPicture = \"\" oCanvas = \"\" oAlgorithm = \"\" oInfo = \"\" oTimer = \"\" aValues = [] # the bars as they are shown now aSteps = [] # the work of the algorithm: [\"compare\", i, j] or [\"swap\", i, j] nStep = 0 nCompares = 0 nSwaps = 0 nSeed = 12345 aHot = [] # the two bars that are touched by the step being shown func main oForm = uiForm(\"Sorting\",C_COUNT * C_BAR + 30,C_HEIGHT + 100) oPicture = uiPi"}, {"u": "tut-60-sorting.html#how-it-works", "t": "Watching sorting algorithms", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # Record, then replay # A sorting algorithm is a loop that finishes in a moment: you could not see it. So the three functions bubbleSort , selectionSort and insertionSort work on a copy of the bars and, instead of drawing, record each step in aSteps : [\"compare\", 3, 4] or [\"swap\", 3, 4] . The timer then calls showStep every 15 milliseconds; each call takes the next recorded step, applies a swap to the bars on screen, adds to the counters and redraws. This separation \u2014 computing in one place and showing in another \u2014 also makes the self test easy: it runs all the steps without waiti"}, {"u": "tut-60-sorting.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Watching sorting algorithms", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # how three sorting algorithms work and how they differ, recording steps and replaying them with a timer, the canvas for bars and highlights, measuring work with counters."}, {"u": "tut-60-sorting.html#exercises", "t": "Watching sorting algorithms", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Add a Shell sort or quicksort and compare the counters. Show a bar's value as text over the bar ( oCanvas.text ). Add a speed track bar that changes the timer interval. Count how many comparisons insertion sort needs for bars that are already sorted."}, {"u": "tut-60-sorting.html#next", "t": "Watching sorting algorithms", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # A maze"}, {"u": "tut-61-maze.html", "t": "A maze", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-61-maze.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "A maze", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # generating a maze with depth-first search and a stack, finding the shortest path with breadth-first search and a queue, storing a grid as a list of lists, drawing walls and a path on a canvas, testing an algorithm with properties instead of fixed answers."}, {"u": "tut-61-maze.html#the-program", "t": "A maze", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # a maze - make it with a stack (depth-first), solve it with a queue (breadth-first), draw it on a canvas load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System.Drawing\" \"System.Windows.Forms\" } C_COLUMNS = 21 C_ROWS = 13 C_CELL = 26 oForm = \"\" oPicture = \"\" oCanvas = \"\" oInfo = \"\" aOpen = [] # aOpen[row][column] = a text of the open sides: \"N\", \"E\", \"S\", \"W\" in any combination aPath = [] # the solution: a list of [row, column] nSeed = 2024 func main oForm = uiForm(\"Maze\",C_COLUMNS * C_CELL + 30,C_ROWS * C_CELL + 100) oPicture = uiPictureBox(oForm,5,5,C_COLUMNS * C_CELL,C_ROWS * C_CELL) oCanvas = ne"}, {"u": "tut-61-maze.html#how-it-works", "t": "A maze", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # The grid # The maze is C_ROWS rows of C_COLUMNS cells. aOpen[row][column] is a text with the open sides of the cell: \"N\" , \"E\" , \"S\" and \"W\" in any order (for example \"NE\" ). A cell that has no letter is closed on all sides. Walls are drawn for every side that is not in the text. Making the maze: depth-first with a stack # Start in the top left cell and walk: choose a random neighbor that has not been visited, open the wall between the two cells and move there, pushing the new cell on the stack . When a cell has no unvisited neighbor, go back by popping the stack. The algorithm "}, {"u": "tut-61-maze.html#what-you-learned", "t": "A maze", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # stack and queue in real algorithms, a grid as a list of lists, shortest path by breadth-first search, testing properties."}, {"u": "tut-61-maze.html#exercises", "t": "A maze", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Add a button that shows the maze being built, one step per timer tick (the tutorial Watching sorting algorithms ). Remove some walls at random (a maze with loops) and check that the solver still finds the shortest way. Let the user move a dot with the arrow keys from the start to the exit."}, {"u": "tut-61-maze.html#next", "t": "A maze", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # An image filter lab"}, {"u": "tut-62-filters.html", "t": "An image filter lab", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-62-filters.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "An image filter lab", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # a picture as rows of [red, green, blue] numbers, filters as functions that take a pixel (or its neighbors) and return a new one, why per-pixel calls across the bridge are slow, and a faster way, the structure of a BMP file and writing it with Ring, little-endian numbers, showing an image in a PictureBox ."}, {"u": "tut-62-filters.html#the-program", "t": "An image filter lab", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # an image filter lab - pixels as numbers, filters as small functions, a BMP file written by hand, before and after side by side load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System.IO\" \"System.Drawing\" \"System.Windows.Forms\" } C_WIDTH = 120 C_HEIGHT = 90 oForm = \"\" oBefore = \"\" oAfter = \"\" oInfo = \"\" aSource = [] # the pixels of the original: aSource[y][x] = [red, green, blue] aResult = [] cFolder = \"\" nFile = 0 func main oForm = uiForm(\"Filter lab\",C_WIDTH * 2 + 60,C_HEIGHT + 190) oBefore = uiPictureBox(oForm,10,10,C_WIDTH,C_HEIGHT) oAfter = uiPictureBox(oForm,C_WIDTH + 40,10,C_WIDTH,C_HEIGHT) u"}, {"u": "tut-62-filters.html#how-it-works", "t": "An image filter lab", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # Pixels as numbers # aSource[y][x] is [r, g, b] , each from 0 to 255. The sample picture is made by pictureColor , a function that returns the color of a point: a sky that gets lighter near the horizon, a circle for the sun (a point is inside if (x \u2212 94)\u00b2 + (y \u2212 22)\u00b2 \u2264 14\u00b2 ), grass, a house and a roof. Everything is plain Ring, so it is fast. Filters # Most filters change each pixel on its own, so mapPixels applies a function to every pixel ( call cFunction(aPixel) ): Filter Rule Gray 0.299 r + 0.587 g + 0.114 b \u2014 the eye sees green brighter than blue Invert 255 \u2212 value for each "}, {"u": "tut-62-filters.html#what-you-learned", "t": "An image filter lab", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # pixels as numbers and filters as functions, 3 \u00d7 3 neighborhoods and writing to a new list, clamping, writing a BMP file and little-endian numbers, avoiding thousands of bridge calls."}, {"u": "tut-62-filters.html#exercises", "t": "An image filter lab", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Add a Threshold filter: black below 128 gray, white above. Add a Mirror filter that reverses each row. Make Blur stronger by repeating it three times. Add a button that saves the result with File.Copy to a file chosen with uiSaveFileName ."}, {"u": "tut-62-filters.html#next", "t": "An image filter lab", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # A chat over TCP"}, {"u": "tut-63-chat.html", "t": "A chat over TCP", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-63-chat.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "A chat over TCP", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # the structure of a chat: one server, many clients, a text protocol with one message per line, why a window program must never wait for the network, reading the bytes that are available, and building lines from them, polling with a timer, testing network code with a pump and a short wait."}, {"u": "tut-63-chat.html#the-idea", "t": "A chat over TCP", "h": "The idea", "x": "The idea # The server listens on a port. Each client connects and sends lines such as Alice: Hello . The server sends every line it receives to all clients, including the sender, so everybody sees the same conversation in the same order. The protocol is the simplest possible: one message per line, the sender's name at the start. The tutorial Sockets explains sockets; here they are used in a window program."}, {"u": "tut-63-chat.html#the-program", "t": "A chat over TCP", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # a chat over TCP - a server that relays lines, two clients, and a timer that polls the sockets load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.IO\" \"System.Net\" \"System.Net.Sockets\" \"System.Threading\" \"System.Drawing\" \"System.Windows.Forms\" } oForm = \"\" oServer = \"\" aClients = [] # the two chat clients aLogs = [] # the two text boxes that show the conversation aInputs = [] # the two text boxes where the user types oTimer = \"\" func main oServer = new ChatServer() nPort = oServer.start() oForm = uiForm(\"Chat on port \" + nPort,620,380) cNames = [\"Alice\",\"Bob\"] for nIndex = 1 to 2 nLeft "}, {"u": "tut-63-chat.html#how-it-works", "t": "A chat over TCP", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # Never wait in a window program # StreamReader.ReadLine() waits until a whole line arrives. In a window program that means the window freezes: it cannot repaint or answer clicks. Ring has one thread, so waiting is not allowed. The solution has two parts: Ask first. NetworkStream.DataAvailable says whether bytes are waiting. LineReader.lines() reads only the bytes that are there ( ReadByte ), adds them to a buffer, and returns a line each time it sees a line feed (byte 10). An unfinished line stays in cPartial until the next call. Ask often. A uiTimer calls pump() every 100 millis"}, {"u": "tut-63-chat.html#what-you-learned", "t": "A chat over TCP", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # server and clients with TcpListener and TcpClient , a line-based protocol, polling with a timer instead of waiting, DataAvailable and ReadByte , testing asynchronous code with a bounded wait."}, {"u": "tut-63-chat.html#exercises", "t": "A chat over TCP", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Show a list of the connected names and tell everybody when somebody joins. Add a third window. Send a private message with /to Bob text : the server must send it only to Bob. Close a client and make the server remove the dead connection from its list."}, {"u": "tut-63-chat.html#where-to-go-next", "t": "A chat over TCP", "h": "Where to go next", "x": "Where to go next # This was the last tutorial of the chapter Projects with windows . The next chapter is Libraries and packages : it starts with Callbacks and events . The Tutorials page lists all the chapters, and you can start any of them."}, {"u": "tut-19-callbacks.html", "t": "Callbacks and events", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-19-callbacks.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Callbacks and events", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # what a callback (a delegate ) is, the three ways to write one: a function name, a text of code, an event handler, the argument and result convention: aDotNetArgs and xDotNetResult , what happens with errors inside a callback, why callbacks must run on the Ring thread."}, {"u": "tut-19-callbacks.html#what-is-a-callback", "t": "Callbacks and events", "h": "What is a callback?", "x": "What is a callback? # Sometimes a .NET method does not know what exactly to do: Method The part it does not know List.Sort how to compare two items List.FindAll which items to keep List.ForEach what to do with each item an event such as Click what to do when it happens You give it a callback : code that .NET calls back when it needs the answer. In .NET the type of such a thing is a delegate ( Action , Func , Predicate , Comparison , EventHandler ...). In Ring you create one with dnCallback(...) ."}, {"u": "tut-19-callbacks.html#the-program", "t": "Callbacks and events", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # callbacks and events - giving Ring code to .NET load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System.Collections.Generic\" \"System.Collections.ObjectModel\" \"System.Collections.Specialized\" } # Callback code can only see global variables nLetters = 0 nChanges = 0 func main oWords = new StringList() oWords.Add(\"pear\") oWords.Add(\"apple\") oWords.Add(\"fig\") oWords.Add(\"avocado\") # 1. A callback that is the NAME of a function. .NET calls it once for each item. ? \"1. ForEach calls showItem for every word:\" oWords.@ForEach(dnCallback(\"showItem\")) # 2. A callback that decides: sort with your own comparis"}, {"u": "tut-19-callbacks.html#how-it-works", "t": "Callbacks and events", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # 1. A function name # dnCallback(\"showItem\") is the simplest form: the name of a Ring function. .NET calls it once per item. The function gets one parameter, a list of the arguments that .NET passes, and returns the result: func showItem aArgs ? \" item: \" + aArgs[1] @ForEach starts with @ because ForEach is a Ring keyword. 2. A callback that decides # Sort([ :comparison = dnCallback(\"compareLength\") ]) passes a comparison. .NET gives the function two items ( aArgs[1] , aArgs[2] ); it returns a negative number if the first goes first, a positive one if the second goes first, and 0"}, {"u": "tut-19-callbacks.html#the-rules", "t": "Callbacks and events", "h": "The rules", "x": "The rules # Warning Code in a callback sees only global variables. It runs later, outside the function that created it. nLetters and nChanges are global in the program. A local variable of main would be unknown to the callback. Note Callbacks run on the Ring thread, during a call. A callback that .NET invokes from another thread (a thread-pool timer, a task) is not run; a message is written on stderr . Use Windows Forms timers ( uiTimer ) for repeating work, and wait for tasks on the Ring thread."}, {"u": "tut-19-callbacks.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Callbacks and events", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # Idea Example a callback by function name dnCallback(\"showItem\") arguments and result aArgs[1] , return code in a text aDotNetArgs[1] , xDotNetResult an event setCollectionChangedEvent(\"...\") , clear...Event() errors travel back try around the .NET call"}, {"u": "tut-19-callbacks.html#exercises", "t": "Callbacks and events", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Sort the words alphabetically by their last letter. Use FindAll to keep the words with more than four letters, and @ForEach to print them in upper case. Make a handler for CollectionChanged that prints the new item ( aDotNetArgs[2].getProp('NewItems') ). Set the same event twice and see that the second handler replaces the first."}, {"u": "tut-19-callbacks.html#next", "t": "Callbacks and events", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Exploring .NET with reflection"}, {"u": "tut-20-reflection.html", "t": "Exploring .NET with reflection", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-20-reflection.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Exploring .NET with reflection", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # what reflection is, how to get a Type from a name or from an object, listing properties, methods and their parameters, enums and their values, where the manual gets its class reference from."}, {"u": "tut-20-reflection.html#reflection", "t": "Exploring .NET with reflection", "h": "Reflection", "x": "Reflection # Reflection means that a program looks at its own types: names, members, parameters. .NET offers it in the class Type and the namespace System.Reflection . It is how the class reference of this manual was made: a Ring program asked every wrapped class what it contains."}, {"u": "tut-20-reflection.html#the-program", "t": "Exploring .NET with reflection", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # exploring .NET from Ring - ask a class what it can do load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.Text\" \"System.Reflection\" } func main # Reflection means: a program looks at its own types. Start from a type name. oType = new Type().GetType([ :typeName = \"System.Text.StringBuilder\" ]) ? \"Type : \" + oType.FullName ? \"Is a class : \" + oType.IsClass ? \"Base type : \" + oType.BaseType.FullName ? \"Assembly : \" + oType.Assembly.GetName(NULL).Name # Its public properties ? nl + \"Properties:\" aProperties = oType.GetProperties(NULL).toList() for oProperty in aProperties ? \" \" + oProperty"}, {"u": "tut-20-reflection.html#how-it-works", "t": "Exploring .NET with reflection", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # A Type # A Type describes a class. Two ways to get one: oType = new Type().GetType([ :typeName = \"System.Text.StringBuilder\" ]) # from the full name oType = oObject.GetType() # from an object that exists Its properties say what it is: FullName , Namespace , IsClass , BaseType , Assembly . Properties and methods # GetProperties(NULL) and GetMethods(NULL) return arrays of PropertyInfo and MethodInfo ; toList() turns an array into a Ring list. Each has a Name . A PropertyInfo has PropertyType and CanWrite ; a MethodInfo has GetParameters() , whose items have ParameterType and Name "}, {"u": "tut-20-reflection.html#using-the-manual-instead", "t": "Exploring .NET with reflection", "h": "Using the manual instead", "x": "Using the manual instead # You often need only to look . The class reference shows, for every wrapped class, its constructors, properties, methods with the Ring calls , and events \u2014 produced by exactly this kind of reflection. Use search ( / ) to jump to a class by name."}, {"u": "tut-20-reflection.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Exploring .NET with reflection", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # reflection lets a program inspect types, GetProperties , GetMethods , GetParameters , overloads show up as repeated names, the class reference is made from reflection."}, {"u": "tut-20-reflection.html#exercises", "t": "Exploring .NET with reflection", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # List the public properties of System.IO.FileInfo and which of them can be written. Print every overload of Math.Round with its parameter types and names. How many are there? List the values of System.IO.FileAttributes . Write a function describe(cTypeName) that prints the name, the base type and the number of methods and properties of any type."}, {"u": "tut-20-reflection.html#next", "t": "Exploring .NET with reflection", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # A project: an object inspector"}, {"u": "tut-151-inspector.html", "t": "A project: an object inspector", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-151-inspector.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "A project: an object inspector", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # GetType() and GetProperties() on any object, reading a property from an object with GetValue , skipping indexers ( GetIndexParameters ), catching properties that cannot be read, showing values: a text or a number as it is, an object by its ToString , filling a ListView and finding a value in it for a test. The tutorial Exploring .NET with reflection asked a type what it can do. An inspector asks an object what it is."}, {"u": "tut-151-inspector.html#the-program", "t": "A project: an object inspector", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # a project: an object inspector - reflection shows the properties of any .NET object, with their types and values, in a list load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.IO\" \"System.Text\" \"System.Reflection\" \"System.Drawing\" \"System.Windows.Forms\" } # controls and data that the event code uses are global variables oForm = \"\" oChoice = \"\" oList = \"\" oStatus = \"\" aObjects = [] # [ name, the .NET object ] cTempFile = \"\" func main # Some objects to look at. They are made here; the inspector does not care what they are. cTempFile = new Path().Combine([ new Path().GetTempPath(), \"ringd"}, {"u": "tut-151-inspector.html#how-it-works", "t": "A project: an object inspector", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # Objects of any kind # The list aObjects has four objects of different classes: a Uri , a DateTime , a FileInfo and a StringBuilder . The inspector does not know which is which. It takes an object, asks GetType() for its type and GetProperties(NULL) for the properties of that type, and for each property oProperty.GetValue(oObject) gives the value that this object has now. Indexers # A property with parameters is an indexer : the property Chars of a StringBuilder needs a number ( sb[2] ). GetIndexParameters() lists the parameters, and a property that has any is skipped, because Ge"}, {"u": "tut-151-inspector.html#what-you-learned", "t": "A project: an object inspector", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # GetType() , GetProperties() and GetValue() , indexers and errors in reflection, showing .NET values in a list, a test that reads the list."}, {"u": "tut-151-inspector.html#exercises", "t": "A project: an object inspector", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Add the fields and the methods of the type in two more lists (or one list with a kind column). Add a button that changes the value of a property that can be written ( CanWrite and SetValue ). Inspect a Form and look for the property Text ; change it and see the window. Add a text box that filters the properties by a part of the name."}, {"u": "tut-151-inspector.html#next", "t": "A project: an object inspector", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Expression trees"}, {"u": "tut-87-expressions.html", "t": "Expression trees", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-87-expressions.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Expression trees", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # code as data: a tree of objects ( System.Linq.Expressions ), Parameter , Add , Multiply , Constant , Condition , @Call , Lambda , ToString() of a tree and NodeType , compiling a tree ( Compile ) and calling the function, building a tree from something the program receives at run time. Entity Framework builds its queries the same way (the tutorial Entity objects and migrations ): a condition is first a tree , which can be turned into SQL, and only a compiled tree is a function you can run."}, {"u": "tut-87-expressions.html#the-program", "t": "Expression trees", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # building code while the program runs - expression trees (System.Linq.Expressions): build, show, compile and call load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.Linq.Expressions\" } func main # The expression class of this namespace is called ExpressionsExpression in Ring (the name Expression is used by another namespace). oExpression = new ExpressionsExpression() oDouble = dnType(\"System.Double\") # 1. An expression tree is code as DATA: a tree of objects. Two parameters a and b, and a node that adds them. oA = oExpression.Parameter([ :type = oDouble, :name = \"a\" ]) oB = oExpression"}, {"u": "tut-87-expressions.html#how-it-works", "t": "Expression trees", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # The class name # The static factory class of the namespace is Expression ; another namespace already has a class with that name, so in Ring it is ExpressionsExpression (see Class generator ). new ExpressionsExpression() is only a handle to reach the factory methods ( Add , Lambda , \u2026). Nodes # Every part of the code is a node made by a factory method: Node Made by Looks like a parameter Parameter([ :type = ..., :name = \"a\" ]) a a number Constant(dnBox(\"System.Double\",10)) 10 an operation Add , Subtract , Multiply , Divide with :left and :right (a + b) a comparison GreaterThan([ "}, {"u": "tut-87-expressions.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Expression trees", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # code as a tree of objects, the factory methods and their named values, ToString , NodeType , Lambda , Compile , DynamicInvoke , building a function from run-time data."}, {"u": "tut-87-expressions.html#exercises", "t": "Expression trees", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Build a * a - b and compile it. Build a function that returns a when a is positive and 0 - a otherwise (the absolute value). Extend the helper binaryFunction with % ( Modulo ) and > ( GreaterThan ). Turn the parser of the tutorial An expression calculator into a function that returns a tree instead of a number."}, {"u": "tut-87-expressions.html#next", "t": "Expression trees", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # A library written in C#"}, {"u": "tut-18-csharp-library.html", "t": "A library written in C#", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-18-csharp-library.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "A library written in C#", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # the steps to use any .NET library from Ring, what a C# class looks like, so you can read the ones you meet, how properties, methods, overloads, static methods, out parameters, events and enums arrive in Ring."}, {"u": "tut-18-csharp-library.html#the-steps", "t": "A library written in C#", "h": "The steps", "x": "The steps # Get the library as a .dll (build it, or copy it from a NuGet package). Wrap it : ring wrapassembly.ring Library.dll classes writes a Ring class for every public C# class. Load it : dnLoad(\"Library.dll\") and dnImportFrom(\"classes/\",\"Namespace\") . Use it : new ClassName() , exactly like the built-in classes."}, {"u": "tut-18-csharp-library.html#the-c-library", "t": "A library written in C#", "h": "The C# library", "x": "The C# library # You do not have to write C#, but reading a little helps. This library is in samples/UsingDotNet/tutorials/Greeter : using System; namespace Tutorial.Greetings { // An enum: Ring uses its values as texts (\"Polite\", \"Casual\") public enum Style { Polite, Casual, Shout } // A class written in C#: the properties, methods and events below are what Ring can use public class Greeter { // a property that can be read and written public string Name { get; set; } = \"World\"; // a property that can only be read public int Greetings { get; private set; } // a method without parameters, and a"}, {"u": "tut-18-csharp-library.html#the-ring-program", "t": "A library written in C#", "h": "The Ring program", "x": "The Ring program # # using a library written in C# - build it, wrap it, call it # (the library is in the folder Greeter: Greeter.cs and Greeter.csproj) load \"dotnet.ring\" nTimes = 0 # event code can only see globals cHere = currentdir() cDll = cHere + \"/Greeter/bin/Greeter.dll\" cClasses = cHere + \"/Greeter/classes\" # STEP 1. Build the C# project (the .NET SDK is needed for this step only) if not fexists(cDll) ? \"building the library...\" system(\"dotnet build Greeter -c Release -o Greeter/bin -nologo -v q > nul\") ok # STEP 2. Make Ring classes for it. The generator is a Ring program of this pack"}, {"u": "tut-18-csharp-library.html#how-c-members-look-in-ring", "t": "A library written in C#", "h": "How C# members look in Ring", "x": "How C# members look in Ring # In C# In Ring public string Name { get; set; } oGreeter.Name and oGreeter.Name = \"Ring\" public int Greetings { get; private set; } oGreeter.Greetings \u2014 read only Hello() and Hello(string name, Style style) Hello(NULL) and Hello([ :name = \"Ann\", :style = \"Casual\" ]) public enum Style { Polite, Casual, Shout } the texts \"Polite\" , \"Casual\" , \"Shout\" public static int Add(int a, int b) new Greeter().Add(20,22) bool TryDivide(double a, double b, out double result) a list [worked, result] public event EventHandler<string> Greeted; oGreeter.setGreetedEvent(\"code\") publi"}, {"u": "tut-18-csharp-library.html#using-a-nuget-package", "t": "A library written in C#", "h": "Using a NuGet package", "x": "Using a NuGet package # A package is a set of .dll files, and the .NET SDK can get it and its dependencies for you with a project file and dotnet publish . The tutorial A NuGet package shows it for a real package, and then the steps are the same: wrapassembly.ring , dnLoad , dnImportFrom . See also Your own assemblies ."}, {"u": "tut-18-csharp-library.html#what-you-learned", "t": "A library written in C#", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # wrap \u2192 load \u2192 use, C# properties, overloads, statics, out , events and enums map to Ring as shown, you can use any .NET library without writing C#."}, {"u": "tut-18-csharp-library.html#exercises", "t": "A library written in C#", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Add a property Prefix (default \"Hello\" ) to Greeter and use it in Hello . Rebuild, rewrap, run. Add a method Repeat(string text, int times) and call it with named arguments. Find a small NuGet package (for example a text or math helper), copy its DLL, wrap it and call one method."}, {"u": "tut-18-csharp-library.html#next", "t": "A library written in C#", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # The generic functions"}, {"u": "tut-160-generic-calls.html", "t": "The generic functions", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-160-generic-calls.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "The generic functions", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # why the tutorials use object code first, and what a generic function does instead, dnNewGeneric with callMethod and getProp : a generic type with no class, dnType : the type object, for static members, dnNew with named values: a constructor that new cannot reach, dnStatic : a static method called by its full class name, dnNull and dnBuffer , a rule of thumb for choosing. The earlier lessons wrote new StringBuilder(\"x\") , oText.Append(\"y\") , oText.Length and oForm { Text = \"Hello\" } . That is the way to use .NET from Ring. The generic functions below do the same work with "}, {"u": "tut-160-generic-calls.html#the-program", "t": "The generic functions", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # the generic functions - dnType, dnNew, dnStatic, dnNewGeneric, callMethod, getProp, dnNull and dnBuffer: what to use when there is no class load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.IO\" \"System.Collections\" } func main # The tutorials so far wrote OBJECT code: new makes an object, the dot calls a method or reads a property, and braces set properties. # That works for every class that was generated, and nearly everything is. A few cases have no class, and for them there are # generic functions. They do the same work with the names as texts. # 1. A closed generic type with no g"}, {"u": "tut-160-generic-calls.html#how-it-works", "t": "The generic functions", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # A generic type with no class # List<T> , Dictionary<K,V> and some others have ready classes for the common item types ( StringList , NumberDictionary ...). A type such as LinkedList<string> has no class, so dnNewGeneric(\"System.Collections.Generic.LinkedList\", [\"System.String\"], []) makes the object, and what comes back is a plain DotNetObject . A plain object has no named methods, but it has two general ones: callMethod(\"AddLast\", [\"b\"]) calls a method and getProp(\"Count\") reads a property. toList() copies a collection into a Ring list. The result of getProp(\"First\") is another"}, {"u": "tut-160-generic-calls.html#what-you-learned", "t": "The generic functions", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # object code is the normal way, and the generic functions are for the exceptions, dnNewGeneric , callMethod and getProp for objects with no class, dnType and dnStatic for static members, dnNew with named values, dnNull and dnBuffer , a rule for choosing."}, {"u": "tut-160-generic-calls.html#exercises", "t": "The generic functions", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Make a HashSet<string> with dnNewGeneric , add three names and print Count . Call Math.Round(2.5) with dnStatic and with a class, and compare the answers. Open a FileStream with :mode = \"Create\" and write a few bytes into it. Read the Count of a Queue<string> made with dnNewGeneric after two Enqueue calls."}, {"u": "tut-160-generic-calls.html#next", "t": "The generic functions", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # A NuGet package"}, {"u": "tut-162-nuget.html", "t": "A NuGet package", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-162-nuget.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "A NuGet package", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # what a NuGet package is and where its DLLs come from, a small project file that asks for a package, and dotnet publish that copies the package and what it needs into one folder, wrapping the DLL and loading its classes, calling the package like any other class, what to look at when a package does not work at once."}, {"u": "tut-162-nuget.html#what-a-package-is", "t": "A NuGet package", "h": "What a package is", "x": "What a package is # A NuGet package is a zip file with one or more DLLs (one set for each version of .NET it supports), and a list of other packages that it needs. nuget.org is the public place where packages are kept: a name and a version number find one, for example Newtonsoft.Json 13.0.3. You do not unzip a package by hand. The .NET SDK does it: a project file names the packages, and dotnet publish downloads them (once; they are kept on your computer afterwards) and copies all the DLLs that the program needs into one folder. That folder is what Ring uses."}, {"u": "tut-162-nuget.html#the-steps", "t": "A NuGet package", "h": "The steps", "x": "The steps # Ask for the package in a project file. Publish : dotnet publish Packages -c Release -o Packages/libs copies the package DLLs to Packages/libs . Wrap : ring wrapassembly.ring Packages/libs/Newtonsoft.Json.dll classes writes the Ring classes. Load and use : dnLoad , dnImportFrom , then new ClassName . The earlier tutorial A library written in C# does steps 3 and 4 for a library that you build yourself. Here the library comes from somebody else, so step 1 and 2 are new."}, {"u": "tut-162-nuget.html#the-project-file", "t": "A NuGet package", "h": "The project file", "x": "The project file # This is all that a project needs to get a package. It is in samples/UsingDotNet/tutorials/Packages : <Project Sdk=\"Microsoft.NET.Sdk\"> <PropertyGroup> <TargetFramework>net9.0</TargetFramework> <AssemblyName>Packages</AssemblyName> <DebugType>none</DebugType> </PropertyGroup> <ItemGroup> <PackageReference Include=\"Newtonsoft.Json\" Version=\"13.0.3\" /> </ItemGroup> </Project> PackageReference is the line that names the package and its version. The project also needs one source file, even when you write no C#, so there is an empty class: // A project needs one source file to bui"}, {"u": "tut-162-nuget.html#the-ring-program", "t": "A NuGet package", "h": "The Ring program", "x": "The Ring program # # a NuGet package - the project file asks for the package, dotnet publish copies its DLLs, the generator wraps them, and the classes are used # (the project is in the folder Packages: Packages.csproj names Newtonsoft.Json, and Empty.cs is the one source file a project needs) load \"dotnet.ring\" cHere = currentdir() cDll = cHere + \"/Packages/libs/Newtonsoft.Json.dll\" cClasses = cHere + \"/Packages/classes\" # STEP 1. Get the package. dotnet publish restores the packages that the project names (from nuget.org the first time, from the # computer afterwards) and copies their DLLs, "}, {"u": "tut-162-nuget.html#how-it-works", "t": "A NuGet package", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # Why publish # The DLL of the package is in the folder where the SDK keeps packages, in a sub-folder for each .NET version it supports, and the packages that it needs are somewhere else. dotnet publish chooses the right DLLs for the .NET of the project and puts them together in Packages/libs . A library that is copied alone may stop at its first call because a DLL that it needs is missing. Keep the folder together, and give it to the people who use your program (the tutorial Giving your program to others ). The DLL called Packages.dll that is also in the folder belongs to the emp"}, {"u": "tut-162-nuget.html#when-a-package-does-not-work-at-once", "t": "A NuGet package", "h": "When a package does not work at once", "x": "When a package does not work at once # What you see What to check dotnet publish fails with Unable to find package the name or the version, and that the computer is online the first time the folder has no DLL with the name you expect some packages are named after one name and hold DLLs with another: look in libs DotNet Error: Could not load file or assembly at a call a DLL that the package needs is not next to the others: publish again and copy the whole folder a method is not found the class that you used is in the other namespace: load it with dnImportFrom System.DllNotFoundException the pac"}, {"u": "tut-162-nuget.html#what-you-learned", "t": "A NuGet package", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # a package is a set of DLLs on nuget.org, and a project file names the ones you need, dotnet publish copies a package with what it needs into one folder, wrap the DLL, load the namespaces with dnImportFrom , and call the classes like any other, a table of what to check when the first call fails."}, {"u": "tut-162-nuget.html#exercises", "t": "A NuGet package", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Read the Count of tags and print every tag with a loop ( Item(0) , Item(1) ). Read the age as a number, add one to it and print the result ( oObject.Item(\"age\").ToString() is a text; number(...) makes it a number). Turn the text [1,2,3] into a JArray with new JArray().Parse(...) and print its Count . Find another small package on nuget.org, name it in a second project file, publish it, wrap it and call one method."}, {"u": "tut-162-nuget.html#next", "t": "A NuGet package", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # A QR code"}, {"u": "tut-165-qr.html", "t": "A QR code", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-165-qr.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "A QR code", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # how a QR code is made of modules and what the levels of error correction mean, the steps of the previous lesson on a second package: project file, dotnet publish , wrap, load, a method chosen by the names of its parameters, constructors with @New , and enums as texts, reading a value from a result that has no Ring class ( getProp ), writing the bytes of a PNG picture to a file."}, {"u": "tut-165-qr.html#what-a-qr-code-is", "t": "A QR code", "h": "What a QR code is", "x": "What a QR code is # A QR code is a square grid of dark and light modules . The three big squares in the corners tell a camera where the code is and how it is turned. Between them the text is stored as bits, together with extra data that lets a camera read the code even when a part of it is dirty or covered. The amount of extra data is the level of error correction : Level Part of the code that can be lost L (low) about 7% M (medium) about 15% Q (quartile) about 25% H (high) about 30% A higher level needs more modules for the same text, so the code gets bigger. The empty border around the code "}, {"u": "tut-165-qr.html#the-steps", "t": "A QR code", "h": "The steps", "x": "The steps # They are the steps of the lesson A NuGet package : a project file names the package, QRCoder here, dotnet publish Qr -c Release -o Qr/libs copies it, wrapassembly.ring writes the Ring classes, dnLoad and dnImportFrom , then the classes are used. The project file is in samples/UsingDotNet/tutorials/Qr , and it is the same as before with another name and version: <Project Sdk=\"Microsoft.NET.Sdk\"> <PropertyGroup> <TargetFramework>net9.0</TargetFramework> <AssemblyName>Qr</AssemblyName> <DebugType>none</DebugType> </PropertyGroup> <ItemGroup> <PackageReference Include=\"QRCoder\" Version"}, {"u": "tut-165-qr.html#the-program", "t": "A QR code", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # a QR code with a NuGet package - QRCoder makes the code, and the program prints it as text and writes it as a PNG picture # (the project is in the folder Qr: Qr.csproj names QRCoder, and Empty.cs is the one source file a project needs) load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System.IO\" } cHere = currentdir() cDll = cHere + \"/Qr/libs/QRCoder.dll\" cClasses = cHere + \"/Qr/classes\" # STEP 1. Get the package (nuget.org the first time) with dotnet publish if not fexists(cDll) ? \"getting the package...\" system(\"dotnet publish Qr -c Release -o Qr/libs -nologo -v q > nul\") ok if not fexists(cDll) ?"}, {"u": "tut-165-qr.html#how-it-works", "t": "A QR code", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # Making the code # QRCodeGenerator makes the data of a code: the grid of modules. Its method CreateQrCode has several versions, so it takes a list of the names of the parameters: [ :plainText = \"RING\", :eccLevel = \"L\" ] . The level is an enum of the package, and an enum in Ring is its name as a text , \"L\" . The data is not a picture yet. Other classes of the package turn it into a picture of one kind or another: AsciiQRCode draws it with text characters. GetGraphic(1) returns a text of lines, and the program prints it. A console shows the dark modules as blocks. PngByteQRCode dra"}, {"u": "tut-165-qr.html#what-you-learned", "t": "A QR code", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # a QR code is a grid of modules, and the level of error correction trades size for safety, the same steps bring any package: project file, publish, wrap, load, parameters by name, enums as texts, @New for a class with several constructors, getProp for a value of a type with no class, and bytes written to a file."}, {"u": "tut-165-qr.html#exercises", "t": "A QR code", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Make a code for a Wi-Fi network: the text is WIFI:T:WPA;S:name;P:password;; . Change the level to H and compare the sizes. Print the codes of three different texts one after the other. Write the PNG with GetGraphic(20) and open the file with the picture viewer of your system. Show the picture in a window: write the file, then load it into a PictureBox as in the tutorial Images ."}, {"u": "tut-165-qr.html#next", "t": "A QR code", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Excel files"}, {"u": "tut-167-excel.html", "t": "Excel files", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-167-excel.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Excel files", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # the steps of the NuGet lessons on a package that needs several DLLs, a workbook, a worksheet and a cell, and how to find a cell by its address, text, numbers and formulas in cells, and a style, reading a file back, including the result of a formula, how to call the methods of ClosedXML that have overloads ."}, {"u": "tut-167-excel.html#what-an-xlsx-file-is", "t": "Excel files", "h": "What an .xlsx file is", "x": "What an .xlsx file is # A .xlsx file is a zip file with XML inside: one part for each sheet, one for the styles, and so on. A package that knows the format can make and read these files itself, so the program does not need Excel, and it works on a server or on Linux. The package here is ClosedXML . It uses a few other packages, and dotnet publish copies all of them, as in the lesson A NuGet package . Part of a file In ClosedXML Example the file a workbook , XLWorkbook new XLWorkbook() a sheet of the file a worksheet oBook.AddWorksheet(\"Sales\") one square a cell , by its address oSheet.Cell(\"B2"}, {"u": "tut-167-excel.html#the-steps", "t": "Excel files", "h": "The steps", "x": "The steps # A project file names the package ( Excel/Excel.csproj here), and dotnet publish Excel -c Release -o Excel/libs copies ClosedXML and the DLLs it needs into Excel/libs . wrapassembly.ring writes Ring classes for the namespace ClosedXML.Excel of ClosedXML.dll . The other DLLs are not wrapped: ClosedXML uses them by itself. dnLoad and dnImportFrom . The classes are used. <Project Sdk=\"Microsoft.NET.Sdk\"> <PropertyGroup> <TargetFramework>net9.0</TargetFramework> <AssemblyName>Excel</AssemblyName> <DebugType>none</DebugType> </PropertyGroup> <ItemGroup> <PackageReference Include=\"ClosedX"}, {"u": "tut-167-excel.html#the-program", "t": "Excel files", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # an Excel file with a NuGet package - ClosedXML writes and reads .xlsx files without Excel on the computer # (the project is in the folder Excel: Excel.csproj names ClosedXML, and Empty.cs is the one source file a project needs) load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System.IO\" } cHere = currentdir() cDll = cHere + \"/Excel/libs/ClosedXML.dll\" cClasses = cHere + \"/Excel/classes\" # STEP 1. Get the package and the packages it needs (nuget.org the first time) with dotnet publish if not fexists(cDll) ? \"getting the package...\" system(\"dotnet publish Excel -c Release -o Excel/libs -nologo -v q >"}, {"u": "tut-167-excel.html#how-it-works", "t": "Excel files", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # Writing # oSheet.Cell(\"B2\").Value = 12.5 puts a number in a cell, and .Value = \"Tea\" puts a text. The type of the value decides the type of the cell: a number stays a number in Excel, so a formula can use it, and a text stays a text. A formula is a text that starts with = , written to FormulaA1 (the cell address style of Excel, A1 ): \"=B2*C2\" . The program builds one for each row with a Ring text ( \"=B\" + nLine + \"*C\" + nLine ). A formula is worked out when a value is read (by ClosedXML here, and by Excel when a person opens the file), so the program reads the file back to print"}, {"u": "tut-167-excel.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Excel files", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # an .xlsx file is made and read by a package, and no Excel is needed, dotnet publish brings a package with all its DLLs, and wrapassembly.ring can wrap just one namespace, cells are found by address, and hold texts, numbers and formulas, the classes of a package that works with interfaces, overloads: named parameters and NULL ."}, {"u": "tut-167-excel.html#exercises", "t": "Excel files", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Add a fourth product, and change the sum to include its row. Change the total of the last row to the average price: \"=AVERAGE(B2:B4)\" . Open a sheet that you made in Excel and print the text of the first column. Write the Ring list of a CSV file (the tutorial Tabular data ) into a sheet, one cell for each field."}, {"u": "tut-167-excel.html#next", "t": "Excel files", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Pictures on every system"}, {"u": "tut-168-pictures.html", "t": "Pictures on every system", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-168-pictures.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Pictures on every system", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # why System.Drawing is not the answer on every system, and what SkiaSharp offers instead, how a package with a native library is published and found, a bitmap, a canvas, a paint and colors, reading pixels, and saving and reading a PNG file,"}, {"u": "tut-168-pictures.html#why-another-picture-library", "t": "Pictures on every system", "h": "Why another picture library", "x": "Why another picture library # The lessons Images and An image filter lab use Bitmap and Graphics of Windows. In current versions of .NET these classes work on Windows only , so a program that makes pictures on a Linux server needs another library. SkiaSharp is one: the drawing engine of Chrome and Android, with a .NET interface. It makes the same picture on every system. A drawing engine is written in C++, so SkiaSharp is two things: a managed DLL ( SkiaSharp.dll ) and a native library for each system ( libSkiaSharp.dll for Windows, libSkiaSharp.so for Linux). The package of the managed part i"}, {"u": "tut-168-pictures.html#the-steps", "t": "Pictures on every system", "h": "The steps", "x": "The steps # They are the steps of the lessons A NuGet package , A QR code and Excel files : a project file, dotnet publish , wrapassembly.ring for the namespace SkiaSharp , then dnLoad and dnImportFrom ."}, {"u": "tut-168-pictures.html#the-program", "t": "Pictures on every system", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # pictures that work on every system - SkiaSharp draws, saves and reads pictures on Windows, Linux and macOS (System.Drawing is Windows only) # (the project is in the folder Skia: Skia.csproj names SkiaSharp and its Linux library, and Empty.cs is the one source file a project needs) load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System.IO\" } cHere = currentdir() cDll = cHere + \"/Skia/libs/SkiaSharp.dll\" cClasses = cHere + \"/Skia/classes\" # STEP 1. Get the package (nuget.org the first time) with dotnet publish: SkiaSharp.dll, and its native library in Skia/libs/runtimes if not fexists(cDll) ? \"getti"}, {"u": "tut-168-pictures.html#how-it-works", "t": "Pictures on every system", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # Bitmap, canvas, paint # An SKBitmap is the picture: a grid of pixels. @New([ :width = 200, :height = 100 ]) makes an empty one (a constructor chosen by the names of its parameters). An SKCanvas is what draws on a bitmap: new SKCanvas(oBitmap) . Its methods are Clear , DrawCircle , DrawRect , DrawText and more, and each of the ones with several versions takes a list of the names of its parameters. An SKPaint says how to draw: its Color , IsAntialias (smooth edges), TextSize . The same paint can draw many things, and it can be changed between them: the program draws the circle in "}, {"u": "tut-168-pictures.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Pictures on every system", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # System.Drawing is Windows-only, and SkiaSharp draws on every system, a package with a native library is published with its runtimes folder, and the bridge finds the library of the running system, bitmap, canvas, paint and colors; pixels; PNG and JPEG."}, {"u": "tut-168-pictures.html#exercises", "t": "Pictures on every system", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Draw a rectangle: oCanvas.DrawRect([ :x = 10, :y = 10, :w = 60, :h = 30, :paint = oPaint ]) , in another color. Move the circle, change its size, and print the color of a pixel on its edge. Save the picture as JPEG with Encode([ :format = \"Jpeg\", :quality = 80 ]) and compare the sizes of the two files. Write a function drawFlag(cColor1, cColor2, cColor3) that draws three stripes and returns the PNG bytes."}, {"u": "tut-168-pictures.html#next", "t": "Pictures on every system", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # PDF files"}, {"u": "tut-169-pdf.html", "t": "PDF files", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-169-pdf.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "PDF files", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # how a PDF page is measured and drawn, a document, a page, and an XGraphics that draws on it, fonts, brushes and pens, saving a file and opening it again to check it, what to look at before using a PDF package in a real program."}, {"u": "tut-169-pdf.html#how-a-pdf-page-works", "t": "PDF files", "h": "How a PDF page works", "x": "How a PDF page works # A PDF page is a sheet with a size in points (one point is 1/72 of an inch): an A4 page is about 595 points wide and 842 high. The point 0,0 is the top left corner, and y grows downwards . Text and lines are drawn at positions in points, and each piece of text has a font and a size. PDFsharp has the same shape as the drawing classes of the earlier lessons: Idea In PDFsharp the file PdfDocument a sheet PdfPage , from oDocument.AddPage(NULL) a surface to draw on XGraphics , from new XGraphics().FromPdfPage(oPage) a font XFont (a name and a size) what text is filled with a b"}, {"u": "tut-169-pdf.html#the-steps", "t": "PDF files", "h": "The steps", "x": "The steps # They are the steps of the lessons A NuGet package , Excel files and Pictures on every system : a project file, dotnet publish , wrapassembly.ring , dnLoad and dnImportFrom . The project file names the package: <Project Sdk=\"Microsoft.NET.Sdk\"> <PropertyGroup> <TargetFramework>net9.0</TargetFramework> <AssemblyName>Pdf</AssemblyName> <DebugType>none</DebugType> </PropertyGroup> <ItemGroup> <PackageReference Include=\"PdfSharp\" Version=\"6.1.1\" /> </ItemGroup> </Project> The program wraps only the namespaces that it uses: PdfSharp.Pdf (the document and the pages), PdfSharp.Pdf.IO (read"}, {"u": "tut-169-pdf.html#the-program", "t": "PDF files", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # a PDF file with a NuGet package - PDFsharp makes the pages: text, lines and a title, then the program reads the file back # (the project is in the folder Pdf: Pdf.csproj names PdfSharp, and Empty.cs is the one source file a project needs) load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System.IO\" } cHere = currentdir() cDll = cHere + \"/Pdf/libs/PdfSharp.dll\" cClasses = cHere + \"/Pdf/classes\" # STEP 1. Get the package and the packages it needs (nuget.org the first time) with dotnet publish if not fexists(cDll) ? \"getting the package...\" system(\"dotnet publish Pdf -c Release -o Pdf/libs -nologo -v q"}, {"u": "tut-169-pdf.html#how-it-works", "t": "PDF files", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # Drawing # oGraphics.DrawString([ :s = ..., :font = ..., :brush = ..., :x = 50, :y = 60 ]) writes a text with its baseline at y . DrawString has several versions, so it takes a list of the names of its parameters, and so does DrawLine . The program writes the rows of a Ring list with a loop that moves nY down by 22 points each time. A page of a real report would also check that nY does not pass the bottom of the page (about 800) and add another page when it does. Fonts # A font is made with the name of a family and a size: dnNew(\"PdfSharp.Drawing.XFont\",[ :familyName = \"Arial\", :"}, {"u": "tut-169-pdf.html#what-you-learned", "t": "PDF files", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # a PDF page is measured in points, from the top left corner, a document has pages, and an XGraphics draws on a page, fonts, brushes and pens are objects; dnNew with parameter names makes a font, a file can be opened again to check its pages and title, check the fonts and the license before a real use."}, {"u": "tut-169-pdf.html#exercises", "t": "PDF files", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Change the title text and the font size, and add a third page. Draw a rectangle around the title: XGraphics has DrawRectangle ; look at the names of its parameters in Pdf/classes/PdfSharp.Drawing.classes.ring . Write the rows of a list of 40 products and add a new page when nY is above 780. Make a function makeInvoice(cCustomer, aItems, cFile) that writes a one-page invoice and returns the total."}, {"u": "tut-169-pdf.html#next", "t": "PDF files", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Text to speech"}, {"u": "tut-170-speech.html", "t": "Text to speech", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-170-speech.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Text to speech", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # how to use the voices of Windows from Ring, writing speech to a file , which can be tested without sound, the rate of a voice, a package with a different DLL for each system , and the project setting that picks the right one. Note This lesson runs on Windows only. The voices are part of Windows, and the program says so on another system. (Linux and macOS have their own speech programs, which a Ring program can start as a command, as in the lesson Working with other programs .)"}, {"u": "tut-170-speech.html#a-package-with-a-dll-for-each-system", "t": "Text to speech", "h": "A package with a DLL for each system", "x": "A package with a DLL for each system # The package System.Speech has the same name and the same classes on every system, but only on Windows does it do anything. A package like that contains an empty DLL for general use and the real one in a folder for Windows. A plain dotnet publish copies the empty one, and the first call stops with Speech is not supported on this platform . The project file therefore says which system the output is for: <Project Sdk=\"Microsoft.NET.Sdk\"> <PropertyGroup> <TargetFramework>net9.0</TargetFramework> <AssemblyName>Speech</AssemblyName> <DebugType>none</DebugType> "}, {"u": "tut-170-speech.html#the-steps", "t": "Text to speech", "h": "The steps", "x": "The steps # The steps are the ones of the lessons on packages ( A NuGet package and the lessons after it): a project file, dotnet publish , wrapassembly.ring (only the namespace System.Speech.Synthesis ), then dnLoad and dnImportFrom ."}, {"u": "tut-170-speech.html#the-program", "t": "Text to speech", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # text to speech - System.Speech (a package from nuget.org) makes a voice of Windows speak, or writes the speech to a WAV file # (the project is in the folder Speech: Speech.csproj names the package for Windows, and Empty.cs is the one source file a project needs) # Run it with --speak to hear the voice; without it the program writes the speech to a file, silently. load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System.IO\" } if not iswindows() ? \"The voices are part of Windows, so this lesson runs on Windows only.\" bye ok cHere = currentdir() cDll = cHere + \"/Speech/libs/System.Speech.dll\" cClasses "}, {"u": "tut-170-speech.html#how-it-works", "t": "Text to speech", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # The voices # new SpeechSynthesizer() is the speaking voice. GetInstalledVoices(NULL) is a list of the voices of the computer, and toList() copies it into a Ring list. Each item describes one voice (its name, language and gender in VoiceInfo ). The names are different on every computer, so the program only counts them. SelectVoice(name) picks one by name. Speech to a file # SetOutputToWaveFile(cFile) sends the speech to a WAV file and not to the speakers, and Speak(cText) makes it. That is quiet, so it can be used in a test: the program checks the size of the file. To end the fil"}, {"u": "tut-170-speech.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Text to speech", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # speech is a part of Windows, brought to Ring by a package, a package with a DLL for each system needs a project with RuntimeIdentifier for the system, speech can be written to a WAV file, which makes it testable without sound, Rate , Speak and SpeakAsync ."}, {"u": "tut-170-speech.html#exercises", "t": "Text to speech", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Run the program with --speak , and change the text. Select a second voice with SelectVoice (print the names first: aVoices[1].VoiceInfo.Name ) and compare how the two sound. Write a function that reads the lines of a text file one by one with Speak . Write the speech to a WAV file and play it with the program of the lesson Sound ."}, {"u": "tut-170-speech.html#next", "t": "Text to speech", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Sound"}, {"u": "tut-164-sound.html", "t": "Sound", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-164-sound.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Sound", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # what to do with a framework class that has no Ring class in the package, how a WAV file is made: a header and the samples of the wave, building binary data in a Ring text, with char() and little-endian numbers, giving the bytes to a SoundPlayer through a MemoryStream , Play , PlaySync and PlayLooping ."}, {"u": "tut-164-sound.html#a-class-that-has-no-ring-class", "t": "Sound", "h": "A class that has no Ring class", "x": "A class that has no Ring class # The package wraps the namespaces that most programs use ( System , System.IO , System.Windows.Forms and others; the class reference lists every class). SoundPlayer is in System.Media , which is not one of them. A class that is not listed has no Ring class, but it is in a DLL of the framework, and the generator that the package uses for your own libraries ( A library written in C# ) can wrap a namespace of any DLL: ring wrapassembly.ring System.Windows.Extensions.dll classes System.Media The last words name the namespaces to wrap (without them, every namespace o"}, {"u": "tut-164-sound.html#the-program", "t": "Sound", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # sound - SoundPlayer is a framework class that has no Ring class in the package: wrapassembly.ring makes one from the DLL of the framework. # The sound is a WAV file made in memory from Ring numbers (a Ring text can hold any byte). Run it with --play to hear it. load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System.IO\" \"System.Windows.Forms\" } if not iswindows() ? \"SoundPlayer is a class of Windows, so this lesson runs on Windows only.\" bye ok cHere = currentdir() cClasses = cHere + \"/Sound/classes\" # STEP 1. Where is the DLL? SoundPlayer is in System.Windows.Extensions.dll, in the folder of the d"}, {"u": "tut-164-sound.html#how-it-works", "t": "Sound", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # A sound is a list of numbers # A microphone measures the air pressure many times a second, and a speaker moves to follow the numbers. A sound that the program makes is a wave (here sin ) that is measured at fixed moments: the sample rate is how many numbers there are in a second: 8000 is enough for a clear note, the frequency is how fast the wave goes up and down: 440 times a second is the note A, the amplitude is the size of the wave: 12000 of a possible 32767 is a clear, not too loud sound. sin(2 * pi * 440 * nSample / nRate) is the value of the wave at sample number nSample ,"}, {"u": "tut-164-sound.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Sound", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # a class without a Ring class is wrapped from its DLL with wrapassembly.ring , and the DLL of a framework is found through a class that lives next to it, a WAV file is a header and samples, with numbers stored little endian, a Ring text is a list of bytes, built with char() , MemoryStream with a named constructor, SoundPlayer with @Load , PlaySync and Play ."}, {"u": "tut-164-sound.html#exercises", "t": "Sound", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Run the program with --play , then change the frequency to 880 (one octave higher) and 220 (one octave lower). Make the note one second long and fade it: multiply every sample by (nSamples - nSample) / nSamples . Play two notes one after the other by adding the samples of the second one to cData before the header is made. Write the sound to a file with new File().WriteAllBytes(cFile, cWav) (the tutorial Files and folders ) and play the file by name: new SoundPlayer().@New([ :soundLocation = cFile ]) ."}, {"u": "tut-164-sound.html#next", "t": "Sound", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Native DLLs"}, {"u": "tut-163-native.html", "t": "Native DLLs", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-163-native.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Native DLLs", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # what P/Invoke is and why the bridge cannot do it alone, how to write [DllImport] for a function, and how the types map, numbers, texts, buffers and structures that go to a native function, how to give the functions to Ring in four steps, what to do when the call fails."}, {"u": "tut-163-native.html#the-problem", "t": "Native DLLs", "h": "The problem", "x": "The problem # The bridge calls the members that .NET already has: a method of a class, a property, a constructor. A function of a native DLL is not a .NET member until C# declares it. The declaration is called P/Invoke ( platform invoke ): [DllImport(\"kernel32.dll\")] before a method with the same name and the same parameters as the native function. So the plan is the one of the tutorial A library written in C# : a few lines of C# declare the functions and give them public methods, the generator wraps that library, and Ring calls the methods. Note This lesson uses the DLLs of Windows, so it run"}, {"u": "tut-163-native.html#the-steps", "t": "Native DLLs", "h": "The steps", "x": "The steps # Declare the functions in C# with [DllImport] and give them public methods. Build the library: dotnet build Native -c Release -o Native/bin . Wrap and load it: wrapassembly.ring , dnLoad , dnImportFrom . Use it: new Win32() and the methods."}, {"u": "tut-163-native.html#the-c-library", "t": "Native DLLs", "h": "The C# library", "x": "The C# library # It is in samples/UsingDotNet/tutorials/Native : using System; using System.Runtime.InteropServices; using System.Text; namespace Tutorial.Native { // Ring cannot declare a function of a native DLL, because the bridge calls members that .NET already knows. // C# can: [DllImport] says in which DLL the function is, and the public methods below give the functions to Ring. public static class Win32 { [DllImport(\"kernel32.dll\")] private static extern int MulDiv(int number, int numerator, int denominator); [DllImport(\"user32.dll\", CharSet = CharSet.Unicode)] private static extern Int"}, {"u": "tut-163-native.html#the-ring-program", "t": "Native DLLs", "h": "The Ring program", "x": "The Ring program # # a function of a native DLL (Windows API) - a small C# library with [DllImport] gives it to Ring, and Ring uses it like any other class # (the library is in the folder Native: Win32.cs and Native.csproj) load \"dotnet.ring\" if not iswindows() ? \"The functions of this lesson are in the DLLs of Windows (kernel32 and user32), so it runs on Windows only.\" bye ok cHere = currentdir() cDll = cHere + \"/Native/bin/Native.dll\" cClasses = cHere + \"/Native/classes\" # STEP 1. Build the C# library (the .NET SDK is needed for this step only) if not fexists(cDll) ? \"building the library..."}, {"u": "tut-163-native.html#how-it-works", "t": "Native DLLs", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # Declaring a function # The documentation of a Windows function shows its C signature, for example int MulDiv(int nNumber, int nNumerator, int nDenominator) in kernel32.dll . The C# declaration repeats it with static extern . The names of the parameters do not matter, the order and the types do. Here the Windows types become C# types like this: In C (Windows) In C# In Ring int , DWORD , long int , uint , int a number ULONGLONG , QWORD ulong a number BOOL bool with [return: MarshalAs(UnmanagedType.Bool)] 1 or 0 LPCWSTR (a text that is only read) string , with CharSet = CharSet.Uni"}, {"u": "tut-163-native.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Native DLLs", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # a native function needs a C# declaration, [DllImport] , before Ring can use it, the public methods of the library use numbers and texts, and C# does the marshalling, buffers and structures are filled inside C#, SetLastError and Win32Exception bring the error of the system to Ring."}, {"u": "tut-163-native.html#exercises", "t": "Native DLLs", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Declare GetTickCount64 in kernel32.dll (it returns a ulong ), give it a public method UptimeSeconds() and print how long the computer has been running. Use GetSystemMetrics(1) to print the height of the screen, and GetSystemMetrics(80) for the number of monitors. Declare CharLowerW the same way as CharUpperW , and write SwapCase in C# that uses both on one letter at a time. Call MulDiv with 0 as the denominator. What does it return? Look the answer up in the documentation of the function."}, {"u": "tut-163-native.html#next", "t": "Native DLLs", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # A project: from CSV to Excel"}, {"u": "tut-171-csv-to-excel.html", "t": "A project: from CSV to Excel", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-171-csv-to-excel.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "A project: from CSV to Excel", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # using three packages from one project file and one dotnet publish , wrapping each DLL into its own folder and loading the classes, reading a CSV with CsvHelper (the header, and fields by the name of their column), keeping rows as documents in LiteDB and finding rows with a query, turning the answers into an Excel file with ClosedXML , and reading it back to check it."}, {"u": "tut-171-csv-to-excel.html#the-plan", "t": "A project: from CSV to Excel", "h": "The plan", "x": "The plan # The program has one job, and each package does one part of it: Step Package What happens 1 CsvHelper the CSV text is read row by row, and a field is taken by the name of its column 2 LiteDB each row is stored as a document in a collection (a file on disk) 3 LiteDB a query finds the rows with 9 units or more 4 Ring the amounts are added up for each region 5 ClosedXML a workbook with two sheets is written: all rows with a formula for the amount, and the totals 6 ClosedXML the file is read back, and the total that the formula gives is checked Ring does the adding in step 4 itself, beca"}, {"u": "tut-171-csv-to-excel.html#one-project-for-three-packages", "t": "A project: from CSV to Excel", "h": "One project for three packages", "x": "One project for three packages # The packages of the earlier lessons each had a project file of their own. Here one project file names all three, and one dotnet publish copies them with everything they need: <Project Sdk=\"Microsoft.NET.Sdk\"> <PropertyGroup> <TargetFramework>net9.0</TargetFramework> <AssemblyName>Report</AssemblyName> <DebugType>none</DebugType> </PropertyGroup> <ItemGroup> <PackageReference Include=\"CsvHelper\" Version=\"33.0.1\" /> <PackageReference Include=\"LiteDB\" Version=\"5.0.21\" /> <PackageReference Include=\"ClosedXML\" Version=\"0.104.2\" /> </ItemGroup> </Project> wrapassembl"}, {"u": "tut-171-csv-to-excel.html#the-program", "t": "A project: from CSV to Excel", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # a project with three packages - CsvHelper reads a CSV text, LiteDB stores the rows and finds some of them, ClosedXML writes an Excel report # (the project is in the folder Report: Report.csproj names the three packages, and Empty.cs is the one source file a project needs) load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System.IO\" \"System.Globalization\" } cHere = currentdir() cLibs = cHere + \"/Report/libs/\" cClasses = cHere + \"/Report/classes/\" # STEP 1. Get the packages (nuget.org the first time) with dotnet publish: one folder with the three DLLs and the DLLs they need if not fexists(cLibs + \"Csv"}, {"u": "tut-171-csv-to-excel.html#how-it-works", "t": "A project: from CSV to Excel", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # Reading the CSV # CsvReader takes a reader of text and a culture, which tells it how numbers are written ( en-US has a decimal point). The text comes from a Ring text here, through a StringReader ; for a file you would give a StreamReader of its path. Read() moves to the next row, and the first Read() followed by ReadHeader() takes the names of the columns, so that GetField(\"units\") finds a field by the name of its column and not by its position. A field comes back as a text; number(...) makes the numbers of it. Keeping the rows in LiteDB # LiteDB is a small database in one file"}, {"u": "tut-171-csv-to-excel.html#what-you-learned", "t": "A project: from CSV to Excel", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # three packages can share one project file and one publish, each wrapped into its own folder, CsvHelper reads rows and fields by the names of the columns, LiteDB keeps documents, finds them with a query, and its collections are used with callMethod , ClosedXML writes sheets with formulas, and reading the file back checks the report, each package does one job, and Ring joins the jobs."}, {"u": "tut-171-csv-to-excel.html#exercises", "t": "A project: from CSV to Excel", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Add two more rows to the CSV text and see how the totals and the report change. Change the query to $.region = 'South' AND $.units >= 5 and print the rows that it finds. Read the CSV from a file: write the text to a file, open it with dnNew(\"System.IO.StreamReader\",[ :path = cFile ]) and give that reader to CsvReader . Add a third sheet By product with the amount of each product, in the same way as the sheet By region ."}, {"u": "tut-171-csv-to-excel.html#next", "t": "A project: from CSV to Excel", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Common mistakes"}, {"u": "tut-161-common-errors.html", "t": "Common mistakes", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-161-common-errors.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Common mistakes", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # how to tell an error of Ring from an error of .NET by its first words, the seven mistakes that cause most of the messages in the first weeks, what each message means and how to fix it, how to catch an error so that a program goes on."}, {"u": "tut-161-common-errors.html#two-kinds-of-messages", "t": "Common mistakes", "h": "Two kinds of messages", "x": "Two kinds of messages # The message starts with It comes from Example Error (R..) Ring itself: the program called something that does not exist, or with the wrong number of values Error (R11) : Error in class name, class not found: stopwatch DotNet Error: .NET: the call reached .NET and .NET refused it DotNet Error: System.ArgumentOutOfRangeException: Index was out of range... A message of Ring is about the shape of the call (a name, a count of values). A message of .NET is about the values and the state of the objects (an index, a missing file, a text that is not a number). After DotNet Error"}, {"u": "tut-161-common-errors.html#the-program", "t": "Common mistakes", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # Each mistake is inside try ... catch ... done , so the program goes on and shows the next one. In your own program the same mistake stops the program and prints the same message with the line number. # common mistakes - each one is made on purpose inside try/catch, and the message that Ring or .NET gives is printed, with the fix # errors on purpose load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.Text\" \"System.Collections.Generic\" } func main # Every mistake is in a try ... catch ... done block, so that the program goes on and shows the next one. In your own program the same # mistake"}, {"u": "tut-161-common-errors.html#the-mistakes-one-by-one", "t": "Common mistakes", "h": "The mistakes, one by one", "x": "The mistakes, one by one # 1. Class not found (R11) # class not found: stopwatch means that Ring has no class of that name. Either the name has a typing mistake, or the namespace was not imported . Stopwatch is in System.Diagnostics , and the program listed only System , System.Text and System.Collections.Generic . Add the namespace to using { ... } at the top. The class reference shows the namespace of every class. 2. new Class() with empty parentheses (R19) # Calling function with less number of parameters after new StringBuilder() : a wrapped class has one init that takes the first value of"}, {"u": "tut-161-common-errors.html#how-to-read-a-message", "t": "Common mistakes", "h": "How to read a message", "x": "How to read a message # Read the first words : Error (R..) is the shape of the call, DotNet Error: is the values. Read the name at the end of an R message ( stopwatch , ad , cuont ): it is the word that Ring did not know. Read the line that Ring prints under the message: the first line is where the call is. For a DotNet Error: read the exception name and the explanation, then look at the values that the call had."}, {"u": "tut-161-common-errors.html#check-the-list", "t": "Common mistakes", "h": "Check the list", "x": "Check the list # Message Likely cause Fix Error (R11) ... class not found typing mistake, or namespace not imported the name, or using { \"Namespace\" } Error (R19) ... less number of parameters new Class() , or an overloaded method with no value new Class , or NULL Error (R20) ... extra number of parameters too many values for an overloaded method, which takes one one list of named values [ :a = 1, :b = 2 ] , or callMethod(\"Name\",[a, b]) for values in order Error (R14) ... Method without definition a method name that the class does not have, or an object that has no class look up the name; call"}, {"u": "tut-161-common-errors.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Common mistakes", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # Error (R..) comes from Ring and DotNet Error: from .NET, the seven common mistakes: a missing namespace, new Class() , an overload with no value, a typing mistake, a .NET exception, a wrong value, and a null, NULL for no values, new Class without parentheses, and dnIsNull() for answers that can be null, try ... catch ... done shows a message without stopping the program."}, {"u": "tut-161-common-errors.html#exercises", "t": "Common mistakes", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Change a name in the program of an earlier lesson and read the message. Which of the seven is it? Write a function safeItem(oList, nIndex) that returns \"\" when the index is outside the list, using try ... catch , and another that checks Count first. Which one is shorter? Find a .NET method that can return null (hint: ask Environment for a variable) and print \"missing\" when it does. Call Math.Max with one value, with a text and with two numbers. Which messages do you get?"}, {"u": "tut-161-common-errors.html#next", "t": "Common mistakes", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # More than one system"}, {"u": "tut-166-systems.html", "t": "More than one system", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-166-systems.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "More than one system", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # what is the same on every system and what is not, how a program asks which system it is on ( isWindows() , isLinux() , isMacOSX() ), paths, upper and lower case in file names, and the end of a line, how to keep a feature of one system (a window) away from the others, a checklist for a portable program."}, {"u": "tut-166-systems.html#what-works-where", "t": "More than one system", "h": "What works where", "x": "What works where # Windows Linux macOS Text, numbers, dates, collections, LINQ yes yes should work Files, folders, streams, compression, JSON, XML yes yes should work HTTP, sockets, web servers, databases (SQLite, EF Core) yes yes should work Windows Forms and WPF (windows, controls, drawing) yes no no The registry, Access ( OLEDB ), named shared memory yes no no The package was tested on Windows and on Linux (Ubuntu 24.04, x64, .NET 9). macOS and arm64 were not tested , so the last column says \"should work\", not \"works\". The extension is a C library that is built once for each system; the ste"}, {"u": "tut-166-systems.html#the-program", "t": "More than one system", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # It asks the system six questions and prints the answers. The program is the same on every system: # one program for Windows, Linux and macOS - what differs between the systems and how a program asks instead of assuming # (the answers are different on each system; the lesson shows the output of Windows and of Linux) load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.IO\" } # A window needs Windows: on another system, asking for it ends the program at once, so the line that asks is behind a question. if isWindows() using { \"System.Windows.Forms\" } ok func main # 1. Which system? Ring answe"}, {"u": "tut-166-systems.html#how-it-works", "t": "More than one system", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # 1. Ask which system # isWindows() , isLinux() and isMacOSX() are functions of Ring. .NET answers too: Environment.OSVersion.Platform is Win32NT on Windows and Unix on Linux and macOS. Use the Ring functions for a choice in the program, and the .NET ones when the platform is only to be shown. 2. Paths # Windows writes data\\report.txt and Linux writes data/report.txt . Do not write the separator yourself: Path.Combine joins the parts with the right one. A / is accepted by every system, also on Windows, so a path in the program text can be written with / . A path that starts with a"}, {"u": "tut-166-systems.html#a-checklist-for-a-program-for-every-system", "t": "More than one system", "h": "A checklist for a program for every system", "x": "A checklist for a program for every system # Paths with Path.Combine and folders from GetFolderPath ; no C:\\ and no \\ . File names the same way every time, and lower case. Windows-only features (windows, registry, clipboard, tray icon, Access, named shared memory) behind isWindows() , with a message or a console version for the others. Commands of the system chosen with isWindows() : cmd /c dir or sh -c ls (the tutorial Time, tasks and other programs ). Time zone names : Windows has Eastern Standard Time and Linux has America/New_York ; see Time zones . Tests on both systems , or at least a lo"}, {"u": "tut-166-systems.html#what-you-learned", "t": "More than one system", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # text, files, network and data work everywhere; windows and the registry are Windows-only, isWindows() , isLinux() and isMacOSX() ask the system, Path.Combine , GetFolderPath and NewLine give the right answer for each system, a window feature must be behind isWindows() , because on Linux the program cannot recover from asking for it, a checklist for programs that run everywhere."}, {"u": "tut-166-systems.html#exercises", "t": "More than one system", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Run the program on a computer with another system (or a Linux system under WSL) and compare the output with the two blocks above. Add a seventh question: what does Path.GetTempPath() start with? Make a function configPath() that returns the path of settings.json inside the folder ApplicationData of the user, for every system. Write a program that prints Windows in a window when it can and in the console when it cannot."}, {"u": "tut-166-systems.html#next", "t": "More than one system", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Testing your programs"}, {"u": "tut-28-testing.html", "t": "Testing your programs", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-28-testing.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Testing your programs", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # why and when to test, writing code that is easy to test, dnAssert and dnTestSummary , testing for errors, self-testing programs for windows."}, {"u": "tut-28-testing.html#why-test", "t": "Testing your programs", "h": "Why test?", "x": "Why test? # A test is a small program that checks that another piece of code gives the right answers. You run all the tests after every change: if one fails, you know what broke and when . The tests of this package ( samples/UsingDotNet/tests/run.sh ) are what makes it possible to change the generator and regenerate thousands of classes with confidence."}, {"u": "tut-28-testing.html#make-code-testable", "t": "Testing your programs", "h": "Make code testable", "x": "Make code testable # Do Because small functions with one job each can be tested alone return results instead of printing or showing them a test can check a returned value pass data in as parameters instead of reading globals a test can try many inputs keep window code thin: the logic is in functions logic is easy to test, windows are not"}, {"u": "tut-28-testing.html#the-program", "t": "Testing your programs", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # testing your programs - small functions, dnAssert, expected errors, a test summary # ring ch13_testing.ring all tests pass # ring ch13_testing.ring --break one test fails on purpose, to show what a failure looks like load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.Text.RegularExpressions\" } func main testFizzBuzz() testWordCount() testDates() testErrors() if find(sysargv,\"--break\") > 0 testBrokenOnPurpose() ok nFailed = dnTestSummary() # prints \"TESTS: n passed, m failed\" and returns the number of failures # ---------------------------------------------------------------- the code "}, {"u": "tut-28-testing.html#how-it-works", "t": "Testing your programs", "h": "How it works", "x": "How it works # dnAssert # dnAssert(\"name\", actual, expected) compares two values. Texts are compared as text, numbers as numbers (with a small tolerance for decimals). A good name says what should be true : \"30 is FizzBuzz\", \"spaces only\", \"a week\". What to test # Kind Example in the program normal cases fizzBuzz(3) is Fizz the edges an empty text, spaces only, the same day surprising input many spaces between words errors a text that is not a number Testing an error # You cannot assert an error directly, so catch it, remember that it happened, and assert on the flag and the message: lRaised ="}, {"u": "tut-28-testing.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Testing your programs", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # small, returning functions are testable, dnAssert + dnTestSummary , test the normal case, the edges, and the errors, windows can test themselves, run all tests after every change."}, {"u": "tut-28-testing.html#exercises", "t": "Testing your programs", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Write isLeapYear(nYear) and test years 1900, 2000, 2023 and 2024. Write a function that formats a number of seconds as h:mm:ss and test five values, including 0 and 3600. Add a test for a function that you wrote in an earlier tutorial. Break a function on purpose and see which tests fail."}, {"u": "tut-28-testing.html#next", "t": "Testing your programs", "h": "Next", "x": "Next # Giving your program to others"}, {"u": "tut-119-distribution.html", "t": "Giving your program to others", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "tut-119-distribution.html#what-you-will-learn", "t": "Giving your program to others", "h": "What you will learn", "x": "What you will learn # what Ring2EXE makes and what it copies for a RingDotNet program, the steps: build, look at the folder, test it without Ring, what the other computer needs (the .NET runtime, and sometimes more), a self check at the start of the program, where a distributed program writes its data, the usual errors and what they mean. Getting started installed the package for you . This lesson is about the people who only use your program."}, {"u": "tut-119-distribution.html#the-program", "t": "Giving your program to others", "h": "The program", "x": "The program # # giving your program to others - a self check that a program made with Ring2EXE can run when it starts # ring ring2exe.ring ch13_distribution.ring -dist -dotnet (the page explains the steps) load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"SQLitePCL\" \"Microsoft.Data.Sqlite\" \"System\" \"System.IO\" } func main ? \"Self check of RingDotNet\" # 1. The .NET runtime. The program can not start without it, so a distributed program checks the version first and says clearly what to # install. (The bridge needs .NET 6 or newer; the files of RingDotNet are made for .NET 9.) cVersion = dnVersion() # a text like \".NE"}, {"u": "tut-119-distribution.html#the-steps", "t": "Giving your program to others", "h": "The steps", "x": "The steps # 1. Build. In the folder of the program (here myprogram.ring ), with the Ring of the package installed: ring ring2exe.ring myprogram.ring -dist -dotnet ( ring2exe.ring is in ring/tools/ring2exe ; give its path, or build ring2exe.exe once and use ring2exe myprogram.ring -dist -dotnet .) The option -dist prepares a folder for distribution and -dotnet adds the files of RingDotNet. Use -gui too for a program with a window, so that no console window opens behind it. 2. Look at the folder. target/windows now holds the program ( myprogram.exe ), ring.dll , the compiled program ( ring.ringo"}, {"u": "tut-119-distribution.html#what-the-other-computer-needs", "t": "Giving your program to others", "h": "What the other computer needs", "x": "What the other computer needs # Need When the .NET runtime , version 6 or newer (the files are made for .NET 9: install the 9.0 runtime) always. This is the runtime , not the SDK. Without it the program stops with \".NET runtime not found\" the ASP.NET Core runtime , same version programs with a web server, or that use Microsoft.AspNetCore , Microsoft.Extensions.* (configuration, data protection, rate limiting, routing...). It is installed together with the \"ASP.NET Core Runtime\" or the \"Hosting Bundle\" the Windows Desktop runtime programs with Windows Forms or WPF windows (Windows only) the Mic"}, {"u": "tut-119-distribution.html#the-self-check", "t": "Giving your program to others", "h": "The self check", "x": "The self check # A program that cannot start should say why , in words the user understands. The program above checks, one by one, that the runtime has the right version, that the bridge and its configuration files are next to the program, that SQLite works ( RingDotNet.Data.dll is loaded first, because it knows where the native library of this system is, and then a database in memory is opened, which proves that the library is found), whether the ASP.NET runtime is installed, and that a folder for the data exists. When a line says 0 , the program can print what to install and stop, instead of"}, {"u": "tut-119-distribution.html#where-the-data-goes", "t": "Giving your program to others", "h": "Where the data goes", "x": "Where the data goes # A program in C:\\Program Files usually may not write next to itself. Put settings and data in a folder of the user: Environment.GetFolderPath(\"ApplicationData\") is the folder for the settings of the user (inside, make a folder with the name of your program), LocalApplicationData for data that stays on this computer, and CommonApplicationData for data of all users (writing there may need rights). Never write into the folder of the program itself."}, {"u": "tut-119-distribution.html#linux-and-macos", "t": "Giving your program to others", "h": "Linux and macOS", "x": "Linux and macOS # The same options work ( ring ring2exe.ring myprogram.ring -dist -dotnet ), and the program is made for the system it is built on. On these systems ring2exe takes the files from ring/lib , so setup.ring copies the managed files there when you install the package; the list takes the SQLite library of one system and processor (it says in the file which line to change for arm64 or an Intel Mac). The distribution for Linux and macOS is prepared in this way but not tested yet : build on the system you distribute for and run the result on a clean computer."}, {"u": "tut-119-distribution.html#when-something-goes-wrong", "t": "Giving your program to others", "h": "When something goes wrong", "x": "When something goes wrong # Message Cause and answer .NET runtime not found (install the .NET runtime or set DOTNET_ROOT) the runtime is missing or in an unusual place: install it, or set DOTNET_ROOT Bridge assembly not found the folder is incomplete: RingDotNet.Bridge.dll must be next to ring_dotnet.dll ; copy the whole target/windows folder Can't find the .NET type ... the assembly of that class is not in the folder (a library of your own, a NuGet package): copy its DLL next to the program and load it with dnLoad ( Your own assemblies ) A library or file cannot be loaded for a web or Access "}, {"u": "tut-119-distribution.html#what-you-learned", "t": "Giving your program to others", "h": "What you learned", "x": "What you learned # ring2exe -dist -dotnet makes a folder that runs without Ring, what is in the folder and what the other computer must have, a self check that names what is missing, user data goes to the folder of the user, the usual errors and their causes."}, {"u": "tut-119-distribution.html#exercises", "t": "Giving your program to others", "h": "Exercises", "x": "Exercises # Build the self check as selfcheck.exe and run it from a folder where Ring is not in the PATH . Add your own library ( dnLoad ) and see what ring2exe does not copy; add it to your distribution by hand. Make the program print \"Install the .NET runtime 9.0 from dotnet.microsoft.com\" and stop when line 1 says no. Make a zip file of the folder with ZipFile (the tutorial Files and folders ) and check its size."}, {"u": "tut-119-distribution.html#where-to-go-next", "t": "Giving your program to others", "h": "Where to go next", "x": "Where to go next # This was the last tutorial of the last chapter. Keep Core concepts , the class reference and Rules and limits at hand, and read the applications for bigger programs."}, {"u": "concepts.html", "t": "Core concepts", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "concepts.html#classes-with-the-same-names-as-net", "t": "Core concepts", "h": "Classes with the same names as .NET", "x": "Classes with the same names as .NET # After using { \"Namespace\" } every public type of that namespace is a Ring class with the same short name and the same members: using { \"System.Text\" } oSB = new StringBuilder(\"Hello\") # with arguments: parentheses (Ring calls init() only with them) oSB.Append(\", Ring\").Append(\"!\") # methods that return a wrapped class return that class ? oSB.ToString() ? oSB.Length # properties and fields are attributes new Console().WriteLine(\"Hi\") # static members are called on an instance ? new Math().Max(3,9) Hello from .NET Max(3,9) = 9 Sqrt(144) = 12 PI = 3.14 Hello,"}, {"u": "concepts.html#short-names-for-static-classes", "t": "Core concepts", "h": "Short names for static classes", "x": "Short names for static classes # A class such as Math , Path or File has only static members, so new Math() just makes an object that gives access to them. If you prefer the .NET style, make that object once at the top of the program and give it the name of the class: load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.IO\" } Math = new Math() Path = new Path() func main ? Math.Max(3,9) # 9 ? Path.Combine([\"a\",\"b\"]) # a\\b ? new Math().Min(3,9) # the class is still there: 3 The names of Ring variables are not case sensitive, so a variable path is the same name as Path , and a local variable with that na"}, {"u": "concepts.html#naming-rules", "t": "Core concepts", "h": "Naming rules", "x": "Naming rules # A Ring method has one name and one parameter list, so a .NET method name that has several overloads becomes one Ring method with one parameter ; you choose the overload with the value you pass (see Named arguments below). A name with a single form keeps the .NET parameters. .NET Ring Rule Append() , Append(string) , Append(char, int) Append(NULL) , Append(\"x\") , Append([ :value = \"x\", :repeatCount = 3 ]) One method: NULL = no parameters, a value = one parameter, a list of :name = value = the overload with those parameter names. Math.Max(a, b) Max(3,9) A name with only one number"}, {"u": "concepts.html#named-arguments", "t": "Core concepts", "h": "Named arguments", "x": "Named arguments # Optional and overloaded parameters are written in Ring as a list of :name = value pairs . When a method has overloads with different numbers of parameters, its plain name takes one parameter and looks at what you pass: You pass What happens NULL (an empty string) the parameterless overload is called: oProcess.Start(NULL) a list of :name = value pairs the overload is chosen by the parameter names of the .NET method; the order does not matter and optional parameters can be left out anything else the overload with one parameter is called: oMath.Round(2.5) oMath = new Math() ? oM"}, {"u": "concepts.html#arguments-in-order-with-callmethod", "t": "Core concepts", "h": "Arguments in order with callMethod", "x": "Arguments in order with callMethod # The plain name of an overloaded method takes one argument ( NULL , a value or a named list). To pass several arguments in order, call the method with callMethod(name, [arguments]) : the bridge chooses the overload from the arguments, as C# does. oSB = new StringBuilder(\"\") oSB.callMethod(\"Append\",[\"a\",3]) # Append(char, int): two arguments in order ? oSB.ToString() # aaa oRegistry.callMethod(\"SetValue\",[\"Port\",8080,\"DWord\"]) # three arguments in order (a Registry key, the tutorial \"The Windows Registry\") oQuery.callMethod(\"Where\",[\"Age\",\">\",18]) # the SqlKa"}, {"u": "concepts.html#properties-and-fields-are-attributes", "t": "Core concepts", "h": "Properties and fields are attributes", "x": "Properties and fields are attributes # .NET properties are Ring attributes. Ring calls the generated getName() / setName(value) for you, so reading and writing is simple, also inside { } : oForm = new Form() { Text = \"Hello\" Width = 400 } ? oForm.Controls.Count # chained: Controls returns a ControlCollection oForm { Height = 300 Text += \"!\" } Assigning a read-only property raises an error. Most properties named like Ring words ( From , To , Step , On ...) are read and set like any other ( oMessage.From = ... ). The few that Ring reserves after a dot ( Get , Next , End , Load ...) are read with"}, {"u": "concepts.html#braces-instead-of-the-dot-operator", "t": "Core concepts", "h": "Braces instead of the dot operator", "x": "Braces instead of the dot operator # Ring lets you write { } after an object, and everything inside is evaluated on that object . It is a simple way to configure a .NET object, and all the samples in this documentation use it: oInfo = new ProcessStartInfo() { FileName = \"cmd\" Arguments = \"/c ver\" RedirectStandardOutput = True UseShellExecute = False CreateNoWindow = True } This is exactly the same as writing oInfo.FileName = \"cmd\" line by line. Inside the braces you can assign properties, call methods ( setClickEvent(\"...\") , Controls.Add(oButton) ) and use += . Use it on any object: oForm { H"}, {"u": "concepts.html#enums-are-strings", "t": "Core concepts", "h": "Enums are strings", "x": "Enums are strings # Enum values are strings in both directions : oForm { WindowState = \"Maximized\" FormBorderStyle = \"FixedDialog\" } oDate = new DateTime(2024,12,25) ? oDate.DayOfWeek # \"Wednesday\" oLabel.TextAlign = \"MiddleCenter\" Flag combinations use the .NET text form: \"Top, Left\" ."}, {"u": "concepts.html#value-types-and-dnnull", "t": "Core concepts", "h": "Value types and dnNull()", "x": "Value types and dnNull() # Structs ( Point , Size , Color , DateTime ) are objects like any other. Their constructors take the numbers: new Point(10,20) , new DateTime(2024,12,25) : oButton { Location = new Point(10,20) Size = new Size(100,30) } oPictureBox.Image = dnNull() # the .NET null dnNull() is the .NET null . A .NET method that returns null gives an object whose isNull() is true, and dnIsNull(value) asks the same of any value: it is true for a null object and false for everything else (a number, a text, a list, an object that is not null), so it needs no check of the type first. oValue"}, {"u": "concepts.html#out-and-ref-parameters", "t": "Core concepts", "h": "out and ref parameters", "x": "out and ref parameters # A method with out or ref parameters returns a list : [result, out values...] : aResult = oDict.TryGetValue(\"key\") # [1, value] or [0, the default of the type: 0 for a number, the null for a text] if aResult[1] ? aResult[2] ok aParsed = new Int32().TryParse(\"123\") # [1, 123]"}, {"u": "concepts.html#extension-methods", "t": "Core concepts", "h": "Extension methods", "x": "Extension methods # An extension method is a static method that C# writes as if it were a method of another type: table.AddColumn(\"Name\") is TableExtensions.AddColumn(table, \"Name\") (this one is from the Spectre.Console package, which has to be wrapped first: Your own assemblies ). The generated Ring class of the extended type has a method for each extension method that is in the same generated set, so it is called like any method of the object. The classes that implement an interface get the extension methods of the interface, and so do the interfaces that inherit it ( oServices.BuildServiceP"}, {"u": "concepts.html#interfaces-and-classes-that-are-not-public", "t": "Core concepts", "h": "Interfaces and classes that are not public", "x": "Interfaces and classes that are not public # Many methods return an interface ( IServiceScope , ICollection ) or an object of a class that is not public (a row of a database library, for example). The object is wrapped as the interface named after the class when there is one ( XLWorksheet comes back as IXLWorksheet ), otherwise as the most specific interface that has a Ring class. A wrapped interface has the members that it inherits from other interfaces ( oScope.Dispose() from IDisposable , oKeys.GetEnumerator() on an ICollection ), and the methods that a class implements explicitly are found"}, {"u": "concepts.html#generics", "t": "Core concepts", "h": "Generics", "x": "Generics # The most useful closed generic types have classes: StringList , NumberList , ObjectList ( List<T> ), StringDictionary , NumberDictionary , ObjectDictionary ( Dictionary<string,T> ), StringSet , StringQueue , StringStack . See Collections & LINQ . Any other closed generic type can be created without a class: oList = dnNewGeneric(\"System.Collections.Generic.List\",[\"System.Int32\"],[]) oList.callMethod(\"Add\",[5]) A generic type has a class of its own too ( new HashSet([ :typeArguments = [\"System.Int32\"] ]) ), and a generic method whose type argument only the result shows takes :typeArgu"}, {"u": "concepts.html#numbers-for-an-object", "t": "Core concepts", "h": "Numbers for an object", "x": "Numbers for an object # Ring has one kind of number. Where a .NET parameter has a type ( int , double , long ) the number becomes that type; where the parameter is an object (an argument of String.Format , an item of params object[] , a value of a Dictionary<string, object> ), a whole number is an Int32 and any other number is a Double . That is what \"{0:D3}\" and \"{0:X}\" need, and what JSON and a database write as 5 . A number beyond the range of Int32 is a Double . For another exact type, dnBox makes the value: ? dnStatic(\"System.String\",\"Format\",[\"{0:D3} {1:X}\",[7,255]]) # 007 FF oTen = dnBo"}, {"u": "concepts.html#return-values", "t": "Core concepts", "h": "Return values", "x": "Return values # .NET returns Ring gets string string numbers ( int , long , double , decimal ...) number bool 1 / 0 enum string byte[] string: a Ring text of bytes, binary safe (a copy) arrays and collections ( string[] , List<T> ...) a wrapped object; toList() copies it into a Ring list any other object a wrapped object (generated class, or a plain DotNetObject ) null an object whose isNull() is true"}, {"u": "concepts.html#bytes-and-byte-buffers", "t": "Core concepts", "h": "Bytes and byte buffers", "x": "Bytes and byte buffers # A Ring text can hold any byte, so a byte[] that .NET returns is a Ring text ( oStream.ToArray() , File.ReadAllBytes , BitConverter.GetBytes ), and a text that you pass to a byte[] parameter becomes the same bytes ( File.WriteAllBytes(path, cBytes) , stream.Write(cBytes, 0, len(cBytes)) ). Both are copies . A method that fills an array that you pass in has to work on the array itself, so it needs a byte buffer: an object that holds a live byte[] . oBuffer = dnBuffer(1024) # a byte[] of 1024 bytes n = oStream.Read(oBuffer, 0, 1024) # .NET writes into the array cBytes = d"}, {"u": "concepts.html#errors", "t": "Core concepts", "h": "Errors", "x": "Errors # A .NET exception becomes a Ring error and can be caught with try / catch : try new Int32().Parse(\"abc\") catch ? cCatchError done DotNet Error: System.FormatException: The input string 'abc' was not in a correct format. Set the environment variable RINGDOTNET_TRACE=1 to get the .NET stack trace in the message."}, {"u": "concepts.html#plain-objects", "t": "Core concepts", "h": "Plain objects", "x": "Plain objects # When no class exists for a type, or when you want the generic style, every wrapped object is also a DotNetObject and has: Method What it does callMethod(name, [args]) call a method; the values are in order, also for a name that has several forms ( oKey.callMethod(\"SetValue\",[\"Port\",8080,\"DWord\"]) ) getProp(name) / setProp(name, value) read or write a property or field toString() ToString() of the object typeName() full .NET type name toList() copy an array or collection into a Ring list isNull() true for the .NET null release() release the handle at once (Ring does it for you w"}, {"u": "concepts.html#where-to-go-next", "t": "Core concepts", "h": "Where to go next", "x": "Where to go next # Core functions : every function of the bridge in one place. Advanced .NET types : generic types and methods, operators, spans, ref structs, pointers. Collections & LINQ and Callbacks & events : the two topics that need the most explanation. Rules and limits : read it before a long program."}, {"u": "advanced-types.html", "t": "Advanced .NET types", "h": "", "x": "The core concepts cover classes, methods, properties, enums and errors. This page is for the types that need a little more: each section says what Ring gives and what it gets back. Most programs never need them; they are here for the libraries that do (a serializer with Deserialize<T> , a date library with operators, a byte-level API with Span<T> )."}, {"u": "advanced-types.html#generic-types", "t": "Advanced .NET types", "h": "Generic types", "x": "Generic types # A generic type also has a Ring class: the class of its definition ( List , Dictionary , HashSet , SortedSet ...). Its objects are made with the type arguments in the named list, together with the constructor parameters if there are any, and the same class wraps every closed type of the definition that .NET gives back (a Dictionary<string, int> returned by a method is a Dictionary ). When another type has the name already ( Queue and Stack of System.Collections ), the name of the generic one ends with the number of its type arguments. dnImport(\"System.Collections.Generic\") oNumb"}, {"u": "advanced-types.html#generic-methods", "t": "Advanced .NET types", "h": "Generic methods", "x": "Generic methods # Generic methods work too (the bridge infers the type arguments from the values), which is what makes LINQ possible. When a type argument appears only in the result ( JsonSerializer.Deserialize<T>(json) ), the values cannot tell it, so you give it: in the named form with :typeArguments , a list of type names, or with callGeneric on a type object ( dnType ) or on an object that wraps an instance. dnImport(\"System.Text.Json\") oJson = new JsonSerializer() oTags = oJson.Deserialize([ :typeArguments = [\"System.Collections.Generic.List`1[[System.String]]\"], :json = '[\"a\",\"b\"]' ]) ? "}, {"u": "advanced-types.html#nested-types", "t": "Advanced .NET types", "h": "Nested types", "x": "Nested types # A nested type has a class too, named after the types around it: SqlMapper.GridReader of Dapper is SqlMapperGridReader , Control.ControlCollection of Windows Forms is ControlCollection (its name in the manifest)."}, {"u": "advanced-types.html#exceptions-and-attributes", "t": "Advanced .NET types", "h": "Exceptions and attributes", "x": "Exceptions and attributes # Exceptions and attributes have classes too: an exception that .NET gives back ( oError.InnerException , the Exception of a task) is wrapped by the class of its type ( ArgumentException , AggregateException ) with its properties and methods. A .NET error that a call raises is still a Ring error with the message; the class is for the exception objects . Only the types marked obsolete are left out."}, {"u": "advanced-types.html#operators-and-conversions", "t": "Advanced .NET types", "h": "Operators and conversions", "x": "Operators and conversions # The operators of a .NET type work in Ring: a class with op_Addition , op_Equality , op_LessThan and the like gets the Ring operator method, so + - * / % = != < > <= >= & | ^ << >> and the unary minus call them. The object may come on either side ( 5 + oBig ); when the type has no op_Equality or op_LessThan , = uses Equals and < uses IComparable or IComparable<T> (a type that only has CompareTo gets the operators < > <= >= ). A cast of C# is convertTo . dnImport(\"System\") dnImport(\"System.Numerics\") oOneHour = new TimeSpan().FromHours(1) oHalfHour = new TimeSpan().Fr"}, {"u": "advanced-types.html#indexers-with-several-indexes", "t": "Advanced .NET types", "h": "Indexers with several indexes", "x": "Indexers with several indexes # An indexer with several indexes ( matrix[row, column] ) takes the indexes as a list: oMatrix.Item([1,2]) reads and oMatrix.SetItem([1,2],4.5) writes. A type that has a one-index indexer too keeps its plain form: Item(1) ."}, {"u": "advanced-types.html#delegate-types-with-extension-methods", "t": "Advanced .NET types", "h": "Delegate types with extension methods", "x": "Delegate types with extension methods # A delegate is a callback in Ring ( dnCallback ) and has no class of its own. A delegate type that extension methods extend does have one, as a library of parser combinators does ( Parser<T> of Sprache): an object of that type has the extension methods as its methods, and Invoke calls the delegate itself. # the Sprache package, wrapped as in \"Your own assemblies\" and loaded with dnLoad and dnImportFrom oDigits = dnStatic(\"Sprache.Parse\",\"Digit\",[]).AtLeastOnce().Text() ? oDigits.Parse(\"12345\") # 12345"}, {"u": "advanced-types.html#spans", "t": "Advanced .NET types", "h": "Spans", "x": "Spans # A parameter of type Span<T> or ReadOnlySpan<T> takes what Ring has for an array: a text ( char ), a byte string or a list ( byte , numbers), or an object that is an array. A Span<T> that the method fills takes a number, the size of a new buffer, or a buffer you made; the filled buffer is part of the answer, after the result and the out values (a list of bytes is a byte string, a list of chars a text). A Span<T> that comes back is an array (a text for char , a byte string for byte ), and a span passed by reference ( ref or out ) is read from the array you give and answered as an array, "}, {"u": "advanced-types.html#ref-structs", "t": "Advanced .NET types", "h": "Ref structs", "x": "Ref structs # A constructor can take a Span<T> or a ref struct the same way ( new Message(\"hello\",3) ). A ref struct of a library ( MessagePackWriter , Utf8JsonReader , a parser cursor) cannot be held by Ring, so the bridge makes it for the call from one value, with its public op_Implicit , a constructor whose first parameter takes it, or a static factory with one value ( FromString , Create... , Parse... , Wrap... ) (a text, bytes, an object such as an IBufferWriter ); what the method does to the struct is lost, and a ref struct that has no such value, or that is only an out parameter, is sti"}, {"u": "advanced-types.html#properties-fields-and-references", "t": "Advanced .NET types", "h": "Properties, fields and references", "x": "Properties, fields and references # A property (or field) of those types is read the same way: a Span property is an array (a text for char ), a ref struct property is read like a ref struct result, a pointer is its address, and a ref property is its value ( oMemory.Span , oStream.PositionPointer ). These properties are read-only in Ring. A method that returns by reference ( ref int Number(int i) ) answers the value too; Ring cannot write through the reference."}, {"u": "advanced-types.html#pointers", "t": "Advanced .NET types", "h": "Pointers", "x": "Pointers # A pointer parameter ( byte* , char* , void* , int* ) takes an address or a buffer. A number is the address (from NativeMemory.Alloc , Marshal.AllocHGlobal , a native library); a text , a byte string , a list of numbers or a ByteBuffer is a buffer that is pinned for the call, so the method can read it (a text and a byte string end with a zero, for C strings) and a ByteBuffer is the live array that the method may fill. The address of a buffer is valid during the call only: a method that keeps the pointer needs memory that you allocate. A pointer that a method returns is its address, a"}, {"u": "advanced-types.html#asynchronous-streams", "t": "Advanced .NET types", "h": "Asynchronous streams", "x": "Asynchronous streams # Ring has no await foreach , so an IAsyncEnumerable<T> that a method returns is read in one of two ways. toList() waits for every item and gives a Ring list; asyncEnumerator() gives the enumerator, asyncNext() waits for the next item (true while there is one), Current is the item and asyncDispose() ends the enumerator, which is the way to read a long or endless stream one item at a time. # a channel of numbers: ReadAllAsync() returns an IAsyncEnumerable<int> oChannel = dnType(\"System.Threading.Channels.Channel\").callGeneric(\"CreateUnbounded\",[\"System.Int32\"],[]) oWriter ="}, {"u": "advanced-types.html#where-to-go-next", "t": "Advanced .NET types", "h": "Where to go next", "x": "Where to go next # Core concepts : the names, overloads, properties and enums that every program uses. Collections & LINQ : the generic collections, and the generic methods of LINQ. The generator : how the classes are made, and the audit that counts what Ring can and cannot use. Rules and limits : read it before a long program."}, {"u": "core-api.html", "t": "Core functions", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "core-api.html#runtime", "t": "Core functions", "h": "Runtime", "x": "Runtime # dnVersion() # Returns the version of the .NET runtime hosted in the process. load \"dotnet.ring\" ? dnVersion() # .NET 9.0.15 dnUseDesktop(), dnUseWeb(), dnUseAll(), dnUseData() # Select the runtime mode before the first .NET call. You normally do not call them: the using { } block calls the right one when it sees the namespaces (see Getting started )."}, {"u": "core-api.html#importing-classes", "t": "Core functions", "h": "Importing classes", "x": "Importing classes # Function What it does using { \"System\" \"System.Text\" } Loads the generated wrapper classes of each namespace (once), like C#'s using . A value that is not a text raises an error. dnImport(\"System.IO\") The function behind using , handy when the name is computed. Returns true. Raises an error when no classes were generated for the name. dnImportFrom(\"myclasses/\",\"MyCompany.Tools\") Loads classes generated for your own assembly with wrapassembly.ring ; the other namespaces of that assembly are loaded from the same folder when an object of one of their types comes back. See Your"}, {"u": "core-api.html#loading-assemblies", "t": "Core functions", "h": "Loading assemblies", "x": "Loading assemblies # Function What it does dnLoad(\"path/to/Library.dll\") Loads an assembly from a path and returns its name. Its dependencies are found next to it. dnLoadName(\"System.Xml\") Loads an assembly by name."}, {"u": "core-api.html#without-wrapper-classes", "t": "Core functions", "h": "Without wrapper classes", "x": "Without wrapper classes # Any type is usable without generated classes. The generated classes ( new X(...) , oObject.Method() , braces) are the usual way to write code; these functions are for a type that has no class, or a constructor that new cannot choose: # a type, for static members oConsole = dnType(\"System.Console\") oConsole.callMethod(\"WriteLine\",[\"Hello from .NET\"]) # a static call in one line ? dnStatic(\"System.Math\",\"Max\",[3,9]) # 9 # a new object oSB = dnNew(\"System.Text.StringBuilder\",[\"Hello\"]) oSB.callMethod(\"Append\",[\", Ring!\"]) ? oSB.toString() # Hello, Ring! ? oSB.getProp(\"Le"}, {"u": "core-api.html#dotnetobject", "t": "Core functions", "h": "DotNetObject", "x": "DotNetObject # Every .NET object that Ring holds is a DotNetObject (the generated classes derive from it): oObject.callMethod(\"Method\",[arg1,arg2]) # call a method oObject.callGeneric(\"Method\",[\"System.Int32\"],[arg1]) # a generic method with its type arguments oObject.getProp(\"Name\") # read a property or field oObject.setProp(\"Name\",value) # write it oObject.toString() # ToString() oObject.typeName() # \"System.Text.StringBuilder\" oObject.toList() # an array or a collection as a Ring list oObject.isNull() # true for the .NET null oObject.convertTo(\"System.Int64\") # an explicit conversion (a cas"}, {"u": "core-api.html#dnrequest", "t": "Core functions", "h": "dnRequest", "x": "dnRequest # dnRequest(operation, params) sends one request to the bridge; everything above is built on it. The operations include type new newnamed call callnamed get set tostring typename tolist describe load loadname release version , among others that the generated classes use. You normally never need it. The generated methods do not even go through it for the common calls: they use the native functions dotnet_call , dotnet_callv and dotnet_callo ( How it works )."}, {"u": "core-api.html#callbacks", "t": "Core functions", "h": "Callbacks", "x": "Callbacks # dnCallback(code) passes Ring code where a delegate is expected. See Callbacks & events ."}, {"u": "core-api.html#test-helpers", "t": "Core functions", "h": "Test helpers", "x": "Test helpers # dnAssert(\"name\", actual, expected) # counts a pass or prints FAIL: name expected <..> got <..> dnTestSummary() # prints \"TESTS: n passed, m failed\" nDotNetPass and nDotNetFail hold the counters. See Testing ."}, {"u": "core-api.html#where-to-go-next", "t": "Core functions", "h": "Where to go next", "x": "Where to go next # Collections & LINQ : lists, dictionaries and queries. Callbacks & events : passing Ring code to .NET. The tutorial The generic functions shows when each of these functions is needed; the programs of the tutorials otherwise use the generated classes ( new , the dot and braces)."}, {"u": "collections-linq.html", "t": "Collections & LINQ", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "collections-linq.html#typed-collections", "t": "Collections & LINQ", "h": "Typed collections", "x": "Typed collections # The common closed generic collections have ready-made classes ( using { \"System.Collections.Generic\" } ): Class .NET type StringList , NumberList , ObjectList List<string> , List<double> , List<object> StringDictionary , NumberDictionary , ObjectDictionary Dictionary<string, T> StringSet HashSet<string> StringQueue , StringStack Queue<string> , Stack<string> using { \"System.Collections.Generic\" } oList = new StringList() oList.Add(\"banana\") oList.Add(\"apple\") oList.Add(\"cherry\") oList.Sort(NULL) ? oList.Count aItems = oList.toList() # toList() copies the collection to a Rin"}, {"u": "collections-linq.html#other-collection-classes", "t": "Collections & LINQ", "h": "Other collection classes", "x": "Other collection classes # System.Collections , System.Collections.Specialized , System.Collections.Concurrent and System.Collections.ObjectModel are wrapped completely: ArrayList , Hashtable , SortedList , Stack , Queue , BitArray , NameValueCollection , ObservableCollection and so on. samples/UsingDotNet/introduction/test10_collections.ring # Collections of the .NET class library: dictionaries and the specialized and generic collections load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System.Collections\" \"System.Collections.Specialized\" \"System.Collections.Generic\" } # Word frequency with Dictionary<string,doubl"}, {"u": "collections-linq.html#sorting-and-searching-with-ring-code", "t": "Collections & LINQ", "h": "Sorting and searching with Ring code", "x": "Sorting and searching with Ring code # Methods that take a delegate ( Predicate , Comparison , Action ) accept dnCallback(...) . The Ring code reads aDotNetArgs and sets xDotNetResult : oList.FindAll(dnCallback(\"xDotNetResult = left(aDotNetArgs[1],1) = 'a'\")) oList.Sort([ :comparison = dnCallback(\"xDotNetResult = len(aDotNetArgs[1]) - len(aDotNetArgs[2])\") ]) oList.@ForEach(dnCallback(\"? aDotNetArgs[1]\")) See Callbacks & events ."}, {"u": "collections-linq.html#linq", "t": "Collections & LINQ", "h": "LINQ", "x": "LINQ # using { \"System.Linq\" } loads Enumerable and a fluent wrapper, dnQuery(...) , that works on a Ring list or any .NET collection . The lambda is Ring code: it reads aDotNetArgs[1] and writes xDotNetResult . using { \"System.Linq\" } aSales = [120, 45, 300, 15, 210, 99, 81] aTop = dnQuery(aSales).Where(\"xDotNetResult = aDotNetArgs[1] >= 100\").OrderByDescending(\"xDotNetResult = aDotNetArgs[1]\").Take(3).ToList() ? dnQuery(aSales).Count() ? dnQuery(aSales).Sum(\"\") ? dnQuery(aSales).Average(\"\") ? dnQuery(aSales).Max(\"\") samples/UsingDotNet/introduction/test11_linq.ring # LINQ over Ring lists and"}, {"u": "collections-linq.html#where-to-go-next", "t": "Collections & LINQ", "h": "Where to go next", "x": "Where to go next # Callbacks & events : the code you pass to Where , Select and the other LINQ methods is a callback. The tutorial chapter Collections and algorithms ."}, {"u": "callbacks-events.html", "t": "Callbacks & events", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "callbacks-events.html#callbacks-with-dncallback", "t": "Callbacks & events", "h": "Callbacks with dnCallback()", "x": "Callbacks with dnCallback() # Pass dnCallback(code) wherever a .NET delegate is expected: Action , Func , Predicate , Comparison , EventHandler , Task continuations... The bridge builds a delegate of the exact type . When .NET invokes it, the Ring code runs, as with events in RingQt. It can read aDotNetArgs (the list of the delegate arguments) and set xDotNetResult (converted to the delegate's return type). nTotal = 0 oList.@ForEach(dnCallback(\"nTotal += len(aDotNetArgs[1])\")) # ForEach is a Ring keyword: @ForEach oList.Sort([ :comparison = dnCallback(\"xDotNetResult = len(aDotNetArgs[1]) - len"}, {"u": "callbacks-events.html#events", "t": "Callbacks & events", "h": "Events", "x": "Events # Every public .NET event gets two methods, as in RingQt: setXXXEvent(code) and clearXXXEvent() . The code runs when the event is raised and can read aDotNetArgs , which is [sender, eventArgs] (both .NET objects). Each event has one handler; setting it again replaces it. using { \"System.Collections.ObjectModel\" } oList = new ObservableStringList() { setCollectionChangedEvent(\"? 'changed: ' + aDotNetArgs[2].getProp('Action')\") Add(\"one\") } CollectionChanged event #1 CollectionChanged event #2 CollectionChanged event #3 Events received : 3 Items : 1 (second handler) Items : 3 action = Add"}, {"u": "callbacks-events.html#calling-ring-from-net-code-you-wrote", "t": "Callbacks & events", "h": "Calling Ring from .NET code you wrote", "x": "Calling Ring from .NET code you wrote # Your own assembly can call Ring as well: when a parameter is typed object , the bridge passes the callback unchanged and your code can call RingDotNet.Bridge.CallRing(callback, returnType, args) . This is how the Entity Framework hooks ( onSaving , onLog ) and value converters are built \u2014 see Entity Framework reference ."}, {"u": "callbacks-events.html#where-to-go-next", "t": "Callbacks & events", "h": "Where to go next", "x": "Where to go next # The .NET class library : the classes you will call most often (text, dates, files, processes). The tutorial chapter Windows Forms is full of event handlers."}, {"u": "foundation.html", "t": "The .NET class library", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "foundation.html#what-is-wrapped", "t": "The .NET class library", "h": "What is wrapped", "x": "What is wrapped # Every namespace below has generated classes, loaded on demand with using { \"Name\" } . Area Namespaces Core System , System.Globalization , System.Numerics , System.Reflection , System.Runtime.InteropServices , System.Runtime.Loader , System.ComponentModel , System.Resources Text System.Text , System.Text.RegularExpressions , System.Text.Json , System.Text.Json.Nodes , System.Text.Encodings.Web , System.Text.Unicode Files and streams System.IO , System.IO.Compression , System.IO.Pipes , System.IO.MemoryMappedFiles , System.Formats.Tar Collections System.Collections , .Generic "}, {"u": "foundation.html#dates-strings-conversions-math", "t": "The .NET class library", "h": "Dates, strings, conversions, math", "x": "Dates, strings, conversions, math # DateTime , TimeSpan , Convert , Math , String helpers, Guid , Random , Environment : samples/UsingDotNet/introduction/test08_system.ring # Dates and times, strings, conversions and math from the .NET class library load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" } # Dates and times oNow = new DateTime(2024,12,25).AddHours(18.5) ? \"Christmas 2024 : \" + oNow.Format(\"dddd, dd MMMM yyyy HH:mm\") ? \"Plus 10 days : \" + oNow.AddDays(10).Format(\"yyyy-MM-dd\") ? \"Day of week : \" + oNow.DayOfWeek oSpan = new TimeSpan().FromMinutes(135) ? \"135 minutes : \" + oSpan.ToString() + \" (\" + "}, {"u": "foundation.html#files-regular-expressions-and-compression", "t": "The .NET class library", "h": "Files, regular expressions and compression", "x": "Files, regular expressions and compression # File , Directory , Path , StreamReader / StreamWriter , MemoryStream , Regex , GZipStream : samples/UsingDotNet/introduction/test09_io_regex.ring # Text files, compression and regular expressions load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.IO\" \"System.IO.Compression\" \"System.Text.RegularExpressions\" } oPath = new Path() oFile = new File() cDir = oPath.Combine([oPath.GetTempPath(),\"ringdotnet_demo\"]) new Directory().CreateDirectory(cDir) # Text files cLog = oPath.Combine([cDir,\"log.txt\"]) oWriter = new StreamWriter(cLog) oWriter.WriteLine(\"2024-05-17"}, {"u": "foundation.html#networking", "t": "The .NET class library", "h": "Networking", "x": "Networking # Uri , IPAddress , Dns , HttpClient , WebUtility , Encoding \u2014 a program that does not need the internet to run: samples/UsingDotNet/introduction/test12_network.ring # HTTP: a small web server and a web client in one program load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.Text\" \"System.Net\" \"System.Net.Http\" \"System.Threading.Tasks\" } # A tiny web server and client in one Ring program: .NET runs the requests on its thread pool, # Ring waits for the tasks and answers cPrefix = \"http://localhost:\" + (20000 + random(20000)) + \"/\" oServer = new HttpListener() oServer.Prefixes.Add(cPrefix) o"}, {"u": "foundation.html#cryptography-and-globalization", "t": "The .NET class library", "h": "Cryptography and globalization", "x": "Cryptography and globalization # Hashes ( SHA256 , MD5 ), HMAC, random bytes, AES, culture-aware formatting: samples/UsingDotNet/introduction/test13_security.ring # Hashes, HMAC and AES encryption load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.Globalization\" \"System.Security.Cryptography\" \"System.Diagnostics\" } # Hashes and HMAC oConvert = new Convert() ? \"SHA256(abc) : \" + lower(oConvert.ToHexString(new SHA256().HashData(\"abc\"))) ? \"MD5(abc) : \" + lower(oConvert.ToHexString(new MD5().HashData(\"abc\"))) oHmac = new HMACSHA256().@New([ :key = \"secret key\" ]) ? \"HMAC : \" + left(lower(oConvert.ToHexS"}, {"u": "foundation.html#xml-json-and-data-tables", "t": "The .NET class library", "h": "XML, JSON and data tables", "x": "XML, JSON and data tables # XmlDocument , XPath , System.Text.Json , DataTable and DataView \u2014 no database needed: samples/UsingDotNet/introduction/test14_xml_json_data.ring # XML with XPath, JSON and data tables load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System.Xml\" \"System.Text.Json\" \"System.Data\" } # XML with XPath oDoc = new XmlDocument() oDoc.LoadXml(\"<shop><item price='3.5'>apple</item><item price='1.2'>fig</item><item price='9'>melon</item></shop>\") oItems = oDoc.SelectNodes(\"//item[@price > 2]\") ? \"Items over 2 : \" + oItems.Count for nLoop = 0 to oItems.Count - 1 oItem = oItems.Item(nLoop) ? \" \" + oIt"}, {"u": "foundation.html#reflection-and-big-numbers", "t": "The .NET class library", "h": "Reflection and big numbers", "x": "Reflection and big numbers # Type , Assembly , MethodInfo , BigInteger , Complex : samples/UsingDotNet/introduction/test15_reflection_numbers.ring # Big numbers, reflection, encoders and concurrent collections load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.Reflection\" \"System.Numerics\" \"System.Text.Encodings.Web\" \"System.Collections.Concurrent\" \"System.Runtime.InteropServices\" } # Big integers: factorial of 40 oBig = new BigInteger() nFactorial = oBig.Parse(\"1\") for nLoop = 2 to 40 nFactorial = oBig.Multiply(nFactorial,nLoop) next ? \"40! : \" + nFactorial.ToString() ? \"2^200 : \" + oBig.Pow(2,200)."}, {"u": "foundation.html#processes-and-threads", "t": "The .NET class library", "h": "Processes and threads", "x": "Processes and threads # using { \"System.Diagnostics\" \"System.Threading.Tasks\" } oInfo = new ProcessStartInfo() { FileName = \"cmd\" Arguments = \"/c ver\" RedirectStandardOutput = True UseShellExecute = False CreateNoWindow = True } oProcess = new Process().Start([ :startInfo = oInfo ]) ? trim(oProcess.StandardOutput.ReadToEnd()) oProcess.WaitForExit(NULL) oTask = new Task().Delay(30) # a Task: wait for it on the Ring thread oTask.Wait(NULL) ? oTask.IsCompleted oResult = new Task().FromResult(\"done\") ? oResult.Result # the Result of a Task<T> Note Ring code runs on the Ring thread only. A callback"}, {"u": "foundation.html#a-bigger-example", "t": "The .NET class library", "h": "A bigger example", "x": "A bigger example # samples/UsingDotNet/introduction/test16_forms_app.ring and demo_todo.ring combine collections, files, JSON and a form. For complete programs see Applications ."}, {"u": "foundation.html#where-to-go-next", "t": "The .NET class library", "h": "Where to go next", "x": "Where to go next # Windows Forms : desktop windows. ASP.NET Core and Entity Framework Core : servers and databases. The tutorial chapters Files and streams and Text and data formats ."}, {"u": "winforms.html", "t": "Windows Forms", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "winforms.html#the-shape-of-a-program", "t": "Windows Forms", "h": "The shape of a program", "x": "The shape of a program # load \"dotnet.ring\" using { # the names start the Windows Desktop runtime and make the Ring thread an STA thread \"System.Drawing\" \"System.Windows.Forms\" # also loads the GUI helpers } oForm = \"\" # globals used by event code oCount = \"\" nClicks = 0 func main oForm = uiForm(\"Click counter\",260,130) oCount = uiLabel(oForm,\"0 clicks\",20,15,220,24) uiButton(oForm,\"Click me\",20,50,100,32,\"clicked()\") uiRun(oForm) func clicked nClicks++ oCount.Text = \"\" + nClicks + \" clicks\" A label and a button: the whole program is about 15 lines The using block first (it starts the desktop "}, {"u": "winforms.html#the-helpers", "t": "Windows Forms", "h": "The helpers", "x": "The helpers # Everything the helpers do can also be written with the plain classes (see the next section); they exist to keep small programs short. They are loaded together with System.Windows.Forms . Helper What it creates uiForm(title, w, h) a form with a client area of w \u00d7 h, centered uiLabel(parent, text, x, y, w, h) a label uiButton(parent, text, x, y, w, h, clickCode) a button; clickCode is Ring code or a function call uiTextBox(parent, x, y, w, h) a text box uiMemo(parent, x, y, w, h) a multi-line text box with scroll bars uiCheckBox(parent, text, x, y, w, h, clickCode) a check box uiLi"}, {"u": "winforms.html#every-control-is-available", "t": "Windows Forms", "h": "Every control is available", "x": "Every control is available # The helpers are optional. The whole of System.Windows.Forms (454 classes), System.Drawing and its sub-namespaces are wrapped, so you can use any control directly: oTree = new TreeView() oTree.Dock = \"Fill\" oRoot = oTree.Nodes.Add(\"Fruit\") oRoot { Nodes.Add(\"Apple\") Nodes.Add(\"Pear\") } oTree.ExpandAll() oForm.Controls.Add(oTree) Group Controls Text and input Label , TextBox , RichTextBox , MaskedTextBox , NumericUpDown , DateTimePicker , MonthCalendar Buttons and choices Button , CheckBox , RadioButton , ComboBox , ListBox , CheckedListBox , TrackBar , ProgressBar L"}, {"u": "winforms.html#events", "t": "Windows Forms", "h": "Events", "x": "Events # oButton.setClickEvent(\"clicked()\") oText.setTextChangedEvent(\"textChanged()\") oForm.setFormClosingEvent(\"closing()\") oPicture.setMouseDownEvent(\"? aDotNetArgs[2].X\") The code of an event runs on the Ring thread when the message loop dispatches it. Details and warnings: Callbacks & events ."}, {"u": "winforms.html#keyboard-in-games", "t": "Windows Forms", "h": "Keyboard in games", "x": "Keyboard in games # A button (or any control that takes the focus) steals the arrow keys, Space and Enter from the form, so the form's KeyDown never sees them. uiGameKeys fixes that: oForm.setKeyDownEvent(\"keyPressed(aDotNetArgs[2].KeyCode)\") uiGameKeys(oForm,\"keyPressed(aDotNetArgs[2].KeyCode)\") # after the controls were added Every control then reports the arrows as normal keys and the form handles them. Tetris, 2048, Sokoban, Snake and Pong use it. samples/UsingDotNet/applications/Tetris \u2014 keys are handled by the form"}, {"u": "winforms.html#self-testing-programs", "t": "Windows Forms", "h": "Self-testing programs", "x": "Self-testing programs # Every sample and application can drive itself and check its results. The checks are in the file selftest/<program>.ring next to the program, and uiRun runs them when the program starts with --selftest : func selfTest oForm.Controls.Item(1).PerformClick() dnAssert(\"one click\",oCount.Text,\"1 clicks\") uiSendKeys(oForm,\"12{+}30{ENTER}\") types into the focused control through the real key processing by posting window messages to your own form \u2014 it can never type into another program. See Testing ."}, {"u": "winforms.html#more", "t": "Windows Forms", "h": "More", "x": "More # Forms samples \u2014 short samples, one idea each. Applications \u2014 43 complete programs. WPF \u2014 the other desktop UI framework."}, {"u": "winforms.html#where-to-go-next", "t": "Windows Forms", "h": "Where to go next", "x": "Where to go next # The tutorial chapter Windows Forms , and the chapter Projects with windows for complete programs. Testing : windows that test themselves."}, {"u": "gallery-forms.html", "t": "Windows Forms samples", "h": "", "x": "Run one with ring 06_listbox.ring from samples/UsingDotNet/Forms ; with --selftest it checks itself and closes. Click Source code on a card to read the program, or click the picture to enlarge it. Hello form The smallest window: a form with a label ring samples/UsingDotNet/Forms/01_hello_form.ring Source code # The smallest window: a form with a label load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System.Drawing\" \"System.Windows.Forms\" } oForm = uiForm(\"Hello\",300,120) oLabel = uiLabel(oForm,\"Hello from Ring and .NET!\",20,40,260,30) oLabel.Font = uiFont(\"Segoe UI\",14,true) uiRun(oForm) Button click A button that"}, {"u": "gallery-apps.html", "t": "Applications", "h": "", "x": "Run one: cd samples/UsingDotNet/applications/Calculator then ring Calculator.ring . With ring Calculator.ring --selftest the application drives its own form, checks the results with dnAssert() and prints SELFTEST OK . Ports of RingQt applications: Calculator, Stopwatch, TicTacToe, FifteenPuzzle, LightsOut, Game2048, MemoryGame, Snake, TowersOfHanoi, AnalogClock, RockPaperScissors, BMICalculator, GameOfLife, Minesweeper, EpochTime, AsciiTable, Calendar, EightQueens, Sudoku, Pong, Tetris, Sokoban, TypingQuiz, JulianDay. The others are new: Notepad, Paint, TodoList, ColorMixer, NumberGuess, Hangm"}, {"u": "wpf.html", "t": "WPF", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "wpf.html#a-first-wpf-window", "t": "WPF", "h": "A first WPF window", "x": "A first WPF window # load \"dotnet.ring\" using { # System.Windows* starts the desktop runtime \"System.Windows\" \"System.Windows.Controls\" } oWindow = wpfWindow(\"Hello\",300,200) oPanel = new StackPanel() oWindow.Content = oPanel oPanel.Children.Add(wpfLabel(\"Hello from WPF\")) wpfRun(oWindow) The using block starts the desktop runtime (it calls dnUseDesktop() when it sees a System.Windows* namespace). Import the WPF namespaces you need: System.Windows , .Controls , .Controls.Primitives , .Media , .Shapes , .Data , .Input , .Documents , .Markup , .Threading , .Media.Imaging , .Media.Animation ..."}, {"u": "wpf.html#names-that-start-with-wpf", "t": "WPF", "h": "Names that start with Wpf", "x": "Names that start with Wpf # WPF has classes with the same names as Windows Forms and System.Drawing classes. To keep both usable in one program, the WPF version of a clashing name starts with Wpf : Windows Forms / System.Drawing WPF Button , TextBox , Label WpfButton , WpfTextBox , WpfLabel Point , Color , Brush WpfPoint , WpfColor , WpfBrush Binding , Rectangle WpfBinding , WpfRectangle The others keep their name: Window , Grid , StackPanel , Canvas , Slider , Ellipse , Thickness ... Note A global variable named like an attribute ( x , width ) is read before obj.X in Ring. Keep program state "}, {"u": "wpf.html#the-helpers", "t": "WPF", "h": "The helpers", "x": "The helpers # Helper Does wpfWindow(title, w, h) a window of the given size, centered wpfLabel(text) , wpfText(text) a Label / TextBlock wpfButton(text, clickCode) a button with a click handler wpfBrush(\"SteelBlue\") , wpfColor(name) a brush or color from a name or #RRGGBB wpfXaml(text) the objects of a XAML text wpfFind(root, name) an element by its x:Name wpfRun(window) shows the window and runs the message loop wpfRun(window) with --selftest runs the checks of selftest/<program>.ring (as for Windows Forms); wpfTest(window, code) and wpfEnd() do the same steps by hand wpfPixel(element, w, h, "}, {"u": "wpf.html#xaml", "t": "WPF", "h": "XAML", "x": "XAML # Describe the window in XAML and find the elements by name: cXaml = '<Window xmlns=\"http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation\" xmlns:x=\"http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml\" Title=\"From XAML\" Width=\"320\" Height=\"180\">' cXaml += '<StackPanel Margin=\"15\"><TextBlock x:Name=\"message\" FontSize=\"18\" Text=\"Press the button\"/>' cXaml += '<Button x:Name=\"press\" Content=\"Press me\" Margin=\"0,10,0,0\"/></StackPanel></Window>' oWindow = wpfXaml(cXaml) oMessage = wpfFind(oWindow,\"message\") oPress = wpfFind(oWindow,\"press\") oPress.setClickEvent(\"oMessage.Text = 'The XAML button w"}, {"u": "wpf.html#data-binding", "t": "WPF", "h": "Data binding", "x": "Data binding # Bind a property of one element to a property of another \u2014 without code: oSlider = new Slider() oSlider.Maximum = 100 oText = new WpfTextBox() oBinding = new WpfBinding().@New([ :path = \"Value\" ]) oBinding.Source = oSlider oText.SetBinding(oText.TextProperty,oBinding) samples/UsingDotNet/Wpf/03_binding_slider.ring \u2014 the text follows the slider"}, {"u": "wpf.html#shapes-and-brushes", "t": "WPF", "h": "Shapes and brushes", "x": "Shapes and brushes # oCanvas = new Canvas() oRect = new WpfRectangle() { Width = 80 Height = 50 Fill = wpfBrush(\"SteelBlue\") } oCanvas.Children.Add(oRect) oCanvas.SetLeft(oRect,10) oCanvas.SetTop(oRect,10) samples/UsingDotNet/Wpf/07_shapes_pixels.ring"}, {"u": "wpf.html#layout", "t": "WPF", "h": "Layout", "x": "Layout # Grid , StackPanel , DockPanel , WrapPanel , Canvas , UniformGrid and the Thickness margin work as in XAML: samples/UsingDotNet/Wpf/08_grid_layout.ring"}, {"u": "wpf.html#events", "t": "WPF", "h": "Events", "x": "Events # Events work like Windows Forms events: setClickEvent , setValueChangedEvent , setTextChangedEvent ... Routed events can be raised from tests with RaiseEvent ."}, {"u": "wpf.html#applications", "t": "WPF", "h": "Applications", "x": "Applications # samples/UsingDotNet/applications/WpfCalculator \u2014 a calculator whose UI is XAML. samples/UsingDotNet/applications/WpfColorMixer \u2014 sliders that mix a color. samples/UsingDotNet/applications/WpfCalculator More: WPF samples \u2014 every one with a screenshot and the source."}, {"u": "wpf.html#where-to-go-next", "t": "WPF", "h": "Where to go next", "x": "Where to go next # The tutorial chapter WPF . ASP.NET Core : the guide for web programs."}, {"u": "gallery-wpf.html", "t": "WPF samples", "h": "", "x": "Run one with ring 06_xaml_window.ring from samples/UsingDotNet/Wpf ; with --selftest it checks itself and closes. See the WPF guide for the concepts. Hello window The smallest WPF window ring samples/UsingDotNet/Wpf/01_hello_window.ring Source code # The smallest WPF window load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System.Windows\" \"System.Windows.Controls\" \"System.Windows.Controls.Primitives\" \"System.Windows.Media\" \"System.Windows.Shapes\" \"System.Windows.Data\" \"System.Windows.Threading\" \"System\" } oWindow = wpfWindow(\"Hello WPF\",320,160) oPanel = new StackPanel() oWindow.Content = oPanel oText = new TextBlo"}, {"u": "aspnet.html", "t": "ASP.NET Core", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "aspnet.html#a-web-server-in-a-few-lines", "t": "ASP.NET Core", "h": "A web server in a few lines", "x": "A web server in a few lines # load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"RingDotNet.Web\" } # starts the ASP.NET Core mode and loads the web server oServer = new WebServer(\"http://localhost:5080\") oServer.onGet(\"/\",\"home\") oServer.onPost(\"/api/echo\",\"echo\") oServer.run() # serves until stop() func home oRequest return \"<h1>Hello</h1>\" + oRequest.path() func echo oRequest return [201,\"text/plain\",\"you sent \" + oRequest.body()] using { \"RingDotNet.Web\" } starts the ASP.NET Core runtime and loads the web server of RingDotNet (it calls dnUseWeb() for you). A handler is a Ring function that receives the request an"}, {"u": "aspnet.html#handlers-and-responses", "t": "ASP.NET Core", "h": "Handlers and responses", "x": "Handlers and responses # A handler returns one of: Return value Response a text status 200, text/html [status, contentType, text] exactly that webJson(value) status 200, application/json webJson turns a Ring list into a JSON array and a list of [name, value] pairs into a JSON object (nested values too): func listTodos oRequest aList = [] for aTodo in aTodos aList + [[\"id\",aTodo[1]],[\"text\",aTodo[2]]] next return webJson(aList) GET /api/todos"}, {"u": "aspnet.html#the-request", "t": "ASP.NET Core", "h": "The request", "x": "The request # Method Returns oRequest.method() \"GET\" , \"POST\" ... oRequest.path() /api/todos oRequest.queryString() ?id=1 oRequest.query(\"id\") one query value oRequest.body() the body as text oRequest.header(\"Accept\") a request header oRequest.form(\"name\") a form field of a POST oRequest.setHeader(name, value) add a response header oRequest.context() the ASP.NET HttpContext , for everything else"}, {"u": "aspnet.html#the-webserver", "t": "ASP.NET Core", "h": "The WebServer", "x": "The WebServer # Method Does new WebServer(url) starts Kestrel; port 0 picks a free port onGet(path, handler) , onPost , onPut , onDelete routes ( route(method, path, handler) for any method) notFound(handler) the handler for unknown paths (default: Not found: GET /x ) url() the real address (when the port was 0) run() serve until stop() serveOne(milliseconds) handle one waiting request; true if one was handled stop() , close() stop serving, stop the server An error raised by a handler becomes a 500 response with the message. nRequests counts the requests served."}, {"u": "aspnet.html#one-ring-thread", "t": "ASP.NET Core", "h": "One Ring thread", "x": "One Ring thread # Kestrel runs on .NET threads and Ring code may only run on the Ring thread. So the requests are queued and serveOne() / run() take them off the queue on the Ring thread. This is the reason you can use any Ring code and global state in a handler without locks \u2014 and also why handlers do not run in parallel. You can mix serving with your own loop (for example next to a GUI: name \"System.Windows.Forms\" and \"RingDotNet.Web\" in the same using block, or call dnUseAll() ): while true oServer.serveOne(100) # handle a request if one arrives within 100 ms doOtherWork() end"}, {"u": "aspnet.html#a-complete-application", "t": "ASP.NET Core", "h": "A complete application", "x": "A complete application # samples/UsingDotNet/applications/WebApi is a to-do list with a web page and a JSON API. It tests itself by calling its own server with HttpClient : samples/UsingDotNet/applications/WebApi/WebApi.ring # A small web application written in Ring on ASP.NET Core: a to-do list with a web page and a JSON API # ring WebApi.ring then open http://localhost:5080/ # GET /api/todos POST /api/todos (the text is the body) DELETE /api/todos?id=1 load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.Net.Http\" \"System.Threading.Tasks\" \"RingDotNet.Web\" } aTodos = [] # [id, text] nNextId = 1 oServe"}, {"u": "aspnet.html#all-of-aspnet-core", "t": "ASP.NET Core", "h": "All of ASP.NET Core", "x": "All of ASP.NET Core # The ASP.NET Core classes are wrapped too: Microsoft.AspNetCore.* (Http, Routing, Authentication, Authorization, Mvc, Builder, Hosting, StaticFiles...), Microsoft.Extensions.* (DependencyInjection, Configuration, Logging, Hosting, Caching...) and Microsoft.Net.Http.Headers . Class names that clash with other namespaces start with Asp . Extension methods ( AddSingleton , AddAuthentication , BuildServiceProvider ...) are methods of the classes that they extend ( Core concepts ). using { \"Microsoft.AspNetCore.Http\" } oPath = new PathString().@New([ :value = \"/api/items\" ]) ? "}, {"u": "aspnet.html#what-is-not-supported", "t": "ASP.NET Core", "h": "What is not supported", "x": "What is not supported # MVC controllers and Razor pages written in C# \u2014 the web server is the Ring one. Handlers that run in parallel (there is one Ring thread). Calling Ring from middleware you wrote in .NET."}, {"u": "aspnet.html#where-to-go-next", "t": "ASP.NET Core", "h": "Where to go next", "x": "Where to go next # Entity Framework Core : the database side of a web program. The tutorial chapters Networking and the web and Security ."}, {"u": "ef.html", "t": "Entity Framework Core", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "ef.html#the-idea", "t": "Entity Framework Core", "h": "The idea", "x": "The idea # A Ring program describes the model with data , because the classes of a model are .NET classes: a list of tables, each with a list of columns. EF Core works on that model in two ways: Class Rows are Best for RingDatabase lists of [name, value] pairs scripts, tools, small apps \u2014 the simplest API RingEntityDb real .NET objects (classes generated from the schema) with navigation properties change tracking, Include , an object-oriented style Both support SQLite (bundled), an in-memory database, and SQL Server, PostgreSQL and MySQL when their EF Core package is available. load \"dotnet.ri"}, {"u": "ef.html#a-table-and-its-rows", "t": "Entity Framework Core", "h": "A table and its rows", "x": "A table and its rows # The schema is a list of tables: [tableName, [ [column, type, isKey, options], ... ]] . Column types: int long double decimal string bool datetime bytes . An int or long key is generated by the database. docs/examples/ef_basic.ring # Entity Framework Core: a table, rows, SQL conditions load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"RingDotNet.Data\" } func main oDb = new RingDatabase(\"Data Source=:memory:\",[ [\"Customers\",[ [\"Id\",\"int\",1], [\"Name\",\"string\",0,[[\"required\",1],[\"maxlength\",40]]], [\"Age\",\"int\",0,[[\"nullable\",1]]], [\"City\",\"string\",0,[[\"index\",1]]] ]] ]) nAnn = oDb.insert(\"Custome"}, {"u": "ef.html#column-options", "t": "Entity Framework Core", "h": "Column options", "x": "Column options # The optional fourth item of a column is a list of [name, value] options: Option Meaning [\"required\",1] NOT NULL for text columns [\"nullable\",1] the column accepts null (a number can then be empty) [\"unique\",1] a unique index \u2014 a duplicate raises an error [\"index\",1] a plain index [\"maxlength\",40] maximum length of a text [\"default\",5] default value (numbers or text) [\"references\",\"Authors\"] foreign key to the key of another table [\"ondelete\",\"cascade\"] cascade , restrict , setnull , noaction [\"convert\",\"upper\"] a value converter (see hooks ) Several columns with isKey = 1 make"}, {"u": "ef.html#relationships", "t": "Entity Framework Core", "h": "Relationships", "x": "Relationships # A column with [\"references\",\"Authors\"] becomes a foreign key. children , parent and include read the related rows: docs/examples/ef_relations.ring # Entity Framework Core: foreign keys, children, parent, include and delete behaviors load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"RingDotNet.Data\" } func main oDb = new RingDatabase(\"Data Source=:memory:\",[ [\"Authors\",[[\"Id\",\"int\",1],[\"Name\",\"string\",0]]], [\"Books\",[[\"Id\",\"int\",1],[\"Title\",\"string\",0],[\"AuthorId\",\"int\",0,[[\"references\",\"Authors\"],[\"ondelete\",\"cascade\"]]]]] ]) nTolkien = oDb.insert(\"Authors\",[[\"Name\",\"Tolkien\"]]) nLewis = oDb.insert("}, {"u": "ef.html#queries-as-data", "t": "Entity Framework Core", "h": "Queries as data", "x": "Queries as data # For anything beyond a SQL condition, describe the query as data. EF Core builds a real LINQ expression tree from it and translates it to SQL, so it works with every provider: docs/examples/ef_queries.ring # Entity Framework Core: queries as LINQ expression trees, aggregates and SQL of any shape load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"RingDotNet.Data\" } func main oDb = new RingDatabase(\"Data Source=:memory:\",[ [\"People\",[[\"Id\",\"int\",1],[\"Name\",\"string\",0],[\"Age\",\"int\",0],[\"City\",\"string\",0],[\"Score\",\"double\",0]]] ]) oDb.insert(\"People\",[[\"Name\",\"Ann\"],[\"Age\",31],[\"City\",\"Cairo\"],[\"Score\","}, {"u": "ef.html#transactions-and-manual-saving", "t": "Entity Framework Core", "h": "Transactions and manual saving", "x": "Transactions and manual saving # oDb.begin() oDb.update(\"Accounts\",nA,[[\"Balance\",70]]) oDb.update(\"Accounts\",nB,[[\"Balance\",80]]) oDb.commit() # or oDb.rollback() oDb.autoSave(false) # changes are written together by save() oDb.insert(\"Accounts\",[[\"Owner\",\"D\"],[\"Balance\",5]]) oDb.insert(\"Accounts\",[[\"Owner\",\"E\"],[\"Balance\",6]]) oDb.save() oDb.autoSave(true) The async versions return a .NET Task ; dbWait waits for it and returns the result: oTask = oDb.insertAsync(\"Accounts\",[[\"Owner\",\"F\"],[\"Balance\",7]]) ? dbWait(oTask) # the key ? len(dbWait(oDb.allAsync(\"Accounts\")))"}, {"u": "ef.html#migrations", "t": "Entity Framework Core", "h": "Migrations", "x": "Migrations # migrate() brings an existing database in line with the model: it creates the missing tables, columns and indexes and returns the list of changes. apply(name, sql) runs a named SQL change once and records it in the table RingMigrations . docs/examples/ef_migrate.ring # Entity Framework Core: migrations of an existing database file load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System.IO\" \"RingDotNet.Data\" } func main cFile = new Path().Combine([new Path().GetTempPath(),\"ringdotnet_docs_migrate.db\"]) if new File().Exists(cFile) new File().Delete(cFile) ok cConnection = \"Data Source=\" + cFile # version"}, {"u": "ef.html#hooks-filters-and-converters", "t": "Entity Framework Core", "h": "Hooks, filters and converters", "x": "Hooks, filters and converters # Ring functions can run when changes are saved or SQL is executed; a table can have a global query filter (soft delete); a column can have a value converter : docs/examples/ef_hooks.ring # Entity Framework Core: hooks, query filters, converters, transactions load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"RingDotNet.Data\" } aSql = [] func main oDb = new RingDatabase(\"Data Source=:memory:\",[ [\"Notes\",[[\"Id\",\"int\",1],[\"Text\",\"string\",0,[[\"convert\",\"upper\"]]],[\"Deleted\",\"int\",0]],[[\"filter\",[\"Deleted\",\"=\",0]]]] ]) oDb.onSaving(dnCallback(\"beforeSave\")) oDb.onLog(dnCallback(\"logSql\")) o"}, {"u": "ef.html#real-entity-classes", "t": "Entity Framework Core", "h": "Real entity classes", "x": "Real entity classes # RingEntityDb uses the same schema, but each table becomes a real .NET class with one property per column, a navigation property to the parent for each foreign key ( AuthorId \u2192 Author ) and a collection on the parent ( Books ). EF tracks the objects like it tracks classes written in C#: docs/examples/ef_entities.ring # Entity Framework Core with real entity classes: navigation properties, tracking, include load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"RingDotNet.Data\" } func main oDb = new RingEntityDb(\"Data Source=:memory:\",[ [\"Authors\",[[\"Id\",\"int\",1],[\"Name\",\"string\",0]]], [\"Books\",[[\"Id"}, {"u": "ef.html#providers", "t": "Entity Framework Core", "h": "Providers", "x": "Providers # The connection text selects the provider: Connection Provider \"Data Source=people.db\" SQLite (bundled). \"Data Source=:memory:\" for an in-memory SQLite database \"inmemory:name\" the EF in-memory provider (no SQL, no migrations; same name = same database) \"sqlserver:Server=.;Database=x;...\" SQL Server \u2014 needs Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.SqlServer.dll next to the other assemblies \"postgres:Host=...;...\" PostgreSQL (Npgsql provider) \"mysql:Server=...;...\" MySQL (Pomelo provider) Warning The SQL Server, PostgreSQL and MySQL providers are loaded by name from their NuGet packages and hav"}, {"u": "ef.html#the-full-ef-api", "t": "Entity Framework Core", "h": "The full EF API", "x": "The full EF API # Everything in EF Core is also wrapped as classes ( using { \"Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore\" } , .ChangeTracking , .Infrastructure , .Metadata , .Metadata.Builders , .Storage , .Query , .Migrations , .Diagnostics , Microsoft.Data.Sqlite ; clashing names start with Ef ). oDb.context() returns the real DbContext : oContext = oDb.context() ? oContext.Database.ProviderName # Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Sqlite The complete list of methods is in the Entity Framework reference . A complete program: samples/UsingDotNet/applications/Contacts \u2014 a contacts book with a console menu."}, {"u": "ef.html#where-to-go-next", "t": "Entity Framework Core", "h": "Where to go next", "x": "Where to go next # Entity Framework reference : every method and option. The tutorial chapter Databases and services ."}, {"u": "ef-reference.html", "t": "Entity Framework reference", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "ef-reference.html#schema-format", "t": "Entity Framework reference", "h": "Schema format", "x": "Schema format # aSchema = [ [ \"TableName\", [ [ \"Column\", \"type\", isKey, [ [\"option\", value], ... ] ], ... ], [ [\"tableOption\", value], ... ] # optional ], ... ] Part Values column type int , long , double , decimal , string (or text ), bool , datetime , bytes isKey 1 for a key column; several give a composite key. A single int / long key is generated by the database column options required , nullable , unique , index , maxlength , default , references , ondelete , convert table options [\"filter\", condition] \u2014 a global query filter"}, {"u": "ef-reference.html#creating-a-database", "t": "Entity Framework reference", "h": "Creating a database", "x": "Creating a database # oDb = new RingDatabase(connection, aSchema) # rows as [name, value] pairs oDb = new RingEntityDb(connection, aSchema) # real entity objects The connection starts with the provider name ( inmemory: , sqlserver: , postgres: , mysql: ) or is a SQLite connection string."}, {"u": "ef-reference.html#ringdatabase", "t": "Entity Framework reference", "h": "RingDatabase", "x": "RingDatabase # Rows # Method Returns insert(table, values) the key (a list for a composite key). values is [[\"Name\",\"Ann\"],[\"Age\",31]] byKey(table, key) the row, or \"\" . The row is tracked : after raw SQL, call discard() to read the new values all(table) all rows where(table, condition, params) rows matching a SQL condition with {0} parameters (SQLite) update(table, key, values) 1 when the row exists, else 0 remove(table, key) 1 when the row existed, else 0 count(table) the number of rows dbValue(row, column) a column of a row ( \"\" for null) Queries # Method Does query(table, spec) rows matchi"}, {"u": "ef-reference.html#ringentitydb", "t": "Entity Framework reference", "h": "RingEntityDb", "x": "RingEntityDb # Method Does newEntity(table) a new object of the generated class entityType(table) the .NET Type properties(table) the property names: columns first, then navigations ( Author , Books ) add(entity) adds the entity (and the entities it refers to); returns the key remove(entity) removes it find(table, key) the tracked entity, or \"\" query(table, spec) tracked entities; the specification also accepts [\"include\",[\"Author\",\"Other.Nested\"]] all(table) , count(table, cond) all entities, number of entities getValue(entity, property) , setValue(entity, property, value) read or write a pro"}, {"u": "ef-reference.html#query-specification", "t": "Entity Framework reference", "h": "Query specification", "x": "Query specification # A query is a list of [item, value] pairs: Item Value where a condition orderby [[column, \"asc\"/\"desc\"], ...] skip , take numbers select [column, ...] \u2014 keeps only those columns (RingDatabase) include [navigation, ...] (RingEntityDb) ignorefilters 1 to ignore the table's global filter A condition is [column, op, value] or a combination: Form Meaning [\"Age\", \">\", 30] comparison: = != < <= > >= [\"Name\", \"contains\", \"an\"] contains , startswith , endswith (text) [\"City\", \"in\", [\"Cairo\",\"Giza\"]] membership [\"Boss\", \"isnull\"] , [\"Boss\", \"notnull\"] null checks [\"and\", c1, c2, ..."}, {"u": "ef-reference.html#providers-and-capabilities", "t": "Entity Framework reference", "h": "Providers and capabilities", "x": "Providers and capabilities # Capability SQLite In-memory SQL Server / PostgreSQL / MySQL tables, rows, relations, queries (expression trees), aggregates \u2714 \u2714 untested where / execute / scalar / sqlRows with SQL \u2714 \u2014 untested migrate / apply \u2714 \u2014 \u2014 transactions \u2714 \u2714 untested typed entities ( RingEntityDb ) \u2714 \u2714 untested"}, {"u": "ef-reference.html#what-is-not-supported", "t": "Entity Framework reference", "h": "What is not supported", "x": "What is not supported # Classes written in C# source \u2014 entity classes are generated from the schema. Lambdas written by hand \u2014 queries are data. Migrations that alter or drop columns (use execute or apply ). Hooks, converters and migrate() in RingEntityDb . The SQL Server, PostgreSQL and MySQL providers have not been tested."}, {"u": "ef-reference.html#where-to-go-next", "t": "Entity Framework reference", "h": "Where to go next", "x": "Where to go next # Your own assemblies : use a library that is not part of .NET. Rules and limits : what to check when something does not work."}, {"u": "ml-chess.html", "t": "Machine learning: the chess end game", "h": "", "x": "A white king and a white rook against a black king: is the game a draw, or in how many moves does white win? The data set has 28,056 positions with their results (the \"chess end game\" data of the UCI repository). The example samples/UsingDotNet/tests/packages/ChessEndgame trains fifteen combinations of algorithm and features on 70 percent of the positions, tests them on the other 30 percent, saves every model, and uses a saved model to predict the result of a position. It has three console programs and a window ( ChessEndgame.ring , below ) where you drag the pieces and the models answer. All "}, {"u": "ml-chess.html#run-it", "t": "Machine learning: the chess end game", "h": "Run it", "x": "Run it # cd samples/UsingDotNet/tests/packages/ChessEndgame ring train.ring # split 70 / 30, train, test, save the models (about a minute) ring evaluate.ring # load the saved models and test them again ring predict.ring a1 b3 c2 # the result of a position: white king a1, white rook b3, black king c2 ring ChessEndgame.ring # the window: the board, the data, the statistics and the models The first run downloads ML.NET (the packages of ChessEndgame.csproj ) from nuget.org and makes the Ring classes of its DLLs; the next runs only load them. ring train.ring --quick tries three algorithms on a seve"}, {"u": "ml-chess.html#70-percent-to-train-30-percent-to-test", "t": "Machine learning: the chess end game", "h": "70 percent to train, 30 percent to test", "x": "70 percent to train, 30 percent to test # A model must be tested on positions that it has not learned from, or its accuracy tells nothing: a model could remember every position it saw. chessSplit() shuffles the positions and writes data/train.csv (19,639 positions) and data/test.csv (8,417 positions). The shuffle uses a generator of numbers that is the same on every computer ( seed * 16807 mod 2147483647 ), so the split is the same each time, and no position is in both files. nSeed = 42 for nPlace = nRows to 2 step -1 # a shuffle (Fisher and Yates) nSeed = (nSeed * 16807) % 2147483647 nOther ="}, {"u": "ml-chess.html#one-way-for-each-position", "t": "Machine learning: the chess end game", "h": "One way for each position", "x": "One way for each position # The board can be turned and mirrored (eight ways) without changing the result of a game, and the data keeps one way for each position: the white king is always in the lower left triangle (a1 b1 c1 d1 b2 c2 d2 c3 d3 d4). A model that only learns from those positions has never seen a king on e4. chessCanonical() turns any position to the way of the data, and chessFeatures() calls it first, so the files that the example writes, and a position that you name, are in the same way. Without it the answers for positions with the king elsewhere were wrong in a way that no tes"}, {"u": "ml-chess.html#the-features", "t": "Machine learning: the chess end game", "h": "The features", "x": "The features # The data gives the six squares: the file and the rank of each piece. chessFeatures() turns a position into numbers, and the example tries three sets of them: Set What the algorithm reads squares the six numbers: the files a to h as 1 to 8, and the ranks onehot each of the six numbers as a one-hot vector (a 0 or a 1 for each of the 8 values: 48 numbers). A linear model cannot tell much from the number of a rank, but it can learn a weight for each square. engineered the six numbers and what a player sees at a glance: the distance between the kings, how near the black king is to th"}, {"u": "ml-chess.html#the-algorithms", "t": "Machine learning: the chess end game", "h": "The algorithms", "x": "The algorithms # ML.NET has algorithms for many classes ( MulticlassClassification.Trainers ), and binary algorithms that OneVersusAll turns into algorithms for many classes: it trains one model for each result, and the model with the highest score answers. Each trainer is one call, and the rest of the pipeline is the same. The methods of ML.NET have many overloads with optional arguments, so the calls give their arguments by name ( [ :labelColumnName = \"Label\", ... ] ): the name finds the overload. Append joins the steps: it is a generic extension method of ML.NET, and the bridge works out it"}, {"u": "ml-chess.html#save-the-models-and-use-them", "t": "Machine learning: the chess end game", "h": "Save the models, and use them", "x": "Save the models, and use them # Each model is saved with the schema of the data that trained it. A model file has the whole pipeline in it: the features, the trainer, and the answer as a word. oMl.Model.Save(oModel,oTrain.Schema,currentdir() + \"/models/lightgbm_engineered.zip\") evaluate.ring and predict.ring start from the files. Load answers a list: the model, and the schema that was saved with it. aLoaded = oMl.Model.callMethod(\"Load\",[currentdir() + \"/models/lightgbm_engineered.zip\"]) aPredicted = chessColumn(aLoaded[1].Transform(chessLoad(oMl,\"data/positions.csv\")),\"System.String\",\"Predict"}, {"u": "ml-chess.html#the-window", "t": "Machine learning: the chess end game", "h": "The window", "x": "The window # ChessEndgame.ring is a window for the same models, a port of the RingQt application ring/applications/chessendgame (which asked models of Azure over the internet) that uses the models that train.ring saved: The window of the chess end game Tab What it does Play Drag the pieces on the board (the images of the RingQt application, drawn on a UiCanvas with DrawImage ), or choose the squares in the boxes. The model that you choose answers, the data says what the real result is, and the window tells whether the model is right. Random position and From the data set the pieces; Ask all th"}, {"u": "ml-chess.html#the-test-of-the-example", "t": "Machine learning: the chess end game", "h": "The test of the example", "x": "The test of the example # samples/UsingDotNet/tests/packages/pkgtest_mlnet_chess.ring runs the three programs the way a user does: train.ring --quick , evaluate.ring and predict.ring . It checks the 70 / 30 split (the number of positions in each file, and that no position is in both), that the models are saved and ranked, that the saved model gives the accuracy that the training measured, and the answers for positions, for a position and the same position turned half a turn (the answer is the same), for another model, and for positions that are not positions (two pieces on a square, kings that"}, {"u": "own-assemblies.html", "t": "Your own assemblies", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "own-assemblies.html#two-ways-to-use-an-assembly", "t": "Your own assemblies", "h": "Two ways to use an assembly", "x": "Two ways to use an assembly # Way When Generic calls \u2014 dnLoad , dnType , dnNew , callMethod a quick call, nothing to generate Generated classes \u2014 wrapassembly.ring the simple oObj.Method() syntax, properties, events, enums, inheritance The examples of this page use the small C# library of the package, samples/UsingDotNet/tests/UserLib (the namespace MyCompany.Tools , the class Calculator ). Build it once with the .NET SDK, and run the Ring programs from the folder of the package: dotnet build samples/UsingDotNet/tests/UserLib -c Release -o samples/UsingDotNet/tests/UserLib/bin"}, {"u": "own-assemblies.html#generic-calls", "t": "Your own assemblies", "h": "Generic calls", "x": "Generic calls # load \"dotnet.ring\" dnLoad(\"samples/UsingDotNet/tests/UserLib/bin/UserLib.dll\") # dependencies are found next to the dll oCalc = dnNew(\"MyCompany.Tools.Calculator\",[10]) oCalc.callMethod(\"Add\",[5]) ? oCalc.getProp(\"Total\") # 15 ? dnStatic(\"MyCompany.Tools.Calculator\",\"Square\",[7]) # 49"}, {"u": "own-assemblies.html#generated-classes", "t": "Your own assemblies", "h": "Generated classes", "x": "Generated classes # Generate Ring classes for every public type of the DLL: cd tools/dotnettools ring wrapassembly.ring ../../samples/UsingDotNet/tests/UserLib/bin/UserLib.dll ../../myclasses This writes two files for each namespace into myclasses/ : a loader and the classes (and classindex.ring , the index of the classes, when every namespace is wrapped). Use them like any other wrapped namespace: load \"dotnet.ring\" dnLoad(\"samples/UsingDotNet/tests/UserLib/bin/UserLib.dll\") dnImportFrom(\"myclasses/\",\"MyCompany.Tools\") oCalc = new Calculator() oCalc.Add(10).Multiply(3) # fluent: methods that "}, {"u": "own-assemblies.html#nuget-packages", "t": "Your own assemblies", "h": "NuGet packages", "x": "NuGet packages # A NuGet package is a set of DLL files. Let the .NET SDK collect them: a project file with a PackageReference and dotnet publish -o libs copy the package and the DLLs it depends on into one folder. Then wrap and load as above: ring wrapassembly.ring libs/Newtonsoft.Json.dll ../../json dnLoad(\"libs/Newtonsoft.Json.dll\") dnImportFrom(\"json/\",\"Newtonsoft.Json.Linq\") # a namespace that needs another namespace of the same DLL loads it from the same folder dnImportFrom(\"json/\",\"Newtonsoft.Json\") The tutorial A NuGet package walks through this with a real package. A package often need"}, {"u": "own-assemblies.html#extension-methods", "t": "Your own assemblies", "h": "Extension methods", "x": "Extension methods # An extension method of a type that is wrapped in the same run is a method of the Ring class of that type, so a fluent API reads as it does in C#: oTable = new Table() oTable.AddColumn(\"Name\") # TableExtensions.AddColumn(oTable, \"Name\") oTable.AddRow([\"Ann\",\"31\"]) The classes that implement an interface get the extension methods of the interface. An extension method of a closed generic type ( IEstimator<ITransformer> , the extended type of ML.NET's Append<TNext> ) is a method of the classes that implement the generic interface, and the type argument of the method is worked o"}, {"u": "own-assemblies.html#which-namespaces-to-wrap", "t": "Your own assemblies", "h": "Which namespaces to wrap", "x": "Which namespaces to wrap # wrapassembly.ring <dll> <folder> [namespace ...] wraps only the namespaces you list ( <dll> can be several paths joined with ; ). Without names, every namespace of the assembly is wrapped. Several assemblies can go into one folder (the index of the classes keeps the types of all of them when each one is wrapped without names), or each one into a folder of its own, as the project lesson does for its three packages. An object that comes back from a namespace that was not imported is wrapped when the folder has the index: its namespace is loaded from that folder."}, {"u": "own-assemblies.html#rules", "t": "Your own assemblies", "h": "Rules", "x": "Rules # Generated loaders use the names of the .NET classes. When a class name is also the name of a class of the library, or of another namespace of the same assembly, the generator adds the prefix of its namespace ( CoreVersion ) and lists the renames \u2014 see Class generator . Keep the DLL and its dependencies in one folder: assemblies are resolved next to the DLL you load. The runtime mode matters: a DLL that needs Windows Forms requires the desktop mode first (a using block that names System.Windows.Forms , or dnUseDesktop() )."}, {"u": "own-assemblies.html#where-to-go-next", "t": "Your own assemblies", "h": "Where to go next", "x": "Where to go next # Class generator : how the wrapper classes are made, and how to make more. The tutorial chapter Libraries and packages ."}, {"u": "generator.html", "t": "Class generator", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "generator.html#what-the-generator-does", "t": "Class generator", "h": "What the generator does", "x": "What the generator does # tools/dotnettools/gendotnet.ring reads the manifest ( tools/dotnettools/dotnetmanifest.ring ), loads the assemblies, and uses reflection to write Ring source: libraries/dotnetlib/generated/ System.ring # the namespace loader: imports, registrations System.classes.ring # all the classes of the namespace (one file: loading and installing thousands of small files is slow) System.IO.ring System.IO.classes.ring ... classindex.ring # [type, namespace] of every class, one call with the whole list: automatic imports (loaded when first needed) The class files are plain Ring: a"}, {"u": "generator.html#regenerate", "t": "Class generator", "h": "Regenerate", "x": "Regenerate # cd tools/dotnettools ring gendotnet.ring # every namespace of the manifest ring gendotnet.ring System.Text System.IO # only these A run with names writes only those namespaces, and the base classes of the other namespaces are left out of its files: a class that derives from Object (the class of System.Object ) derives from DotNetObject instead, and the line dnImport(\"System\") is not written. The files of such a run are not the files of the full run, so run the command without names before you use or commit the result."}, {"u": "generator.html#check-a-change-of-the-generator", "t": "Class generator", "h": "Check a change of the generator", "x": "Check a change of the generator # A rule of the generator, a change of the bridge or a new namespace changes the generated files, sometimes by thousands of lines. checkgenerator.ring makes the library again and checks the result before you commit it: ring tools/dotnettools/checkgenerator.ring It takes about a minute and a half. The checks: the whole library is generated, without errors; the size of the change of the generated files: files and lines against the last commit, and the kinds of line that changed. A small fix changes a few files; a rule that copies the methods of a base class into e"}, {"u": "generator.html#the-manifest", "t": "Class generator", "h": "The manifest", "x": "The manifest # Each entry describes one namespace: Key Meaning :namespace the .NET namespace, also the name used with using { \"...\" } :assemblies assemblies that contain the types (default System.Private.CoreLib ) :all true wraps every public class and struct of the namespace :exclude type names that :all must skip :types explicit or extra types: [.NET name, Ring class name, init() parameter count] \u2014 closed generic types and nested types go here :uses namespaces imported together with this one (their classes are returned by its methods) :extra Ring files the loader also loads (for example linq"}, {"u": "generator.html#closed-generic-types", "t": "Class generator", "h": "Closed generic types", "x": "Closed generic types # The generic collections have classes through :types . Add your own with a line like: [\"System.Collections.Generic.List`1[[System.Int32]]\",\"IntList\",0] The third item forces the number of parameters of init() (0 here). After regenerating, new IntList() is a List<int> ."}, {"u": "generator.html#name-clashes", "t": "Class generator", "h": "Name clashes", "x": "Name clashes # The Ring class names are global. wrapassembly.ring (see Your own assemblies ) reserves the names of the library first, so a class of your assembly with the name of a class of the library gets the prefix of its namespace: YamlDotNet.Core.Version is CoreVersion , because System.Version is a class of the library. The generator lists the renames when it runs. When two namespaces contain a class with the same short name, the later one gets a prefix: System.Timers.Timer becomes TimersTimer , WPF's Button becomes WpfButton , ASP.NET's MediaTypeHeaderValue becomes AspMediaTypeHeaderValu"}, {"u": "generator.html#overload-rules", "t": "Class generator", "h": "Overload rules", "x": "Overload rules # The generator names the methods of a class like this: a name with overloads of several parameter counts is one method that takes one parameter: NULL calls the parameterless overload, a list of :name = value pairs chooses the overload by the parameter names, and any other value calls the overload with one parameter; a name with one parameter count keeps its parameters ( Math.Max(a, b) ); the bridge picks among overloads with the same count by the types of the values; the constructors other than init are @New ; ToString() keeps its parameterless form, and its forms with paramete"}, {"u": "generator.html#how-a-wrapper-class-looks", "t": "Class generator", "h": "How a wrapper class looks", "x": "How a wrapper class looks # Class StringBuilder from Object Capacity MaxCapacity Length Func Equals P1 dn_x = dotnet_call(\"s4:call\",nHandle,\"s6:Equals\",P1) if islist(dn_x) return dnFinish(dn_x[2]) ok return dn_x Func Append P1 if dotnet_isnamed(P1) dn_x = dotnet_calla(\"s9:callnamed\",nHandle,\"s6:Append\",P1) if islist(dn_x) return dnAny(dn_x) ok return dn_x ok return dnObj(dotnet_callo(\"s4:call\",nHandle,\"s6:Append\",P1)) Func getLength dn_x = dotnet_call(\"s3:get\",nHandle,\"s6:Length\") if islist(dn_x) return dnFinish(dn_x[2]) ok return dn_x This is an extract of the class that is in libraries/dotne"}, {"u": "generator.html#wrapping-your-own-assembly", "t": "Class generator", "h": "Wrapping your own assembly", "x": "Wrapping your own assembly # tools/dotnettools/wrapassembly.ring uses the same generator for a single DLL: Your own assemblies ."}, {"u": "generator.html#what-the-generator-cannot-wrap", "t": "Class generator", "h": "What the generator cannot wrap", "x": "What the generator cannot wrap # tools/dotnettools/auditassembly.ring reports, for one or more assemblies, how much of their public API Ring can use and what gets in the way. It applies the rules of the bridge itself, so it says what the generator does: ring auditassembly.ring Library.dll Each line has a count, a tag and an example. The tags are OK (supported: wrapped classes, enums as texts, delegates as callbacks, extension methods, events), INFO (facts) or GAP (not usable today): operators that Ring has no syntax for ( ++ , op_True ) and the members of a ref struct that no value can make (a"}, {"u": "generator.html#where-to-go-next", "t": "Class generator", "h": "Where to go next", "x": "Where to go next # Testing : how the package and your own programs are tested. Function index and the Class reference : what has been generated."}, {"u": "testing.html", "t": "Testing", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "testing.html#the-test-suite", "t": "Testing", "h": "The test suite", "x": "The test suite # sh samples/UsingDotNet/tests/run.sh ring.exe samples/UsingDotNet/tests/run.sh runs: every samples/UsingDotNet/tests/test_*.ring (language bridge, collections, LINQ, system, IO, net, services, web, data, entity framework, keyboard, WPF, your own assemblies...), the package tests (see below), the console samples, every tutorial program (the ones with a window test themselves with --selftest , the others just have to run without an error), every Windows Forms and WPF sample with --selftest , every application with --selftest , and prints 0 failed when everything passes. It needs "}, {"u": "testing.html#package-tests", "t": "Testing", "h": "Package tests", "x": "Package tests # samples/UsingDotNet/tests/packages/pkgtest_*.ring are real programs against NuGet packages: 188 tests, one for each package (the ML.NET package has several). Each one asks dnAssert about what the package gives back (HTML, a deserialized list, a determinant, a retried call, rows from a query), so a change of the bridge that breaks a library fails here before it fails in a program. The packages that need a class written in .NET have a small helper project, and the tests that need a native library (LibGit2Sharp, ML.NET, SQLite) run on Windows and on Linux. Writing a package test #"}, {"u": "testing.html#dnassert", "t": "Testing", "h": "dnAssert", "x": "dnAssert # load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" } dnAssert(\"max\",new Math().Max(3,9),9) dnAssert(\"abs\",new Math().Abs(-5),5) dnTestSummary() TESTS: 2 passed, 0 failed A failing assertion prints FAIL: name expected <9> got <3> and counts a failure. dnTestSummary() returns the number of failures."}, {"u": "testing.html#programs-that-test-themselves", "t": "Testing", "h": "Programs that test themselves", "x": "Programs that test themselves # The checks of a window program are kept in a file of their own, so the program stays as short as the lesson that shows it. The program is the usual one (here it is the file counter.ring ): oForm = uiForm(\"Click counter\",260,130) ... uiRun(oForm) and next to it, in the folder selftest , the file with the same name ( selftest/counter.ring ) holds the checks: func selfTest oForm.Controls.Item(1).PerformClick() dnAssert(\"one click\",oCount.Text,\"1 clicks\") ring counter.ring # the normal program ring counter.ring --selftest # the test: prints SELFTEST OK With --selfte"}, {"u": "testing.html#typing-keys-in-tests", "t": "Testing", "h": "Typing keys in tests", "x": "Typing keys in tests # uiSendKeys(oForm,\"12{+}30{ENTER}\") types into the focused control of your own form . It posts window messages through the real Windows Forms key processing, so it behaves like a user and can never type into another program (which SendKeys could). Keys: {LEFT} {RIGHT} {UP} {DOWN} {ENTER} {ESC} {BACKSPACE} {TAB} {DELETE} , {+} for a plus sign."}, {"u": "testing.html#testing-the-screenshots", "t": "Testing", "h": "Testing the screenshots", "x": "Testing the screenshots # The screenshots in this documentation are made by docs/tools/shoot_all.sh : it starts each sample, finds its window, captures it with PrintWindow and closes it."}, {"u": "testing.html#where-to-go-next", "t": "Testing", "h": "Where to go next", "x": "Where to go next # Rules and limits : the points to know before a long program. The tutorial chapter Testing and release shows tests in a real project."}, {"u": "rules-and-limits.html", "t": "Rules and limits", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "rules-and-limits.html#ring-rules-for-net-code", "t": "Rules and limits", "h": "Ring rules for .NET code", "x": "Ring rules for .NET code # Use parentheses with new when you pass arguments # new StringBuilder(\"x\") \u2014 with parentheses, because Ring calls init() only then. A class without arguments works both ways ( new MemoryStream , new MemoryStream() ). For StringBuilder , Random and Version , which take an optional first argument, write new StringBuilder or new StringBuilder(\"x\") , not new StringBuilder() . Event code can only see global variables # The code of an event ( setClickEvent(\"...\") ) runs outside the function that set it up. Create the widgets and the program state as globals at the top, then"}, {"u": "rules-and-limits.html#net-rules", "t": "Rules and limits", "h": ".NET rules", "x": ".NET rules # Put the using block before the first .NET call # The runtime mode (desktop, web, both) is chosen by the using { } block (or by dnUseDesktop() , dnUseWeb() , dnUseAll() ) and must come before any other .NET call : the first call starts the runtime and the mode does not change afterwards. If a program needs both Windows Forms/WPF and ASP.NET Core, name both in the first using block. Callbacks run on the Ring thread only # Events from timers on other threads ( System.Threading.Timer , Task.Run(...) ) do not run Ring code; they print a message on stderr instead. Use the Windows Forms "}, {"u": "rules-and-limits.html#limits", "t": "Rules and limits", "h": "Limits", "x": "Limits # Not supported Why / what to do Declaring .NET classes or interfaces in Ring Ring is not compiled to .NET. Write them in C#, build a DLL and wrap it . .NET members named like some Ring words ( Get , And , Or , Not , Next , End , If , For , New , Load , Call , Put , Give , See ) Ring reserves these after a dot (others, such as From , To , Set , Step , In , On , Off , are fine). A method gets an @ at the start ( @Next , @Call ), and so does a property: oNode.@Next reads it and oNode.@Next = x writes it; getProp(\"Name\") and setProp(\"Name\", value) work too, and a method that kept its name "}, {"u": "rules-and-limits.html#troubleshooting", "t": "Rules and limits", "h": "Troubleshooting", "x": "Troubleshooting # Message Cause Runtime Error in loading the dynamic library ring_dotnet.dll is not on the PATH. Add the package bin folder, or install the package into the Ring folder. DotNet Error: No generated classes for the namespace X the name is wrong, or the namespace is not in the manifest \u2014 see Class generator . Error in property name, property not found: x a global variable named like the attribute, or an object that is a plain DotNetObject (use getProp(\"x\") ). Calling function with less number of parameters a constructor that needs arguments: new DateTime(2024,12,25) ; or an overlo"}, {"u": "rules-and-limits.html#where-to-go-next", "t": "Rules and limits", "h": "Where to go next", "x": "Where to go next # Function index and the Class reference : look up a function or a class. Core concepts : the naming rules behind most of the points above. Quick introduction to Ring : if the problem is the language and not .NET."}, {"u": "reference.html", "t": "Function index", "h": "", "x": "This page is generated from the comments in libraries/dotnetlib/*.ring . Search it with the box at the top of the page."}, {"u": "reference.html#core-functionsring", "t": "Function index", "h": "Core (functions.ring)", "x": "Core (functions.ring) # Function Description dnRequest(cOperation, aParameters) Send a request to the bridge: dnRequest(\"call\", [oTarget, \"Method\", arg1, ...]), returns one value. The common answers (a text, a number, a yes/no, one object) are read directly, without making a list of items. dnSend(cOperation, nHandle, cName, cArgs) The request of a generated method for a member of an object: the operation and the name are ready wire items (\"s4:call\", \"s6:Append\"), the handle is a number (0 is the null object) and the arguments are packed. It makes the same request text as dnRequest, without a l"}, {"u": "reference.html#dotnetobject-and-friends-classesring", "t": "Function index", "h": "DotNetObject and friends (classes.ring)", "x": "DotNetObject and friends (classes.ring) # Function Description DotNetObject.init(pHandle) (the parameter has another name: \"this.nHandle = ...\" would run Ring's property setter machinery, which breaks an outer assignment like oForm.Text = new Path().GetFileName(...)) DotNetObject.dnDefaultObject(cTypeName) The default object of a class (used by the class body of the generated classes, for \"new X\" without parentheses) DotNetObject.isNull() True for the null reference DotNetObject.callMethod(cMethod, aParameters) Call a method by its name: oObject.callMethod(\"Append\", [\"text\"]). It works for eve"}, {"u": "reference.html#linq-linqring", "t": "Function index", "h": "LINQ (linq.ring)", "x": "LINQ (linq.ring) # Function Description dnQuery(xSource) A query on a Ring list or a .NET collection: dnQuery(aList).Where(\"...\").ToList() DotNetQuery.init(xSource) a query on xSource, a Ring list or a .NET collection (made by dnQuery) DotNetQuery.Where(cCode) the items for which the code gives true DotNetQuery.Select(cCode) the code applied to every item (the results are the new items) DotNetQuery.SelectMany(cCode) the code gives a list for every item; the lists are joined into one DotNetQuery.OrderBy(cCode) sort by the value that the code gives for an item DotNetQuery.OrderByDescending(cCode"}, {"u": "reference.html#windows-forms-helpers-guiring", "t": "Function index", "h": "Windows Forms helpers (gui.ring)", "x": "Windows Forms helpers (gui.ring) # Function Description uiForm(cTitle, nWidth, nHeight) A form with a client area of nWidth x nHeight, centered on the screen uiLabel(oParent, cText, nX, nY, nWidth, nHeight) A label with a text, placed at (nX, nY) in the parent (a form or a container) uiButton(oParent, cText, nX, nY, nWidth, nHeight, cClickCode) A button; cClickCode is Ring code run on click (\"\" for none) uiTextBox(oParent, nX, nY, nWidth, nHeight) A one-line text box (uiMemo has several lines); read and write its Text uiMemo(oParent, nX, nY, nWidth, nHeight) A text box with several lines, scro"}, {"u": "reference.html#wpf-helpers-wpfring", "t": "Function index", "h": "WPF helpers (wpf.ring)", "x": "WPF helpers (wpf.ring) # Function Description wpfWindow(cTitle, nWidth, nHeight) A window of nWidth x nHeight, centered on the screen wpfXaml(cXaml) Build the objects of a XAML text: wpfXaml('<Button xmlns=\"http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation\" Content=\"Hi\"/>') wpfFind(oRoot, cName) The element called cName (x:Name / Name in XAML) inside oRoot wpfBrush(cColor) A brush from a name or a code: wpfBrush(\"Red\"), wpfBrush(\"#336699\") wpfColor(cColor) A color from a name or a code wpfLabel(cText) A Label (a content control, the class WpfLabel) with a text wpfText(cText) A TextBloc"}, {"u": "reference.html#web-server-webring", "t": "Function index", "h": "Web server (web.ring)", "x": "Web server (web.ring) # Function Description webJson(xValue) The response of a handler as JSON: a Ring list, or a list of [name, value] pairs for a JSON object RingRequest.init(pHandle) a request that the server received; pHandle is its handle RingRequest.method() the HTTP method: \"GET\", \"POST\"... RingRequest.path() the path of the address, without the query string: \"/api/echo\" RingRequest.queryString() the whole query string: \"q=ring&page=2\" RingRequest.query(cName) a query string value: /search?q=ring --> query(\"q\") RingRequest.body() the body of the request as a text RingRequest.header(cNam"}, {"u": "reference.html#entity-framework-dataring", "t": "Function index", "h": "Entity Framework (data.ring)", "x": "Entity Framework (data.ring) # Function Description dbValue(aRow, cColumn) The value of a column in a row: dbValue(aRow,\"Name\") dbWait(oTask) wait for a Task of the async methods and return its result RingDatabase.init(cConnection, aSchema) cConnection is a SQLite connection string (\"Data Source=people.db\") or starts with the provider: \"inmemory:shop\", \"sqlserver:Server=.;Database=x;...\", \"postgres:Host=...\", \"mysql:Server=...\" (those need the EF Core package of the provider next to RingDotNet.Data.dll) RingDatabase.insert(cTable, aValues) add a row, returns the key RingDatabase.byKey(cTable, "}, {"u": "reference.html#generated-classes", "t": "Function index", "h": "Generated classes", "x": "Generated classes # The wrapper classes are generated; there are two files for each namespace in libraries/dotnetlib/generated/ (a loader and the classes). The members of a class are the members of the .NET class (see the .NET documentation ), named by the rules . To see the Ring name of a method, open its class file or call dnDescribe(\"Namespace.Type\") ."}, {"u": "reference.html#where-to-go-next", "t": "Function index", "h": "Where to go next", "x": "Where to go next # Class reference : every wrapped class, one page for each namespace. Core concepts : how to read the names."}, {"u": "ring-intro.html", "t": "Quick introduction to Ring", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "ring-intro.html#who-this-page-is-for", "t": "Quick introduction to Ring", "h": "Who this page is for", "x": "Who this page is for # This is an appendix: the rest of the documentation does not depend on reading it in order. If you already write Ring, go back to Getting started . If you come from C#, Python, JavaScript or another language, read this page once: Ring differs from some languages in a few ways, and knowing them helps when you read Ring code and the messages that come from .NET. The five programs are in samples/UsingDotNet/tutorials ( ring_basics.ring \u2026 ring_errors_files.ring ); run one with: ring ring_basics.ring Note This is a quick tour, not the manual. The Ring language documentation (<"}, {"u": "ring-intro.html#1-values-text-and-numbers", "t": "Quick introduction to Ring", "h": "1. Values, text and numbers", "x": "1. Values, text and numbers # # Ring in 15 minutes, part 1: values, text, numbers and comparisons (no .NET here: plain Ring) # 1. A variable needs no declaration and no type; the type belongs to the value. \"?\" prints a line. nCount = 3 # a number cName = \"Ring\" # a text lReady = True # a yes/no value: True is 1, False is 0 ? nCount ? cName ? lReady ? type(nCount) + \" \" + type(cName) + \" \" + type(lReady) # 2. All numbers are doubles. Whole numbers print without decimals, others with at most a few. ? 7 / 2 ? 7 % 2 # the remainder ? floor(7 / 2) # the whole part ? 2 ** 10 # power (\"^\" is a bit op"}, {"u": "ring-intro.html#2-lists", "t": "Quick introduction to Ring", "h": "2. Lists", "x": "2. Lists # # Ring in 15 minutes, part 2: lists (the only collection), pairs, nested lists # 1. A list holds anything. Indexes start at 1. \"+\" adds an item at the end. aNames = [\"Ann\",\"Bob\",\"Cy\"] aNames + \"Dee\" ? len(aNames) ? aNames[1] + \" \" + aNames[len(aNames)] aNames[2] = \"Rob\" # change an item ? aNames # 2. Walk a list with for ... in. The item is a copy: change the list by index. for cName in aNames ? \"hello \" + cName next for nIndex = 1 to len(aNames) aNames[nIndex] = upper(aNames[nIndex]) next ? aNames # 3. Search, insert, delete, sort ? find(aNames,\"CY\") # the position (0: not found) i"}, {"u": "ring-intro.html#3-decisions-loops-functions", "t": "Quick introduction to Ring", "h": "3. Decisions, loops, functions", "x": "3. Decisions, loops, functions # # Ring in 15 minutes, part 3: decisions, loops and functions # Ring runs a file from the top. If the file has a function called main, Ring calls it by itself when it reaches the end of the file. # The code at the top level (before the first func) runs first. A func continues until the next func or class, so statements go BEFORE the functions. nTotal = 0 # a global variable: defined at the top, outside every function func main # 1. if / but (else if) / else, closed with ok nAge = 17 if nAge >= 18 ? \"adult\" but nAge >= 13 ? \"teenager\" else ? \"child\" ok # 2. for c"}, {"u": "ring-intro.html#4-classes-and-objects", "t": "Quick introduction to Ring", "h": "4. Classes and objects", "x": "4. Classes and objects # # Ring in 15 minutes, part 4: classes and objects (the code at the top comes first, the classes go LAST: a func or class continues until the next func or class) func main # 1. new makes an object. The parentheses call init(). oAnn = new Person(\"Ann\",31) ? oAnn.name oAnn.birthday() ? oAnn.describe() # 2. Attributes are read and written with a dot. An attribute that the class does not have is an error. oAnn.age = 40 ? oAnn.age # 3. Braces: set attributes (and call methods) of the object inside { }. This is the style of this documentation. oBob = new Person(\"Bob\",25) { ci"}, {"u": "ring-intro.html#5-errors-files-and-other-files", "t": "Quick introduction to Ring", "h": "5. Errors, files and other files", "x": "5. Errors, files and other files # # Ring in 15 minutes, part 5: errors, files, arguments and other files func main # 1. try / catch / done: the error text is in cCatchError. raise() makes an error of your own. try nResult = 10 / 0 catch ? \"caught: \" + substr(cCatchError,14) # a runtime error starts with \"Error (R1) : \", then the text done try checkAge(-5) catch ? \"caught: \" + cCatchError done checkAge(30) ? \"30 is fine\" # 2. Files: write() and read() work with the whole text of a file; fexists() asks if it exists cFile = \"ring_intro_demo.txt\" write(cFile,\"line one\" + nl + \"line two\" + nl) ? f"}, {"u": "ring-intro.html#ring-and-other-languages", "t": "Quick introduction to Ring", "h": "Ring and other languages", "x": "Ring and other languages # Some differences from other popular languages, as facts: Often in other languages In Ring declarations, types none: the value has the type == to compare = (the context decides); != for different arrays from 0 lists from 1 integers and decimals one number type, a double \"a\" + 1 and 1 + \"a\" the same the left side decides methods overloaded by parameters one function per name, with a fixed number of parameters { } blocks words in the first style ( ok , next , end , off , done ); braces are allowed too string is characters text is a sequence of bytes (any byte, usually U"}, {"u": "ring-intro.html#where-to-go-next", "t": "Quick introduction to Ring", "h": "Where to go next", "x": "Where to go next # A Ring program uses .NET with two lines: load \"dotnet.ring\" using { \"System\" \"System.Text\" } oBuilder = new StringBuilder(\"Hello\") ? oBuilder.Append(\", Ring!\").ToString() Then: Getting started : install the package and run the first program. Core concepts : how .NET names, overloads, properties and enums look in Ring. The tutorial chapter The basics : ten short lessons. Rules and limits : the points of Ring that matter when you use .NET."}];